Lab.catalog([{"id":"kotlin","title":"Kotlin","description":"The language Android is written in, from val to sealed classes and exceptions.","requires":[],"file":"tracks/kotlin.js","lessons":[{"id":"val","title":"Naming a value with val","tagline":"Give a value a name, then use the name.","level":"beginner","goals":["Store a value under a name with <code>val</code>","Use the name to get the value back","Pick good names for your values"],"remember":"val name = value makes a named box. Use the name without quotes to get the value back.","terms":[{"term":"variable","def":"A named box that holds a value."},{"term":"val","def":"Makes a variable whose value is set once and never replaced."},{"term":"value","def":"A piece of data, such as <code>\"Ada\"</code> or <code>36</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val x = 5</code>. What does <code>println(x * 2)</code> print?","options":["<code>x * 2</code>","<code>10</code>","<code>5</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>x</code> holds 5, so <code>x * 2</code> is 10."},{"q":"Which is a valid variable name?","options":["<code>first name</code>","<code>firstName</code>","<code>1stName</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Names can’t contain spaces or start with a digit. <code>firstName</code> is the usual Kotlin style."}]},{"id":"var","title":"Values that change with var","tagline":"Some boxes need new contents over time.","level":"beginner","goals":["Make a value that can change with <code>var</code>","Update a value with <code>+=</code>, <code>-=</code> and <code>++</code>","Choose between <code>val</code> and <code>var</code>"],"remember":"var can get a new value, val can’t. Start with val; switch to var only when the value must change.","terms":[{"term":"var","def":"Makes a variable that can be given a new value later."},{"term":"reassign","def":"Put a new value in an existing <code>var</code>: <code>score = 10</code>."},{"term":"+= and -=","def":"Short for adding to or subtracting from a variable: <code>x += 2</code> means <code>x = x + 2</code>."},{"term":"++","def":"Adds 1 to a variable: <code>count++</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>var x = 4</code>, then <code>x += 3</code>, then <code>x++</code>. What is <code>x</code>?","options":["<code>7</code>","<code>8</code>","<code>5</code>"],"a":1,"why":"4 + 3 is 7, and ++ adds 1: 8."},{"q":"A person’s birth year in your program. val or var?","options":["<code>val</code>, it never changes","<code>var</code>, just in case"],"a":0,"why":"It doesn’t change, so <code>val</code>. Use <code>var</code> only when the program changes it."}]},{"id":"types","title":"Types","tagline":"Every value is a kind of thing: text, whole number, decimal, yes/no.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name the four basic types, including <code>Boolean</code>","Write a type yourself after a colon","See how types catch mistakes before running"],"remember":"Every value has a type: String, Int, Double, Boolean. A box keeps its type, so mistakes show up before running.","terms":[{"term":"type","def":"The kind of a value: text, whole number, decimal, true/false."},{"term":"Boolean","def":"A type with two values: <code>true</code> and <code>false</code>."},{"term":"type inference","def":"Kotlin working out a variable’s type from its first value."},{"term":"type mismatch","def":"The error you get when a value doesn’t fit a variable’s type."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What type is <code>val ready = false</code>?","options":["<code>String</code>","<code>Boolean</code>","<code>Int</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>true</code> and <code>false</code> are the two <code>Boolean</code> values."},{"q":"What type is <code>val code = \"42\"</code>?","options":["<code>Int</code>","<code>String</code>"],"a":1,"why":"The quotes make it text, even though it looks like a number."},{"q":"When does Kotlin report a type mismatch?","options":["Before the program runs","Only when that line runs"],"a":0,"why":"It is a compile error, so you find it straight away."}]},{"id":"templates","title":"Putting values into text","tagline":"Build sentences from variables with $.","level":"beginner","goals":["Put a variable into text with <code>$name</code>","Put a calculation into text with <code>${ }</code>","Join pieces of text with <code>+</code>"],"remember":"\"$name\" puts a variable into text; \"${a + b}\" puts a calculation in. + joins texts.","terms":[{"term":"string template","def":"Text with <code>$name</code> or <code>${...}</code> inside, replaced by values when the program runs."},{"term":"concatenation","def":"Joining texts together with <code>+</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val n = 3</code>. What does <code>println(\"Items: $n\")</code> print?","options":["<code>Items: $n</code>","<code>Items: 3</code>","<code>Items: n</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>$n</code> is replaced with the value 3."},{"q":"<code>val n = 3</code>. What does <code>println(\"Next: ${n + 1}\")</code> print?","options":["<code>Next: 4</code>","<code>Next: 3 + 1</code>","<code>Next: 31</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The expression inside <code>${ }</code> is calculated first."}]},{"id":"conversion","title":"Converting between types","tagline":"\"5\" is text. Turn it into a number before doing maths.","level":"beginner","goals":["Turn text into a number with <code>toInt()</code>","Turn numbers into text with <code>toString()</code>","Know when a conversion crashes"],"remember":"Text that looks like a number is still text. Convert with toInt() / toDouble() before maths; toString() goes the other way.","terms":[{"term":"toInt()","def":"Turns text like <code>\"5\"</code> (or a decimal) into an <code>Int</code>."},{"term":"toDouble()","def":"Turns text or a whole number into a <code>Double</code>."},{"term":"toString()","def":"Turns a value into text."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>println(\"2\" + 2)</code> print?","options":["<code>4</code>","<code>22</code>","An error"],"a":1,"why":"Text on the left, so <code>+</code> joins: \"2\" then 2."},{"q":"What is <code>7.8.toInt()</code>?","options":["<code>8</code>","<code>7</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Converting to <code>Int</code> drops the decimals; it doesn’t round."},{"q":"What happens with <code>\"ten\".toInt()</code>?","options":["It gives 10","The program crashes","It gives 0"],"a":1,"why":"There is no number in the text, so conversion fails while running."}]},{"id":"booleans","title":"True or false","tagline":"Comparisons answer yes-or-no questions.","level":"beginner","goals":["Compare values with <code>==</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code> and friends","Get a <code>true</code> or <code>false</code> answer","Tell <code>=</code> and <code>==</code> apart"],"remember":"Comparisons (== != < > <= >=) give true or false. = stores a value, == compares.","terms":[{"term":"comparison","def":"A question like <code>a &gt; b</code> whose answer is <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>."},{"term":"==","def":"Asks whether two values are equal. Not the same as <code>=</code>, which stores a value."},{"term":"!=","def":"Asks whether two values are different."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is <code>5 != 5</code>?","options":["<code>true</code>","<code>false</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>!=</code> asks “are they different?”. They are not."},{"q":"Which one compares two values?","options":["<code>a = b</code>","<code>a == b</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>==</code> compares; <code>=</code> stores."},{"q":"What is <code>10 &lt;= 10</code>?","options":["<code>true</code>","<code>false</code>"],"a":0,"why":"“Less than or equal”: they are equal, so yes."}]},{"id":"logic","title":"Combining conditions","tagline":"and, or, not.","level":"beginner","goals":["Combine conditions with <code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>||</code>","Flip a condition with <code>!</code>","Read combined conditions like a sentence"],"remember":"&& is and (both true), || is or (at least one), ! is not (flips it).","terms":[{"term":"&&","def":"“and”: true only if both sides are true."},{"term":"||","def":"“or”: true if at least one side is true."},{"term":"!","def":"“not”: turns true into false and false into true."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is <code>true || false</code>?","options":["<code>true</code>","<code>false</code>"],"a":0,"why":"For <b>or</b>, one true side is enough."},{"q":"What is <code>!(5 &gt; 3)</code>?","options":["<code>true</code>","<code>false</code>"],"a":1,"why":"5 &gt; 3 is true, and <code>!</code> flips it to false."}]},{"id":"if","title":"Making decisions with if","tagline":"Run some steps only when a condition is true.","level":"beginner","goals":["Run steps only when a condition is true","Handle the other case with <code>else</code>","Check several cases with <code>else if</code>"],"remember":"if (condition) { ... } runs only when the condition is true. else covers the rest. Only one branch runs.","terms":[{"term":"if","def":"Runs a block only when its condition is true."},{"term":"else","def":"The block that runs when the <code>if</code> condition is false."},{"term":"block","def":"Steps grouped between <code>{</code> and <code>}</code>."},{"term":"condition","def":"A true/false question, like <code>age &lt; 6</code>, that decides which way to go."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val x = 5</code>. What prints?<pre class=\"code\"><code>if (x &gt; 10) {\n    println(\"big\")\n} else {\n    println(\"small\")\n}</code></pre>","options":["<code>big</code>","<code>small</code>","Both"],"a":1,"why":"5 is not greater than 10, so the else block runs."},{"q":"In an <code>if / else if / else</code> chain, how many blocks run?","options":["Exactly one","Every block whose condition is true","None or all"],"a":0,"why":"Kotlin runs the first true branch, or <code>else</code>, and skips the rest."}]},{"id":"if-expression","title":"if gives back a value","tagline":"Choose a value, not just an action.","level":"beginner","goals":["Pick one of two values with <code>if</code>","Know why this <code>if</code> needs an <code>else</code>","Explain what an expression is"],"remember":"val x = if (condition) a else b picks one of two values. The else is required.","terms":[{"term":"expression","def":"Code that produces a value, like <code>2 + 3</code> or <code>if (a) x else y</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val n = 4</code>. What is <code>if (n &gt; 3) \"high\" else \"low\"</code>?","options":["<code>\"high\"</code>","<code>\"low\"</code>"],"a":0,"why":"4 is greater than 3, so the first value is chosen."},{"q":"Why does <code>val x = if (c) 1</code> fail?","options":["Missing else: x needs a value either way","if can’t give back numbers"],"a":0,"why":"Without <code>else</code>, there is no value when the condition is false."}]},{"id":"when","title":"Choosing among many cases","tagline":"when: a tidy way to write a long if chain.","level":"beginner","goals":["Replace a long <code>if</code> chain with <code>when</code>","Match several values or a range in one case","Get a value back from <code>when</code>"],"remember":"when (x) { value -> result ... else -> fallback } picks the first matching case. a..b is a range, both ends included.","terms":[{"term":"when","def":"Picks the first matching case from a list. Can give back a value."},{"term":"range","def":"<code>a..b</code>: all numbers from a to b, both included."},{"term":"->","def":"Separates a <code>when</code> case from its result."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Is 10 in <code>1..10</code>?","options":["Yes","No"],"a":0,"why":"<code>..</code> includes both ends."},{"q":"Two cases match the value. Which one runs?","options":["The first one","The last one","Both"],"a":0,"why":"<code>when</code> stops at the first match."}]},{"id":"while","title":"Repeating with while","tagline":"Do the same steps again and again, as long as a condition is true.","level":"beginner","goals":["Repeat steps with a <code>while</code> loop","Use a counter to end the loop","Spot and avoid an infinite loop"],"remember":"while (condition) { ... } repeats the block as long as the condition is true. Change the counter inside, or it never stops.","terms":[{"term":"loop","def":"Code that repeats a block of steps."},{"term":"while","def":"Repeats a block as long as its condition is <code>true</code>. Checks before every round."},{"term":"loop counter","def":"A <code>var</code> that changes each round, so the loop knows when to stop."},{"term":"infinite loop","def":"A loop whose condition never becomes false, so it never ends."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>var n = 10</code>, then <code>while (n &lt; 5) { println(n) }</code>. What is printed?","options":["10","Nothing","10, for ever"],"a":1,"why":"The condition is checked first. 10 &lt; 5 is false, so the block never runs."},{"q":"What makes a <code>while</code> loop stop?","options":["Its condition becomes false","It has run 10 times","The block reaches its <code>}</code>"],"a":0,"why":"At <code>}</code> it goes back up and checks again. Only a false condition ends it."}]},{"id":"for-ranges","title":"Counting with for","tagline":"Go through a range of numbers, one at a time.","level":"beginner","goals":["Count through numbers with <code>for</code> and <code>1..5</code>","Count down or skip with <code>downTo</code> and <code>step</code>","Leave out the end with <code>until</code>"],"remember":"for (x in 1..5) runs once per number. until leaves out the end, downTo counts down, step jumps.","terms":[{"term":"for","def":"Runs a block once for each value in a range. The name in brackets holds the current value."},{"term":"until","def":"<code>a until b</code>: from a up to b, leaving b out."},{"term":"downTo","def":"<code>a downTo b</code>: counts down from a to b, both included."},{"term":"step","def":"How far to jump each round: <code>0..10 step 2</code> gives 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10."}],"quiz":[{"q":"How many times does <code>for (i in 1 until 4)</code> run its block?","options":["3","4","5"],"a":0,"why":"1, 2, 3. <code>until</code> stops before 4."},{"q":"Which numbers does <code>for (n in 0..6 step 3)</code> go through?","options":["0, 3, 6","0, 3","3, 6"],"a":0,"why":"It starts at 0 and jumps by 3. The end, 6, is part of the range."},{"q":"What does <code>for (n in 3..1) { println(n) }</code> print?","options":["3, 2, 1","1, 2, 3","Nothing"],"a":2,"why":"<code>3..1</code> would have to go up from 3 to 1, which is impossible, so the range is empty. Use <code>downTo</code>."}]},{"id":"functions","title":"Writing your own function","tagline":"Give a group of steps a name, then use it as often as you like.","level":"beginner","goals":["Write your own function with <code>fun</code>","Call a function by its name","Reuse steps instead of copying them"],"remember":"fun name() { ... } defines a function. name() calls it: the steps run, then the program continues after the call.","terms":[{"term":"function","def":"A named group of steps you can run whenever you need them."},{"term":"define","def":"Write a function with <code>fun</code>: its name and its steps. This does not run it."},{"term":"call","def":"Run a function by writing its name with brackets: <code>greet()</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"When do the steps inside a function run?","options":["When the program starts","Each time the function is called","Once, where the function is written"],"a":1,"why":"Defining only describes the steps. Each call runs them."},{"q":"<code>main</code> calls <code>cheer()</code> three times. How many times do <code>cheer</code>’s steps run?","options":["1","3","4"],"a":1,"why":"Once per call."}]},{"id":"parameters","title":"Giving a function inputs","tagline":"Parameters let one function work with different values.","level":"beginner","goals":["Give a function inputs with parameters","Pass values to it as arguments","Match arguments to parameters in order and type"],"remember":"Parameters (name: Type) are a function’s inputs. Arguments are the values you pass in a call, in the same order.","terms":[{"term":"parameter","def":"An input a function asks for, written <code>name: Type</code> in its brackets."},{"term":"argument","def":"The value you pass for a parameter when you call: the <code>\"Tea\"</code> in <code>printPrice(\"Tea\", 3)</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In <code>fun add(a: Int, b: Int)</code>, what are <code>a</code> and <code>b</code>?","options":["Parameters","Arguments","Types"],"a":0,"why":"The names in the definition are parameters. The values in a call are arguments."},{"q":"How many arguments does a call to <code>fun move(x: Int, y: Int, speed: Int)</code> need?","options":["1","2","3"],"a":2,"why":"One for each parameter, separated by commas."}]},{"id":"return","title":"Getting a result back","tagline":"return hands a value back to whoever called the function.","level":"beginner","goals":["Get a value back from a function with <code>return</code>","Write a function’s return type","Use the short <code>= value</code> form"],"remember":"fun f(...): Type { return value } gives a value back. No return type means Unit. Short form: fun f(...) = value.","terms":[{"term":"return","def":"Ends the function and hands a value back to where it was called."},{"term":"return type","def":"The type of value a function gives back, written after its brackets: <code>): Int</code>."},{"term":"Unit","def":"The return type of a function that gives back no useful value. Used when you leave the return type out."},{"term":"single-expression function","def":"A short form, <code>fun f(n: Int) = n * 2</code>, where the value after <code>=</code> is the result."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>println(next(4))</code> print, with <code>fun next(n: Int) = n + 1</code>?","options":["4","5","Nothing"],"a":1,"why":"The short form gives back the value after <code>=</code>: 4 + 1."},{"q":"A function has no return type written. What does it return?","options":["<code>Int</code>","<code>Unit</code>, no useful value","Whatever it prints"],"a":1,"why":"Leaving the return type out means <code>Unit</code>. Printing is not returning."},{"q":"What happens to the lines after <code>return</code> in the same block?","options":["They run after returning","They never run: return ends the function","They run first"],"a":1,"why":"<code>return</code> leaves the function straight away."}]},{"id":"default-args","title":"Default and named arguments","tagline":"Give parameters a usual value, and name arguments to make calls clear.","level":"beginner","goals":["Give a parameter a default value","Name arguments in a call to make it clear","Skip arguments that have a default"],"remember":"name: Type = value gives a parameter a default. name = value in a call is a named argument: any order, skip what has a default.","terms":[{"term":"default value","def":"A value a parameter uses when the call leaves it out: <code>size: String = \"medium\"</code>."},{"term":"named argument","def":"An argument written with its parameter’s name, like <code>size = \"large\"</code>. Order doesn’t matter."}],"quiz":[{"q":"With <code>fun greet(name: String, greeting: String = \"Hello\")</code>, what does <code>greet(\"Ada\")</code> print?","options":["<code>Hello, Ada!</code>","<code>Ada, Hello!</code>","An error"],"a":0,"why":"<code>greeting</code> uses its default, <code>\"Hello\"</code>."},{"q":"Is <code>greet(greeting = \"Hi\", name = \"Lin\")</code> allowed, with the names in a different order?","options":["Yes, names decide where each value goes","No, arguments must follow the parameter order"],"a":0,"why":"With named arguments, the order doesn’t matter."}]},{"id":"scope","title":"Where a name exists","tagline":"A box made inside a block lives only inside that block.","level":"beginner","goals":["Know where a name can be used","Use outer names from inside a block","Avoid confusing names that hide each other"],"remember":"A name exists from where it is made to the end of its block. Inner blocks can see outer names, not the other way round.","terms":[{"term":"scope","def":"The part of the code where a name exists and can be used."},{"term":"local variable","def":"A box made inside a block or function. It exists only until that block’s <code>}</code>."},{"term":"shadowing","def":"An inner box with the same name as an outer one, hiding it inside the inner block."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A box is made inside a <code>for</code> block. Can you use it after the loop?","options":["Yes","No, it only exists inside the block"],"a":1,"why":"It is local to the block and disappears at its <code>}</code>."},{"q":"Two different functions each make a <code>val message</code>. What happens?","options":["A compile error: the name is taken","Nothing bad: each one is local to its own function","The second replaces the first"],"a":1,"why":"Each <code>message</code> lives only inside its own function, so they never meet."}]},{"id":"null","title":"When there might be no value","tagline":"Sometimes there is nothing to put in the box. Kotlin calls that null.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what <code>null</code> means","Allow a missing value with a <code>?</code> type","Read a number safely with <code>toIntOrNull()</code>"],"remember":"null means “no value”. Only a type with ? (like String? or Int?) may hold it.","terms":[{"term":"null","def":"A special value meaning “no value here”."},{"term":"nullable type","def":"A type with <code>?</code> after it, like <code>String?</code>. Its box may hold null."},{"term":"toIntOrNull()","def":"Turns text into an <code>Int</code>, or gives null if there is no number in it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which line compiles?","options":["<code>val a: Int = null</code>","<code>val a: Int? = null</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Only a nullable type, with <code>?</code>, may hold null."},{"q":"What is <code>\"twelve\".toIntOrNull()</code>?","options":["<code>12</code>","<code>0</code>","<code>null</code>"],"a":2,"why":"There are no digits in the text, so it gives back null instead of crashing."},{"q":"<code>val score: Int? = 5</code>. Why does <code>score + 1</code> not compile?","options":["The box is an <code>Int?</code>, so Kotlin must assume it might be null","You can’t add to 5"],"a":0,"why":"Kotlin goes by the type. <code>Int?</code> means “a number, or nothing”, and you can’t add 1 to nothing."}]},{"id":"null-safety","title":"Working safely with null","tagline":"Use a value that might be missing, without crashing.","level":"beginner","goals":["Check for <code>null</code> before using a value","Use <code>?.</code> and <code>?:</code> to avoid crashes","Know why <code>!!</code> is risky"],"remember":"Check with if (x != null), call with ?., fall back with ?:. Avoid !!: it crashes when the value is null.","terms":[{"term":"safe call (?.)","def":"Makes the call only when the value is not null; otherwise the result is null."},{"term":"Elvis (?:)","def":"<code>a ?: b</code> gives <code>a</code>, or <code>b</code> when <code>a</code> is null."},{"term":"smart cast","def":"After a check like <code>x != null</code>, Kotlin treats <code>x</code> as not null inside that block."},{"term":"!!","def":"Says “this is not null”. Crashes with a <code>NullPointerException</code> if it is."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val n: Int? = null</code>. What is <code>n ?: 5</code>?","options":["<code>5</code>","<code>null</code>","<code>0</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The left side is null, so Elvis gives the right side."},{"q":"<code>val s: String? = null</code>. What does <code>println(s?.toIntOrNull())</code> print?","options":["<code>null</code>","<code>0</code>","It crashes"],"a":0,"why":"The safe call is skipped because <code>s</code> is null, and the whole thing becomes null."},{"q":"What does <code>!!</code> do when the value is null?","options":["Crashes the program","Gives back 0","Skips the line"],"a":0,"why":"It promises the value is not null. When that promise is broken, you get a <code>NullPointerException</code>."}]},{"id":"lists","title":"Lists","tagline":"Keep many values together, in order.","level":"beginner","goals":["Keep many values in order with <code>listOf</code>","Read an item by its index, starting at 0","Get a list’s length with <code>size</code>"],"remember":"listOf(a, b, c) keeps items in order. Indexes start at 0, so the last one is size - 1.","terms":[{"term":"list","def":"Several values kept in order in one box, made with <code>listOf(...)</code>."},{"term":"index","def":"An item’s position in a list, counting from 0."},{"term":"List<String>","def":"The type of a list whose items are all Strings."},{"term":"out of bounds","def":"Asking for an index the list doesn’t have. The program crashes."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val n = listOf(5, 8, 2)</code>. What is <code>n[1]</code>?","options":["<code>5</code>","<code>8</code>","<code>2</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Index 0 is 5, index 1 is 8."},{"q":"A list has 4 items. What is its last index?","options":["<code>4</code>","<code>3</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Counting from 0: 0, 1, 2, 3."},{"q":"What is <code>listOf(5, 8, 2).size</code>?","options":["<code>3</code>","<code>2</code>","<code>15</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>size</code> counts the items. It doesn’t add them up."}]},{"id":"list-loop","title":"Going through a list","tagline":"Do something with every item, one by one.","level":"beginner","goals":["Do something with every item using <code>for</code>","Get each item’s position with <code>indices</code>","Get item and position together with <code>withIndex()</code>"],"remember":"for (item in list) runs once per item. Use indices or withIndex() when you also need the position.","terms":[{"term":"for (item in list)","def":"A loop that runs its block once for every item, with <code>item</code> holding the current one."},{"term":"indices","def":"The range of valid indexes of a list, like <code>0..2</code>."},{"term":"withIndex()","def":"Lets a loop get the index and the item together: <code>for ((i, item) in list.withIndex())</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does this print?<pre class=\"code\"><code>var total = 0\nfor (n in listOf(2, 3, 5)) {\n    total += n\n}\nprintln(total)</code></pre>","options":["<code>10</code>","<code>5</code>","<code>2</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The loop adds every item: 2 + 3 + 5."},{"q":"<code>val names = listOf(\"Ada\", \"Sam\", \"Lin\")</code>. What is <code>names.indices</code>?","options":["<code>0..2</code>","<code>1..3</code>","<code>0..3</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Three items have the indexes 0, 1 and 2."},{"q":"In <code>for ((i, name) in names.withIndex())</code>, what is <code>i</code> in the first round?","options":["<code>0</code>","<code>1</code>","<code>\"Ada\"</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>i</code> gets the index, and indexes start at 0. The item goes into <code>name</code>."}]},{"id":"mutable-lists","title":"Lists that change","tagline":"Add and remove items while the program runs.","level":"beginner","goals":["Make a list that can change with <code>mutableListOf</code>","Add, remove and replace items","Know when to stay with <code>listOf</code>"],"remember":"mutableListOf can change: add() at the end, remove() an item, list[i] = x to replace. listOf can only be read.","terms":[{"term":"mutable","def":"Can be changed after it is made."},{"term":"mutableListOf","def":"Makes a list you can add to, remove from and change."},{"term":"add() / remove()","def":"<code>add(x)</code> puts <code>x</code> at the end; <code>remove(x)</code> takes the first <code>x</code> out."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val l = mutableListOf(\"a\", \"b\")</code>, then <code>l.add(\"c\")</code>, then <code>l.remove(\"a\")</code>. What is <code>l</code>?","options":["<code>[b, c]</code>","<code>[a, b]</code>","<code>[a, b, c]</code>"],"a":0,"why":"\"c\" goes on the end, then \"a\" is taken out."},{"q":"Can a <code>val</code> hold a mutable list that you add to?","options":["Yes: val stops you replacing the list, not changing what is in it","No: you need var"],"a":0,"why":"The box keeps the same list. Adding items changes the list, not the box."},{"q":"<code>val l = mutableListOf(\"x\")</code>. What does <code>l.remove(\"y\")</code> do?","options":["Nothing changes","The program crashes","It removes \"x\""],"a":0,"why":"There is no \"y\" to remove, so the list stays as it is. (<code>remove</code> gives back <code>false</code> to say so.)"}]},{"id":"sets","title":"Sets","tagline":"A collection where every value appears only once.","level":"beginner","goals":["Keep each value once with <code>setOf</code>","Check if a value is there with <code>in</code>","Choose between a set and a list"],"remember":"setOf keeps each value once. Ask contains(x), or x in set; there are no positions.","terms":[{"term":"set","def":"A collection where each value appears at most once, made with <code>setOf(...)</code>."},{"term":"duplicate","def":"The same value a second time."},{"term":"contains()","def":"Gives <code>true</code> if the value is in the collection. <code>x in s</code> means the same."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is <code>setOf(1, 1, 2, 2, 2).size</code>?","options":["<code>5</code>","<code>2</code>","<code>1</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Only two different values: 1 and 2."},{"q":"<code>val s = setOf(\"a\", \"b\")</code>. What is <code>\"c\" in s</code>?","options":["<code>true</code>","<code>false</code>"],"a":1,"why":"\"c\" was never put in the set."},{"q":"When is a set a better choice than a list?","options":["When each value should appear once and you mainly ask “is it in there?”","When you need items by position"],"a":0,"why":"Sets drop duplicates and answer <code>contains</code>. Positions need a list."}]},{"id":"maps","title":"Maps","tagline":"Look up a value by its key, like a word in a dictionary.","level":"beginner","goals":["Store key–value pairs with <code>mapOf</code>","Look up a value by its key","Handle a missing key without crashing"],"remember":"mapOf(key to value) looks values up by key. A missing key gives null, so use ?: or getOrDefault.","terms":[{"term":"map","def":"A collection of key-value pairs, made with <code>mapOf(...)</code>."},{"term":"key","def":"What you look a value up by. Each key appears once in a map."},{"term":"to","def":"Joins a key and its value into a pair: <code>\"Ada\" to 36</code>."},{"term":"getOrDefault()","def":"Looks up a key, or gives the fallback you pass when the key is missing."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val m = mapOf(\"a\" to 1, \"b\" to 2)</code>. What is <code>m[\"b\"]</code>?","options":["<code>2</code>","<code>\"b\"</code>","<code>null</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The key \"b\" belongs to the value 2."},{"q":"Same map. What is <code>m[\"z\"]</code>?","options":["<code>null</code>","<code>0</code>","The program crashes"],"a":0,"why":"There is no key \"z\", so the lookup gives null."},{"q":"<code>val m = mutableMapOf(\"a\" to 1)</code>, then <code>m[\"a\"] = 5</code>. What does <code>println(m)</code> print?","options":["<code>{a=5}</code>","<code>{a=1, a=5}</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A key appears only once. Setting it again replaces its value."}]},{"id":"lambdas","title":"Functions as values","tagline":"A piece of code you can keep in a box and use later.","level":"beginner","goals":["Keep a piece of code in a <code>val</code> as a lambda","Call a lambda and read its type","Use <code>it</code> for a single parameter"],"remember":"{ x: Int -> x * 2 } is a function you can keep in a val and call. (Int) -> Int is its type. With one known parameter, call it it.","terms":[{"term":"lambda","def":"A function without a name, written in <code>{ }</code>, that you can keep in a variable."},{"term":"it","def":"The name you can use for a lambda’s only parameter when Kotlin already knows there is one."},{"term":"function type","def":"The type of a function value, like <code>(Int) -&gt; Int</code> or <code>() -&gt; Unit</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val f = { a: Int -&gt; a + 1 }</code>. What is <code>f(9)</code>?","options":["<code>10</code>","<code>9</code>","<code>a + 1</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Calling <code>f</code> runs its code with <code>a</code> = 9."},{"q":"What does the type <code>(String) -&gt; Int</code> mean?","options":["Takes a String, gives back an Int","Takes an Int, gives back a String"],"a":0,"why":"Inputs are in the brackets on the left, the result is after the arrow."},{"q":"In <code>val triple: (Int) -&gt; Int = { it * 3 }</code>, what is <code>it</code>?","options":["The one value the lambda receives","A variable you must create first"],"a":0,"why":"The type says there is one Int parameter, so Kotlin lets you call it <code>it</code>."}]},{"id":"function-params","title":"Functions that take functions","tagline":"Hand a function a job to do, written as a lambda.","level":"beginner","goals":["Write a function that takes another function","Pass a lambda as an argument","Write a lambda after the brackets"],"remember":"A parameter like action: (Int) -> Int takes a function. Pass a lambda; when it is last, write it after the (); when it is the only argument, drop the ().","terms":[{"term":"function-type parameter","def":"A parameter whose type is a function type, like <code>action: () -&gt; Unit</code>. It takes a lambda."},{"term":"trailing lambda","def":"A lambda written after the round brackets of a call, when it is the last argument. If it is the only one, the empty <code>()</code> can go."}],"quiz":[{"q":"With <code>fun applyTo(n: Int, action: (Int) -&gt; Int) = action(n)</code>, what is <code>applyTo(3) { it + 1 }</code>?","options":["<code>4</code>","<code>3</code>","<code>1</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The lambda runs with <code>it</code> = 3, and 3 + 1 is 4."},{"q":"Which call means the same as <code>twice({ println(\"Hi\") })</code>?","options":["<code>twice { println(\"Hi\") }</code>","<code>twice(\"Hi\")</code>","<code>twice() println(\"Hi\")</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The lambda is the only argument, so it can go after the call and the empty <code>()</code> can be dropped."},{"q":"In <code>applyTo(5) { it * 2 }</code>, how does Kotlin know the lambda has one parameter called <code>it</code>?","options":["The parameter type <code>(Int) -&gt; Int</code> says it takes one Int","<code>it</code> is always 5","It guesses from the <code>*</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The function you pass it to says what kind of lambda it expects."}]},{"id":"collection-ops","title":"Transforming lists","tagline":"Ask a list questions in one line: filter, map, sum and more.","level":"beginner","goals":["Keep matching items with <code>filter</code>","Change every item with <code>map</code>","Answer questions with <code>sum</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>any</code>, <code>all</code>"],"remember":"filter keeps, map changes each item, sum/count/any/all give one answer, sortedBy puts in order. Each gives back something new.","terms":[{"term":"filter","def":"Gives a new list with only the items where the lambda gives true."},{"term":"map (on a list)","def":"Gives a new list where every item has been turned into something else by the lambda."},{"term":"any / all","def":"<code>any</code>: does at least one item match? <code>all</code>: do they all match?"},{"term":"sortedBy","def":"Gives a new list in order, smallest first, by the value the lambda picks for each item."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is <code>listOf(1, 2, 3, 4).filter { it &gt; 2 }</code>?","options":["<code>[3, 4]</code>","<code>[1, 2]</code>","<code>2</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Only 3 and 4 are greater than 2. <code>filter</code> gives back a list, not a count."},{"q":"What is <code>listOf(1, 2, 3).map { it + 10 }</code>?","options":["<code>[11, 12, 13]</code>","<code>36</code>","<code>[1, 2, 3, 10]</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>map</code> adds 10 to each item and keeps them all in a new list."},{"q":"What is <code>listOf(5, 8, 12).all { it &gt; 6 }</code>?","options":["<code>true</code>","<code>false</code>"],"a":1,"why":"5 is not greater than 6, so not all items match. (<code>any</code> would be true.)"}]},{"id":"classes","title":"Your own types: classes","tagline":"Bundle related values into one thing with its own name.","level":"beginner","goals":["Make your own type with <code>class</code>","Create objects from a class","Read and change an object’s properties"],"remember":"class Name(val a: Type, var b: Type) is the plan; Name(x, y) makes an object; object.a reads a property.","terms":[{"term":"class","def":"A plan for your own type: which properties every object of that type has."},{"term":"object","def":"One real thing made from a class, like <code>Player(\"Ada\", 0)</code>."},{"term":"property","def":"A value that belongs to an object, read with a dot: <code>ada.name</code>."},{"term":"constructor","def":"The parentheses after the class name: the values needed to make an object."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Given <code>class Dog(val name: String)</code>, how do you make a dog?","options":["<code>Dog(\"Rex\")</code>","<code>class Dog(\"Rex\")</code>","<code>Dog.name = \"Rex\"</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Write the class name and pass the constructor values in parentheses."},{"q":"<code>class Counter(var count: Int)</code>. After <code>val a = Counter(0)</code>, <code>val b = Counter(0)</code> and <code>a.count += 1</code>, what is <code>b.count</code>?","options":["<code>0</code>","<code>1</code>","<code>2</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>a</code> and <code>b</code> are separate objects, each with its own <code>count</code>."},{"q":"<code>class Car(val color: String, var km: Int)</code> and <code>val car = Car(\"red\", 0)</code>. Which line compiles?","options":["<code>car.km = 100</code>","<code>car.color = \"blue\"</code>","Both"],"a":0,"why":"<code>km</code> is a <code>var</code>, so it can change. <code>color</code> is a <code>val</code>."}]},{"id":"methods","title":"Functions inside a class","tagline":"Objects can do things, not only hold values.","level":"beginner","goals":["Write a function inside a class","Call it on an object with a dot","Use <code>this</code> to mean the object itself"],"remember":"A function inside a class works on the object it is called on: object.doSomething(). this means that object.","terms":[{"term":"member function","def":"A function written inside a class and called on an object: <code>ada.win(10)</code>. Also called a method."},{"term":"this","def":"Inside a class: the object the function was called on."},{"term":"state","def":"The current values of an object’s properties. Each object keeps its own."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>class Cup(var ml: Int) { fun fill() { ml = 250 } }</code>. After <code>val a = Cup(0)</code>, <code>val b = Cup(0)</code> and <code>a.fill()</code>, what is <code>b.ml</code>?","options":["<code>0</code>","<code>250</code>","<code>500</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>fill</code> ran on <code>a</code>, so only <code>a.ml</code> changed."},{"q":"Inside a member function, what does <code>this</code> mean?","options":["The object the function was called on","The class itself, for every object at once","The <code>main</code> function"],"a":0,"why":"In <code>ada.win(10)</code>, <code>this</code> is <code>ada</code>."},{"q":"How do you call <code>fill</code> on the cup <code>a</code>?","options":["<code>a.fill()</code>","<code>fill(a)</code>","<code>Cup.fill()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The object, a dot, then the function call."}]},{"id":"encapsulation-basics","title":"Hiding the inside","tagline":"private: let a class guard its own data.","level":"beginner","goals":["Hide a class’s data with <code>private</code>","Let others read but not change with <code>private set</code>","Change data only through checking functions"],"remember":"private: only the class itself can use it. private set under a var: everyone can read it, only the class can change it.","terms":[{"term":"private","def":"Only code inside the same class can use this property or function."},{"term":"private set","def":"Written under a <code>var</code> property: anyone can read it, only the class can change it."},{"term":"encapsulation","def":"Keeping an object’s inside hidden, so outside code goes through its functions."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Inside <code>class Phone</code> there is <code>private var pin = 0</code>. Where can <code>pin</code> be used?","options":["Only inside <code>Phone</code>","Anywhere in the program","Only in <code>main</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>private</code> limits it to the class it is written in."},{"q":"<code>var level = 1</code> with <code>private set</code> under it, in <code>class Game</code>. Outside, what does <code>println(game.level)</code> do?","options":["Prints <code>1</code>","Compile error","Prints nothing"],"a":0,"why":"Only changing is private. Reading is open to everyone."},{"q":"Why make a property private?","options":["So every change goes through the class’s own functions","It makes the program run faster","Kotlin requires it for every property"],"a":0,"why":"The class can check each change and keep its data valid."}]},{"id":"data-classes","title":"Data classes","tagline":"One word gives you printing, comparing and copying.","level":"beginner","goals":["Make a class that only holds values with <code>data</code>","Print and compare objects by their values","Make a changed copy with <code>copy</code>"],"remember":"data class: readable printing (toString), == compares values (equals), and copy(name = value) for a changed copy.","terms":[{"term":"data class","def":"A class for holding values. Kotlin writes <code>toString</code>, <code>equals</code> and <code>copy</code> for it."},{"term":"toString","def":"Turns an object into text. <code>println</code> uses it."},{"term":"equals","def":"Decides whether two objects are equal. <code>==</code> uses it."},{"term":"copy","def":"Makes a new object with the same values, except the ones you name."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>data class Cat(val name: String, val age: Int)</code>. What does <code>println(Cat(\"Tom\", 2))</code> print?","options":["<code>Cat(name=Tom, age=2)</code>","<code>Cat@1b6d3586</code>","<code>Tom 2</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A data class prints its name and each property with its value."},{"q":"<code>val a = Cat(\"Tom\", 2)</code>, then <code>val b = a.copy(age = 3)</code>. What is <code>a.age</code> now?","options":["<code>2</code>","<code>3</code>","An error"],"a":0,"why":"<code>copy</code> makes a new object. The original keeps its values."},{"q":"With the data class <code>Cat</code>, what is <code>Cat(\"Tom\", 2) == Cat(\"Tom\", 2)</code>?","options":["<code>true</code>","<code>false</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Same values in every property, so they are equal."}]},{"id":"enums","title":"A fixed set of choices","tagline":"enum class: a type with a short, fixed list of values.","level":"beginner","goals":["Make a type with a fixed list of values","Use an entry with a dot, like <code>Light.GREEN</code>","Cover every entry in a <code>when</code> without <code>else</code>"],"remember":"enum class Name { A, B, C } is a type with only those values. A when that covers every entry needs no else.","terms":[{"term":"enum class","def":"A type with a fixed list of values, such as <code>enum class Light { RED, YELLOW, GREEN }</code>."},{"term":"entry","def":"One value of an enum class, used with a dot: <code>Light.RED</code>."},{"term":"exhaustive","def":"A <code>when</code> that covers every possible value, so it needs no <code>else</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>enum class Coin { HEADS, TAILS }</code>. Which line is valid?","options":["<code>val c = Coin.HEADS</code>","<code>val c = Coin(\"HEADS\")</code>","<code>val c = HEADS</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Entries are used with the type name and a dot. You don’t create new ones."},{"q":"A <code>when</code> that gives back a value covers every entry of an enum. Does it need <code>else</code>?","options":["No","Yes, always"],"a":0,"why":"Kotlin can see that every possible value is handled."},{"q":"What does <code>println(Coin.TAILS)</code> print?","options":["<code>TAILS</code>","<code>Coin.TAILS</code>","<code>1</code>"],"a":0,"why":"An entry prints as its name."}]},{"id":"inheritance","title":"Building on a class","tagline":"A new class can start from an existing one and change what it needs.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Build a class on another with <code>open</code> and <code>:</code>","Change a parent’s function with <code>override</code>","Run the parent’s version with <code>super</code>"],"remember":"open class Parent, then class Child : Parent(). Mark a function open in the parent to override it in the child; super calls the parent’s version.","terms":[{"term":"inheritance","def":"Making a new class from an existing one, so it gets the parent’s properties and functions."},{"term":"open","def":"Allows a class to be built on, or a function to be overridden."},{"term":"override","def":"Replaces an open function from the parent with the child’s own version."},{"term":"super","def":"Inside the child: the parent’s version, as in <code>super.greet()</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>class Base</code> has no <code>open</code>. Does <code>class Child : Base()</code> compile?","options":["No: classes are closed unless marked open","Yes"],"a":0,"why":"Kotlin classes are final by default."},{"q":"Dog overrides <code>sound()</code> from Animal. <code>val d = Dog(\"Rex\")</code>. Which version does <code>d.sound()</code> run?","options":["Dog’s","Animal’s","Both"],"a":0,"why":"The object is a Dog, so its own override runs."},{"q":"Inside an override, what does <code>super.greet()</code> do?","options":["Runs the parent’s <code>greet</code>","Runs the override again","Makes a new parent object"],"a":0,"why":"<code>super</code> means the parent’s version of the function."}]},{"id":"interfaces","title":"Promises a class keeps","tagline":"interface: different classes, one shared promise.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Write a promise as an <code>interface</code>","Make a class keep it with <code>override</code>","Treat different classes the same through one interface"],"remember":"interface lists what a class promises to do. class X : TheInterface keeps it with override. Code that needs the interface accepts any class that keeps it.","terms":[{"term":"interface","def":"A list of functions a class promises to have, without saying how they work."},{"term":"implement","def":"To keep an interface’s promise: <code>class Dog : Speaker</code> plus an <code>override</code> for each function."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does an interface hold?","options":["Functions a class promises to have","A finished object, ready to use","Only text values"],"a":0,"why":"It says what a class can do. Each class writes how."},{"q":"<code>Square</code> and <code>Circle</code> both keep the <code>Shape</code> interface. Which can you pass to <code>fun report(shape: Shape)</code>?","options":["Both","Only <code>Square</code>","Neither"],"a":0,"why":"Any class that keeps the promise counts as a <code>Shape</code>."},{"q":"Which line makes Dog keep the <code>Speaker</code> interface?","options":["<code>class Dog : Speaker</code>","<code>class Dog : Speaker()</code>","<code>class Dog(Speaker)</code>"],"a":0,"why":"An interface has no constructor, so there are no parentheses after its name."}]},{"id":"objects","title":"One-of-a-kind objects","tagline":"When there should be exactly one: object.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Make exactly one of something with <code>object</code>","Use it by its name","Put shared things in a <code>companion object</code>"],"remember":"object Name { ... } makes exactly one, used by its name. A companion object holds what belongs to the class itself.","terms":[{"term":"object declaration","def":"<code>object Name { ... }</code>: a type with exactly one object, made by Kotlin and used by its name."},{"term":"companion object","def":"A block inside a class for things that belong to the class itself. Reached with the class name: <code>Player.guest()</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Five different functions all use <code>object Scoreboard</code>. How many Scoreboards are there?","options":["Exactly one","Five","None until someone writes <code>Scoreboard()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"An <code>object</code> always has exactly one, and every use reaches that same one."},{"q":"<code>class Player(val name: String) { companion object { fun guest() = Player(\"Guest\") } }</code>. How do you call <code>guest</code>?","options":["<code>Player.guest()</code>","<code>guest.Player()</code>","<code>Player(\"Ada\").guest</code>"],"a":0,"why":"What is in the companion object belongs to the class, so you reach it through the class name."},{"q":"Which is a good fit for <code>object</code>?","options":["The app’s settings","Each song in a playlist"],"a":0,"why":"There is one set of settings. There are many songs, so a song is a class."}]},{"id":"sealed","title":"A closed family of types","tagline":"sealed: every possible case, known in advance.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Make a closed family of types with <code>sealed</code>","Check which type a value is with <code>is</code>","Handle every case in a <code>when</code> without <code>else</code>"],"remember":"sealed closes a family of types. when over it uses is Type -> and needs no else when every member is handled.","terms":[{"term":"sealed","def":"Marks a class or interface whose members are all known: they are written next to it (same package and module; in this course, the same file)."},{"term":"is","def":"Checks whether a value is of a type: <code>is Done</code>. After it, Kotlin treats the value as that type."},{"term":"exhaustive","def":"A <code>when</code> that covers every possible case, so it needs no <code>else</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a <code>when</code> over a sealed type leave out <code>else</code>?","options":["Kotlin knows every member, so it can check all are handled","<code>else</code> is never allowed in <code>when</code>","Sealed types can’t be null"],"a":0,"why":"All members are written next to the sealed type, so Kotlin can see whether each one has a branch."},{"q":"You add <code>class Paused : Download</code>. What happens to <code>show</code>, which has no <code>else</code>?","options":["It stops compiling until it gets a Paused branch","Paused values print nothing","Nothing changes"],"a":0,"why":"The <code>when</code> is no longer exhaustive, and Kotlin points to it."},{"q":"In <code>is Done -&gt; \"${d.size} MB\"</code>, why does <code>d.size</code> work?","options":["After <code>is Done</code>, Kotlin knows <code>d</code> is a Done","Every Download has a size","It doesn’t compile"],"a":0,"why":"The <code>is</code> check works like a null check: inside that branch, Kotlin treats <code>d</code> as a Done."}]},{"id":"exceptions","title":"When something goes wrong at run time","tagline":"Catch a problem instead of crashing.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Catch a problem with <code>try</code> and <code>catch</code>","Run steps either way with <code>finally</code>","Report a problem yourself with <code>throw</code>"],"remember":"try { risky } catch (e: Exception) { handle } stops a crash. finally always runs. throw reports a problem yourself.","terms":[{"term":"exception","def":"An object describing a problem that happened while the program ran."},{"term":"throw","def":"Report a problem: stops the current code and hands the exception up to the caller."},{"term":"try / catch","def":"<code>try</code> runs risky code; if it throws, <code>catch</code> runs instead of crashing."},{"term":"finally","def":"A block after <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code> that runs every time."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does this print?<pre class=\"code\"><code>try {\n    println(\"A\")\n    throw Exception(\"oops\")\n    println(\"B\")\n} catch (e: Exception) {\n    println(\"C\")\n}</code></pre>","options":["<code>A</code> then <code>C</code>","<code>A</code>, <code>B</code>, <code>C</code>","Only <code>C</code>"],"a":0,"why":"\"A\" prints first. The throw skips the rest of <code>try</code>, so \"B\" never prints, and <code>catch</code> prints \"C\"."},{"q":"When does a <code>finally</code> block run?","options":["Every time","Only when something went wrong","Only when nothing went wrong"],"a":0,"why":"<code>finally</code> runs whether an exception happened or not."},{"q":"An exception is thrown and no <code>catch</code> handles it. What happens?","options":["The program crashes","Kotlin ignores it","The line is tried again"],"a":0,"why":"An exception that is never caught stops the program: a crash."}]},{"id":"extensions","title":"Adding functions to existing types","tagline":"Give Int or String a new function of your own.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Add your own function to <code>Int</code> or <code>String</code>","Use <code>this</code> for the value before the dot"],"remember":"fun Type.name() adds a function to an existing type. Inside, this is the value before the dot.","terms":[{"term":"extension function","def":"A function added to an existing type from outside it: <code>fun Int.isEven()</code>."},{"term":"receiver","def":"The value an extension is called on, the one before the dot. Inside, it is <code>this</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>fun Int.plusOne() = this + 1</code>. What is <code>5.plusOne()</code>?","options":["<code>6</code>","<code>5</code>","An error"],"a":0,"why":"<code>this</code> is 5, so 5 + 1 is 6."},{"q":"Does an extension function change the <code>String</code> type itself?","options":["No, it is a function you call with a dot","Yes, it is added inside String"],"a":0,"why":"String stays as it is. You call your function as if it were part of it."},{"q":"In <code>\"Oslo\".exclaim()</code>, what is the receiver?","options":["<code>\"Oslo\"</code>","<code>exclaim</code>","<code>String</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The receiver is the value before the dot."}]},{"id":"scope-functions","title":"let, apply, also, run","tagline":"Four small helpers that run a block on one object.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Run a block on one object with <code>let</code>, <code>apply</code>, <code>also</code>, <code>run</code>","Tell <code>it</code> from <code>this</code> in each one","Know which ones give back the object"],"remember":"let and also use it; apply and run use this. apply and also give back the object; let and run give back the block’s result.","terms":[{"term":"let","def":"Runs a block with the object as <code>it</code> and gives back the block’s result. Often used with <code>?.</code>."},{"term":"apply","def":"Runs a block with the object as <code>this</code> and gives back the object. Good for setting up."},{"term":"also","def":"Runs a block with the object as <code>it</code> and gives back the object. Good for an extra step, like printing."},{"term":"run","def":"Runs a block with the object as <code>this</code> and gives back the block’s result."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which scope function uses <code>this</code> and gives back the object itself?","options":["<code>apply</code>","<code>let</code>","<code>run</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>apply</code>: <code>this</code> inside, the object out. Ideal for setting things up."},{"q":"What is <code>val x = 10.let { it * 2 }</code>?","options":["<code>20</code>","<code>10</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>let</code> gives back the block’s result: 10 * 2."},{"q":"<code>name</code> is <code>null</code>. What does <code>name?.let { println(it) }</code> do?","options":["Nothing: the block doesn’t run","Prints <code>null</code>","Crashes"],"a":0,"why":"<code>?.</code> stops at null, so <code>let</code> is never called."}]},{"id":"properties","title":"Smarter properties","tagline":"Properties that check new values or work themselves out.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Check new values with a setter","Work a value out on every read with a getter","Use <code>field</code> to reach the property’s own box"],"remember":"set(value) { field = ... } checks new values; get() = ... works a value out on every read.","terms":[{"term":"setter","def":"Code that runs whenever a property gets a new value: <code>set(value) { ... }</code>."},{"term":"getter","def":"Code that runs whenever a property is read: <code>get() = ...</code>."},{"term":"backing field","def":"The box that really holds a property’s value. In a setter or getter it is called <code>field</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Inside <code>set(value) { ... }</code>, what is <code>field</code>?","options":["The box that really holds the property’s value","The new value being assigned","Another name for the class"],"a":0,"why":"<code>value</code> is the new value; <code>field</code> is where the property keeps it."},{"q":"<code>val area: Int get() = width * height</code>. When is the area worked out?","options":["Every time <code>area</code> is read","Once, when the object is made"],"a":0,"why":"A getter runs on every read, so it always matches the current values."},{"q":"Does <code>val isAlive: Boolean get() = health &gt; 0</code> keep a box of its own?","options":["No: the getter works the answer out on every read","Yes: it stores true or false once"],"a":0,"why":"A property with only a getter that never uses <code>field</code> has no backing field. It follows <code>health</code> automatically."}]},{"id":"late-init","title":"Values that arrive later","tagline":"lateinit and by lazy: no value yet, and that’s fine.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Fill a property later with <code>lateinit var</code>","Work a value out on first use with <code>by lazy</code>","Choose between the two"],"remember":"lateinit var: set it before you read it. val x by lazy { ... }: worked out on first read, then remembered.","terms":[{"term":"lateinit","def":"Marks a <code>var</code> that gets its value after the object is made, before anyone reads it."},{"term":"by lazy","def":"<code>val x by lazy { ... }</code>: the block runs on the first read, and its value is remembered."},{"term":"initialize","def":"Give a variable or property its first value."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You read a <code>lateinit</code> property before giving it a value. What happens?","options":["The program crashes","You get <code>null</code>","You get an empty text"],"a":0,"why":"It throws <code>UninitializedPropertyAccessException</code>, and if nothing catches it, the program stops."},{"q":"A <code>by lazy</code> property is read three times. How many times does its block run?","options":["Once","Three times","Never"],"a":0,"why":"It runs on the first read; the next reads get the remembered value."},{"q":"Which line compiles?","options":["<code>lateinit var name: String</code>","<code>lateinit var age: Int</code>","<code>lateinit val name: String</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>lateinit</code> needs a <code>var</code>, and a type like <code>String</code>, not <code>Int</code>."}]}]},{"id":"git","title":"Git and the command line","description":"Save, share and undo your work: the terminal, commits, branches, merging, rebasing, GitHub and pull requests, from zero.","requires":[],"file":"tracks/git.js","lessons":[{"id":"terminal","title":"The terminal","tagline":"A window where you type commands instead of clicking.","level":"beginner","goals":["Open a terminal and run a command","Find out where you are with <code>pwd</code> and <code>ls</code>","Move between folders with <code>cd</code> and make one with <code>mkdir</code>"],"remember":"pwd says where you are, ls shows what is here, cd moves you, cd .. goes up one level.","terms":[{"term":"terminal, command","def":"The terminal is a window where you type commands, such as <code>pwd</code>, run them with Enter, and read what they print."},{"term":"prompt","def":"The sign, here <code>$</code>, where the terminal waits for your next command."},{"term":"working directory","def":"The folder the terminal is standing in. Commands act on it."},{"term":"path","def":"The folders that lead to a place, separated by <code>/</code>, such as <code>/home/ada/projects</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>cd ..</code> do?","options":["Moves up one level, to the folder that holds this one","Runs the previous command again","Deletes the current folder"],"a":0,"why":"<code>..</code> always means the folder one level up."},{"q":"<code>cd projects</code> printed nothing. What happened?","options":["It failed","It worked: many commands print nothing when they succeed","The terminal froze"],"a":1,"why":"When something goes wrong, you get a message. Silence usually means it worked."},{"q":"In the Windows Command Prompt, which command lists what is in a folder?","options":["<code>pwd</code>","<code>dir</code>","<code>mkdir</code>"],"a":1,"why":"The Command Prompt uses <code>dir</code> where macOS, Linux and PowerShell use <code>ls</code>."}]},{"id":"what-git","title":"What version control is","tagline":"Keep every version of your project without a pile of copies.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what version control keeps for you","Say why copies named like <code>Main_final_v2.kt</code> stop working","Check that Git is installed"],"remember":"Version control keeps one copy of your files plus a list of saved versions, each with a note, an author and a time.","terms":[{"term":"version control","def":"A tool that saves versions of a project and keeps the full history."},{"term":"version","def":"A saved snapshot of the whole project, with a note about what changed."},{"term":"Git","def":"The most used version control tool. You run it in the terminal with <code>git</code>."},{"term":"option","def":"A word starting with <code>-</code> or <code>--</code> that changes what a command does, like <code>--version</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does a saved version in Git hold?","options":["Only the one file you changed","A snapshot of the project, plus what changed, who and when","A copy with <code>_v2</code> in its name"],"a":1,"why":"A version saves the whole project as it was, together with a note and its author and time."},{"q":"You run <code>git --version</code> and see <code>git version 2.43.0</code>. What does it mean?","options":["Git is installed and ready","Your project has 2.43 versions","Git must be updated first"],"a":0,"why":"The command only asks Git for its own version number. Getting one back means Git is there."}]},{"id":"init","title":"Starting a repository","tagline":"Tell Git which folder to keep a history for.","level":"beginner","goals":["Tell Git your name and email, once per computer","Turn a folder into a repository with <code>git init</code>","Ask Git what it sees with <code>git status</code>"],"remember":"git init once per project folder, then git status any time you want to know what Git sees.","terms":[{"term":"repository","def":"A project folder that Git keeps a history for. Short: repo."},{"term":".git","def":"The hidden folder <code>git init</code> creates, where Git stores the history."},{"term":"git status","def":"Asks Git what it sees in the repository right now."},{"term":"git config","def":"Sets Git options, such as your name. <code>--global</code> means for every project on this computer."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does Git keep a repository’s history?","options":["In a hidden <code>.git</code> folder inside the project","In your home folder","In a file named history.txt"],"a":0,"why":"<code>git init</code> creates <code>.git</code> inside the project folder, and everything Git saves goes there."},{"q":"After <code>git init</code>, <code>ls</code> shows nothing. Did it work?","options":["No, run it again","Yes: <code>.git</code> is hidden, and <code>ls -a</code> shows it"],"a":1,"why":"Names that start with a dot are hidden from a plain <code>ls</code>."},{"q":"You set your name with <code>git config --global</code>. When do you need to do it again?","options":["For every new project","Only on a new computer","Every day"],"a":1,"why":"<code>--global</code> saves it for every project on this computer."}]},{"id":"commits","title":"Saving a snapshot","tagline":"Pick changes with git add, save them with git commit.","level":"beginner","goals":["Tell the working folder, the staging area and commits apart","Put a file in the staging area with <code>git add</code>","Save a commit with a clear message"],"remember":"Edit, git add what belongs together, git commit -m \"what it does\".","terms":[{"term":"commit","def":"A saved snapshot of the project, with a message, an author and an id like <code>14d1672</code>."},{"term":"staging area","def":"The waiting list for the next commit. <code>git add</code> puts changes on it."},{"term":"working tree","def":"Git’s name for the working folder: your project as you see and edit it. Git prints it in messages like <code>working tree clean</code>."},{"term":"untracked","def":"A file Git sees but does not save, because it was never added."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>git add Main.kt</code> do?","options":["Saves a commit","Puts Main.kt in the staging area for the next commit","Creates a file named Main.kt"],"a":1,"why":"<code>add</code> only fills the waiting list. Nothing is saved until <code>git commit</code>."},{"q":"Which commit message will help you most a month from now?","options":["<code>\"stuff\"</code>","<code>\"Greet Oslo too\"</code>","<code>\"changes\"</code>"],"a":1,"why":"A good message says what the commit does."},{"q":"<code>git status</code> lists <code>todo.txt</code> under “Untracked files”. What does that mean?","options":["Git sees it but won’t save it until you add it","The file is broken","It is already in a commit"],"a":0,"why":"Untracked files have never been added, so no commit holds them."}]},{"id":"history","title":"Looking at history","tagline":"See what changed, when, and why.","level":"beginner","goals":["List your commits with <code>git log</code>","See changes you have not added yet with <code>git diff</code>","Open one commit with <code>git show</code>"],"remember":"git log lists commits, git diff shows what you have not added yet, git show opens one commit.","terms":[{"term":"hash","def":"A commit’s unique id, a long code like <code>7e085e9...</code>. The first 7 characters are usually enough."},{"term":"diff","def":"A list of changed lines: <code>+</code> for added, <code>-</code> for removed."},{"term":"git log","def":"Lists the commits, newest first."},{"term":"HEAD","def":"Marks the commit you are on now. In <code>git log</code> it shows as <code>(HEAD -&gt; main)</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In <code>git log</code>, which commit is listed first?","options":["The oldest","The newest"],"a":1,"why":"Newest first, so the latest work is at the top."},{"q":"A line in <code>git diff</code> starts with <code>-</code>. What does it mean?","options":["The line was removed","The line was added","The line has an error"],"a":0,"why":"<code>-</code> marks removed lines and <code>+</code> marks added ones."},{"q":"You changed <code>Main.kt</code> and ran <code>git add</code>. Now <code>git diff</code> prints nothing. Why?","options":["The change was lost","<code>git diff</code> only shows changes you have not added yet","Git is broken"],"a":1,"why":"The change is safe in the staging area. <code>git diff</code> shows only what is not added yet."}]},{"id":"ignore","title":"Ignoring files","tagline":"Keep secrets and generated files out of your history.","level":"beginner","goals":["Tell which files should never be committed","Write a <code>.gitignore</code> file","Check with <code>git status</code> that Git skips them"],"remember":"Secrets and generated files go in .gitignore, and .gitignore goes in a commit.","terms":[{"term":".gitignore","def":"A file listing what Git should skip, one pattern per line."},{"term":"pattern","def":"A line in <code>.gitignore</code> that matches names. <code>*</code> means any text, a trailing <code>/</code> means a folder."},{"term":"generated file","def":"A file a tool makes, such as compiled output or a log. It can always be made again."},{"term":"secret","def":"A password or private key. It must never be in a commit."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these belongs in <code>.gitignore</code>?","options":["<code>Main.kt</code>","<code>secrets.txt</code> with a private key","<code>.gitignore</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Secrets must never be saved. Your code and the <code>.gitignore</code> itself should be."},{"q":"What does the pattern <code>*.log</code> match?","options":["Only a file literally named *.log","Every file whose name ends in .log","Every folder"],"a":1,"why":"<code>*</code> stands for any text, so <code>debug.log</code> and <code>run.log</code> both match."},{"q":"Should you commit the <code>.gitignore</code> file?","options":["Yes, so everyone on the project skips the same files","No, it is private to you"],"a":0,"why":"Once it is committed, the rules come with the project."}]},{"id":"branches","title":"Branches","tagline":"Try an idea on its own line while the working version stays safe.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain a branch as a label on a commit","Make a branch and move to it with <code>git switch -c</code>","Read <code>HEAD</code> to see which branch you are on"],"remember":"A branch is a label on a commit. git switch -c makes one and moves you to it; HEAD shows where you are.","terms":[{"term":"branch","def":"A label that points to a commit and moves forward when you commit on it."},{"term":"HEAD","def":"Marks where you are: <code>HEAD -&gt; main</code> means you are on the branch main."},{"term":"main","def":"The usual name of a project’s first branch, where the working version lives."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is a branch in Git?","options":["A full copy of the project folder","A label that points to a commit","A backup on another computer"],"a":1,"why":"A branch is only a label, which is why making one is instant."},{"q":"<code>git branch</code> prints <code>* forecast</code> and <code>main</code>. What does the <code>*</code> mean?","options":["forecast is the branch you are on","forecast has unsaved files","forecast is the oldest branch"],"a":0,"why":"The <code>*</code> marks your current branch, the same one <code>HEAD</code> points to."},{"q":"You switch from <code>forecast</code> to <code>main</code>. What happens to <code>Forecast.kt</code>, which exists only on forecast?","options":["It is deleted for good","It leaves the folder, and comes back when you switch to forecast","It stays in the folder"],"a":1,"why":"Git makes the folder match the branch you are on. The file is safe in the forecast commit."}]},{"id":"merging","title":"Merging","tagline":"Bring the work from one branch into another.","level":"beginner","goals":["Bring a finished branch into <code>main</code> with <code>git merge</code>","Tell a fast-forward from a merge commit","Read what Git prints after a merge"],"remember":"Be on the branch that receives the work, then git merge the other one: a fast-forward if only one side moved, a merge commit if both did.","terms":[{"term":"merge","def":"<code>git merge name</code> brings the commits of branch <code>name</code> into the branch you are on."},{"term":"fast-forward","def":"A merge where Git only moves the branch label forward, because the branch you are on has no new commits of its own."},{"term":"merge commit","def":"A commit with two parents that joins two branches. Git makes one when both branches have new commits."},{"term":"parent","def":"The commit right before a commit. A merge commit has two."}],"quiz":[{"q":"When does <code>git merge</code> do a fast-forward?","options":["When the branch you are on has no new commits of its own","When none of the files changed","Every time"],"a":0,"why":"Then the other branch already contains everything, so Git only has to move the label forward."},{"q":"How many parents does a merge commit have?","options":["One","Two","None"],"a":1,"why":"It joins two branches, so it points back to the last commit of each."},{"q":"You are on <code>main</code> and run <code>git merge units</code>. Which branch moves?","options":["<code>main</code>","<code>units</code>","Both"],"a":0,"why":"A merge changes only the branch you are on. <code>units</code> stays where it was."}]},{"id":"conflicts","title":"Merge conflicts","tagline":"When both branches changed the same line, Git asks you to decide.","level":"beginner","goals":["Spot a conflict in what <code>git merge</code> and <code>git status</code> print","Read the conflict markers and write the right result","Finish the merge with <code>git add</code> and <code>git commit</code>, or cancel it"],"remember":"Conflict: edit the file to the result you want, delete the markers, git add it, git commit. git merge --abort cancels.","terms":[{"term":"merge conflict","def":"Both branches changed the same lines in different ways. Git stops the merge and asks you to choose."},{"term":"conflict markers","def":"The lines <code>&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;</code>, <code>=======</code> and <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> that Git writes around the two versions."},{"term":"resolve","def":"Edit the file into its final form, remove the markers, and <code>git add</code> it."},{"term":"--abort","def":"<code>git merge --abort</code> cancels a merge that stopped, and puts your files back as they were."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What sits between <code>&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt; HEAD</code> and <code>=======</code>?","options":["The version from the branch you are on","The version from the branch being merged in","The version from before both changes"],"a":0,"why":"<code>HEAD</code> is where you are. The side being merged in comes after <code>=======</code>."},{"q":"You edited the conflicted file into its final form. Which step tells Git it is fixed?","options":["<code>git add Main.kt</code>","<code>git status</code>","Saving the file"],"a":0,"why":"Saving changes the file, but Git keeps it marked as conflicted until you <code>git add</code> it."},{"q":"The conflict is too big to fix right now. What gets you back to before the merge?","options":["<code>git merge --abort</code>","Deleting <code>Main.kt</code>","<code>git commit</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>--abort</code> cancels the merge and puts your files back as they were."}]},{"id":"remotes","title":"Working with GitHub","tagline":"Keep a copy of your repository online, and stay in sync with it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Put a repository on GitHub with <code>git remote add</code> and <code>git push</code>","Copy a repository from GitHub with <code>git clone</code>","Bring in other people’s commits with <code>git fetch</code> and <code>git pull</code>"],"remember":"push uploads your commits; clone copies a whole repository; fetch downloads; pull downloads and merges.","terms":[{"term":"remote, origin","def":"A copy of your repository somewhere else, such as GitHub. <code>origin</code> is the usual name for your main one."},{"term":"push","def":"<code>git push</code> uploads your new commits to the remote."},{"term":"clone","def":"<code>git clone</code> copies a whole repository from a remote into a new folder."},{"term":"fetch, pull","def":"<code>git fetch</code> downloads new commits without touching your files. <code>git pull</code> fetches, then merges them into your branch."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>git push</code> do?","options":["Uploads your new commits to the remote","Downloads commits from the remote","Saves a snapshot on your laptop"],"a":0,"why":"Push sends your commits up. Fetch and pull bring commits down; commit saves a snapshot."},{"q":"What is <code>origin</code>?","options":["A short name for the remote’s address","The first commit","A branch on your laptop"],"a":0,"why":"<code>git remote add origin</code> saved the GitHub address under that name."},{"q":"Which command updates your files with commits from GitHub?","options":["<code>git pull</code>","<code>git fetch</code>","<code>git push</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>pull</code> downloads and merges. <code>fetch</code> only downloads."}]},{"id":"pull-requests","title":"Pull requests and review","tagline":"Ask your team to look at a branch before it joins main.","level":"beginner","goals":["Open a pull request on GitHub from a branch you pushed","Review one: comment, approve or request changes","Merge it on GitHub and bring the result to your laptop"],"remember":"Push a branch, open a pull request, fix review comments with more commits on that branch, merge on GitHub, then pull main.","terms":[{"term":"pull request","def":"A page on GitHub that asks for a branch to be reviewed and merged into another branch, usually <code>main</code>."},{"term":"review","def":"A teammate reads the changes, comments, and then chooses Comment, Approve or Request changes."},{"term":"base, compare","def":"In a pull request, <b>base</b> is the branch that receives the work (<code>main</code>); <b>compare</b> is your branch."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is a pull request?","options":["A request on GitHub to review a branch and merge it","A command that downloads commits","A copy of a repository"],"a":0,"why":"It is a page on GitHub around your branch. <code>git pull</code> is a different thing, despite the name."},{"q":"A reviewer chose <b>Request changes</b>. What do you do?","options":["Commit the fix on the same branch and push","Open a new pull request","Merge anyway"],"a":0,"why":"The pull request follows its branch, so new commits appear in it."},{"q":"The pull request was merged on github.com. How does your laptop get it?","options":["<code>git switch main</code>, then <code>git pull</code>","It arrives on its own","<code>git push</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Your laptop changes only when you download. Pull brings GitHub’s new <code>main</code> into yours."}]},{"id":"rebase","title":"Rebase","tagline":"Move your branch to the end of main, for a straight history.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Replay your branch on top of <code>main</code> with <code>git rebase</code>","Compare a rebased history with a merged one","Know when not to rebase: commits others already have"],"remember":"git rebase main replays your branch on top of main as new commits. Rebase only commits nobody else has.","terms":[{"term":"rebase","def":"<code>git rebase main</code> replays the commits of your branch, one by one, on top of <code>main</code>."},{"term":"replayed commit","def":"A copy of a commit made by rebase: the same change on a new parent, so it gets a new id."},{"term":"shared branch","def":"A branch that is on GitHub and that others may have pulled, like <code>main</code>. Don’t rebase its commits."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is different about your commits after a rebase?","options":["They have new ids","They lose their messages","They are uploaded to GitHub"],"a":0,"why":"Rebase makes each commit again on a new parent, so each gets a new id."},{"q":"Which branch is safe to rebase?","options":["Your own branch that nobody else has pulled","<code>main</code>, which the whole team uses","Any branch, at any time"],"a":0,"why":"Rebase replaces commits. Only do that with commits nobody else has."},{"q":"You rebased <code>rain</code> onto <code>main</code>. On <code>main</code>, what does <code>git merge rain</code> make?","options":["A fast-forward","A merge commit","A conflict"],"a":0,"why":"<code>rain</code> now starts at the end of <code>main</code>, so Git only moves the label."}]},{"id":"undo","title":"Undoing things","tagline":"Throw away an edit, take back a commit, or cancel one that is already shared.","level":"beginner","goals":["Throw away changes you haven’t committed with <code>git restore</code>","Take back your last commit with <code>git reset HEAD~1</code>","Undo a pushed commit safely with <code>git revert</code>"],"remember":"Not committed: git restore. Committed but not pushed: git reset HEAD~1. Already pushed: git revert.","terms":[{"term":"restore","def":"<code>git restore file</code> throws away uncommitted edits. With <code>--staged</code> it only unstages the file."},{"term":"reset","def":"<code>git reset HEAD~1</code> moves your branch back one commit and keeps its changes in your files."},{"term":"revert","def":"<code>git revert id</code> makes a new commit that does the opposite of commit <code>id</code>. Safe for pushed commits."},{"term":"HEAD~1","def":"The commit one before <code>HEAD</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A wrong commit is already on GitHub. Which command undoes it without rewriting history?","options":["<code>git revert</code>","<code>git reset HEAD~1</code>","<code>git restore</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Revert adds a new commit that undoes the old one, so history only grows."},{"q":"What does <code>git restore Main.kt</code> do to your uncommitted edits in that file?","options":["Throws them away","Commits them","Stages them"],"a":0,"why":"The file goes back to the way it was when you last added or committed it. Those edits can’t be brought back."},{"q":"What does <code>HEAD~1</code> mean?","options":["The commit one before <code>HEAD</code>","The very first commit","The next commit"],"a":0,"why":"<code>~1</code> means one step back from <code>HEAD</code>."}]}]},{"id":"kdeep","title":"Kotlin, deeper","description":"More of the Kotlin that libraries are built on: generic types, delegates, sequences, builders, Result and calling Java.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/kotlin-deep.js","lessons":[{"id":"generics","title":"Generic types","tagline":"One class or function that works for any type you pick.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Read a type like <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code> as “list of String”","Write a class with a type parameter <code>&lt;T&gt;</code>","Write a function that works for any type"],"remember":"class Box<T>(val item: T): T stands in for a type, picked each time a Box is made. fun <T> name(...) does the same for a function.","terms":[{"term":"type parameter","def":"A stand-in for a type, in angle brackets: the <code>T</code> in <code>class Box&lt;T&gt;</code>. It gets a real type each time the class or function is used."},{"term":"generic","def":"A class or function with a type parameter, so one piece of code works for many types, like <code>List</code> or <code>Box&lt;T&gt;</code>."},{"term":"angle brackets","def":"<code>&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code> around a type, as in <code>List&lt;Int&gt;</code>: they say which type is used."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val b = Box(3.5)</code>. What type is <code>b</code>?","options":["<code>Box&lt;Double&gt;</code>","<code>Box&lt;T&gt;</code>","<code>Box&lt;Int&gt;</code>"],"a":0,"why":"3.5 is a Double, so Kotlin picks Double for T."},{"q":"Which line is a correct generic function?","options":["<code>fun &lt;T&gt; first(items: List&lt;T&gt;): T</code>","<code>fun first(items: List&lt;T&gt;): T</code>","<code>fun first(&lt;T&gt; items: List): T</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The type parameter is named in angle brackets after <code>fun</code>, before the function name."},{"q":"<code>fun unpack(box: Box&lt;String&gt;)</code>. Does <code>unpack(Box(5))</code> compile?","options":["No: 5 is not a String","Yes: any box fits"],"a":0,"why":"The parameter wants a box of Strings, and 5 is an Int."}]},{"id":"variance","title":"in and out","tagline":"When a Shelter<Cat> may stand in for a Shelter<Animal>.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why a <code>Shelter&lt;Cat&gt;</code> isn’t always a <code>Shelter&lt;Animal&gt;</code>","Mark a type parameter <code>out</code> when a class only gives T out","Mark it <code>in</code> when a class only takes T in"],"remember":"out T: T only comes out, so a Shelter<Cat> fits where a Shelter<Animal> is wanted. in T: T only goes in, so it works the other way round.","terms":[{"term":"variance","def":"The rule for whether a <code>Shelter&lt;Cat&gt;</code> may be used where a <code>Shelter&lt;Animal&gt;</code> is wanted. Set with <code>out</code>, <code>in</code>, or neither."},{"term":"out","def":"On a type parameter: T only comes out (read or returned). Then a <code>Shelter&lt;Cat&gt;</code> fits where a <code>Shelter&lt;Animal&gt;</code> is wanted."},{"term":"in (on a type parameter)","def":"T only goes in, as a parameter. Then a <code>Feeder&lt;Animal&gt;</code> fits where a <code>Feeder&lt;Cat&gt;</code> is wanted."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>fun countAll(animals: List&lt;Animal&gt;)</code>. Can you pass a <code>List&lt;Cat&gt;</code>?","options":["Yes","No"],"a":0,"why":"A <code>List</code> can only be read, so Kotlin declares it with <code>out</code>."},{"q":"<code>fun adopt(animals: MutableList&lt;Animal&gt;)</code>. Can you pass a <code>MutableList&lt;Cat&gt;</code>?","options":["No","Yes"],"a":0,"why":"<code>adopt</code> could add a Dog to your list of cats. A <code>MutableList</code> takes items in too, so it has no <code>out</code>."},{"q":"<code>interface Printer&lt;___ T&gt; { fun print(item: T) }</code>. Which word fits the gap?","options":["<code>in</code>","<code>out</code>"],"a":0,"why":"T is only a parameter here: it goes in."}]},{"id":"delegation","title":"Delegation","tagline":"Hand a property’s work to a helper with by.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what <code>by</code> means in <code>val x by lazy { }</code>","Run code every time a property changes, with <code>Delegates.observable</code>","Read properties from a map with <code>by map</code>"],"remember":"by hands a property to a delegate: by lazy, by Delegates.observable(start) { _, old, new -> }, by map.","terms":[{"term":"delegate","def":"A helper object that a property hands its work to, named after <code>by</code>: <code>lazy</code>, <code>Delegates.observable</code>, a map."},{"term":"delegated property","def":"A property written with <code>by</code>, whose reading (and setting, for a <code>var</code>) is done by its delegate."},{"term":"import","def":"A line at the top of a file that brings in a name from a library: <code>import kotlin.properties.Delegates</code>."},{"term":"_","def":"Used as a lambda parameter name, it means “there is a value here, but I don’t need it”."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In <code>val books by lazy { ... }</code>, what does <code>by</code> mean?","options":["Reading <code>books</code> is handled by the lazy helper","<code>books</code> is a copy of <code>lazy</code>","The block runs on every read"],"a":0,"why":"<code>by</code> hands the property to a delegate, here <code>lazy</code>."},{"q":"With <code>Delegates.observable(0) { _, old, new -> ... }</code>, when does the lambda run?","options":["After every new value is set","Every time the property is read","Once, when the object is made"],"a":0,"why":"It observes changes: each time the property is set, it runs with the old and the new value."},{"q":"<code>class Settings(map: Map&lt;String, String&gt;) { val theme: String by map }</code>. What is <code>Settings(mapOf(\"theme\" to \"dark\")).theme</code>?","options":["<code>\"dark\"</code>","<code>\"theme\"</code>","<code>null</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The property reads the map’s value under the key with its own name, <code>\"theme\"</code>."}]},{"id":"inline","title":"Inline functions","tagline":"Copy a function into the place that calls it, and get two new powers.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what <code>inline</code> does to a function that takes a lambda","Use <code>return</code> inside such a lambda to leave the outer function","Check a type parameter while the program runs, with <code>reified</code>"],"remember":"inline pastes a function and its lambda where it is called. That allows return inside the lambda, and reified T for is T checks.","terms":[{"term":"inline function","def":"A function marked <code>inline</code>: its body and its lambda are pasted where it is called."},{"term":"non-local return","def":"A <code>return</code> inside the lambda of an inline function. It leaves the function where the call is written."},{"term":"reified","def":"Marks a type parameter of an inline function whose real type is still known while the program runs, so <code>is T</code> works."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>inline</code> do to a function?","options":["Pastes its body, with the lambda, where it is called","Makes its lambda run twice","Lets it be called before it is written"],"a":0,"why":"The program does the same, without packing the lambda into an object first."},{"q":"Inside the lambda of an inline function, <code>return</code> leaves...","options":["the function where the call is written","only the lambda","the inline function, which carries on"],"a":0,"why":"It works like a <code>return</code> written right there, because after pasting, that’s where the code is."},{"q":"Which line compiles?","options":["<code>inline fun &lt;reified T&gt; isA(x: Animal) = x is T</code>","<code>fun &lt;reified T&gt; isA(x: Animal) = x is T</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The second gives <code>Only type parameters of inline functions can be reified.</code>"}]},{"id":"operators","title":"Operator overloading","tagline":"Teach your own class what +, [ ] and ( ) mean.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Make <code>+</code> work on your own class with <code>operator fun plus</code>","Read items with <code>[ ]</code> through <code>operator fun get</code>","Call an object like a function with <code>invoke</code>"],"remember":"a + b calls a.plus(b), a[i] calls a.get(i), a(x) calls a.invoke(x), once the function is marked operator.","terms":[{"term":"operator overloading","def":"Giving a symbol like <code>+</code> or <code>[ ]</code> a meaning for your own class."},{"term":"operator","def":"Marks a function with one of the fixed names (<code>plus</code>, <code>get</code>, <code>invoke</code>...) so the matching symbol calls it."},{"term":"invoke","def":"The operator behind round brackets on an object: <code>hello(\"Ada\")</code> runs <code>hello.invoke(\"Ada\")</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"On your own class, <code>a + b</code> calls...","options":["<code>a.plus(b)</code>","<code>b.plus(a)</code>","<code>a.add(b)</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The left side’s <code>plus</code> runs, with the right side as its argument."},{"q":"<code>shelf[0]</code> calls...","options":["<code>shelf.get(0)</code>","<code>shelf.invoke(0)</code>","<code>shelf.first()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Square brackets are the <code>get</code> operator."},{"q":"<code>Greeter</code> has <code>operator fun invoke(name: String)</code> and <code>val hi = Greeter(\"Hi\")</code>. Which call works?","options":["<code>hi(\"Lin\")</code>","<code>Greeter.hi(\"Lin\")</code>","<code>hi[\"Lin\"]</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Round brackets on the object call <code>invoke</code>."}]},{"id":"value","title":"Value classes","tagline":"A separate type around one value, at no extra cost.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Wrap one value in its own type with <code>value class</code>","Let Kotlin catch swapped arguments for you","Know the rules: one <code>val</code>, and <code>@JvmInline</code> above it"],"remember":"@JvmInline value class Meters(val value: Int): its own type while you code, usually a plain Int while it runs.","terms":[{"term":"value class","def":"A class around exactly one <code>val</code>. It is its own type while you write code, and the plain value while the program runs."},{"term":"annotation","def":"A note for the compiler, written with <code>@</code> before a name, like <code>@JvmInline</code>."},{"term":"@JvmInline","def":"The annotation a value class needs on Android: store it as the plain value inside."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why use <code>value class Meters(val value: Int)</code> instead of a plain <code>Int</code>?","options":["Kotlin catches a <code>Minutes</code> passed where <code>Meters</code> is wanted","It makes the number bigger","It lets the value change"],"a":0,"why":"Each value class is its own type, so mixing them up doesn’t compile."},{"q":"Which line compiles?","options":["<code>@JvmInline value class Grams(val amount: Int)</code>","<code>@JvmInline value class Point(val x: Int, val y: Int)</code>","<code>@JvmInline value class Grams(var amount: Int)</code>"],"a":0,"why":"One property, and a <code>val</code>. With two, Kotlin says <code>Inline class must have exactly one primary constructor parameter.</code> (an older name for a value class)."},{"q":"While the program runs, what is a <code>Meters(5000)</code> usually stored as?","options":["The plain Int 5000","An object holding one Int","A String"],"a":0,"why":"That’s the point of a value class: the safety is checked while compiling, and no extra object is made."}]},{"id":"sequences","title":"Sequences","tagline":"Do the work one item at a time, and only as much as needed.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain how a chain on a sequence differs from one on a list","Turn a list into a sequence with <code>asSequence</code>, and back with <code>toList</code>","Tell when a sequence saves work"],"remember":"A list chain does each step for all items; a sequence takes one item through all steps, and only when a final step like first() or toList() asks.","terms":[{"term":"Sequence","def":"A chain of steps that takes one item through all of them before the next item, and only when a result is asked for."},{"term":"lazy","def":"Waiting to do work until the result is needed, like <code>by lazy</code> and sequences."},{"term":"final step","def":"A call like <code>first()</code>, <code>sum()</code> or <code>toList()</code> that asks a sequence for a result and makes the work happen."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A sequence chain has <code>map</code> and <code>filter</code> but no final step. How many times does <code>map</code> run?","options":["0","Once per item","Once"],"a":0,"why":"A sequence is lazy: nothing runs until a final step asks for a result."},{"q":"On a list, <code>listOf(1, 2, 3).map { ... }.filter { ... }</code>: what happens first?","options":["<code>map</code> runs on all three items, then <code>filter</code>","<code>map</code> and <code>filter</code> on item 1, then on item 2..."],"a":0,"why":"A list chain finishes each step for the whole list before the next step. The other order is what a sequence does."},{"q":"When does <code>asSequence()</code> help most?","options":["Many items, several steps, and you need only the first few results","A list of three items","When you want the items in reverse order"],"a":0,"why":"Then the sequence can stop early and skip most of the work."}]},{"id":"generators","title":"Building sequences","tagline":"Write a sequence step by step with sequence { } and yield.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Build your own sequence with <code>sequence { }</code> and <code>yield</code>","Explain how the block pauses after each <code>yield</code>","Take a few items from a sequence that never ends, with <code>take</code>"],"remember":"sequence { yield(x) } hands out x and pauses until the next item is asked for. take(n) gets at most n items.","terms":[{"term":"sequence { }","def":"Builds a sequence from a block of code that hands out items with <code>yield</code>."},{"term":"yield","def":"Inside <code>sequence { }</code>: hand out one item, then pause until the next one is asked for."},{"term":"take","def":"<code>take(n)</code> gives at most the first n items, so even an endless sequence can end."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What happens right after <code>yield(x)</code> inside <code>sequence { }</code>?","options":["The block pauses until the next item is asked for","The block ends","The block starts again from the top"],"a":0,"why":"It remembers its place and its variables, and carries on from there next time."},{"q":"<code>evens</code> is the endless sequence 0, 2, 4, 6... What is <code>evens.take(3).toList()</code>?","options":["<code>[0, 2, 4]</code>","<code>[2, 4, 6]</code>","It never finishes"],"a":0,"why":"<code>take(3)</code> asks for the first three items, then stops."},{"q":"Which line never finishes, on the endless <code>evens</code>?","options":["<code>evens.toList()</code>","<code>evens.take(3).toList()</code>","<code>evens.first()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>first()</code> needs one item and <code>take(3)</code> three, but <code>toList()</code> wants them all."}]},{"id":"dsl","title":"Building a DSL","tagline":"Lambdas with a receiver let code read like a description.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Read a function type with a receiver, like <code>Menu.() -> Unit</code>","Write a builder function that sets up an object from a lambda","Explain why such a builder is type-safe"],"remember":"Type.() -> Unit is a lambda with receiver: inside it, the object is this. A builder makes the object, runs the lambda on it, and gives it back.","terms":[{"term":"lambda with receiver","def":"A lambda of a type like <code>Menu.() -> Unit</code>. Inside it, the object it runs on is <code>this</code>."},{"term":"builder","def":"A function that makes an object, lets a lambda with receiver fill it in, and gives it back."},{"term":"DSL","def":"Domain-specific language: a small language for one job, here made of Kotlin builders."},{"term":"type-safe","def":"Checked by Kotlin before the program runs: a wrong type or an unknown function doesn’t compile."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Inside a <code>Menu.() -> Unit</code> lambda, what is <code>this</code>?","options":["The Menu the lambda runs on","The lambda itself","Nothing: <code>this</code> only works inside a class"],"a":0,"why":"That’s what the receiver is: the object before the dot in <code>Menu.()</code>."},{"q":"Which scope function you know takes a lambda with receiver?","options":["<code>apply</code>","<code>let</code>","<code>also</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Inside <code>apply</code> (and <code>run</code>) the object is <code>this</code>. <code>let</code> and <code>also</code> use <code>it</code>."},{"q":"<code>Menu</code> has no <code>dessert</code> function. What happens with <code>menu { dessert(\"Cake\") }</code>?","options":["It doesn’t compile","It runs and skips that line","It crashes while running"],"a":0,"why":"The builder is type-safe: Kotlin says <code>Unresolved reference 'dessert'.</code>"}]},{"id":"errors","title":"Result and error handling","tagline":"A failure you expect can be an ordinary value.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Turn an exception into a value with <code>runCatching</code>","Read a <code>Result</code> with <code>getOrDefault</code>, <code>onSuccess</code> and <code>onFailure</code>","Choose between throwing an exception and returning a <code>Result</code>"],"remember":"runCatching { } turns a throw into a Result. Get the value out with getOrDefault or getOrNull, or react with onSuccess / onFailure.","terms":[{"term":"Result","def":"A box holding either a success with a value, or a failure with an exception: <code>Result&lt;Int&gt;</code>."},{"term":"runCatching","def":"Runs a block and gives back a <code>Result</code>: a success with its value, or a failure if it threw."},{"term":"success / failure","def":"The two cases of a Result. <code>onSuccess</code> runs only for a success, <code>onFailure</code> only for a failure."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>runCatching { \"abc\".toInt() }</code> give?","options":["A failure holding the exception","A crash","<code>0</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The exception is caught and kept inside the Result. Printed: <code>Failure(java.lang.NumberFormatException: For input string: \"abc\")</code>."},{"q":"Which gives the number inside <code>r</code>, or <code>-1</code> if it failed?","options":["<code>r.getOrDefault(-1)</code>","<code>r.getOrNull(-1)</code>","<code>r.onFailure(-1)</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>getOrNull()</code> takes no argument and gives null on failure; <code>onFailure</code> takes a lambda, not a number."},{"q":"Your own code calls <code>setAge(-5)</code>, which should never happen. Throw an exception or return a Result?","options":["Throw: it is a bug, not a normal outcome","Return a Result","Neither: print a message and carry on"],"a":0,"why":"A Result is for failures the caller should expect and handle. A bug should stop loudly so you find it."}]},{"id":"java-interop","title":"Calling Java","tagline":"Kotlin and Java share one app. Mind the nulls at the border.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Call Java code from Kotlin","Spot a platform type and give it a safe Kotlin type","Make a companion function easy to call from Java with <code>@JvmStatic</code>"],"remember":"A value from Java has a platform type (String!): give it a Kotlin type, String? if it might be null. @JvmStatic makes companion functions plain to call from Java.","terms":[{"term":"Java","def":"The older language Android was first written in. Kotlin and Java code can call each other in one app."},{"term":"platform type","def":"The type of a value from Java, shown as <code>String!</code>: Kotlin doesn’t know if it can be null."},{"term":"@Nullable / @NotNull","def":"Annotations in Java code that tell Kotlin a value may be null (<code>String?</code>) or never is (<code>String</code>)."},{"term":"@JvmStatic","def":"On a function in a <code>companion object</code>: Java can call it as <code>Settings.load()</code> instead of <code>Settings.Companion.load()</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A Java method without annotations returns a String. What type does Kotlin see?","options":["<code>String!</code>: might be null, might not","<code>String</code>: never null","<code>String?</code>: always nullable"],"a":0,"why":"That’s a platform type. Kotlin can’t know, so it leaves the choice to you."},{"q":"A Java method is marked <code>@Nullable</code>. Its String arrives in Kotlin as...","options":["<code>String?</code>","<code>String</code>","<code>String!</code>"],"a":0,"why":"With the annotation, Kotlin knows it may be null and checks your code for it."},{"q":"Without <code>@JvmStatic</code>, how does Java call <code>load()</code> in <code>Settings</code>’s companion object?","options":["<code>Settings.Companion.load()</code>","<code>Settings.load()</code>","It can’t"],"a":0,"why":"Java sees the companion object as an object named <code>Companion</code> inside the class."}]}]},{"id":"oop","title":"OOP basics","description":"How to design with objects: modelling real things, identity, encapsulation, composition and inheritance, polymorphism, abstraction, cohesion and domain modelling, all in plain Kotlin.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/oop.js","lessons":[{"id":"objects","title":"Objects model things","tagline":"Read the problem: things become classes, actions become functions.","level":"beginner","goals":["Turn a short description into classes (the nouns) and functions (the verbs)","Decide which class a rule belongs in: the one that holds its data","Spot a rule that is repeated outside its object"],"remember":"Nouns in the description become classes, verbs become functions. Each rule goes in the class that holds the data it is about.","terms":[{"term":"modelling","def":"Describing real things in code: the things become classes, what they know becomes properties, what they do becomes functions."},{"term":"behaviour","def":"What an object can do: its member functions, like <code>lend()</code>. A rule about the object’s data belongs in its behaviour."}],"quiz":[{"q":"“A <em>lamp</em> can be <em>switched</em> on.” Which word most likely becomes a class?","options":["lamp","switched","on"],"a":0,"why":"A lamp is a thing (a noun), so it becomes a class. <em>Switched</em> is something done to it, so it becomes a function."},{"q":"A shop app says: “A customer adds items to a basket.” Which word most likely becomes a function?","options":["adds","customer","basket"],"a":0,"why":"Verbs are things an object does. Customer and basket are things, so they are more likely classes."},{"q":"Where should the rule “a book that is out can’t be lent” live?","options":["Inside <code>Book</code>, in <code>lend()</code>","In every screen that lends books","In <code>main</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Next to the data it is about, written once. Every screen then calls <code>book.lend()</code>."}]},{"id":"identity","title":"Same object or equal object","tagline":"A variable points to an object, and two names can point to the same one.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what <code>val b = a</code> copies","Ask “same object?” with <code>===</code> and “equal?” with <code>==</code>","Make a real copy of a data class object with <code>copy()</code>"],"remember":"val b = a copies the arrow, not the object. === asks “same object?”; == asks “equal?”.","terms":[{"term":"reference","def":"What a variable holds for an object: an arrow to it, not the object itself. <code>val b = a</code> copies the arrow."},{"term":"identity","def":"Being one particular object. <code>a === b</code> is <code>true</code> only when both point at the same object."},{"term":"equality","def":"Meaning the same thing, asked with <code>==</code>. A data class compares property values; a plain class compares identity."}],"quiz":[{"q":"After <code>val a = Book(\"Rain\")</code> and <code>val b = a</code>, how many Book objects exist?","options":["One","Two","None"],"a":0,"why":"Only one constructor call. <code>b</code> is a second arrow to the same book."},{"q":"<code>data class P(val x: Int)</code>. What do <code>P(1) == P(1)</code> and <code>P(1) === P(1)</code> give?","options":["<code>true</code> and <code>false</code>","<code>true</code> and <code>true</code>","<code>false</code> and <code>false</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Equal values, but each <code>P(1)</code> is a separate object."},{"q":"<code>fun refill(c: Cup) { c.tea = \"mint\" }</code>. After <code>refill(mine)</code>, what is <code>mine.tea</code>?","options":["<code>\"mint\"</code>","What it was before","It doesn’t compile"],"a":0,"why":"The function got an arrow to the same cup, so its change is the caller’s change."}]},{"id":"encapsulation","title":"Encapsulation: keeping data valid","tagline":"Write each rule once, and let only the class change its data.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name a rule an object must always keep (its invariant)","Check the rule in every function that changes the state","Share a list without letting others change it"],"remember":"Name the invariant, let only the class change its state, and check the rule in every function that changes it.","terms":[{"term":"invariant","def":"A rule about an object that must always be true, like “booked is never more than seats”."},{"term":"valid","def":"An object is valid while all its invariants hold."},{"term":"encapsulation","def":"Keeping state private so only the class’s own functions change it, and those functions keep the rules."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is an invariant?","options":["A rule about an object that must always be true","A property that is a <code>val</code>","A function that never changes"],"a":0,"why":"Like “booked seats never go over the seats on the bus”."},{"q":"<code>val items = mutableListOf&lt;String&gt;()</code> is a public property. Can outside code add to it?","options":["Yes: <code>val</code> only stops it pointing to another list","No: <code>val</code> makes the list unchangeable"],"a":0,"why":"The arrow is fixed, but the list it points at can still change. Keep it private."},{"q":"The app says 43 of 40 seats are booked. With good encapsulation, where do you look first?","options":["In Bus’s own functions","Everywhere in the program","In <code>main</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Only Bus’s functions can change <code>booked</code>, so the bug has to be there."}]},{"id":"composition","title":"Composition","tagline":"Build a bigger object from smaller parts it has.","level":"beginner","goals":["Build a class from parts it keeps as properties (“has a”)","Let an object do its work by asking its parts","Reuse one part class in several bigger classes"],"remember":"Composition: build an object from parts it has (“has a”), and let it do its work by asking those parts.","terms":[{"term":"composition","def":"Building an object from other objects it keeps as properties, and doing the work by asking them."},{"term":"has a","def":"The test for composition: “a phone has a battery”, so Battery is a part of Phone."},{"term":"part","def":"An object kept inside another as a property, like the <code>battery</code> in a Phone."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which pair fits composition best?","options":["A car and its engine","A dog and an animal","A square and a shape"],"a":0,"why":"“A car has an engine.” The other two are “is a” sentences."},{"q":"In <code>class Phone { private val battery = Battery() }</code>, how should Phone use power?","options":["Call <code>battery.use(30)</code>","Change <code>battery.level</code> directly","Copy the Battery code into Phone"],"a":0,"why":"The part keeps its own rules. The phone asks; the battery decides."},{"q":"Why hand a part in through the constructor, as in <code>Drone(Battery())</code>?","options":["The code making the drone picks its battery","It makes the drone faster","Kotlin requires it for every part"],"a":0,"why":"The drone works with any Battery it is given. Making it yourself inside the class also works, but then the class always picks."}]},{"id":"inheritance","title":"Inheritance, carefully","tagline":"Build on a class only when “is a” is always true, and know what that ties you to.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Use the “is a” test before writing <code>: Parent()</code>","Explain how a change inside a parent can break a child","Call the parent’s version with <code>super</code>, not your own function again"],"remember":"Inherit only when “X is a Y” is always true, from a class made to be a parent. A child depends on its parent’s insides, so a change there can break it.","terms":[{"term":"is a","def":"The test for inheritance: “a savings account is an account” must be true always, not only sometimes."},{"term":"base class","def":"Another name for a parent class, the class others build on."},{"term":"fragile base class","def":"When a change inside a parent breaks a child that didn’t change, because the child relied on how the parent worked inside."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which sentence passes the “is a” test?","options":["A savings account is an account","A car is an engine","A phone is a battery"],"a":0,"why":"It is true always. The other two are “has a”: composition."},{"q":"What is the fragile base class problem?","options":["A change inside a parent breaks a child that didn’t change","A parent class can’t be changed at all","A child class has too many functions"],"a":0,"why":"The child relied on how the parent worked inside, and that changed."},{"q":"Why does Kotlin ask for <code>open</code> before a class can be a parent?","options":["So being built on is a choice the class’s author made","To make the class run faster","Because open classes can’t have properties"],"a":0,"why":"A class meant to be a parent is written and changed with its children in mind."}]},{"id":"composition-vs-inheritance","title":"Choosing between them","tagline":"Composition or inheritance? Count what each one costs.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Spot when inheritance multiplies into too many classes","Swap a part while the program runs, using composition","Pick composition or inheritance for a new case"],"remember":"Start with composition: parts combine freely and can be swapped. Use inheritance for a true, lasting “is a” in a small family.","terms":[{"term":"trade-off","def":"A choice where each option gives you something and costs you something, like less code (inheritance) against free combining (composition)."},{"term":"combination","def":"One pick from each group, like wheels with a laser. With inheritance each combination needs its own class."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Robots have 3 kinds of mover and 2 kinds of tool. With composition, how many part classes do you write?","options":["5","6","9"],"a":0,"why":"3 movers plus 2 tools. With one class per combination you would need 6 at the bottom alone."},{"q":"Which one can change while the program runs?","options":["A <code>var tool: Tool</code> part","The parent class of an object","Neither"],"a":0,"why":"A part is a property, so a <code>var</code> can point to a new part. The parent is fixed when the object is made."},{"q":"When is inheritance a good fit?","options":["A small family where “is a” is always true","Whenever two classes share one function","When you need many combinations"],"a":0,"why":"Many combinations and shared bits of code are both better handled with parts."}]},{"id":"polymorphism","title":"Polymorphism","tagline":"One call, many behaviours, or one when over every kind: where each kind’s code lives.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Compare a <code>when</code> over kinds with one override per class","Explain how the real object, not the variable’s type, picks the code that runs","Choose by what grows: new kinds favour overrides, new operations favour sealed and <code>when</code>"],"remember":"Polymorphism: one call, and the real object decides which override runs. Overrides make new kinds cheap; sealed and when make new operations cheap. Pick by what grows.","terms":[{"term":"polymorphism","def":"One call that behaves differently depending on the object behind it, like <code>d.cost(km)</code> on a Bike or a Drone."},{"term":"dynamic dispatch","def":"Picking which override runs from the real object, while the program runs. The variable’s type only decides what you may call."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>val d: Delivery = Van()</code>. Whose <code>cost</code> runs for <code>d.cost(5)</code>?","options":["Van’s","Delivery’s","Bike’s"],"a":0,"why":"The real object decides, and it is a Van. Delivery only promises <code>cost</code>; it has no body to run."},{"q":"A delivery app gets a new kind of vehicle every month. Which style keeps each change small?","options":["An interface with one class per kind","A <code>when</code> over kinds in every function","One big class with a Boolean per kind"],"a":0,"why":"New kinds are what grows, so each one should be a single new class. The callers stay the same."},{"q":"A sealed family of shapes is fixed, but keeps getting new operations: area, outline, a label. Which style keeps each change small?","options":["A <code>when</code> with no <code>else</code> per operation","A new override in every shape class","A <code>when</code> with an <code>else</code> branch"],"a":0,"why":"A new operation is then one new function. With no <code>else</code>, Kotlin also checks that every shape is handled."}]},{"id":"abstraction","title":"Abstraction","tagline":"Show a simple surface, and hide the detail behind it.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Design a class whose public functions say what, not how","Keep the steps private so they can change freely","Tell abstraction apart from encapsulation"],"remember":"Keep the public surface small and named after what the caller wants. Keep the how private, so it can change.","terms":[{"term":"abstraction","def":"Giving something a simple surface and hiding the detail behind it, so callers only need to know what it does."},{"term":"surface","def":"Everything other code can use from a class: its public functions and properties."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which is the better surface for a music player class?","options":["<code>play()</code> and <code>next()</code>","<code>setIndex()</code>, <code>readFile()</code> and <code>startSpeaker()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"They say what the caller wants. The other set exposes how it is done."},{"q":"Playlist changes how it stores its songs inside. What must callers of <code>next()</code> change?","options":["Nothing","Every call to <code>next()</code>","Only the first call"],"a":0,"why":"They only ever used the surface, and the surface stayed the same."},{"q":"Which question does abstraction answer?","options":["How little does the caller need to know?","How do I keep this data valid?","Which class is the parent?"],"a":0,"why":"Keeping data valid is encapsulation. The two often work together."}]},{"id":"interface-or-abstract","title":"Interface or abstract class","tagline":"Two ways to describe a family. Pick by what the family shares.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Write an <code>abstract class</code> with an <code>abstract fun</code>","Explain why you can’t make an object of an abstract class","Choose between an interface and an abstract class"],"remember":"Interface: a promise of what a class can do; a class can keep many. Abstract class: shared values and code for close relatives, with abstract functions the children fill in; one parent only, and no objects of its own.","terms":[{"term":"abstract class","def":"A class that can’t be made into an object itself. It can hold values and finished functions, plus abstract functions for its children to write."},{"term":"abstract function","def":"A function with no body, like <code>abstract fun sound(): String</code>. Every child must <code>override</code> it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which one can hold a stored value with a starting value, like <code>var meals = 0</code>?","options":["An abstract class","An interface","Neither"],"a":0,"why":"An interface only promises. An abstract class is a class, so it can store values."},{"q":"A Duck must be both a <code>Swimmer</code> and a <code>Flyer</code>. Which choice is sure to compile?","options":["Both are interfaces","Both are abstract classes"],"a":0,"why":"A class can keep many interfaces but has only one parent class."},{"q":"Animal is an abstract class. What happens with <code>Animal(\"Cat\")</code>?","options":["It doesn’t compile","It makes an animal with no sound","It crashes when <code>sound()</code> is called"],"a":0,"why":"Kotlin stops it before the program runs: <code>Cannot create an instance of an abstract class.</code>"}]},{"id":"delegation","title":"Delegation in design","tagline":"Hand the work to a helper object, and let Kotlin write the forwarding.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Pass calls on to a helper object instead of inheriting from a class","Use <code>by</code> to let Kotlin write the forwarding functions","Override only the functions that should behave differently"],"remember":"Delegation: keep a helper and pass calls on to it. class X(private val inner: I) : I by inner forwards every function you don’t override.","terms":[{"term":"delegation","def":"Handing a job to a helper object instead of doing it yourself or inheriting it."},{"term":"delegate","def":"The helper object that receives the calls, like <code>inner</code> in <code>Logbook by inner</code>."},{"term":"forwarding","def":"A function that only passes the call on to the delegate, like <code>override fun size() = inner.size()</code>. <code>by</code> writes these for you."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In <code>class A(private val b: Speaker) : Speaker by b</code>, A doesn’t override <code>bye()</code>. Whose <code>bye()</code> runs?","options":["<code>b</code>’s","A’s own, empty one","None: it doesn’t compile"],"a":0,"why":"<code>by b</code> forwards every function A doesn’t write itself."},{"q":"Why does the counting logbook built with <code>by</code> count 3, not 6?","options":["The helper’s <code>addAll</code> calls the helper’s own <code>add</code>","<code>by</code> skips <code>addAll</code>","The count is reset after each call"],"a":0,"why":"There is no parent whose code calls back into ours. The helper only ever calls itself."},{"q":"Which line forwards the Storage interface to a helper called <code>inner</code>?","options":["<code>class Logged(private val inner: Storage) : Storage by inner</code>","<code>class Logged : Storage(inner)</code>","<code>class Logged by Storage</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The helper comes in through the constructor; <code>: Storage by inner</code> keeps the interface by forwarding to it."}]},{"id":"cohesion-coupling","title":"Cohesion and coupling","tagline":"Code is easy to change when each class has one focus and classes are linked loosely.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Spot a class that does unrelated jobs (low cohesion)","Spot code that reaches into another class’s insides (tight coupling)","Count which classes a change touches, and bring that number down"],"remember":"High cohesion: each class has one focus. Loose coupling: classes use each other only through their surfaces. Then a change stays in one place.","terms":[{"term":"cohesion","def":"How well the things inside one class belong together. High cohesion: one clear focus."},{"term":"coupling","def":"How much one class depends on another. Tight: it reaches into the other’s insides. Loose: it uses only the other’s surface."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A class adds up prices, sends emails and formats dates. What is the problem?","options":["Low cohesion: three unrelated jobs","Tight coupling to Cart","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"Each job is a separate reason to change. They belong in separate classes."},{"q":"Which line is loosely coupled to Cart?","options":["<code>cart.total()</code>","<code>cart.prices.sum()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"It uses Cart’s surface. The other depends on how Cart stores its prices inside."},{"q":"How do private properties help keep coupling loose?","options":["Other classes can only use the surface, so they can’t depend on the insides","Private code runs faster","They make classes shorter"],"a":0,"why":"What others can’t reach, they can’t depend on, so it is free to change."}]},{"id":"domain-modeling","title":"Modelling a domain","tagline":"Turn a description of a problem into types that make mistakes hard to write.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Pick the right tool for each part of a description: class, data class, enum or sealed","Put each rule in the class that owns the data","Let the compiler point to every place a new case must be handled"],"remember":"Turn the words of the problem into types: data class for values, classes for things with rules, enum for simple choices, sealed for cases with their own data. Put each rule where its data lives.","terms":[{"term":"domain","def":"The part of the world a program is about, like members, books and loans for a library."},{"term":"domain model","def":"The types that describe a domain in code, with each rule placed in the class that owns its data."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A café has cup sizes small, medium and large, with nothing else to store. Which tool fits?","options":["An enum class","A sealed interface","A data class"],"a":0,"why":"A fixed list of simple choices, none carrying its own data."},{"q":"Where does the rule “a book on loan can’t be lent again” belong?","options":["In <code>Book.lendTo()</code>","In every screen with a Lend button","In the Member class"],"a":0,"why":"In the class that owns the book’s state, written once."},{"q":"You add <code>object Repairing : BookState</code>. What does Kotlin do?","options":["Every <code>when</code> over BookState with no <code>else</code> stops compiling until it handles Repairing","Nothing, until the program runs","The program crashes at the start"],"a":0,"why":"A sealed family is known in full, so Kotlin can check every <code>when</code> before the program runs."}]}]},{"id":"solid","title":"SOLID principles","description":"Five rules of thumb that keep your classes easy to change, one lesson each, with small Kotlin programs you can run and fix.","requires":["oop"],"file":"tracks/solid.js","lessons":[{"id":"srp","title":"Single responsibility","tagline":"One class, one reason to change.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Spot a class that does two different jobs","Ask “who would ask me to change this?”","Split it into two classes with one job each"],"remember":"One class, one job. If two different people would ask you to change a class, split it into two.","terms":[{"term":"design principle","def":"A rule of thumb for arranging code so it stays easy to change. It is advice, not a rule the compiler checks."},{"term":"SOLID","def":"Five design principles, one per letter: Single responsibility, Open/closed, Liskov substitution, Interface segregation, Dependency inversion."},{"term":"single responsibility","def":"A class has one job, so it has one reason to change. Ask: who would ask me to change this?"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does “single responsibility” mean for a class?","options":["It has one job, so one reason to change","It has only one function","Only one object can be made from it"],"a":0,"why":"A class can have several functions, as long as they all serve the same job."},{"q":"A <code>Profile</code> class loads the user’s name and also picks the colors of the profile screen. Who might ask you to change it?","options":["Two different people: whoever owns the data and the designer","Nobody, it works","Only the person who wrote it"],"a":0,"why":"Two different askers means two jobs. Split it into a class for the data and one for the look."},{"q":"You split <code>Cart</code> into <code>Cart</code> and <code>Receipt</code>. The designer changes the wording. Which class do you edit?","options":["<code>Receipt</code> only","<code>Cart</code> only","Both"],"a":0,"why":"The wording is <code>Receipt</code>’s one job, so <code>Cart</code> stays untouched."}]},{"id":"open-closed","title":"Open/closed","tagline":"Add new behaviour without editing code that already works.","level":"intermediate","goals":["See why a growing <code>when</code> must be edited again and again","Add a new case as a new class behind an interface","Leave working code untouched when the app grows"],"remember":"Open for extension, closed for modification: add a new class that keeps the interface instead of editing code that already works.","terms":[{"term":"open/closed principle","def":"Code should be open for extension (you can add behaviour) and closed for modification (you don’t edit what already works)."},{"term":"extension","def":"In open/closed: adding new behaviour, for example a new class that keeps an existing interface. Not the same as a Kotlin extension function."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does “closed for modification” mean?","options":["You don’t edit code that already works to add something new","Nobody may ever read the code","The class is <code>private</code>"],"a":0,"why":"New behaviour goes into new code. The old code stays as it is."},{"q":"The shop wants a 20% off discount. With the <code>Discount</code> interface, what do you write?","options":["A new class that keeps <code>Discount</code>","A new branch inside <code>checkout</code>","A second <code>checkout</code> function"],"a":0,"why":"One new class, whose <code>newTotal</code> returns <code>total * 8 / 10</code>. <code>checkout</code> already works with any <code>Discount</code>."}]},{"id":"liskov","title":"Liskov substitution","tagline":"A class that keeps an interface must keep its promise too.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Tell the difference between matching an interface and keeping its promise","Spot a class that compiles but surprises the code using it","Fix it so any class that keeps the interface can stand in for it"],"remember":"Matching the interface is not enough: every class that keeps it must do what it promises, so code can use any of them without surprises.","terms":[{"term":"Liskov substitution principle","def":"Wherever code expects a type, any subtype must work there without surprises."},{"term":"subtype","def":"A class that keeps an interface, or a child class of a parent. A <code>ListStore</code> is a subtype of <code>NoteStore</code>."},{"term":"substitution","def":"Putting one thing in the place of another, like passing a <code>DemoStore</code> where a <code>NoteStore</code> is expected."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the compiler check when a class keeps an interface?","options":["The shape: names, parameters, return types","That every function does what the comment promises","Nothing at all"],"a":0,"why":"The meaning is up to you. That is why the promise belongs in a comment, and why this principle exists."},{"q":"<code>StuckCounter</code> keeps <code>Counter</code>, but its <code>add()</code> does nothing. What does the principle say?","options":["It breaks Liskov substitution: it can’t stand in for a <code>Counter</code>","It is fine, because it compiles","It is fine, because no exception is thrown"],"a":0,"why":"Code that trusts the promise gets <code>0</code> after three taps. Compiling is not the same as keeping a promise."}]},{"id":"isp","title":"Interface segregation","tagline":"Small interfaces, so no class promises what it can’t do.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Spot an interface that forces a class to fake a function","Split one big interface into small ones","Let a class keep only the interfaces it really can"],"remember":"Several small interfaces beat one big one: each class keeps only the promises it can really keep.","terms":[{"term":"interface segregation principle","def":"No class should be forced to promise functions it can’t keep. Split a big interface into small ones."},{"term":"segregation","def":"Splitting apart. Here: splitting one big interface into several small ones."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A class has to write <code>override fun download() = \"\"</code> because its interface forces it. What is the better fix?","options":["Split the interface so the class doesn’t have to promise <code>download</code>","Keep the empty version, it compiles","Add even more functions to the interface"],"a":0,"why":"A forced fake function is the sign of an interface that is too big."},{"q":"How does a class keep two interfaces?","options":["<code>class Song : Playable, Downloadable</code>","<code>class Song : Playable : Downloadable</code>","It can’t: only one interface per class"],"a":0,"why":"A class can keep several interfaces, separated by commas."},{"q":"Can a <code>Radio</code> (only <code>Playable</code>) go in a <code>List&lt;Downloadable&gt;</code>?","options":["No, it doesn’t keep <code>Downloadable</code>","Yes, every class fits every list","Yes, but only once"],"a":0,"why":"Kotlin refuses it with a type mismatch. That is what keeps the Save button away from radios."}]},{"id":"dip","title":"Dependency inversion","tagline":"Ask for an interface and let someone pass the real thing in.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Spot a class that builds the helper it needs inside itself","Ask for an interface in the constructor instead","Pass in a real or a made-up helper from outside"],"remember":"Don’t build your helpers inside. Ask for an interface in the constructor, and let whoever makes the object pass the real one in.","terms":[{"term":"dependency","def":"A helper a class needs to do its job. <code>WeatherScreen</code> depends on a weather source."},{"term":"dependency inversion principle","def":"The class that does the main work (like the screen) depends on an interface, not on one exact helper class. The helpers keep that interface."},{"term":"dependency injection","def":"Passing a dependency in from outside, usually through the constructor: <code>WeatherScreen(FakeWeather(\"Sunny\"))</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is a dependency?","options":["A helper a class needs to do its job","A class that has no functions","A line that has a bug"],"a":0,"why":"The weather source is a dependency of the screen; the sender is a dependency of the reminder."},{"q":"Which version lets you pass made-up weather in?","options":["<code>class WeatherScreen(private val source: WeatherSource)</code>","<code>class WeatherScreen { private val source = OnlineWeather() }</code>","Both"],"a":0,"why":"Only a constructor parameter opens a way in. Built inside, the helper is fixed."},{"q":"In the after picture, which way does <code>OnlineWeather</code>’s arrow point?","options":["Up, at the <code>WeatherSource</code> interface it keeps","Down, at nothing","At <code>WeatherScreen</code>, which builds it"],"a":0,"why":"Both the screen and the helper point at the interface. That flip is what “inversion” means."}]}]},{"id":"patterns","title":"Design patterns","description":"Named answers to problems that keep coming back, written the Kotlin way, and when to leave them out.","requires":["solid"],"file":"tracks/patterns.js","lessons":[{"id":"what-patterns","title":"What a design pattern is","tagline":"Good answers to problems that keep coming back, with names.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what a design pattern is, and what it is not","Spot a pattern you already used in Kotlin","Decide when plain code is the better choice"],"remember":"A design pattern is a named answer to a common problem. Use it when you have that problem, not just in case.","terms":[{"term":"design pattern","def":"A named, well-tested shape of code for a problem that keeps coming up. You adapt it to your program; it is not a library."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is a design pattern?","options":["A named, well-tested shape of code for a problem that keeps coming up","A library you download and add to your program","A rule every program must follow"],"a":0,"why":"It is an idea with a name. You write the code yourself, shaped to your program."},{"q":"When is a pattern worth adding?","options":["When the problem it solves is really in your code","Always, just in case","Only in programs with more than ten classes"],"a":0,"why":"A pattern adds classes and names. That only pays off when it removes a real problem."},{"q":"Which Kotlin keyword already gives you the Singleton pattern?","options":["<code>object</code>","<code>class</code>","<code>val</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>object</code> makes exactly one, reachable by its name from everywhere."}]},{"id":"singleton","title":"Singleton","tagline":"Exactly one, shared by everyone, and the dangers that come with it.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Spot the bug that two copies of one thing cause","Make a singleton with <code>object</code>","Name the dangers of global state, and when to pass an object in instead"],"remember":"object makes a singleton: one object for everyone. Handy, but any code can change it, so keep it for things that truly exist once.","terms":[{"term":"singleton","def":"A design pattern: a type with exactly one object, which every part of the program reaches. In Kotlin: <code>object</code>."},{"term":"global state","def":"Values that any part of the program can read and change, like the <code>var</code> properties of a singleton."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The menu and the game each write <code>Sound()</code> for a <code>class Sound</code>. How many Sound objects are there?","options":["Two","One","None"],"a":0,"why":"Every <code>Sound()</code> makes a new object. That is how the mute bug happens."},{"q":"What is the biggest danger of a singleton with <code>var</code> properties?","options":["Any function can change it without saying so","It makes the program slower","It can’t have functions"],"a":0,"why":"It is global state. A change can come from anywhere, which makes bugs hard to find."},{"q":"A chat app shows many messages. Should <code>Message</code> be an <code>object</code>?","options":["No: there are many messages, so it is a class","Yes: every app needs singletons"],"a":0,"why":"A singleton is for something that exists once. Many messages means many objects."}]},{"id":"factory","title":"Factory","tagline":"One function decides which object to make.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Recognise creation code that is copied in many places","Write a factory function that gives back an interface type","Add a new kind of object by changing one place"],"remember":"A factory is one function that decides which class to make and gives back the interface. New kind: one class, one line.","terms":[{"term":"factory","def":"A function (often in a companion object) whose job is to create objects, so the code that uses them doesn’t need to know which class to make."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does <code>makeEnemy</code> return <code>Enemy</code> and not <code>Bat</code>?","options":["So callers can use any enemy without knowing its class","Because Bat is private","It makes the program faster"],"a":0,"why":"Callers only rely on the interface. Which class is behind it is the factory’s business."},{"q":"You add a Ghost enemy. With a factory, what changes?","options":["One new class and one new line in the factory","Every place in the game that makes enemies","Nothing: Kotlin finds Ghost on its own"],"a":0,"why":"All the creating lives in one place, so that is the only place to update."},{"q":"<code>Player.guest()</code> in a companion object gives back a ready-made Player. What is it?","options":["A small factory","A singleton","A copy of every player"],"a":0,"why":"It is a function whose job is making an object, so it is a factory."}]},{"id":"builder","title":"Builder","tagline":"Put an object together step by step, then build it.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Build an object step by step with a builder class","Use named arguments and <code>apply</code>, Kotlin’s lighter builders","Choose between the three"],"remember":"A builder collects parts step by step and build() makes the object. In Kotlin, named arguments and apply often do the job with less code.","terms":[{"term":"builder","def":"A design pattern: a helper object that collects the parts step by step, then makes the finished object with <code>build()</code>."},{"term":"chaining","def":"Calling one function after another with dots, <code>a.size(25).add(\"olives\")</code>, where each call works on what the previous one gave back."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>class Alarm</code> has a default for every setting except <code>hour</code>. To make <code>Alarm(hour = 6, daily = true)</code>, do you need a builder class?","options":["No: named and default arguments already do the job","Yes: any class with more than two settings needs one"],"a":0,"why":"In Kotlin, named and default arguments cover most of what a builder does for a fixed set of settings."},{"q":"What does <code>Profile().apply { name = \"Lin\" }</code> give back?","options":["The Profile itself, with <code>name</code> set","The text <code>\"Lin\"</code>","<code>Unit</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>apply</code> gives back the object it was called on. That is why it works as a builder."},{"q":"Why does each builder step end with <code>return this</code>?","options":["So the next step can be chained with a dot","To make the finished object","To make the builder private"],"a":0,"why":"Giving back the builder lets the next call work on it. <code>build()</code> makes the object."}]},{"id":"observer","title":"Observer","tagline":"Subscribe once, and hear about every change.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Keep a list of listeners and notify them all","Subscribe and unsubscribe with lambdas","Know when a direct call is simpler"],"remember":"Observer: keep a list of listener lambdas, subscribe adds one, and every change calls them all.","terms":[{"term":"observer","def":"A design pattern: one object keeps a list of listeners and calls them all when something changes."},{"term":"listener","def":"A function handed in to be called later, when something happens. Here a lambda such as <code>(Int) -&gt; Unit</code>."},{"term":"subscribe","def":"To add yourself to the list of listeners. <code>unsubscribe</code> takes you off it again."},{"term":"notify","def":"To call every listener in the list with the news."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Two listeners have subscribed. <code>addItem()</code> is called once. How many listener calls happen?","options":["Two: each listener once","One: only the first listener","None until you call them by name"],"a":0,"why":"The loop calls every listener in the list."},{"q":"What does the Observer pattern buy the cart?","options":["It doesn’t need to know who is listening","It turns the cart into a singleton","It sorts the listeners by name"],"a":0,"why":"New parts subscribe themselves. The cart’s code stays the same."},{"q":"A part of the screen goes away but never unsubscribes. What happens?","options":["The cart keeps calling its listener","Its listener is removed on its own"],"a":0,"why":"The lambda stays in the list until someone removes it."}]},{"id":"strategy","title":"Strategy","tagline":"Hand in the way a job is done, and swap it when needed.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Pull a changing rule out into its own class or lambda","Swap a strategy while the program runs","Tell when a plain <code>if</code> is enough"],"remember":"Strategy: put each way of doing a job behind one interface or function type, hand one in, swap it when needed.","terms":[{"term":"strategy","def":"A design pattern: one job with several ways to do it. Each way is a separate object or lambda that you hand in and can swap."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A new rule arrives: shipping costs 2 on Sundays. With the Strategy pattern, what do you change?","options":["Write one new Shipping class (or lambda)","Edit the <code>pay</code> function in Checkout","Edit every existing rule"],"a":0,"why":"The checkout only knows the interface. A new rule is new code, not an edit to old code."},{"q":"Which of these is a strategy you have already used?","options":["The lambda in <code>sortedBy { it.price }</code>","<code>listOf(1, 2, 3)</code>","<code>println(\"Hi\")</code>"],"a":0,"why":"You hand <code>sortedBy</code> the rule for what to sort by. Swap the lambda, get a different order."},{"q":"There are exactly two ways to charge shipping, and that will never change. What is the best choice?","options":["An <code>if</code>","An interface and two classes"],"a":0,"why":"A pattern earns its extra classes when rules keep coming. For two fixed choices, an <code>if</code> is clearer."}]},{"id":"adapter","title":"Adapter","tagline":"Make a class you can’t change fit the interface you need.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Recognise when two pieces of code don’t fit together","Write an adapter class that translates between them","Know when changing the code is better than wrapping it"],"remember":"An adapter keeps the interface your code expects on the outside and calls the class you can’t change on the inside.","terms":[{"term":"adapter","def":"A design pattern: a class that keeps the interface your code expects on the outside, and calls a different class on the inside, translating between them."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You can’t edit OldSensor, and <code>report</code> needs a Thermometer. What does the adapter do?","options":["Keeps the Thermometer promise and calls OldSensor inside","Changes OldSensor’s code","Copies OldSensor’s code into report"],"a":0,"why":"It sits between the two and translates, so neither side has to change."},{"q":"Both classes are yours, and both are easy to change. Do you need an adapter?","options":["Usually not: change one to fit the other","Yes, always"],"a":0,"why":"An extra class in between only pays off for code you can’t or shouldn’t edit."},{"q":"With <code>km() = counter.miles * 1.6</code>, what is <code>MilesAdapter(MilesCounter(5.0)).km()</code>?","options":["<code>8.0</code>","<code>5.0</code>","<code>3.1</code>"],"a":0,"why":"5.0 × 1.6 is 8.0."}]},{"id":"decorator","title":"Decorator","tagline":"Wrap an object to add something, layer by layer.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Add behaviour by wrapping instead of making subclasses","Stack wrappers in any order","Forward the rest with <code>by</code>"],"remember":"A decorator is the same interface wrapped around another object: it adds its part and passes the call on. by inner forwards the rest.","terms":[{"term":"decorator","def":"A design pattern: a class that keeps the same interface as the object it holds, adds something, and passes the call on."},{"term":"wrap","def":"To hold another object inside and stand in front of it, so calls go through you first."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Coffee costs 250 and Milk adds 50. What does <code>Milk(Milk(Coffee())).price()</code> give?","options":["<code>350</code>","<code>300</code>","<code>250</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Two Milk layers, each adding 50 to what is inside: 250 + 50 + 50."},{"q":"What is the difference between a decorator and an adapter?","options":["A decorator keeps the same interface and adds behaviour; an adapter changes the interface to fit","There is no difference","A decorator needs inheritance; an adapter doesn’t"],"a":0,"why":"Both wrap an object. The adapter translates, the decorator adds."},{"q":"In <code>class HappyHour(private val inner: Drink) : Drink by inner</code>, HappyHour doesn’t write <code>name()</code>. What does <code>name()</code> do?","options":["It calls <code>inner.name()</code>","It doesn’t compile","It gives back an empty text"],"a":0,"why":"<code>by inner</code> forwards every function you don’t override to <code>inner</code>."}]},{"id":"facade","title":"Facade","tagline":"One simple front door for a job that needs many parts.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Spot the same sequence of calls repeated in many places","Write a facade class with one simple function","Keep a facade small, and the parts still usable"],"remember":"A facade is one simple function in front of several parts, calling them in the right order. The parts stay usable on their own.","terms":[{"term":"facade","def":"A design pattern: one class with a simple function that runs a common job across several parts, in the right order."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does a facade give its callers?","options":["One simple call for a job that needs several parts","A copy of every part","A list of listeners"],"a":0,"why":"The facade knows the steps and their order, so callers don’t have to."},{"q":"MovieNight is in place. Can other code still use <code>Lights</code> on its own?","options":["Yes: the parts are still there","No: the facade hides them for good"],"a":0,"why":"A facade is a shortcut for the common job, not a wall."},{"q":"A facade has grown to 40 functions covering the whole app. What is the risk?","options":["It has become one big class with many reasons to change","None: a bigger facade is a better facade"],"a":0,"why":"That breaks single responsibility. Keep a facade to one job, or split it."}]},{"id":"state-machine","title":"State machine","tagline":"Always in exactly one state, and only allowed moves between them.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Replace contradicting Booleans with one state","Write the transitions in one function with <code>when</code>","Use a sealed interface when a state carries data"],"remember":"A state machine is in exactly one state at a time. Events move it only along the transitions you wrote, all in one when.","terms":[{"term":"state machine","def":"A design where something is always in exactly one of a fixed set of states, and only the listed transitions move it to another."},{"term":"event","def":"Something that happens and may change the state, such as someone pressing Lock."},{"term":"transition","def":"A rule: in state A, event E leads to state B."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A player has <code>var isPlaying</code> and <code>var isPaused</code>. How many combinations can the two make?","options":["Four, and one of them makes no sense","Three","Two"],"a":0,"why":"Two Booleans give 2 × 2 = 4 combinations. Playing and paused at once is the impossible one."},{"q":"With the door rules above, the door is LOCKED and the event OPEN arrives. What is the state after?","options":["<code>LOCKED</code>","<code>OPEN</code>","<code>CLOSED</code>"],"a":0,"why":"From LOCKED, only UNLOCK leads anywhere. Any other event leaves the state as it is."},{"q":"One state needs its own data, like a percent while loading. Which Kotlin tool fits?","options":["A sealed interface with a class for that state","An enum plus a few Booleans","A plain String"],"a":0,"why":"Each member of a sealed family can carry its own properties, and <code>when</code> still checks you handled them all."}]}]},{"id":"android","title":"Android fundamentals","description":"Your first real app: install Android Studio, run it on a virtual phone, see how a screen lives, add resources and languages, then open screens, ask for permissions and handle Back.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/android.js","lessons":[{"id":"what-android","title":"What Android is","tagline":"The program that runs the phone, and the apps that run on top of it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Say what an operating system does for apps","Tell an app, Android and Google Play apart","Match an Android version to its API level"],"remember":"Android is the operating system that runs the phone and its apps. Each version has an API level: Android 16 is 36.","terms":[{"term":"operating system","def":"The main program a device runs. It starts apps, shares the screen and the hardware between them, and keeps them apart."},{"term":"app","def":"A program that runs on top of Android, like a weather app or your own."},{"term":"Google Play","def":"The app store on most Android phones. People download apps from it, and Android installs them."},{"term":"API level","def":"The number of an Android version: Android 15 is 35, Android 16 is 36, Android 17 is 37."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is Android?","options":["The operating system that runs the phone and its apps","An app you download from Google Play","A programming language"],"a":0,"why":"Android is the main program on the phone. Apps, including yours, run on top of it."},{"q":"An app supports API level 30 and newer. Which phone can install it?","options":["A phone on API level 33","A phone on API level 29","Both"],"a":0,"why":"33 is newer than 30. A phone on 29 is older than the oldest version the app supports."},{"q":"An app wants to show text on the screen. How does it happen?","options":["The app tells Android what to show, and Android draws it","The app switches the screen’s pixels on by itself","Google Play draws it"],"a":0,"why":"Apps never touch the hardware directly. They ask Android, which shares the screen between apps."}]},{"id":"studio","title":"Installing Android Studio","tagline":"One program to write, build and try your apps.","level":"beginner","goals":["Install Android Studio with the Android SDK","Create a virtual phone to test your apps on","Say what the IDE, the SDK and the emulator each do"],"remember":"Android Studio is where you write and run apps; the SDK builds them; the emulator is a phone in a window.","terms":[{"term":"IDE","def":"Integrated development environment: a code editor that can also build and run your code. Android Studio is one."},{"term":"Android SDK","def":"Software development kit: the tools and Android libraries that turn your code into an app."},{"term":"emulator","def":"A program that pretends to be an Android phone, in a window on your computer. Each virtual phone is also called an AVD (Android Virtual Device)."},{"term":"system image","def":"A copy of one Android version for the emulator to run."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the Android SDK give you?","options":["The tools and libraries that build Android apps","An app store","A keyboard for typing code"],"a":0,"why":"SDK means software development kit: what you need to turn code into an Android app."},{"q":"What is a system image?","options":["A copy of one Android version for the emulator to run","A screenshot of your app","The app’s icon"],"a":0,"why":"The emulator needs an Android to run. The system image you pick decides which version."},{"q":"Where do you create a virtual phone?","options":["In Device Manager","In Google Play","In the Android SDK"],"a":0,"why":"Device Manager, in Android Studio, lists your virtual phones. <b>+</b> and <b>Create Virtual Device</b> make a new one."}]},{"id":"first-app","title":"Your first app","tagline":"Create a project, run it on the emulator, change one word.","level":"beginner","goals":["Create a project from the Empty Activity template","Run the app on the emulator","Change the code and see the change after Run"],"remember":"Code changes reach the phone only when you click Run.","terms":[{"term":"template","def":"A ready-made project Android Studio writes for you, like Empty Activity."},{"term":"package name","def":"A unique name for the app, written like a web address backwards: <code>com.example.hello</code>."},{"term":"Gradle","def":"The build tool Android Studio uses to turn the project into an app."},{"term":"Run","def":"The ▶ button: builds the app, installs it on the chosen phone and opens it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the package name <code>com.example.hello</code> for?","options":["It is a unique name for the app","It is the text on the screen","It is the Android version"],"a":0,"why":"Android tells apps apart by this name, so no two apps on a phone share one."},{"q":"What does Minimum SDK set?","options":["The oldest Android version the app runs on","The newest Android version","How big the app can be"],"a":0,"why":"It is an API level. Phones older than it can’t install the app."},{"q":"You click Run. What happens?","options":["The app is built, installed on the phone and opened","The code is sent to Google Play","Only the spelling is checked"],"a":0,"why":"Run does the whole trip: build, install, open."}]},{"id":"project-files","title":"What’s in a project","tagline":"Four groups of files, and what each one is for.","level":"beginner","goals":["Find the manifest, the Kotlin code, the resources and the Gradle files","Say what each one is for","Read the app’s Android versions in build.gradle.kts"],"remember":"manifests: what the app is. java: the code. res: everything else. Gradle Scripts: how it is built.","terms":[{"term":"AndroidManifest.xml","def":"The app’s ID card for Android: its name, icon and screens."},{"term":"resources","def":"Everything in an app that isn’t code, in the <b>res</b> folder: pictures, the icon, text, colours."},{"term":"build.gradle.kts","def":"Build settings written in Kotlin. The app’s one holds <code>applicationId</code>, <code>minSdk</code> and <code>targetSdk</code>."},{"term":"minSdk / targetSdk","def":"<code>minSdk</code>: the oldest API level the app runs on. <code>targetSdk</code>: the API level it was tested on."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where do pictures and the app icon go?","options":["In res","In java","In Gradle Scripts"],"a":0,"why":"res holds everything that isn’t code. Pictures go in drawable, the icon in mipmap."},{"q":"What does the manifest tell Android?","options":["What the app is: its name, icon and screens","How to turn Kotlin into an app","Which apps the user likes"],"a":0,"why":"Android reads the manifest to know which screen to open when the icon is tapped."},{"q":"Hello has <code>minSdk = 24</code>. A phone is on API level 23. What happens?","options":["It can’t install Hello","It installs Hello, which then crashes","It installs Hello and runs it"],"a":0,"why":"API level 23 is older than the oldest version Hello supports."}]},{"id":"activity","title":"Activities","tagline":"One screen, one class, and the function Android calls first.","level":"beginner","goals":["Say what an Activity is","Read MainActivity line by line","Put text on the screen with <code>setContent</code> and <code>Text</code>"],"remember":"An Activity is one screen. Android creates it and calls onCreate, where setContent puts your UI on the screen.","terms":[{"term":"Activity","def":"One screen of an app: an object Android creates and calls. Yours builds on <code>ComponentActivity</code>."},{"term":"onCreate","def":"The function Android calls once, right after it creates the Activity. You set up the screen here."},{"term":"Bundle","def":"A small bag of values Android can hand back to a screen. <code>savedInstanceState</code> is <code>null</code> when the screen opens fresh."},{"term":"setContent","def":"Puts a UI on the screen: whatever its block describes, such as <code>Text(\"Hello\")</code>, which shows one line of text."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is an Activity?","options":["One screen of an app, which Android creates and calls","A function you call from <code>main</code>","A file in res"],"a":0,"why":"Android makes the Activity object and calls its functions, such as <code>onCreate</code>."},{"q":"Who creates the <code>MainActivity</code> object?","options":["Android","Your code, with <code>MainActivity()</code>","Gradle"],"a":0,"why":"Android reads the manifest and creates the Activity itself when the screen opens."},{"q":"What does <code>setContent { Text(\"Hi\") }</code> do?","options":["Shows a screen with the text Hi","Prints Hi below the code","Saves Hi in a Bundle"],"a":0,"why":"<code>setContent</code> puts its block on the screen, and <code>Text</code> shows a line of text."}]},{"id":"lifecycle","title":"The activity lifecycle","tagline":"Android tells your screen when it appears, goes away and comes back.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name the three states an open screen moves through","Know which callback Android calls at each step","Watch the callbacks in Logcat with <code>Log.d</code>"],"remember":"Up: onCreate, onStart, onResume. Down: onPause, onStop, onDestroy. Home stops the screen; it doesn’t destroy it.","terms":[{"term":"lifecycle","def":"The states a screen moves through, from created to destroyed, and the order Android moves it in."},{"term":"callback","def":"A function you write and Android calls when something happens, like <code>onStart</code> when the screen becomes visible."},{"term":"Created, Started, Resumed","def":"The object exists; the screen is visible; the screen is in front and gets taps."},{"term":"Logcat","def":"Android Studio’s log window. <code>Log.d(\"Hello\", \"onStart\")</code> writes the message <code>onStart</code> with the tag <code>Hello</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Hello is in front. Ada presses Home. Which callbacks run?","options":["<code>onPause</code>, then <code>onStop</code>","<code>onPause</code> only","<code>onPause</code>, <code>onStop</code>, <code>onDestroy</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The screen leaves the front, then leaves view. It isn’t destroyed, because Ada can come back to it."},{"q":"In which state can the user tap things on your screen?","options":["Resumed","Started","Created"],"a":0,"why":"Started means visible. Resumed means in front, receiving taps."},{"q":"Why does your <code>onStart</code> call <code>super.onStart()</code>?","options":["<code>ComponentActivity</code> does its own work there, and the app crashes if it is skipped","It makes the log line appear","It runs <code>onStart</code> a second time"],"a":0,"why":"Like <code>super.onCreate</code>, each <code>super</code> call lets <code>ComponentActivity</code> do its part. Android checks that it ran."}]},{"id":"config-changes","title":"Rotation and configuration changes","tagline":"Turn the phone, and you get a brand-new Activity.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name changes that make Android recreate an Activity","Predict the callbacks when the phone rotates","Explain why values kept in the Activity are lost"],"remember":"A configuration change, like rotation or dark theme, destroys the Activity and creates a new one. Its properties start over.","terms":[{"term":"configuration","def":"The settings a screen depends on: screen shape and size, light or dark theme, language, font size."},{"term":"configuration change","def":"One of those settings changing while the app is open, such as rotating the phone."},{"term":"recreation","def":"What Android does on a configuration change: destroy the Activity, then create a new one for the new configuration."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these is a configuration change?","options":["Switching the phone to dark theme","Getting a new message","Tapping text in the app"],"a":0,"why":"Dark theme changes how every screen should look, so Android recreates the Activity."},{"q":"Why does Android create a new Activity on a configuration change?","options":["So the new screen starts with the right layout, text and colours","To free memory","Because the app crashed"],"a":0,"why":"A fresh object is built for the new settings from the start."},{"q":"<code>MainActivity</code> has <code>private var name = \"Ada\"</code>, and the app changed it to <code>\"Lin\"</code>. The phone rotates. What is <code>name</code> in the new <code>MainActivity</code>?","options":["<code>\"Ada\"</code>","<code>\"Lin\"</code>","<code>null</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The new object starts with the starting value. The <code>\"Lin\"</code> was in the old object, which is gone."}]},{"id":"resources","title":"Resources","tagline":"Keep words, colours and pictures in res/, and reach them through R.","level":"beginner","goals":["Move the text your app shows into <code>strings.xml</code>","Name a colour and add a picture to <code>res/</code>","Read a resource in code through its id in <code>R</code>"],"remember":"Text, colours and pictures live in res/. R gives each one an id, and getString(R.string.name) reads a text.","terms":[{"term":"resource","def":"Something your app uses that is not code: a text, a colour, a picture. Resources live in <code>res/</code>."},{"term":"strings.xml","def":"The file in <code>res/values/</code> that holds the app’s texts, each with a name."},{"term":"R","def":"A class Android generates when the app builds, with an <code>Int</code> id for every resource, like <code>R.string.greeting</code>."},{"term":"drawable","def":"A picture resource in <code>res/drawable/</code>. Its id is <code>R.drawable.</code> plus the file name without the ending."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is <code>R.string.greeting</code>?","options":["An <code>Int</code> id that points at the text","The text “Hello, Ada!” itself","The file <code>strings.xml</code>"],"a":0,"why":"R holds ids. <code>getString(R.string.greeting)</code> turns the id into the text."},{"q":"Where does a picture called <code>logo.png</code> go, and what is its id?","options":["<code>res/drawable/</code>, <code>R.drawable.logo</code>","<code>res/values/</code>, <code>R.string.logo</code>","Next to <code>MainActivity.kt</code>, <code>R.logo</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Pictures are drawables. The id is the file name without <code>.png</code>."},{"q":"How does an XML file, like the manifest, point at the text <code>app_name</code>?","options":["<code>@string/app_name</code>","<code>R.string.app_name</code>","<code>getString(app_name)</code>"],"a":0,"why":"XML uses <code>@</code> plus the type and name. <code>R.string.app_name</code> is the Kotlin way."}]},{"id":"localization","title":"Other languages","tagline":"Add a folder per language, and Android picks the right one for each phone.","level":"beginner","goals":["Translate your app with a <code>values-es</code> style folder","Write counts like “1 visit” and “3 visits” with <code>plurals</code>","Avoid the dotted and dotless i bug when changing case"],"remember":"values/ has every string; values-xx overrides some. Counts use plurals with the count twice. Compare words with uppercase(), no locale.","terms":[{"term":"language folder","def":"A folder like <code>res/values-es/</code>. The code after the dash names the language its strings are for."},{"term":"default resources","def":"The folders without a dash, like <code>values/</code>. Android falls back to them, so they must have every string."},{"term":"plurals","def":"A string with one <code>&lt;item&gt;</code> per form, such as <code>one</code> and <code>other</code>. Android picks the form from the count and the language."},{"term":"Locale","def":"A description of a language (and maybe a region) for code, like <code>Locale.forLanguageTag(\"tr\")</code>. Case changes and number formats follow it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Hello has <code>values/</code> and <code>values-es/</code>. Where does a Spanish phone get a string that is only in <code>values/</code>?","options":["From <code>values/</code>","Nowhere: the text is empty","From Google Translate"],"a":0,"why":"A string missing from the language folder falls back to the default folder."},{"q":"On an English phone, what does <code>resources.getQuantityString(R.plurals.visit_count, 1, 1)</code> return, with the plurals from this lesson?","options":["<code>\"1 visit\"</code>","<code>\"1 visits\"</code>","<code>\"%d visit\"</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The first <code>1</code> picks <code>quantity=\"one\"</code>, the second replaces <code>%d</code>."},{"q":"What does <code>\"title\".uppercase()</code> return on a phone set to Turkish?","options":["<code>\"TITLE\"</code>","<code>\"TİTLE\"</code>"],"a":0,"why":"With no locale, <code>uppercase()</code> uses the same rules on every phone. Only <code>uppercase(Locale.forLanguageTag(\"tr\"))</code> gives the dotted <code>İ</code>."}]},{"id":"context","title":"Context","tagline":"The object your code uses to reach Android, and which one to keep.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what a <code>Context</code> is and where you already have one","Choose between the Activity and <code>applicationContext</code>","Avoid keeping a closed screen in memory by mistake"],"remember":"An Activity is a Context for its own screen. Anything that lives longer keeps applicationContext.","terms":[{"term":"Context","def":"The object your code uses to reach Android: resources, files, messages, other screens. An Activity is one."},{"term":"applicationContext","def":"One <code>Context</code> for the whole app. It lives as long as the app runs, so long-lived objects may keep it."},{"term":"toast","def":"A short message at the bottom of the screen that goes away by itself: <code>Toast.makeText(this, \"Saved\", Toast.LENGTH_SHORT).show()</code>."},{"term":"memory leak","def":"Memory that can’t be freed because something still points to an object nobody needs, like a closed screen."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can <code>MainActivity</code> call <code>getString(R.string.greeting)</code> with nothing in front?","options":["An Activity is a <code>Context</code>, and <code>getString</code> belongs to <code>Context</code>","<code>getString</code> works anywhere in Kotlin","Android Studio adds it while the app runs"],"a":0,"why":"The Activity inherits <code>getString</code> from <code>Context</code>."},{"q":"An <code>object</code> keeps the first Activity it was given. The phone rotates. What happens to that old Activity?","options":["It stays in memory, because the object still points to it","The object switches to the new Activity by itself","The app stops at once"],"a":0,"why":"Android frees only what nothing points to. The object still points to the old screen."},{"q":"A cache lives as long as the app and needs a <code>Context</code>. What should it keep?","options":["<code>applicationContext</code>","The Activity that created it","A new Activity for each call"],"a":0,"why":"Keep the context that lives as long as the cache: the app’s."}]},{"id":"intents","title":"Intents","tagline":"Name the screen you want, or describe the job and let Android find an app.","level":"beginner","goals":["Open your own second screen with an <code>Intent</code>","Send a value to that screen with <code>putExtra</code>","Open a web link in another app"],"remember":"An explicit Intent names your screen; an implicit Intent names a job. startActivity sends it; catch ActivityNotFoundException.","terms":[{"term":"Intent","def":"A message that asks Android to start something, like another screen."},{"term":"explicit intent","def":"An Intent that names the exact class to start, for screens in your own app: <code>Intent(this, DetailActivity::class.java)</code>."},{"term":"implicit intent","def":"An Intent that describes a job, like <code>ACTION_VIEW</code> with a web address, so Android finds an app that can do it."},{"term":"extra","def":"A value carried by an Intent under a key: <code>putExtra(\"title\", \"Shopping\")</code>, read with <code>getStringExtra(\"title\")</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>Intent(Intent.ACTION_VIEW, Uri.parse(\"https://example.com\"))</code> is","options":["implicit: it describes a job","explicit: it names a class"],"a":0,"why":"It names an action and an address, not a class. Android picks the app."},{"q":"In <code>DetailActivity</code>, what type does <code>intent.getStringExtra(\"title\")</code> have?","options":["<code>String?</code>","<code>String</code>","<code>Int</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The key might be missing, so the result can be <code>null</code>. Use <code>?:</code> to give a default."},{"q":"To open your own <code>SettingsActivity</code>, which intent fits?","options":["An explicit one: <code>Intent(this, SettingsActivity::class.java)</code>","An implicit one with <code>ACTION_VIEW</code>"],"a":0,"why":"For your own screens, name the class. Then no other app can receive it."}]},{"id":"permissions","title":"Permissions","tagline":"The camera, location and microphone need the user’s yes, asked at the right moment.","level":"beginner","goals":["Declare a permission in the manifest","Check a permission and ask for it while the app runs","Keep the app working when the answer is no"],"remember":"List it in the manifest, check it, ask when the user wants the feature, and keep working if the answer is no.","terms":[{"term":"permission","def":"Android’s permission for an app to use something protected, listed in the manifest with <code>&lt;uses-permission&gt;</code>."},{"term":"runtime permission","def":"A permission the user must also grant while the app runs, through a system dialog. Camera, location and microphone are examples."},{"term":"launcher","def":"The object <code>registerForActivityResult</code> returns. Its <code>launch(...)</code> shows the request, and the answer arrives in your lambda."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Is the manifest line enough to use the camera?","options":["No: the user must also say yes while the app runs","Yes: the manifest line grants it","Only on tablets"],"a":0,"why":"The camera is a runtime permission. The manifest line makes it possible to ask; the user decides."},{"q":"When is the best moment to ask for the camera?","options":["When the user taps “Add photo”","In <code>onCreate</code>, as the app opens","After the app has been open for a week"],"a":0,"why":"Asked in context, the reason is clear to the user."},{"q":"The user tapped “Don’t allow” twice. What can still turn the camera on?","options":["Only the Settings app","Calling <code>launch</code> again","Restarting the phone"],"a":0,"why":"After two denials Android doesn’t show the dialog any more. The user can change it in Settings."}]},{"id":"manifest","title":"The manifest in depth","tagline":"One file tells Android what your app contains, what it needs, and what it can do for other apps.","level":"beginner","goals":["Read the main parts of <code>AndroidManifest.xml</code>","Let other apps share text into a screen with an <code>intent-filter</code>","Use <code>exported</code> and <code>queries</code> correctly"],"remember":"Every component is listed in the manifest. An intent-filter says which jobs a screen accepts, and a screen with a filter must set exported.","terms":[{"term":"component","def":"A part of an app Android can start: an activity, service, receiver or provider. Each one is listed in the manifest."},{"term":"intent filter","def":"A list in the manifest of the implicit intents a component accepts, such as <code>SEND</code> with <code>text/plain</code>."},{"term":"exported","def":"<code>android:exported=\"true\"</code> lets other apps start the component; <code>\"false\"</code> keeps it for your app only."},{"term":"share sheet","def":"The list of apps Android shows when you tap Share. A screen appears in it when its intent filter accepts <code>SEND</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which component does work without a screen, like playing music?","options":["<code>&lt;service&gt;</code>","<code>&lt;activity&gt;</code>","<code>&lt;provider&gt;</code>"],"a":0,"why":"An activity is a screen, and a provider shares data with other apps. A service works without a screen."},{"q":"What does <code>android:exported=\"false\"</code> mean on an Activity?","options":["Only your own app can start it","It can’t be started at all","It is hidden from the home screen only"],"a":0,"why":"Other apps can’t start it; your app still can."},{"q":"Your app targets Android 14 and opens a web link with <code>startActivity</code>. Does it need <code>&lt;queries&gt;</code>?","options":["No: starting another app works without it","Yes: without it the link never opens"],"a":0,"why":"<code>&lt;queries&gt;</code> is only for checking in advance. Starting an app is not filtered; catch <code>ActivityNotFoundException</code>."}]},{"id":"backstack","title":"The back stack and tasks","tagline":"Each new screen goes on top, and Back takes the top one off.","level":"beginner","goals":["Predict where Back goes after several screens","Close a screen from code with <code>finish()</code>","Use a launch mode to avoid two copies of one screen"],"remember":"startActivity puts a screen on top; Back and finish() take it off. singleTop reuses the top screen through onNewIntent.","terms":[{"term":"task","def":"The screens the user opened for one job, kept in order. The Recents screen shows tasks."},{"term":"back stack","def":"The order of the screens in a task, newest on top. Back takes the top one off."},{"term":"finish()","def":"Closes the Activity it is called on, like Back does. The screen below comes back."},{"term":"launch mode","def":"A manifest setting that says what <code>startActivity</code> does when the screen may already exist: <code>standard</code>, <code>singleTop</code>, <code>singleTask</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The stack is List › Detail. The user does Back. What happens to Detail?","options":["It is taken off and destroyed; List comes back","It moves under List","It stays on top, paused"],"a":0,"why":"Back takes the top screen off and destroys it."},{"q":"DetailActivity is <code>standard</code> and on top. The app starts DetailActivity again. What is the stack?","options":["List › Detail › Detail","List › Detail","Detail"],"a":0,"why":"<code>standard</code> always makes a new copy, even of the screen on top."},{"q":"With <code>launchMode=\"singleTop\"</code>, the same case: where does the new Intent arrive?","options":["In <code>onNewIntent</code> of the Detail on top","In <code>onCreate</code> of a new Detail","Nowhere: it is dropped"],"a":0,"why":"<code>singleTop</code> reuses the copy on top and hands it the new Intent."}]},{"id":"predictive-back","title":"Predictive back","tagline":"Show where Back leads before the user lets go, and step in only when you must.","level":"beginner","goals":["Describe what the predictive back gesture shows","Run your own code on Back with <code>addCallback</code>","Turn that code on only while it is needed"],"remember":"Register a back callback in advance and keep it enabled only while Back must do something else; otherwise Android shows the preview.","terms":[{"term":"predictive back","def":"The back gesture that shows, while the finger is still down, the screen Back will lead to."},{"term":"OnBackPressedCallback","def":"Code you register to run on Back. It runs only while its <code>isEnabled</code> is <code>true</code>."},{"term":"onBackPressedDispatcher","def":"The Activity’s list of back callbacks. <code>addCallback</code> adds one."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can’t predictive back work with code that decides after Back happens?","options":["Android must know when the swipe starts whether your app handles Back","The animation is too fast for Kotlin","Back is only for the home screen"],"a":0,"why":"The preview is drawn during the swipe, so the answer is needed before the user lets go."},{"q":"No back callback is enabled. The user swipes back on the second screen. What happens?","options":["The previous screen peeks out, and letting go closes this one","Nothing: Back needs a callback","The app closes"],"a":0,"why":"With no enabled callback, Android handles Back and shows the preview."},{"q":"Your app targets Android 16 and overrides <code>onBackPressed()</code>. On an Android 16 phone, is it called?","options":["No","Yes, as before","Only on tablets"],"a":0,"why":"For apps that target Android 16 and run on it, <code>onBackPressed()</code> is not called. Use a callback."}]}]},{"id":"compose","title":"Jetpack Compose","description":"Build Android UI with Kotlin functions that redraw themselves when state changes.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/compose.js","lessons":[{"id":"what-compose","title":"What Compose is","tagline":"Describe the screen in Kotlin functions, and Compose draws it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what “declarative” means: describe the result, not the steps","Write a function marked <code>@Composable</code> that shows text","See a composable in the Preview without starting the emulator"],"remember":"A composable is a @Composable function that describes what to show for its inputs. @Preview draws one in Android Studio.","terms":[{"term":"annotation","def":"A word starting with <code>@</code> above a function, like <code>@Composable</code>, that gives it extra meaning."},{"term":"declarative","def":"Describing what the screen should show, instead of giving step-by-step changes to make."},{"term":"@Composable","def":"An annotation that marks a function as a composable: a function that describes part of the UI."},{"term":"@Preview","def":"An annotation on a composable with no parameters. Android Studio draws it in the editor."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does “declarative” mean in Compose?","options":["You describe what the screen should show, and Compose draws it","You give step-by-step orders to change each part of the screen","The screen is drawn once and never changes"],"a":0,"why":"You write what the result looks like for the current data. Compose does the drawing."},{"q":"Which function can call <code>Text(\"Hi\")</code>?","options":["A function marked <code>@Composable</code>","Any Kotlin function","Only <code>fun main()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Composables can only be called from other composables, or inside <code>setContent { }</code>."},{"q":"What does <code>@Preview</code> do?","options":["Android Studio draws that composable in the editor","It runs the app on the emulator","It hides the composable from the real app"],"a":0,"why":"It lets you see a composable while you write it. The app itself doesn’t change."}]},{"id":"text-and-modifiers","title":"Text and modifiers","tagline":"Make text bigger and bolder, and give it room.","level":"beginner","goals":["Change how text looks with <code>fontSize</code> and <code>fontWeight</code>","Add space and set a size with a <code>Modifier</code>","Tell <code>dp</code> and <code>sp</code> apart"],"remember":"Text styles with fontSize (in sp) and fontWeight. A Modifier sets space and size (in dp), and more.","terms":[{"term":"Modifier","def":"A chain of instructions about size, space and more, passed to a composable as <code>modifier = Modifier...</code>."},{"term":"padding","def":"Empty space inside a composable’s edges: <code>Modifier.padding(16.dp)</code>."},{"term":"dp","def":"The unit for sizes and spaces. It looks about the same size on every screen."},{"term":"sp","def":"The unit for text size. It also grows with the text size someone picks in the phone’s settings."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which unit do you use for <code>fontSize</code>?","options":["<code>sp</code>","<code>dp</code>","Pixels"],"a":0,"why":"<code>sp</code> is for text, and it grows with the phone’s text size setting."},{"q":"What does <code>Modifier.fillMaxWidth()</code> do?","options":["Makes the composable as wide as the space it is in","Makes the letters wider","Adds space on the left and right"],"a":0,"why":"The area of the composable grows. The letters keep their size."},{"q":"Where does <code>Modifier.padding(16.dp)</code> add space?","options":["On all four sides","Only on the left","Only above"],"a":0,"why":"With one value, padding adds the same space on every side."}]},{"id":"layouts","title":"Arranging things","tagline":"Put composables in a column, in a row, or on top of each other.","level":"beginner","goals":["Stack composables top to bottom with <code>Column</code> and side by side with <code>Row</code>","Center things and put even space between them","Layer composables on top of each other with <code>Box</code>"],"remember":"Column stacks down, Row goes across, Box layers on top. Alignment centers, Arrangement.spacedBy adds space between.","terms":[{"term":"Column","def":"Puts its children from top to bottom."},{"term":"Row","def":"Puts its children side by side."},{"term":"Box","def":"Puts its children on top of each other."},{"term":"children","def":"The composables called inside a layout’s braces."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which layout puts its children on top of each other?","options":["<code>Box</code>","<code>Column</code>","<code>Row</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A Box layers its children. Column and Row put them next to each other."},{"q":"In a <code>Column</code>, which argument centers the children across the screen?","options":["<code>horizontalAlignment = Alignment.CenterHorizontally</code>","<code>verticalArrangement = Arrangement.spacedBy(8.dp)</code>","<code>Modifier.fillMaxSize()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A Column stacks children downward; alignment decides where each one sits across."},{"q":"A Column of three Texts has <code>Arrangement.spacedBy(8.dp)</code>. Where is the space?","options":["8 dp between each pair: two gaps","8 dp above, between and below every Text","8 dp only below the last Text"],"a":0,"why":"<code>spacedBy</code> only adds space between children, not at the edges."}]},{"id":"images","title":"Images and icons","tagline":"Show pictures and small symbols from your drawables.","level":"beginner","goals":["Show a picture from <code>res/drawable</code> with <code>Image</code>","Describe a picture for people who can’t see it with <code>contentDescription</code>","Add a small symbol with <code>Icon</code>"],"remember":"Image(painter = painterResource(R.drawable.x), contentDescription = ...) shows a picture. Use null only for decoration.","terms":[{"term":"painterResource","def":"Loads a drawable from <code>res/drawable</code> so <code>Image</code> or <code>Icon</code> can draw it."},{"term":"contentDescription","def":"The words a screen reader says for an image. <code>null</code> means “skip it, it’s decoration”."},{"term":"screen reader","def":"A tool that reads the screen aloud for people who can’t see it well. On Android it is TalkBack."},{"term":"Icon","def":"A small symbol in the color of the content around it. It takes the drawable’s own size; icons from Vector Asset are 24 dp."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>painterResource(R.drawable.glass)</code> do?","options":["Loads the glass drawable so it can be drawn","Draws a new picture of a glass","Saves a picture into <code>res/drawable</code>"],"a":0,"why":"It reads the drawable you added to the project and gives Compose a painter for it."},{"q":"A picture is pure decoration. Which <code>contentDescription</code> fits?","options":["<code>null</code>","<code>\"image\"</code>","<code>\"\"</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>null</code> tells the screen reader to skip it. Any text would be read aloud."},{"q":"You add a star with <b>New &gt; Vector Asset</b> and show it with <code>Icon</code>, with no size. How big is it?","options":["24 dp","The whole screen width","1 dp"],"a":0,"why":"Vector Asset saves icons at 24 dp, and an <code>Icon</code> takes its drawable’s own size. <code>Modifier.size(...)</code> changes it."}]},{"id":"buttons","title":"Buttons and clicks","tagline":"Run your own code when someone taps.","level":"beginner","goals":["Make a tappable button with <code>Button</code> and <code>onClick</code>","Put text and an icon inside a button","Pick a button style, and switch a button off with <code>enabled = false</code>"],"remember":"Button(onClick = { ... }) { Text(\"...\") }: the first lambda runs on each tap, the second is what the button shows.","terms":[{"term":"Button","def":"A tappable composable. <code>onClick</code> says what happens, the trailing lambda what it shows."},{"term":"onClick","def":"The lambda a button runs each time it is tapped."},{"term":"enabled","def":"When <code>false</code>, the button turns grey and ignores taps."}],"quiz":[{"q":"When does the code inside <code>onClick = { ... }</code> run?","options":["Each time the button is tapped","Once, when the screen appears","Never, unless you call it yourself"],"a":0,"why":"Compose keeps the lambda and runs it for every tap."},{"q":"Which style fits a less important action?","options":["<code>TextButton</code>","<code>Button</code>","Both are the same"],"a":0,"why":"<code>Button</code> is filled and stands out, for the main action. <code>TextButton</code> is plain text."},{"q":"In <code>Button(onClick = { ... }) { ... }</code>, what goes in the second pair of braces?","options":["What the button shows","The code to run on a tap","The button’s color"],"a":0,"why":"The trailing lambda holds the content, like a <code>Text</code>. The code to run on a tap goes in <code>onClick</code>."}]},{"id":"state","title":"State","tagline":"Values that update the screen when they change.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why a plain <code>var</code> doesn’t update the screen","Hold a changing value with <code>remember { mutableStateOf(...) }</code>","Change it in <code>onClick</code> and watch the screen follow"],"remember":"var x by remember { mutableStateOf(start) }: change x, and every composable that reads it runs again.","terms":[{"term":"state","def":"A value Compose watches, made with <code>mutableStateOf</code>. Changing it updates the screen."},{"term":"recomposition","def":"Compose running a composable again because a state it read has changed."},{"term":"remember","def":"Keeps a value between runs of a composable, instead of making it again each time."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>mutableStateOf(0)</code> give you?","options":["A box holding 0 that Compose watches","A plain number 0","A list of numbers"],"a":0,"why":"When its value changes, Compose runs the composables that read it again."},{"q":"Why wrap it in <code>remember { }</code>?","options":["So the same box is kept each time the composable runs again","So the value is saved to a file","So the button can be tapped"],"a":0,"why":"Without it, each run would make a fresh box with the starting value."},{"q":"What is recomposition?","options":["Compose running a composable again after a state it read changed","Restarting the whole app","Building the project in Android Studio"],"a":0,"why":"It is how the screen catches up with new state."}]},{"id":"recomposition","title":"How Compose redraws","tagline":"Only the parts that read a changed state run again.","level":"beginner","goals":["Tell which composables run again after a state change","Explain why writing the same value changes nothing","Keep composables quick and free of side effects, because they can run often"],"remember":"When a state changes, only the composables that read it, or get a changed input, run again. The rest are skipped.","terms":[{"term":"skipping","def":"Compose leaving a composable out of a recomposition because none of its inputs changed."},{"term":"side effect","def":"Work beyond describing the screen, like writing to Logcat or saving a file. It belongs in <code>onClick</code>, not in a composable’s body."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>glasses</code> is 0. You run <code>glasses = 0</code>. What runs again?","options":["Nothing","Every composable","Only <code>WaterScreen</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The value didn’t change, so there is nothing to redraw."},{"q":"Where should code that saves the count to a file go?","options":["In <code>onClick</code>","In the body of <code>GlassCount</code>","In <code>Title</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A composable body can run many times or be skipped. <code>onClick</code> runs once per tap."},{"q":"<code>Title()</code> takes no parameters and reads no state. What happens to it when <code>glasses</code> changes?","options":["It is skipped","It runs again","It disappears from the screen"],"a":0,"why":"Skipped composables stay on the screen as they were. Their code just doesn’t run."}]},{"id":"hoisting","title":"State hoisting","tagline":"Move state up, so several composables can share it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Turn a composable with its own state into one that takes a value and an event","Pass state down and events up between composables","Preview a stateless composable with any value"],"remember":"Hoist state to the composable that calls everyone who needs it. Values go down as parameters, events go up as lambdas.","terms":[{"term":"state hoisting","def":"Moving state up into a calling composable, and passing the value and an event function down instead."},{"term":"stateless","def":"A composable that holds no state of its own: it shows what it is given."},{"term":"stateful","def":"A composable that holds state with <code>remember</code>."},{"term":"event","def":"Something that happened, like a tap, reported upward by calling a lambda such as <code>onAdd()</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Two composables need the same <code>glasses</code>. Where should the state live?","options":["In the composable that calls both of them","Inside one of them","In both, one copy each"],"a":0,"why":"Hoisting it to the common caller gives one place for the value, shared by both."},{"q":"In <code>GlassCounter(glasses: Int, onAdd: () -&gt; Unit)</code>, what is <code>onAdd</code> for?","options":["Reporting a tap upward","Holding the number","Drawing the button"],"a":0,"why":"Events go up as lambdas. The owner of the state decides what to do."},{"q":"Why are stateless composables easy to preview?","options":["You can pass in any value, like 8 glasses","They don’t need <code>@Composable</code>","They have no parameters"],"a":0,"why":"The preview calls them with whatever value you want to see."}]},{"id":"saveable","title":"Surviving rotation","tagline":"Keep state when the Activity is created again.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why <code>remember</code> loses its value when the phone rotates","Keep a value through rotation with <code>rememberSaveable</code>","Know which values it keeps, and when the app starts fresh"],"remember":"remember forgets when the Activity is recreated; rememberSaveable puts simple values in the saved state and gets them back.","terms":[{"term":"rememberSaveable","def":"Like <code>remember</code>, but the value also survives rotation and Android closing the app in the background."},{"term":"saved state","def":"A small bag of values Android keeps for an Activity while it is recreated: the <code>Bundle</code> that <code>onCreate</code> receives as <code>savedInstanceState</code>."},{"term":"Recents screen","def":"The phone’s list of recently used apps. Swiping an app away there closes it for good."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does <code>remember</code> lose its value when the phone rotates?","options":["The Activity is destroyed and created again","Rotation clears the phone’s memory","Compose resets every value once a minute"],"a":0,"why":"What <code>remember</code> kept belonged to the old Activity."},{"q":"Android closes your app in the background, and the person comes back. Which value is still there?","options":["One kept with <code>rememberSaveable</code>","One kept with <code>remember</code>","Neither"],"a":0,"why":"The saved state is restored in this case too."},{"q":"The person swipes the app away from Recents and opens it again. What does <code>rememberSaveable</code> give back?","options":["The starting value: a fresh start","The last saved value"],"a":0,"why":"Closing an app on purpose throws its saved state away."}]},{"id":"text-input","title":"Text fields","tagline":"Let people type, and use what they typed.","level":"beginner","goals":["Add a box people can type in with <code>TextField</code>","Keep what they type with <code>rememberTextFieldState</code>","Read the typed text and turn it into a number"],"remember":"val s = rememberTextFieldState() and TextField(state = s): read the typed text with s.text.toString().","terms":[{"term":"TextField","def":"A box people can type in. It brings up the keyboard when tapped."},{"term":"TextFieldState","def":"Holds a text field’s text. Read it with <code>.text.toString()</code>."},{"term":"rememberTextFieldState","def":"Makes a <code>TextFieldState</code> and keeps it, through rotation too."},{"term":"label","def":"A small composable inside a text field that says what to type."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why call <code>.toString()</code> on <code>goalState.text</code> before <code>toIntOrNull()</code>?","options":["It holds typed characters, but isn’t a String yet","To delete the text","To show the keyboard"],"a":0,"why":"<code>toIntOrNull()</code> works on a String, so turn the characters into one first."},{"q":"The person types 12 and rotates the phone. What does the field show?","options":["12","Nothing","The starting text"],"a":0,"why":"<code>rememberTextFieldState</code> uses <code>rememberSaveable</code> inside."},{"q":"What is <code>label = { Text(\"Daily goal\") }</code> for?","options":["Telling people what to type in the field","Setting the starting text","Choosing the keyboard"],"a":0,"why":"The label is shown inside the field as a hint about its purpose."}]},{"id":"lists","title":"Long lists","tagline":"Show hundreds of rows, but build only the ones on screen.","level":"beginner","goals":["Show a scrolling list with <code>LazyColumn</code>","Add rows with <code>items</code> and a single row with <code>item</code>","Explain why a long list should not be a <code>Column</code>"],"remember":"For a long list use LazyColumn { items(list) { value -> ... } }. It builds only the rows on screen.","terms":[{"term":"LazyColumn","def":"A scrolling column that builds only the rows on screen. <code>LazyRow</code> is the same, side to side."},{"term":"lazy","def":"Doing work only when it is needed. A lazy list builds a row when it scrolls into view."},{"term":"items","def":"Inside a <code>LazyColumn</code>: <code>items(list) { value -> ... }</code> adds one row for every value. <code>item { }</code> adds a single row."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why use <code>LazyColumn</code> instead of <code>Column</code> for two years of history?","options":["It builds only the rows on screen, and it scrolls","It sorts the days","It makes the text bigger"],"a":0,"why":"A <code>Column</code> builds all 730 rows at once. <code>LazyColumn</code> builds what you can see."},{"q":"In <code>items(days) { day -> Text(day) }</code>, what is <code>day</code>?","options":["One value from the list, a different one for each row","The whole list","The number of rows"],"a":0,"why":"Like the variable in a <code>for</code> loop, it is the current value."},{"q":"Which one shows a list that scrolls from left to right?","options":["<code>LazyRow</code>","<code>LazyColumn</code>","<code>Row</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>LazyRow</code> is the side-to-side version. A plain <code>Row</code> builds everything and doesn’t scroll."}]},{"id":"lazy-keys","title":"Keys in lists","tagline":"Give every row a name tag so its state stays with it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why a row’s remembered state can jump to another row","Give list rows a <code>key</code>","Choose a key that is unique and never changes"],"remember":"items(list, key = { it.id }): a unique id that never changes keeps each row’s state with its item.","terms":[{"term":"key","def":"A value that names one row in a lazy list, given with <code>key = { ... }</code>. Remembered state follows the key."},{"term":"unique","def":"Not shared with anything else. No two rows may have the same key."},{"term":"mutableStateListOf","def":"Makes a list you can <code>add</code> to and <code>remove</code> from, which Compose watches like state."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Without keys, how does a lazy list match a row’s remembered state?","options":["By the row’s position","By the row’s text","It doesn’t remember anything"],"a":0,"why":"By position, so state can land on the wrong item when rows move."},{"q":"Which is the best key for today’s drinks?","options":["The drink’s id number","The drink’s size in ml","The row’s position"],"a":0,"why":"An id is unique and stays the same. Sizes repeat, and positions change."},{"q":"Two rows get the same key. What happens?","options":["The app crashes","Compose picks one at random","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"Keys must be unique. A repeated key throws <code>IllegalArgumentException: Key \"...\" was already used.</code>"}]},{"id":"scaffold","title":"Screen structure","tagline":"A top bar, the content and an add button, each in its place.","level":"beginner","goals":["Build a screen with <code>Scaffold</code>","Add a top bar and a floating action button","Keep content clear of the bars with <code>innerPadding</code>"],"remember":"Scaffold(topBar = { ... }, floatingActionButton = { ... }) { innerPadding -> ... }, and always use innerPadding.","terms":[{"term":"Scaffold","def":"A ready-made screen layout with slots for a top bar, a floating action button and the content."},{"term":"slot","def":"A parameter that takes a piece of UI as a lambda, like <code>topBar = { ... }</code>."},{"term":"FAB","def":"Floating action button: a square button with rounded corners for the screen’s main action, made with <code>FloatingActionButton</code>."},{"term":"innerPadding","def":"The space the bars take, status bar included, handed to the content. Apply it with <code>Modifier.padding(innerPadding)</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>innerPadding</code> tell you?","options":["How much room the bars take","The size of the FAB","The space between list rows"],"a":0,"why":"Apply it to the content so nothing hides behind the bars."},{"q":"Where does a <code>FloatingActionButton</code> go?","options":["In the <code>floatingActionButton</code> slot","Inside the <code>TopAppBar</code>","Anywhere in the content"],"a":0,"why":"The slot puts it in the bottom corner, above the content."},{"q":"Inside a Scaffold, do you still need <code>safeDrawingPadding()</code> on the content?","options":["No: <code>innerPadding</code> already includes the status bar","Yes, always","Only in dark mode"],"a":0,"why":"Scaffold works out the room for the bars and the status bar, and hands it to you as <code>innerPadding</code>."}]},{"id":"theming","title":"Theming","tagline":"Pick colors and text styles once; the whole app follows, even in dark mode.","level":"beginner","goals":["Give the app its own colors with <code>lightColorScheme</code> and <code>darkColorScheme</code>","Read colors and text styles from <code>MaterialTheme</code>","Make a screen that really turns dark in dark mode"],"remember":"Read colors and text styles from MaterialTheme, put the screen in a Scaffold or Surface, and dark mode works.","terms":[{"term":"theme","def":"The colors and text styles of the whole app, set once with <code>MaterialTheme</code> and read everywhere inside it."},{"term":"color role","def":"A color named by its job, such as <code>primary</code> or <code>onSurface</code> (“text on a surface”), not by its shade."},{"term":"Surface","def":"A composable that paints a theme color behind its content and sets the text color to match. <code>Scaffold</code> does the same for a whole screen."},{"term":"dynamic color","def":"On Android 12 and newer, colors taken from the phone’s wallpaper. The template turns it on with <code>dynamicColor = true</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You set <code>primary</code> to water blue, but on your Android 14 phone the buttons are green. Why?","options":["<code>dynamicColor</code> is on, so the colors come from the wallpaper","Android 14 can’t show blue","The hex number is wrong"],"a":0,"why":"The template turns on dynamic color for Android 12 and newer. Set <code>dynamicColor</code> to <code>false</code> to see your own colors."},{"q":"What is <code>onPrimary</code> for?","options":["Text and icons drawn on top of the primary color","A brighter primary color","The color of the top bar only"],"a":0,"why":"“On” roles are for content that sits on top of the matching color, so it stays readable."},{"q":"Dark mode is on, but your screen stays white. What is missing?","options":["A <code>Scaffold</code> or <code>Surface</code> to paint the background","A call to <code>isSystemInDarkTheme()</code> in every Text","Nothing: screens are always white"],"a":0,"why":"The theme only holds the colors. <code>Scaffold</code> or <code>Surface</code> paints them behind your content."}]},{"id":"overlays","title":"Dialogs and sheets","tagline":"Ask before resetting, offer choices from the bottom.","level":"beginner","goals":["Show an <code>AlertDialog</code> controlled by a Boolean state","Close it from its buttons and from <code>onDismissRequest</code>","Show a <code>ModalBottomSheet</code> with a few choices"],"remember":"A dialog or sheet is on screen while you call it: if (show) { AlertDialog(...) }, and every way out sets show = false.","terms":[{"term":"dialog","def":"A small box over the screen that asks a question, made with <code>AlertDialog</code>."},{"term":"bottom sheet","def":"A panel that slides up from the bottom of the screen, made with <code>ModalBottomSheet</code>."},{"term":"onDismissRequest","def":"Runs when the user tries to close the dialog or sheet without a button: tapping outside, Back, or swiping a sheet down."}],"quiz":[{"q":"How do you close an <code>AlertDialog</code>?","options":["Set the state that controls it to false","Call <code>close()</code> on it","Compose closes it after any tap"],"a":0,"why":"It is on screen while the <code>if</code> calls it. Change the state and it is gone."},{"q":"When does <code>onDismissRequest</code> run?","options":["When the user taps outside or presses Back","When the user taps the confirm button","When the dialog first appears"],"a":0,"why":"Buttons have their own <code>onClick</code>. <code>onDismissRequest</code> is for the other ways out."},{"q":"Which line must sit above a function that uses <code>ModalBottomSheet</code>?","options":["<code>@OptIn(ExperimentalMaterial3Api::class)</code>","<code>@Preview</code>","None"],"a":0,"why":"The bottom sheet is marked experimental, so you opt in."}]},{"id":"gestures","title":"Gestures","tagline":"Make anything tappable, and let the user drag it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Make any element respond to taps with <code>clickable</code>","Follow a finger with <code>draggable</code>","Move an element with <code>offset</code>"],"remember":"clickable { } for taps on anything; draggable reports how far the finger moved, and offset moves the element.","terms":[{"term":"clickable","def":"A modifier that runs a lambda when the element is tapped, with a ripple and screen reader support."},{"term":"draggable","def":"A modifier that reports finger movement in one direction, as <code>delta</code> pixels."},{"term":"offset","def":"A modifier that shifts an element by a number of pixels, with <code>IntOffset(x, y)</code>."},{"term":"Float","def":"A decimal number type like <code>Double</code>, but smaller. Written with an <code>f</code>: <code>0f</code>, <code>2.5f</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>delta</code> mean in <code>rememberDraggableState { delta -> ... }</code>?","options":["How far the finger moved since the last call","The total distance from the start","Whether the finger is down"],"a":0,"why":"It is a small step. Adding them up gives the whole distance."},{"q":"You want a whole card to open its details when tapped. What do you use?","options":["<code>Modifier.clickable { ... }</code>","<code>Modifier.draggable(...)</code>","<code>Modifier.offset { ... }</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>clickable</code> is for taps. <code>draggable</code> is for movement, <code>offset</code> moves things."},{"q":"What is <code>2.7f.roundToInt()</code>?","options":["<code>3</code>","<code>2</code>","<code>2.7</code>"],"a":0,"why":"It rounds to the nearest whole number, and 2.7 is closer to 3."}]},{"id":"effects","title":"Side effects","tagline":"Do something when a screen appears, not on every redraw.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why waiting doesn’t belong in a composable’s body","Run code when a composable appears with <code>LaunchedEffect</code>","Restart it by changing its key"],"remember":"LaunchedEffect(key) { ... } runs when it appears, starts over when the key changes, and stops when it leaves the screen.","terms":[{"term":"side effect","def":"Something a composable does besides describing the screen: waiting, saving, sending."},{"term":"LaunchedEffect","def":"Runs a block when it appears on screen, starts it over when its key changes, and stops it when it leaves."},{"term":"key (of an effect)","def":"The value in <code>LaunchedEffect(key)</code>. A new value restarts the block."},{"term":"delay","def":"<code>delay(ms)</code> waits that many milliseconds without freezing the screen. It is a <code>suspend</code> function, so it runs inside a coroutine such as the block of <code>LaunchedEffect</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The screen recomposes because some other state changed. Does a <code>LaunchedEffect(Unit)</code> block start again?","options":["No","Yes, every time","Only if it already finished"],"a":0,"why":"Recomposition doesn’t restart it. Only a new key, or leaving and coming back, does."},{"q":"What happens to a running <code>LaunchedEffect</code> block when its key changes?","options":["It is stopped and starts again from the top","It keeps going and a second copy starts","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"One block per effect: the old one is stopped, a new one starts."},{"q":"The user leaves the screen during <code>delay(5000)</code>. What happens to the block?","options":["It is stopped","It finishes the wait in the background","The app waits before leaving"],"a":0,"why":"The effect left the screen, so its block is stopped."}]},{"id":"disposable-effect","title":"Cleaning up","tagline":"Start listening when a screen appears, stop when it goes.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Pair a start with a stop using <code>DisposableEffect</code>","Put the cleanup in <code>onDispose</code>","Predict when the cleanup runs"],"remember":"DisposableEffect(key) { start; onDispose { stop } }: the stop runs when the screen leaves or the key changes.","terms":[{"term":"DisposableEffect","def":"An effect for start and stop pairs. Its block runs when it appears; <code>onDispose</code> runs when it leaves or its key changes."},{"term":"onDispose","def":"The cleanup block that must end every <code>DisposableEffect</code>."},{"term":"dispose","def":"To clean something up when you are done with it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"When does <code>onDispose</code> run?","options":["When the composable leaves the screen, or the key changes","Every time the screen recomposes","Only when the app closes"],"a":0,"why":"Those are the two moments when the old effect is no longer wanted."},{"q":"Which job fits <code>DisposableEffect</code> better than <code>LaunchedEffect</code>?","options":["Add a listener now and remove it later","Wait 5 seconds, then hide a tip","Show a dialog"],"a":0,"why":"It is made for start and stop pairs. Waiting belongs in <code>LaunchedEffect</code>."},{"q":"The user switches from the Home bottle to the Office bottle. How many listeners from this screen does the Home bottle have afterwards?","options":["None","One","Two"],"a":0,"why":"The key changed, so <code>onDispose</code> removed the Home listener before the block added one to Office."}]},{"id":"snapshot-flow","title":"Watching state over time","tagline":"React each time a state changes, even one your code doesn’t change.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Read a list’s scroll position with <code>rememberLazyListState</code>","Turn state changes into a stream of values with <code>snapshotFlow</code>","Handle each new value with <code>collect</code>"],"remember":"snapshotFlow { state } hands out a new value each time the state changes; collect { } handles each one, inside LaunchedEffect.","terms":[{"term":"snapshotFlow","def":"Watches the state read inside its braces and hands out a new value each time the result changes."},{"term":"flow","def":"A stream of values that arrive one by one over time."},{"term":"collect","def":"Runs a lambda for every value of a flow, and keeps waiting for more. Lives inside <code>LaunchedEffect</code>."},{"term":"firstVisibleItemIndex","def":"On a list state: the index of the row at the top of the screen."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The top row stays index 4 while the user scrolls a little. How many new values does <code>snapshotFlow</code> hand out?","options":["None","One per scroll movement","One per second"],"a":0,"why":"It hands out a value only when the result is different from the previous one."},{"q":"Why does the <code>collect</code> go inside <code>LaunchedEffect</code>?","options":["It keeps waiting for values, and must stop when the screen leaves","So it runs only once","It doesn’t need to"],"a":0,"why":"<code>LaunchedEffect</code> is the place that may wait, and it stops when the screen goes. <code>collect</code> runs for every new value, not once."},{"q":"What is <code>listState.firstVisibleItemIndex</code>?","options":["The index of the row at the top of the screen","How many rows the list has","The last row the user tapped"],"a":0,"why":"It changes as the user scrolls, which is why it is worth watching."}]},{"id":"navigation","title":"Moving between screens","tagline":"One Activity, many screens, and a back stack that Compose keeps for you.","level":"beginner","goals":["Describe screens as routes with <code>@Serializable</code>","Set up a <code>NavHost</code> and move with <code>navigate</code>","Pass a value to the next screen and read it with <code>toRoute</code>"],"remember":"Routes are @Serializable types; NavHost maps each route to a screen; navigate(route) pushes, Back or popBackStack() pops.","terms":[{"term":"route","def":"A type that names a screen, and may carry its data, like <code>History(val goal: Int)</code>."},{"term":"NavHost","def":"Shows the screen for the current route. Each <code>composable&lt;Route&gt; { }</code> inside it says what to show."},{"term":"navigate","def":"<code>navController.navigate(route)</code> puts that route’s screen on top of the back stack."},{"term":"@Serializable","def":"Marks a class or object so it can be stored and restored. Navigation needs it on every route."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>navController.navigate(History(goal = 6))</code> do?","options":["Puts the History screen, with goal 6, on top of the back stack","Replaces the whole app with a new Activity","Sets today’s goal to 6"],"a":0,"why":"Navigate pushes a screen. Back pops it."},{"q":"Which route should carry data?","options":["<code>@Serializable data class History(val goal: Int)</code>","<code>@Serializable object History</code>","Neither can"],"a":0,"why":"A data class has properties for the data. An <code>object</code> is for screens that need none."},{"q":"The user makes the Back gesture on the start screen. What happens?","options":["They leave the app","Nothing","The start screen opens again"],"a":0,"why":"There is nothing left to pop, so Back leaves the app, as on any app’s first screen."}]}]},{"id":"tools","title":"Android Studio and debugging","description":"The tools you use every day on your Water tracker: find your way around Android Studio, read Logcat, pause code in the debugger, look inside the screen, install with adb, check with lint, and let GitHub check every pull request.","requires":["android","compose"],"file":"tracks/tools.js","lessons":[{"id":"studio-tour","title":"A tour of Android Studio","tagline":"Find your way around the window: where the files, the code, the running app and the messages are.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name the main parts of the Android Studio window","Open a tool window such as Logcat or Terminal, and get a closed one back","Use three shortcuts: Search Everywhere, quick fixes and comment line"],"remember":"Toolbar to run, Project for files, editor for code, tool windows for the rest. Shift twice finds anything; Alt+Enter fixes.","terms":[{"term":"tool window","def":"A panel on the edge of the Android Studio window for one job, such as Build, Logcat or Terminal. Find them under View &gt; Tool Windows."},{"term":"Running Devices","def":"The tool window that shows the emulator’s screen while your app runs."},{"term":"Search Everywhere","def":"Press Shift twice and type to find a file, a function or a menu action."},{"term":"quick fix","def":"A fix Studio offers for the marked code under the cursor. Alt+Enter (Option+Enter on macOS) opens the list."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where do you choose which device your app runs on?","options":["The target device menu in the toolbar","The Project window","The status bar"],"a":0,"why":"The toolbar holds what to run, where to run it, and the Run and Debug buttons."},{"q":"A word is underlined in red and the cursor is on it. What does <kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> do?","options":["Deletes the line","Shows quick fixes for it","Runs the app"],"a":1,"why":"Alt+Enter (Option+Enter on macOS) lists the fixes Studio knows for the marked code."},{"q":"You closed the Terminal window. How do you get it back?","options":["Restart Android Studio","View &gt; Tool Windows &gt; Terminal","Create a new project"],"a":1,"why":"Every tool window is listed under View &gt; Tool Windows. Its icon on the window edge works too."}]},{"id":"logcat","title":"Logcat","tagline":"Write messages from your app, find yours among thousands, and read a crash.","level":"beginner","goals":["Pick a log level, and keep the tag in one <code>TAG</code> constant","Filter Logcat to your app, a tag or a level","Find a crash and read which line caused it"],"remember":"Log.d(TAG, \"...\") writes a line. package:mine finds yours, level:WARN the serious ones, is:crash the crash.","terms":[{"term":"log level","def":"How serious a message is: Verbose, Debug, Info, Warn or Error, written with <code>Log.v</code>, <code>Log.d</code>, <code>Log.i</code>, <code>Log.w</code> or <code>Log.e</code>."},{"term":"query","def":"What you type in the box at the top of Logcat to keep only some lines, such as <code>package:mine level:WARN</code>."},{"term":"stack trace","def":"The list of <code>at</code> lines under a crash: the function calls that led to it, newest first."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which query keeps only crashes?","options":["<code>level:ERROR</code>","<code>is:crash</code>","<code>tag:crash</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>is:crash</code> matches crash entries. <code>level:ERROR</code> also keeps every other error line."},{"q":"In a crash, which line usually points at the code to fix?","options":["The last line of the block","The first <code>at</code> line that names your own package and file","The <code>FATAL EXCEPTION</code> line"],"a":1,"why":"The stack trace lists calls newest first. The first one in your own code is where your code was when it failed."},{"q":"Which call writes a warning?","options":["<code>Log.d(TAG, \"goal is 0\")</code>","<code>Log.w(TAG, \"goal is 0\")</code>","<code>Log.v(TAG, \"goal is 0\")</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>w</code> is Warn. <code>d</code> is Debug and <code>v</code> is Verbose, both less serious."}]},{"id":"debugger","title":"The debugger","tagline":"Pause your running app on a line and look at the real values inside.","level":"beginner","goals":["Set a breakpoint and start the app with Debug","Run your code one line at a time with Step Over","Read values in the Variables pane to find a bug"],"remember":"Breakpoint on the line, Debug instead of Run, then Step Over and watch Variables until a value surprises you.","terms":[{"term":"debugger","def":"A tool that pauses your running app so you can look at its values and run it one line at a time."},{"term":"breakpoint","def":"A red dot in the gutter next to a line. When the app was started with Debug, it pauses before that line runs."},{"term":"Step Over, Step Into, Step Out","def":"Run the current line and pause on the next; go inside the function called on this line; finish the current function and go back to its caller."},{"term":"Variables pane","def":"The part of the Debug window that lists every value the paused function can see."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You are paused on a line that calls <code>priceOf(item)</code>, and you want to see what happens inside <code>priceOf</code>. Which button?","options":["Step Over","Step Into","Resume Program"],"a":1,"why":"Step Into goes inside the called function. Step Over would run the whole call as one step."},{"q":"What does a red dot in the gutter mean?","options":["A line that crashed","A breakpoint: the app pauses before this line runs","A line that was never run"],"a":1,"why":"Clicking the gutter sets a breakpoint. It works when the app was started with Debug."},{"q":"You have seen enough and want the app to run on normally. What do you press?","options":["Step Over","Resume Program","Step Into"],"a":1,"why":"Resume Program lets the app run until it reaches a breakpoint again."}]},{"id":"layout-inspector","title":"Layout Inspector","tagline":"Look inside your running app’s screen: which composables it is made of, and what each one received.","level":"beginner","goals":["Open Layout Inspector on a running app","Find a composable in the Component Tree and read its values in Attributes","See how often each composable recomposes or skips"],"remember":"Running Devices, Toggle Layout Inspector: the tree shows what was drawn, Attributes shows what it received. Reset, tap once, read the counts.","terms":[{"term":"Layout Inspector","def":"An Android Studio tool that shows the composables of your running app’s screen and the values they received."},{"term":"Component Tree","def":"The Layout Inspector pane that lists the composables, nested the way they are drawn."},{"term":"Attributes","def":"The Layout Inspector pane that shows the values of the selected composable, such as its text."},{"term":"recomposition count","def":"How many times a composable ran again, next to how many times Compose skipped it. Turn them on under View Options."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where do you start Layout Inspector?","options":["In the Running Devices window, with Toggle Layout Inspector","In Logcat, with a query","Under File &gt; New"],"a":0,"why":"Run the app, then use Toggle Layout Inspector in the Running Devices window."},{"q":"You select a Text in the Component Tree. Where do you read the text it received?","options":["Attributes","Logcat","The status bar"],"a":0,"why":"Attributes shows the values of the selected composable."},{"q":"You tap once, and a composable’s skip count goes up by 1. What happened?","options":["It ran again","Compose skipped it: nothing it received changed","It crashed"],"a":1,"why":"The first column counts recompositions, the second counts skips."}]},{"id":"adb","title":"adb","tagline":"Talk to the phone or emulator from the terminal: install apps, run commands on it, read its log.","level":"beginner","goals":["List connected devices with <code>adb devices</code>","Build an APK with <code>./gradlew assembleDebug</code>, then install and remove it with <code>adb install</code> and <code>adb uninstall</code>","Run a command on the device with <code>adb shell</code>, and read its log with <code>adb logcat</code>"],"remember":"adb devices to see, ./gradlew assembleDebug to build the APK, adb install to add, adb uninstall to remove, adb shell to run a command on the device, adb logcat to read the log.","terms":[{"term":"adb","def":"Android Debug Bridge: a command line tool that installs apps on a device, runs commands on it and reads its log."},{"term":"APK","def":"One file that holds a whole Android app, ready to install, such as <code>app-debug.apk</code>."},{"term":"serial","def":"The name adb gives each device, such as <code>emulator-5554</code>. Pick one with <code>-s</code> right after <code>adb</code>."},{"term":"gradlew","def":"A small file in the top folder of a project that runs Gradle, the build tool. <code>./gradlew assembleDebug</code> builds the debug APK."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>adb devices</code> show?","options":["Every app on the phone","The emulators and phones adb can talk to","The files in your project"],"a":1,"why":"It lists each connected device with its serial and state."},{"q":"Which command runs <code>pm</code> on the device?","options":["<code>pm list packages</code>","<code>adb shell pm list packages</code>","<code>adb pm shell list packages</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>adb shell</code> followed by a command runs that command on the device. Typed alone, <code>pm</code> would run on your computer, which has no such tool."},{"q":"<code>adb logcat -s Water</code> keeps printing lines. How do you stop it?","options":["Close Android Studio","Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd>","Uninstall the app"],"a":1,"why":"Ctrl+C stops the command that is running in the terminal."}]},{"id":"lint","title":"Lint","tagline":"A tool that reads your code and warns you about likely bugs before anyone runs it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Run lint with <code>./gradlew lint</code> and find its report","Fix a lint error, such as state made without <code>remember</code>","Use a baseline so old warnings don’t hide new ones"],"remember":"Run never runs lint: ./gradlew lint does. Fix the errors, and baseline old warnings so new ones stand out.","terms":[{"term":"lint","def":"A tool that reads your code, resources and manifest without running them, and reports likely bugs."},{"term":"issue id, severity","def":"Each lint issue has an id, such as <code>UnrememberedMutableState</code>, and a severity, such as warning or error. Errors make <code>./gradlew lint</code> fail."},{"term":"baseline","def":"A file, <code>lint-baseline.xml</code>, that records known issues, so lint reports only new ones."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Does pressing Run also run a full lint check?","options":["Yes, every time","No: you run it yourself, for example with <code>./gradlew lint</code>","Only when the app crashes"],"a":1,"why":"Lint is not part of the normal build. Run it yourself, or let a server run it for you."},{"q":"What does a lint baseline do?","options":["Fixes old issues automatically","Records today’s issues, so later runs report only new ones","Turns lint off"],"a":1,"why":"Old issues are filtered out, so a new one stands out instead of hiding among them."},{"q":"Lint finds one warning and no errors. What does <code>./gradlew lint</code> do?","options":["It fails","It succeeds, and the warning is in the report","It deletes the code"],"a":1,"why":"By default only errors make the command fail. Warnings are reported."}]},{"id":"ci","title":"Continuous integration","tagline":"Let GitHub build and check every pull request for you, on a clean machine.","level":"beginner","goals":["Say what continuous integration is and why teams use it","Read a minimal GitHub Actions workflow line by line","Find a failed check on a pull request and see which step failed"],"remember":"A workflow in .github/workflows/ runs on every pull request: check out, set up Java and Gradle, then ./gradlew lint assembleDebug.","terms":[{"term":"continuous integration (CI)","def":"A server builds and checks every change automatically, on a clean machine, before it is merged."},{"term":"workflow","def":"A YAML file in <code>.github/workflows/</code> that says when GitHub Actions runs and what it does."},{"term":"job, step","def":"A job runs on one machine. Its steps run in order; <code>uses:</code> runs a ready-made action, <code>run:</code> runs a command."},{"term":"check","def":"The result a workflow shows on a pull request: a green tick when it passed, a red cross when it failed."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where must the workflow file be?","options":["In the <code>app</code> folder","In <code>.github/workflows/</code>","Anywhere, if it ends in <code>.yml</code>"],"a":1,"why":"GitHub only discovers workflows in <code>.github/workflows</code>."},{"q":"The <code>run:</code> step with lint fails. What happens next?","options":["The steps after it run anyway","The steps after it don’t run, and the pull request shows a red cross","GitHub fixes the code"],"a":1,"why":"A failed step stops the job. Fix the code and push again to rerun it."},{"q":"Why does the job start with <code>actions/checkout</code>?","options":["To choose a branch name","The machine starts empty, so it first downloads your repository","To install Java"],"a":1,"why":"Every run gets a fresh machine. Without checkout, there is no code to build."}]}]},{"id":"coroutines","title":"Coroutines","description":"Keep the app responsive during slow work: start coroutines, run things at the same time, pick the thread, cancel, handle errors, share values safely and pass values through channels.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/coroutines.js","lessons":[{"id":"why-async","title":"Why apps must not wait","tagline":"One thread draws the screen. Make it wait, and the whole app freezes.","level":"beginner","goals":["Say what the main thread of an app does","Explain why a long wait on the main thread freezes the screen","Recognise an ANR and what causes it"],"remember":"The main thread draws the screen and runs every tap, one job at a time. Never make it wait.","terms":[{"term":"thread","def":"One line of steps that the phone works through, one step at a time."},{"term":"main thread","def":"The thread that draws an app’s screen and runs the code for taps."},{"term":"blocking","def":"Making a thread stop and wait, so it can do nothing else meanwhile. <code>Thread.sleep(3000)</code> blocks for 3 seconds."},{"term":"ANR","def":"“Application Not Responding”: Android’s dialog when the main thread hasn’t responded to a tap for 5 seconds."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the main thread of an app do?","options":["Draws the screen and runs the code for taps","Only talks to servers","Nothing while the app is open"],"a":0,"why":"All screen work happens on the main thread, one job at a time."},{"q":"The main thread is stuck in a long job and hasn’t responded to a tap for 5 seconds. What does Android do?","options":["Shows a dialog saying the app isn’t responding","Runs the tap on another thread","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"That dialog is an ANR. The user can close the app from it."},{"q":"What is the difference between <code>Thread.sleep(1000)</code> and <code>delay(1000)</code> inside <code>LaunchedEffect</code>?","options":["<code>sleep</code> stops the whole thread; <code>delay</code> waits without holding the thread up","None","<code>delay</code> waits 1000 seconds"],"a":0,"why":"Both wait 1 second, but only <code>delay</code> leaves the main thread free to draw and handle taps."}]},{"id":"basics","title":"Your first coroutine","tagline":"Start work that waits, while the rest of the program keeps going.","level":"beginner","goals":["Write a <code>suspend</code> function that waits with <code>delay</code>","Start a coroutine with <code>launch</code>","Predict the order in which lines print"],"remember":"suspend fun can pause; delay pauses the coroutine, not the thread; launch { } starts a new coroutine and returns at once.","terms":[{"term":"coroutine","def":"A piece of work that can pause and carry on later, leaving its thread free while it waits."},{"term":"suspend","def":"Marks a function that may pause, like <code>suspend fun sync()</code>. It can only be called from a coroutine or another <code>suspend</code> function."},{"term":"launch","def":"<code>launch { ... }</code> starts a new coroutine that runs the block, and returns at once."},{"term":"runBlocking","def":"Keeps <code>main()</code> running until the coroutines inside finish. Fine in small programs, never in an app."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>delay(1000)</code> do in a coroutine?","options":["Pauses the coroutine for 1 second, leaving the thread free","Stops the whole thread for 1 second","Waits 1000 seconds"],"a":0,"why":"The coroutine waits; meanwhile the thread can run other coroutines."},{"q":"Inside <code>runBlocking</code>: <code>launch { println(\"A\") }</code>, then <code>println(\"B\")</code>. What prints first?","options":["<code>B</code>","<code>A</code>","Either, at random"],"a":0,"why":"<code>launch</code> returns at once, so <code>B</code> prints. The new coroutine runs when the thread is free. Every time."},{"q":"Where can you call a <code>suspend</code> function?","options":["In a coroutine or in another <code>suspend</code> function","Anywhere","Only in <code>main</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Only a coroutine can pause, so a function that may pause needs one around it."}]},{"id":"structured","title":"Structured concurrency","tagline":"Every coroutine has a parent, and a parent waits for its children.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what a scope is and how parents and children relate","Wait for several coroutines with <code>coroutineScope</code>","Start a coroutine from a button with <code>rememberCoroutineScope</code>"],"remember":"Coroutines live in a scope. A parent waits for its children, and stopping it stops them. coroutineScope { } returns when all its children are done.","terms":[{"term":"scope","def":"The owner that coroutines are started in. It waits for them, and stopping it stops them."},{"term":"structured concurrency","def":"Every coroutine has a parent: a parent waits for its children, and stopping a parent stops its children."},{"term":"coroutineScope","def":"<code>coroutineScope { ... }</code> makes a new scope inside a <code>suspend</code> function and returns when all its children have finished."},{"term":"rememberCoroutineScope","def":"In a composable, gives a scope that belongs to the screen, for starting coroutines from <code>onClick</code>. It is stopped when the screen leaves."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A parent coroutine is stopped. What happens to its children?","options":["They are stopped too","They keep running on their own","They start again"],"a":0,"why":"Children belong to their parent. That is what keeps work from running on after nobody needs it."},{"q":"The block of <code>coroutineScope { }</code> has reached its end, but one child is still running. What happens?","options":["It waits for the child, then returns","It returns at once","It stops the child"],"a":0,"why":"A scope is finished only when all its children are."},{"q":"Why does a Sync button need <code>rememberCoroutineScope</code>?","options":["<code>onClick</code> is not a coroutine; the scope lets it start one that stops when the screen leaves","It makes the button faster","Buttons can only call <code>suspend</code> functions"],"a":0,"why":"<code>scope.launch</code> starts the coroutine, and the scope belongs to the screen."}]},{"id":"async","title":"Doing things at the same time","tagline":"Start two jobs together and wait for both answers.","level":"beginner","goals":["Start work that gives back a result with <code>async</code>","Get the result with <code>await</code>","Explain why two waits at once take half the time"],"remember":"async { } starts work and returns a Deferred; await() gives the result, pausing until it is ready. Start them all, then await.","terms":[{"term":"async","def":"<code>async { ... }</code> starts a coroutine and returns a <code>Deferred</code> that will hold the block’s result."},{"term":"Deferred","def":"The box <code>async</code> gives back. It holds the result once the work is done."},{"term":"await","def":"<code>deferred.await()</code> gives the result, pausing the coroutine until it is ready."},{"term":"System.currentTimeMillis","def":"<code>System.currentTimeMillis()</code> gives the current time in milliseconds, handy for measuring how long something took."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>async { loadGoal() }</code> give back?","options":["A <code>Deferred</code>: a box that will hold the result","The goal, right away","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"It returns at once with the box; the result goes in when the load is done."},{"q":"What happens at <code>goal.await()</code> when the goal hasn’t arrived yet?","options":["The coroutine pauses until it has","It gives back 0","The program crashes"],"a":0,"why":"<code>await</code> is a <code>suspend</code> function: it pauses until the value is ready."},{"q":"Two loads take 1 second each. Which order of lines takes about 1 second?","options":["async, async, then await, await","async, await, then async, await"],"a":0,"why":"Starting both before awaiting lets the two waits overlap."}]},{"id":"dispatchers","title":"Which thread runs it","tagline":"Send heavy work to a background thread, then come back with the result.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name <code>Dispatchers.Main</code>, <code>IO</code> and <code>Default</code> and what each is for","Move work off the main thread with <code>withContext</code>","Explain why <code>suspend</code> alone doesn’t move work to another thread"],"remember":"A dispatcher picks the thread: Main for the screen, IO for files and network, Default for heavy work. withContext(...) { } runs a block there and comes back; launch(Dispatchers.Default) { } starts a whole coroutine there.","terms":[{"term":"dispatcher","def":"Decides which thread runs a coroutine. <code>Dispatchers.Main</code> is the main thread of an app."},{"term":"Dispatchers.IO","def":"Background threads for work that waits on files or the network."},{"term":"Dispatchers.Default","def":"Background threads for heavy calculations, such as adding up or sorting a long list."},{"term":"withContext","def":"<code>withContext(dispatcher) { ... }</code> runs the block on that dispatcher, then gives back its last value on the original thread."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which dispatcher fits reading a file?","options":["<code>Dispatchers.IO</code>","<code>Dispatchers.Main</code>","<code>Dispatchers.Default</code>"],"a":0,"why":"IO is for work that waits on files or the network."},{"q":"A <code>suspend</code> function adds up a huge list, with no <code>withContext</code>. It is called from <code>LaunchedEffect</code>. Which thread does the adding?","options":["The main thread","A background thread","No thread at all"],"a":0,"why":"<code>suspend</code> doesn’t switch threads. The work runs where the caller is: the main thread."},{"q":"After <code>withContext(Dispatchers.Default) { ... }</code> finishes, where does the coroutine carry on?","options":["On the thread it was on before","On a Default thread from then on"],"a":0,"why":"Only the block moves. The rest of the coroutine stays where it was."}]},{"id":"cancellation","title":"Cancelling work","tagline":"Stop a coroutine you no longer need, or one that takes too long.","level":"beginner","goals":["Stop a coroutine with its <code>Job</code> and <code>cancel()</code>","Make a busy loop stop when cancelled with <code>ensureActive()</code>","Give up on slow work with <code>withTimeoutOrNull</code>"],"remember":"job.cancel() stops a coroutine at its next suspend call. Busy loops need ensureActive(). withTimeoutOrNull gives null when time runs out.","terms":[{"term":"Job","def":"The handle <code>launch</code> gives back. Use it to cancel the coroutine."},{"term":"cancel","def":"<code>job.cancel()</code> asks a coroutine to stop. At its next check, such as a <code>delay</code>, a <code>CancellationException</code> is thrown: cancelling is cooperative."},{"term":"ensureActive","def":"<code>ensureActive()</code> stops the coroutine right there if it has been cancelled. Use it in loops that never pause."},{"term":"withTimeoutOrNull","def":"<code>withTimeoutOrNull(ms) { ... }</code> cancels the block if it takes longer than <code>ms</code> milliseconds, and then gives back <code>null</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>launch</code> give back?","options":["A <code>Job</code>, a handle to stop the coroutine","The result of the block","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"Keep the <code>Job</code> in a <code>val</code> to cancel the coroutine later."},{"q":"A coroutine runs a long loop with no <code>suspend</code> calls and no <code>ensureActive()</code>. You call <code>cancel()</code>. What happens?","options":["It keeps going until the loop ends","It stops at once"],"a":0,"why":"Cancelling is cooperative. Without a check, the loop never notices."},{"q":"What does <code>withTimeoutOrNull(500) { delay(2000); \"done\" }</code> give back?","options":["<code>null</code>","<code>\"done\"</code>","It crashes"],"a":0,"why":"The block needs 2 seconds but has 0.5, so it is cancelled and the answer is <code>null</code>."}]},{"id":"exceptions","title":"Errors in coroutines","tagline":"Catch a failure where it happens, and keep one failure from stopping the rest.","level":"beginner","goals":["Catch an error from a <code>suspend</code> function with <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code>","Explain how a failed child cancels its parent and siblings","Keep the other children running with <code>supervisorScope</code>"],"remember":"Catch with try/catch inside the coroutine. An uncaught failure cancels the parent and siblings; supervisorScope lets a child fail alone.","terms":[{"term":"propagation","def":"An uncaught error travelling up: the failed child cancels its parent, which cancels the other children."},{"term":"supervisorScope","def":"Like <code>coroutineScope</code>, but a failed child cancels neither the scope nor its siblings."},{"term":"SupervisorJob","def":"The kind of job behind <code>supervisorScope</code>: one whose children can fail alone."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>try { scope.launch { throw Exception(\"x\") } } catch (e: Exception) { ... }</code>. Does the <code>catch</code> run?","options":["No: <code>launch</code> returned before the exception","Yes"],"a":0,"why":"The <code>try</code> only covers starting the coroutine. Put the <code>try</code> inside it."},{"q":"In <code>coroutineScope</code>, one child fails and nobody catches it. What happens to the other children?","options":["They are cancelled","They keep running","They fail with their own errors"],"a":0,"why":"The error goes to the parent, which cancels the rest and then fails."},{"q":"In <code>supervisorScope</code>, an <code>async</code> child fails. Where do you see the error?","options":["When you call <code>await()</code> on it","Nowhere","In the other children"],"a":0,"why":"The error is kept in the <code>Deferred</code> until <code>await()</code> throws it."}]},{"id":"shared-state","title":"Shared state","tagline":"When coroutines on several threads change one value, guard it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why <code>glasses++</code> from several threads loses updates","Protect a value with <code>Mutex</code> and <code>withLock</code>","Avoid the problem by changing a value on one thread only"],"remember":"glasses++ from several threads can lose updates. Guard it with mutex.withLock { }, or change it on one thread only.","terms":[{"term":"race condition","def":"A bug where the result depends on which thread happens to go first, like two <code>glasses++</code> losing an update."},{"term":"Mutex","def":"A lock for coroutines: <code>val mutex = Mutex()</code>. Only one coroutine at a time can hold it."},{"term":"withLock","def":"<code>mutex.withLock { ... }</code> runs the block while holding the lock. Others pause until it is free."},{"term":"confinement","def":"Changing a value on one thread only, such as the main thread, so no lock is needed."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can <code>glasses++</code> lose updates when several threads run it?","options":["It is three steps (read, add, write), and two threads can read the same old value","Kotlin rounds big numbers","Threads can’t change a <code>var</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Both read 5, both write 6, and one update is lost."},{"q":"Another coroutine is inside <code>mutex.withLock { }</code>. What does yours do when it gets there?","options":["Pauses until the lock is free, without blocking its thread","Skips the block","Crashes"],"a":0,"why":"<code>withLock</code> waits like <code>delay</code> does: the coroutine pauses, and the thread is free."},{"q":"Every coroutine that changes <code>glasses</code> runs on the main thread, and the change is a plain <code>glasses++</code>. Do you need a <code>Mutex</code>?","options":["No: only one thread ever touches it","Yes, always"],"a":0,"why":"That is confinement. One thread runs one <code>glasses++</code> at a time."}]},{"id":"channels","title":"Channels","tagline":"Pass values from one coroutine to another, one at a time.","level":"beginner","goals":["Send and receive values through a <code>Channel</code>","Predict when <code>send</code> and <code>receive</code> pause","End a channel with <code>close</code> and read it with a <code>for</code> loop"],"remember":"A Channel passes values between coroutines: send waits for a receiver, receive waits for a value, close ends the for loop.","terms":[{"term":"Channel","def":"A pipe between coroutines: values go in with <code>send</code> and come out, in order, with <code>receive</code>."},{"term":"send","def":"<code>channel.send(value)</code> puts a value in. On a <code>Channel()</code>, it pauses until a receiver takes it."},{"term":"receive","def":"<code>channel.receive()</code> takes the next value out, pausing while the channel is empty."},{"term":"close","def":"<code>channel.close()</code> says no more values are coming, so a <code>for</code> loop over the channel ends."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Two coroutines receive from one channel. The sender sends 4 values. How many receivers get each value?","options":["Exactly one","Both","It depends on the order"],"a":0,"why":"Receiving takes the value out of the channel, so each value goes to one receiver."},{"q":"What does <code>receive()</code> do when the channel is empty?","options":["Pauses until a value is sent","Gives back <code>null</code>","Throws"],"a":0,"why":"It waits, like <code>delay</code>, without blocking the thread."},{"q":"When does <code>for (drink in drinks) { }</code> end?","options":["When the channel is closed and empty","After one value","Never"],"a":0,"why":"<code>close()</code> marks the end; the loop still gets every value sent before it."}]}]},{"id":"flow","title":"Flow","description":"How Flow streams values to your UI, from cold flows and operators to flows of flows, errors, fast producers, hot flows, sharing, lifecycle-aware collection and wrapping callbacks.","requires":["coroutines"],"file":"tracks/flow.js","lessons":[{"id":"cold","title":"A stream of values","tagline":"A flow hands out values one by one, and does nothing until someone collects it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Build a flow with <code>flow { }</code> and <code>emit</code>","Get its values one by one with <code>collect</code>","Explain why a flow is cold: each <code>collect</code> runs it from the top"],"remember":"flow { emit(x) } is a recipe; collect runs it from the top, again for every collector.","terms":[{"term":"Flow<Int>","def":"A stream of Ints that arrive one by one over time. Read “flow of Int”."},{"term":"flow { }","def":"Makes a flow. Its block runs only when someone collects the flow."},{"term":"emit","def":"<code>emit(value)</code> hands one value from the flow’s block to the collector."},{"term":"cold","def":"A cold flow does nothing until it is collected, and runs again from the top for each collector."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A flow’s block starts with <code>println(\"hi\")</code>. The program makes the flow but never calls <code>collect</code>. What is printed?","options":["Nothing","<code>hi</code>","<code>hi</code>, then the values"],"a":0,"why":"The block runs only when the flow is collected."},{"q":"The <code>glasses</code> flow above is collected twice. How many times is “Counting starts” printed?","options":["Once","Twice"],"a":1,"why":"A cold flow runs its block again from the top for every <code>collect</code>."},{"q":"When does <code>glasses.collect { ... }</code> return?","options":["Right away","After the first value","When the flow’s block reaches its end"],"a":2,"why":"<code>collect</code> waits for every value, and returns once the block has finished."}]},{"id":"operators","title":"Transforming flows","tagline":"map, filter and onEach work on each value as it arrives.","level":"beginner","goals":["Change each value of a flow with <code>map</code>","Drop values with <code>filter</code>","Do something with each value on the way with <code>onEach</code>"],"remember":"map changes, filter drops, onEach looks; each value goes through the whole chain on its own, and only when you collect.","terms":[{"term":"operator","def":"A function like <code>map</code> or <code>filter</code> that takes a flow and gives back a new flow."},{"term":"onEach","def":"Runs a lambda for each value and passes the value on unchanged."},{"term":"ml","def":"Millilitres: 1,000 ml is one litre. A glass is about 200 to 250 ml."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A flow emits 1, 2 and 3. What does <code>.map { it * 10 }</code> hand to <code>collect</code>?","options":["10, 20 and 30","60","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"why":"<code>map</code> changes each value on its own, as it arrives."},{"q":"<code>.onEach { println(it) }</code> comes before <code>.filter { it &gt; 5 }</code>. Is the value 3 printed?","options":["Yes","No"],"a":0,"why":"<code>onEach</code> is above <code>filter</code> in the chain, so it sees 3 before <code>filter</code> drops it."},{"q":"With <code>map</code> and <code>filter</code> and no <code>collect</code>, what runs?","options":["Nothing","The flow, but nothing is printed","Only <code>map</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Operators make a new recipe. Nothing runs until <code>collect</code>."}]},{"id":"flatmap","title":"Flows of flows","tagline":"When each value starts a flow of its own, flatMapLatest keeps only the newest.","level":"beginner","goals":["Start a flow for each value with <code>flatMapLatest</code>","Explain why it cancels the old inner flow","Choose <code>flatMapConcat</code> or <code>flatMapMerge</code> when old work must go on"],"remember":"flatMapLatest cancels the old inner flow, flatMapConcat waits its turn, flatMapMerge runs them all at once.","terms":[{"term":"inner flow","def":"The flow that each value starts, like <code>search(text)</code> for each typed text."},{"term":"flatMapLatest","def":"Starts an inner flow for each value, and cancels the previous one when a new value arrives."},{"term":"flatMapConcat","def":"Runs the inner flows one after another, in order, each to its end."},{"term":"flatMapMerge","def":"Runs the inner flows at the same time and passes values on as they arrive."}],"quiz":[{"q":"With <code>flatMapLatest</code>, a new value arrives while the inner flow for the old one is still running. What happens to it?","options":["It is cancelled","It finishes first","It runs alongside the new one"],"a":0,"why":"A new value cancels the inner flow still running for the previous one."},{"q":"Photos must upload one at a time, in order, and every upload must finish. Which operator fits?","options":["<code>flatMapConcat</code>","<code>flatMapLatest</code>","<code>flatMapMerge</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>flatMapConcat</code> starts the next inner flow only after the previous one has ended."},{"q":"What does <code>map { text -&gt; search(text) }</code> give, when <code>search</code> returns a flow?","options":["A flow of flows","A flow of results","A list"],"a":0,"why":"<code>map</code> only wraps each value; it doesn’t collect the flows it makes."}]},{"id":"errors-retry","title":"Errors and retries","tagline":"retry tries again; catch turns a failure into a value.","level":"beginner","goals":["Turn an exception in a flow into a value with <code>catch</code>","Try a failing flow again with <code>retry</code>","Put <code>retry</code> and <code>catch</code> in the right order"],"remember":"retry first, catch after it: try again, then show a fallback. catch only sees errors from above it.","terms":[{"term":"catch { } (on a flow)","def":"An operator that handles an exception thrown above it in the chain, and can <code>emit</code> a value instead."},{"term":"retry(n)","def":"When the flow above it fails, runs it again from the top, up to <code>n</code> more times."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The chain is <code>.retry(2)</code> then <code>.catch</code>, and the server fails every time. How many tries are made in all?","options":["3","2","1"],"a":0,"why":"The first try, plus 2 more. Then the exception reaches <code>catch</code>."},{"q":"An exception is thrown inside <code>collect { }</code>. Does a <code>.catch</code> earlier in the chain handle it?","options":["No","Yes"],"a":0,"why":"<code>catch</code> only sees errors from above it. <code>collect</code> is below it."},{"q":"What does <code>catch</code> do after it has emitted its fallback value?","options":["The flow ends normally","The flow starts again","The program crashes"],"a":0,"why":"The exception is handled, so the flow simply ends, and <code>collect</code> returns."}]},{"id":"backpressure","title":"Fast producers","tagline":"When values come faster than you can handle them: buffer, conflate or collectLatest.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why a slow collector slows the flow down","Keep every value with <code>buffer</code>, or only the newest with <code>conflate</code>","Cancel old work with <code>collectLatest</code>"],"remember":"buffer keeps every value, conflate keeps the newest, collectLatest cancels the old work.","terms":[{"term":"producer and collector","def":"The producer is the side that makes values (the flow’s block). The collector is the side that handles them (<code>collect</code>)."},{"term":"buffer","def":"A queue between producer and collector. <code>buffer()</code> lets the producer run ahead; every value is still handled."},{"term":"conflate","def":"Keeps only the newest value while the collector is busy; the ones in between are dropped."},{"term":"collectLatest","def":"Like <code>collect</code>, but a new value cancels the lambda still working on the old one."}],"quiz":[{"q":"No operator. The producer is faster than the collector. What does the producer do?","options":["Waits in <code>emit</code> until the collector is done","Keeps going at full speed","Drops values"],"a":0,"why":"By default they take turns: <code>emit</code> returns only when the collector’s lambda has finished."},{"q":"With <code>collectLatest</code>, value 2 arrives while the lambda is still handling 1. What happens?","options":["The work on 1 is cancelled, and the lambda starts with 2","2 waits until 1 is done","2 is dropped"],"a":0,"why":"A new value cancels the old work and starts the lambda again."},{"q":"With <code>buffer()</code>, how many values are lost?","options":["None","All but the newest","Half"],"a":0,"why":"The values wait in the queue and every one is handled, in order."}]},{"id":"hot","title":"Hot flows","tagline":"A StateFlow holds the current value; a SharedFlow announces events to whoever is listening.","level":"beginner","goals":["Hold a value that screens can watch with <code>MutableStateFlow</code>","Announce one-off events with <code>MutableSharedFlow</code>","Explain what makes a flow hot"],"remember":"StateFlow: always one value, handed to every new collector. SharedFlow: events for whoever is collecting right now.","terms":[{"term":"hot flow","def":"A flow that exists and takes values with nobody collecting. Collecting it starts no code, and it never ends."},{"term":"StateFlow","def":"A hot flow that always holds one value, <code>.value</code>, and hands it to every new collector."},{"term":"SharedFlow","def":"A hot flow that hands each value to the collectors there at that moment, and keeps nothing."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A StateFlow holds 4. A new screen starts collecting it. What does the screen get first?","options":["4, right away","Nothing until the value changes","Every value it ever held"],"a":0,"why":"A StateFlow hands its current value to every new collector at once."},{"q":"<code>messages.emit(\"Hi\")</code> runs on a <code>MutableSharedFlow</code> while nobody collects. What happens to “Hi”?","options":["It is lost","It waits for the first collector","The program crashes"],"a":0,"why":"A SharedFlow made with <code>MutableSharedFlow()</code> keeps nothing. Only the collectors there at that moment get a value."},{"q":"<code>glasses.value</code> is 3, and the code sets it to 3 again. What do the collectors get?","options":["Nothing","3 once more","0"],"a":0,"why":"A StateFlow hands out a value only when it is different from the current one."}]},{"id":"statein-sharein","title":"Sharing a flow","tagline":"stateIn and shareIn run one cold flow once and share it with every collector.","level":"beginner","goals":["Share one run of a cold flow with <code>stateIn</code>","Say when it runs with <code>SharingStarted</code>","Choose between <code>stateIn</code> and <code>shareIn</code>"],"remember":"stateIn and shareIn run a cold flow once, in a scope, for every collector. stateIn has a current value; shareIn is for events.","terms":[{"term":"stateIn","def":"Runs a cold flow once in a scope and shares it as a <code>StateFlow</code>, which starts with the value you give."},{"term":"shareIn","def":"Like <code>stateIn</code>, but gives a <code>SharedFlow</code>: no starting value, and a new collector gets only what comes next."},{"term":"SharingStarted","def":"When the shared cold flow runs: <code>WhileSubscribed(5000)</code> while someone collects, plus 5 seconds; <code>Eagerly</code> at once, until the scope ends."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>stateIn(this, SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5000), 0)</code>. A screen collects before the sensor has sent anything. What does it get?","options":["0","Nothing yet","An error"],"a":0,"why":"A StateFlow always has a value. Until the cold flow emits, it is the starting value you passed, 0."},{"q":"Three screens collect one <code>stateIn</code> StateFlow. How many times does the cold flow run?","options":["Once","Three times"],"a":0,"why":"That is the point of sharing: one run, its values handed to every collector."},{"q":"The screen should show the latest sips at once when it opens. Which one fits?","options":["<code>stateIn</code>","<code>shareIn</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>stateIn</code> makes a StateFlow, which always holds a value and hands it to a new collector right away. <code>shareIn</code> makes a SharedFlow, which keeps nothing: a new collector waits for the next value."}]},{"id":"lifecycle","title":"Collecting safely in the UI","tagline":"Collect while the screen is visible, stop when it isn’t.","level":"beginner","goals":["Read a flow as state in Compose with <code>collectAsStateWithLifecycle</code>","Collect only while the screen is started with <code>repeatOnLifecycle</code>","Explain why collecting in the background wastes battery"],"remember":"In Compose, collectAsStateWithLifecycle(); elsewhere, repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED) inside lifecycleScope.launch. Both collect only while the screen is visible.","terms":[{"term":"collectAsStateWithLifecycle","def":"Collects a flow into state a composable reads, only while the screen is at least started."},{"term":"initialValue","def":"For a plain <code>Flow</code>, which has no current value: <code>collectAsStateWithLifecycle(initialValue = 0)</code> shows 0 until the first value arrives."},{"term":"lifecycleScope","def":"A scope that belongs to an Activity. Its coroutines are cancelled when the Activity is destroyed."},{"term":"repeatOnLifecycle","def":"<code>repeatOnLifecycle(Lifecycle.State.STARTED) { }</code> runs its block each time the screen is started and cancels it each time it is stopped."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The user presses Home. What does <code>collectAsStateWithLifecycle</code> do?","options":["Stops collecting","Keeps collecting","Destroys the StateFlow"],"a":0,"why":"Home stops the screen, and collecting stops with it. The StateFlow itself keeps its value."},{"q":"The user comes back. What does the screen show first?","options":["The StateFlow’s current value","The value it showed before Home","0"],"a":0,"why":"Collecting starts again, and a StateFlow hands its current value at once."},{"q":"When are the coroutines in <code>lifecycleScope</code> cancelled?","options":["When the Activity is destroyed","At every <code>onStop</code>","Never"],"a":0,"why":"The scope belongs to the Activity. Stopping and starting inside it is the job of <code>repeatOnLifecycle</code>."}]},{"id":"callbackflow","title":"Wrapping callbacks","tagline":"callbackFlow turns a listener into a flow, and removes the listener when collecting stops.","level":"beginner","goals":["Turn a listener into a flow with <code>callbackFlow</code>","Send values from the listener with <code>trySend</code>","Remove the listener in <code>awaitClose</code>"],"remember":"In callbackFlow: subscribe a listener that calls trySend, then awaitClose { unsubscribe }.","terms":[{"term":"callbackFlow","def":"Makes a cold flow from a listener: subscribe it in the block, and unsubscribe in <code>awaitClose</code>."},{"term":"trySend","def":"Puts a value into a <code>callbackFlow</code> without waiting, so a plain listener can call it."},{"term":"awaitClose","def":"Keeps a <code>callbackFlow</code> open while someone collects; its lambda runs when collecting stops."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the listener call <code>trySend</code> and not <code>emit</code>?","options":["The listener is a plain function and can’t wait","<code>trySend</code> is faster","<code>emit</code> only works with numbers"],"a":0,"why":"The bottle calls the listener like any function. Only <code>trySend</code> works there, because it never waits."},{"q":"When does the lambda of <code>awaitClose</code> run?","options":["When collecting stops","Right after <code>subscribe</code>","After every sip"],"a":0,"why":"<code>awaitClose</code> waits while someone collects. When the collecting coroutine is cancelled, its lambda runs."},{"q":"Two screens collect <code>sipsOf(bottle)</code>. How many listeners does the bottle have?","options":["Two","One"],"a":0,"why":"<code>callbackFlow</code> is cold: each collector runs the block and subscribes its own listener."}]},{"id":"suspend-callback","title":"One-shot callbacks","tagline":"suspendCancellableCoroutine turns a single answer into a suspend function.","level":"beginner","goals":["Turn a one-time callback into a suspend function with <code>suspendCancellableCoroutine</code>","Hand back the answer with <code>resume</code>, or an error with <code>resumeWithException</code>","Stop the request with <code>invokeOnCancellation</code>"],"remember":"One answer: suspendCancellableCoroutine, resume once, and stop the request in invokeOnCancellation. Many answers: callbackFlow.","terms":[{"term":"suspendCancellableCoroutine","def":"Pauses the calling coroutine and hands you a continuation; what you pass to <code>resume</code> becomes its result."},{"term":"continuation","def":"A handle to a paused coroutine. <code>resume(value)</code> wakes it with a value, <code>resumeWithException(e)</code> makes it throw <code>e</code>."},{"term":"invokeOnCancellation","def":"Sets a lambda that runs if the waiting coroutine is cancelled, to stop the request."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A library calls your callback once with a result. Which tool fits?","options":["<code>suspendCancellableCoroutine</code>","<code>callbackFlow</code>","<code>MutableStateFlow</code>"],"a":0,"why":"One answer becomes a suspend function. A callback that fires many times becomes a <code>callbackFlow</code>."},{"q":"When does the lambda of <code>invokeOnCancellation</code> run?","options":["When the waiting coroutine is cancelled","After every <code>resume</code>","Right away"],"a":0,"why":"It is there for cancelling only, so you can stop the request."},{"q":"The callback calls <code>continuation.resumeWithException(e)</code>. What happens in the caller?","options":["The call throws <code>e</code>","It gets 0","It waits forever"],"a":0,"why":"The paused coroutine wakes up with the exception, which a <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code> around the call can handle."}]}]},{"id":"arch","title":"App architecture","description":"Give the Recipes app structure step by step: ViewModels, one UI state, layers, repositories and use cases, then wiring it all together with Hilt.","requires":["android","flow"],"file":"tracks/architecture.js","lessons":[{"id":"why-architecture","title":"Why structure matters","tagline":"Give each part of the app one job, so it can grow without breaking.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Spot the different jobs one big screen function is doing","Explain what goes wrong when they all live in one place","Name the three jobs the next lessons pull apart"],"remember":"Split code by job: drawing, screen state, data. Then each part has one reason to change.","terms":[{"term":"separation of concerns","def":"Splitting code by job, so each piece does one thing and has one reason to change."},{"term":"architecture","def":"How an app’s code is split into parts, and how those parts talk to each other."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The code is split by job. Ada wants new colours for the list. Which part changes?","options":["Only the drawing part","The data part","All three"],"a":0,"why":"Colours are about drawing. Screen state and data don’t change."},{"q":"What does separation of concerns mean?","options":["Each piece of code has one job","Every function goes in its own file","Screens never share code"],"a":0,"why":"It is about jobs. Sharing a data part between screens is one of the things it makes possible."},{"q":"Why is a refresh written inside a composable hard to reuse?","options":["Another screen can’t call it without drawing this screen","Room lets only one screen read a table","A refresh must always live in <code>MainActivity</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The refresh is part of what <code>RecipesScreen</code> draws. To use it elsewhere, it needs its own place."}]},{"id":"viewmodel","title":"ViewModel","tagline":"A home for screen state and work that survives turning the phone.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Move screen state and work out of a composable into a <code>ViewModel</code>","Start work in <code>viewModelScope</code> so turning the phone doesn’t cancel it","Get the screen’s ViewModel in Compose with <code>viewModel { }</code>"],"remember":"viewModel { } makes the ViewModel once and hands back the same one after a rotation; work in viewModelScope stops only when the screen is closed for good.","terms":[{"term":"ViewModel","def":"A class that holds a screen’s state and work. Android keeps it through configuration changes, and clears it when the screen is closed for good."},{"term":"viewModelScope","def":"A coroutine scope that belongs to a ViewModel. It is cancelled when the ViewModel is cleared, not on rotation."},{"term":"viewModel","def":"<code>viewModel { RecipesViewModel(...) }</code> in a composable: makes the ViewModel the first time, and hands back the same one after that."},{"term":"init","def":"An <code>init { }</code> block in a class runs once, when an object of the class is made."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The phone rotates. What happens to the <code>RecipesViewModel</code>?","options":["The new Activity gets the same object","A new one is made","It is saved to a file and read back"],"a":0,"why":"Android keeps the ViewModel in memory and hands it to the new Activity."},{"q":"When is <code>viewModelScope</code> cancelled?","options":["When the ViewModel is cleared, for example after <code>finish()</code>","At every rotation","At every <code>onStop</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The scope belongs to the ViewModel, which outlives rotations and stops."},{"q":"Android closed the app in the background to free memory. Ada comes back. What about the ViewModel?","options":["A new one is made: a ViewModel is not saved","The old one comes back with its values","The app can’t open again"],"a":0,"why":"A ViewModel lives in memory only. Values that must come back after that belong in saved state or in Room."}]},{"id":"ui-state","title":"UI state","tagline":"Put everything the screen shows into one object.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Describe a whole screen with one data class","Expose it read-only with <code>asStateFlow()</code>","Change it with <code>copy</code>, so the screen always gets one consistent picture"],"remember":"One data class for the whole screen; a private MutableStateFlow inside, asStateFlow() outside; every change is a copy.","terms":[{"term":"UI state","def":"Everything a screen shows, in one object, such as <code>RecipesUiState</code>."},{"term":"asStateFlow","def":"<code>_uiState.asStateFlow()</code> gives a read-only <code>StateFlow</code> of the same values: others can collect it but not set <code>.value</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why one <code>RecipesUiState</code> instead of three separate flows?","options":["The screen gets all values together, so they never disagree","It uses less memory","Compose can only collect one flow"],"a":0,"why":"Each change is one new, whole object. The screen can’t catch it half done."},{"q":"What does <code>asStateFlow()</code> give the screen?","options":["A read-only view of the same StateFlow","A copy that never changes again","A second <code>MutableStateFlow</code>"],"a":0,"why":"It follows every new value, but has no way to set one."},{"q":"<code>state</code> is <code>RecipesUiState(isRefreshing = true, note = \"Offline\")</code>. What is <code>state.copy(isRefreshing = false)</code>?","options":["<code>isRefreshing = false</code>, <code>note = \"Offline\"</code>","<code>isRefreshing = false</code>, <code>note = \"\"</code>","The same object, changed"],"a":0,"why":"<code>copy</code> makes a new object and keeps every value you don’t name."}]},{"id":"udf","title":"Unidirectional data flow","tagline":"State goes down to the screen, events go up to the ViewModel.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Split a screen into a part that knows the ViewModel and a part that only draws","Send taps up as events, through lambdas","Follow one tap around the loop: event, new state, redraw"],"remember":"State down as parameters, events up as lambdas; only the ViewModel changes state.","terms":[{"term":"unidirectional data flow","def":"State flows down from the ViewModel to the composables; events flow up from the composables to the ViewModel."},{"term":"event","def":"Something that happened, usually something the user did, like a tap. Composables report events; the ViewModel decides what they mean."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In unidirectional data flow, which way do events go?","options":["Up, from the composables to the ViewModel","Down, from the ViewModel to the composables","Both ways"],"a":0,"why":"Events go up, state comes down."},{"q":"Why does <code>RecipesContent</code> take state and lambdas instead of the ViewModel?","options":["It can be previewed and reused with any state","A composable may not hold a ViewModel","It makes the app start faster"],"a":0,"why":"A preview passes sample state and empty lambdas, with no database or server."},{"q":"The note on screen is wrong. Where do you look first?","options":["Where the ViewModel puts the note into <code>uiState</code>","In every composable that draws text","In the Room table"],"a":0,"why":"Only the ViewModel changes the state, so the note comes from there."}]},{"id":"layers","title":"Layers","tagline":"UI on top, data below, and dependencies that only point down.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Name the UI layer, the data layer and the optional domain layer","Place each class of the Recipes app in its layer and package","Check the rule: a layer may use the one below, never the one above"],"remember":"UI layer on top, data layer below, domain layer optional in between; a layer uses the one below, never the one above.","terms":[{"term":"UI layer","def":"The classes that show data and react to the user: composables and ViewModels."},{"term":"data layer","def":"The classes that get and save data: DAOs, the server API, DataStore."},{"term":"domain layer","def":"An optional layer between UI and data, for rules that several screens share."},{"term":"package","def":"A named folder of Kotlin files. <code>package com.example.recipes.data</code> on the first line says which one a file is in."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which layer is <code>RecipesViewModel</code> in?","options":["The UI layer","The data layer","The domain layer"],"a":0,"why":"It holds the state of one screen, so it belongs with the screen."},{"q":"Which of these breaks the rule?","options":["<code>RecipeDao</code> imports <code>RecipesViewModel</code>","<code>RecipesViewModel</code> imports <code>RecipeDao</code>","Neither"],"a":0,"why":"Dependencies point down. The data layer must not know the UI layer."},{"q":"Does every app need a domain layer?","options":["No, it is optional","Yes, it sits between every ViewModel and the data"],"a":0,"why":"A ViewModel may use the data layer directly. The domain layer helps only when rules are shared or a ViewModel grows too big."}]},{"id":"repository","title":"Repositories","tagline":"One class that knows where the recipes come from.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Gather the DAO, the Session and DataStore behind one <code>RecipeRepository</code>","Give the ViewModel one repository instead of three data sources","Make the repository in <code>MainActivity</code> and pass it to <code>viewModel { }</code>"],"remember":"A repository is the one door to one kind of data; ViewModels use it and never the data sources behind it.","terms":[{"term":"repository","def":"A data-layer class that is the one door to one kind of data, like <code>RecipeRepository</code> for recipes."},{"term":"data source","def":"One place a repository gets data from or saves it to: the DAO, the server through <code>Session</code>, DataStore."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>RecipesViewModel</code> need to know about where recipes come from?","options":["Nothing: it asks the repository","The table name and the server address","Which one is faster"],"a":0,"why":"That is the repository’s job. The ViewModel only uses <code>recipes</code>, <code>refresh</code> and <code>toggleFavorite</code>."},{"q":"Which of these is a data source?","options":["<code>RecipeDao</code>","<code>RecipeRepository</code>","<code>RecipesViewModel</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The DAO talks to one place, Room. The repository sits in front of the sources."},{"q":"Where is the repository made, for now?","options":["In <code>MainActivity.onCreate</code>, then passed to <code>viewModel { }</code>","Inside each composable","Inside <code>RecipeDao</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The Activity builds the parts and hands the repository to the ViewModel through its constructor."}]},{"id":"ssot","title":"Single source of truth","tagline":"Every piece of data has one owner; everyone else reads it from there.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Name the one owner of each piece of data in the Recipes app","Change data only through its owner, and let the change come back by itself","Explain why the offline-first loop never shows two different answers"],"remember":"One owner per piece of data: change it only through the owner, read it only from the owner.","terms":[{"term":"single source of truth","def":"The one owner of a piece of data. Only it changes the data; everyone else reads from it and asks it for changes."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In the Recipes app, what is the single source of truth for recipes?","options":["The recipes table in Room","<code>uiState.recipes</code> in the ViewModel","Whatever the server sent last"],"a":0,"why":"Every screen reads the table, and every change, from a tap or a refresh, is written there."},{"q":"A refresh downloads 40 recipes. Where do they go first?","options":["Into Room; the screens get them from Room’s Flow","Straight into <code>uiState</code>","Into the screen, then into Room"],"a":0,"why":"The server’s answer goes to the owner. The Flow brings it to every screen."},{"q":"Who owns <code>isRefreshing</code>?","options":["<code>RecipesViewModel</code>","Room","<code>RecipesContent</code>"],"a":0,"why":"It is a screen-only value, so the screen’s ViewModel owns it."}]},{"id":"usecases","title":"Use cases","tagline":"A small class for one rule that several ViewModels share.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Recognise a rule that belongs neither in a ViewModel nor in a repository","Write a use case with <code>operator fun invoke</code> and call it like a function","Decide when an app doesn’t need one"],"remember":"A use case holds one shared rule; operator fun invoke lets ViewModels call it like a function. Add one only when it earns its file.","terms":[{"term":"use case","def":"A small domain-layer class with one job, one rule shared by several ViewModels, like <code>GetQuickRecipesUseCase</code>."},{"term":"operator fun invoke","def":"A function named <code>invoke</code> and marked <code>operator</code>: then <code>getQuickRecipes()</code> calls <code>getQuickRecipes.invoke()</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"With <code>operator fun invoke()</code>, what does <code>getQuickRecipes()</code> do?","options":["Calls <code>getQuickRecipes.invoke()</code>","Makes a new use case","Doesn’t compile; you need <code>.invoke()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>operator</code> lets the object be called like a function, which runs <code>invoke</code>."},{"q":"Which layer is a use case in?","options":["The domain layer","The data layer","The UI layer"],"a":0,"why":"It sits between the ViewModels, which use it, and the repositories it uses."},{"q":"<code>FavoritesViewModel</code> shows <code>repository.recipes</code> with one filter no other screen uses. Does it need a use case?","options":["No: it can use the repository directly","Yes: every ViewModel must go through a use case"],"a":0,"why":"The domain layer is optional. A use case earns its place when a rule is shared or a ViewModel grows too big."}]},{"id":"ui-state-patterns","title":"Loading, empty and error","tagline":"Give every look of the screen its own place in the state.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Name the four looks of a screen: loading, content, empty and error","Start the state as loading, so an empty list never shows by mistake","Choose between flags in a data class and a sealed interface"],"remember":"Draw loading, content, empty and error; start as loading, so an empty list only shows when it is true.","terms":[{"term":"loading state","def":"The look of a screen while there is nothing to show yet, such as a spinner or “Loading…”."},{"term":"empty state","def":"The look when loading worked but the list has no items: a short message, often with a button."},{"term":"error state","def":"The look when loading failed and there is nothing saved to show: a message and a way to try again."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Room answered with an empty list, the refresh worked and <code>note</code> is <code>\"\"</code>. Which look does the screen show?","options":["Empty: “No recipes yet”","Loading","Error, with Try again"],"a":0,"why":"Loading is over and nothing failed. The list is really empty, so “No recipes yet” is true now."},{"q":"Ada taps Refresh while 40 recipes are on screen. What should she see?","options":["The 40 recipes, with a small sign that it is refreshing","Only a spinner until the refresh ends","An empty screen"],"a":0,"why":"Flags let the list and <code>isRefreshing</code> be true together, so nothing she was reading disappears."},{"q":"When does a sealed interface like <code>Loading</code>, <code>Ready</code>, <code>Failed</code> fit best?","options":["When the looks never appear together","When the list must stay on screen during a refresh","Never: flags are always better"],"a":0,"why":"A sealed value is one look at a time, and <code>when</code> makes you draw each one. Flags fit when two things show together."}]},{"id":"mvvm-mvi","title":"MVVM and MVI","tagline":"A function per event, or every event as a value through one function.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Tell MVVM and MVI apart by how the screen sends its events","Write a sealed intent type and a <code>reduce</code> function that returns the next state","Choose the style that fits a screen"],"remember":"MVVM: one ViewModel function per event. MVI: one sealed intent type, one onIntent, and reduce(state, intent) returns the next state.","terms":[{"term":"MVVM","def":"Model, View, ViewModel: the screen draws the ViewModel’s state and calls one of its functions for each event."},{"term":"MVI","def":"Model, View, Intent: the screen sends every event as a value of one sealed type to a single <code>onIntent</code>."},{"term":"intent (MVI)","def":"A value that describes what the user wants, like <code>ServingsIntent.More</code>. Not Android’s <code>Intent</code> class."},{"term":"reducer","def":"A function like <code>reduce(state, intent)</code> that only computes the next state from the current one and an intent."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In MVI, how does the screen add a serving?","options":["<code>viewModel.onIntent(ServingsIntent.More)</code>","<code>viewModel.more()</code>","<code>state.servings += 1</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Every event is a value sent to the one entry point. <code>viewModel.more()</code> is the MVVM way."},{"q":"What does <code>reduce</code> take and give back?","options":["The current state and an intent in, the next state out","An intent in, a network result out","Nothing: it changes the screen directly"],"a":0,"why":"It only computes. That is why the same intents always lead to the same state."},{"q":"A settings screen has two switches that never affect each other. Which style fits?","options":["MVVM: one function per switch","MVI with a reducer"],"a":0,"why":"MVI’s extra type pays off when many events change the same state. Two separate switches don’t need it."}]},{"id":"di","title":"Dependency injection","tagline":"Build the app’s shared objects in one place, once, and hand them out.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why <code>onCreate</code> is the wrong place to build the app’s objects","Build them once in an <code>AppContainer</code>, kept by your own <code>Application</code> class","Hand dependencies to ViewModels from the container"],"remember":"Build shared objects once in a container, keep the container in your Application class, and hand the objects in through constructors.","terms":[{"term":"container","def":"One object that builds the app’s shared objects, once, and hands them out, like <code>AppContainer</code>."},{"term":"Application","def":"The class Android makes one object of when the app’s process starts, before any Activity. Your own subclass is named in the manifest."},{"term":"as","def":"<code>x as RecipesApp</code> tells Kotlin to treat <code>x</code> as a <code>RecipesApp</code>. If it is something else, the app crashes with a <code>ClassCastException</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the container live in <code>RecipesApp</code> and not in <code>MainActivity</code>?","options":["RecipesApp lives as long as the app; the Activity is made again on every turn","An Activity can’t hold a property","Android deletes containers in Activities"],"a":0,"why":"There is one <code>Application</code> object per app process, so what it keeps is built once."},{"q":"What does <code>(application as RecipesApp).container</code> do?","options":["Treats the app’s Application object as your RecipesApp and reads its container","Makes a new RecipesApp","Turns the Activity into an Application"],"a":0,"why":"<code>application</code> has the plain type <code>Application</code>. <code>as</code> lets Kotlin see your class and its <code>container</code>."},{"q":"With <code>val container by lazy { AppContainer(this) }</code>, when is the container built?","options":["The first time something reads <code>container</code>","Every time something reads it","When the app is installed"],"a":0,"why":"<code>by lazy</code> runs the block once, on first use, and keeps the result."}]},{"id":"hilt","title":"Hilt","tagline":"Mark your classes, and let Hilt write the container.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Set up Hilt with <code>@HiltAndroidApp</code> and <code>@AndroidEntryPoint</code>","Tell Hilt how to build your own classes with <code>@Inject constructor</code>","Get a ViewModel built by Hilt with <code>@HiltViewModel</code> and <code>hiltViewModel()</code>"],"remember":"@HiltAndroidApp on the Application, @AndroidEntryPoint on the Activity, @Inject constructor on your classes, @HiltViewModel and hiltViewModel() for the ViewModel.","terms":[{"term":"Hilt","def":"A library for dependency injection on Android. From your annotations it writes, at build time, the code that builds and hands out your objects."},{"term":"@Inject constructor","def":"Marks the constructor Hilt calls to build your class. Hilt builds each parameter first. The word <code>constructor</code> is needed so the annotation has something to sit on."},{"term":"@HiltViewModel","def":"Marks a ViewModel that Hilt may build. On the screen, <code>hiltViewModel()</code> asks for it, and gives back the same one after a turn."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>@Inject constructor(...)</code> tell Hilt?","options":["How to build this class: call this constructor after building its parameters","To keep one object for the whole app","To build it on a background thread"],"a":0,"why":"It marks the constructor Hilt calls. How many objects exist is a different question, for a later lesson."},{"q":"Why does <code>class RecipeRepository @Inject constructor(...)</code> need the word <code>constructor</code>?","options":["So the annotation has a constructor to sit on","It makes a second constructor","Hilt only builds classes that use the word"],"a":0,"why":"Without an annotation, the parentheses are enough. To mark the constructor, Kotlin needs the word in front of them."},{"q":"A class asks for a type Hilt can’t make. When do you find out?","options":["When you build: Hilt names the missing type","Only when that screen opens","Never: Hilt passes null"],"a":0,"why":"Hilt checks the whole picture at build time, so a missing binding is a build error. A missing <code>@AndroidEntryPoint</code> is the one you only find when the screen opens."}]},{"id":"hilt-modules","title":"Hilt modules","tagline":"Tell Hilt how to make the objects you can’t mark.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Write a Hilt module with <code>@Module</code> and <code>@InstallIn</code>","Make objects you can’t edit with <code>@Provides</code> functions","Ask Hilt for the app’s <code>Context</code> with <code>@ApplicationContext</code>"],"remember":"A @Module object, @InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class), and one @Provides function per object Hilt can’t build with a constructor.","terms":[{"term":"module (Hilt)","def":"An <code>object</code> marked <code>@Module</code> and <code>@InstallIn</code> whose functions tell Hilt how to make objects it can’t build with a constructor."},{"term":"@Provides","def":"Marks a module function that makes one object, like the DAO. Hilt hands in its parameters too."},{"term":"@InstallIn","def":"<code>@InstallIn(SingletonComponent::class)</code> puts a module in Hilt’s component for the whole app, so every class can use what it makes."},{"term":"@ApplicationContext","def":"Marks a <code>Context</code> parameter that should get the app’s own <code>Context</code>, not an Activity’s."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Room writes <code>RecipeDao</code> for you. How does Hilt learn to make one?","options":["A <code>@Provides</code> function in a <code>@Module</code>","Put <code>@Inject</code> on the DAO interface","Hilt finds DAOs by itself"],"a":0,"why":"You can’t mark a constructor in code Room writes, so a module function says how to get one."},{"q":"<code>provideSession</code> takes a <code>DataStore&lt;Preferences&gt;</code>. Where does it come from?","options":["Hilt calls <code>provideStore</code> and hands it in","You pass it when you call <code>provideSession</code>","Kotlin makes an empty one"],"a":0,"why":"You never call <code>@Provides</code> functions yourself. Hilt calls them, and builds their parameters first."},{"q":"What does <code>@ApplicationContext context: Context</code> get?","options":["The app’s own Context, which lives as long as the app","The Context of the Activity on screen","A new Context every time"],"a":0,"why":"Hilt always has the app’s Context. Being as long-lived as the app, it is safe to keep."}]},{"id":"hilt-scopes","title":"Scopes in Hilt","tagline":"Decide how many of each object exist, and how long they live.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Predict when Hilt builds a new object and when it hands out the same one","Keep one database for the whole app with <code>@Singleton</code>","Share one object inside a ViewModel with <code>@ViewModelScoped</code>"],"remember":"Unscoped: a new object per request. @Singleton: one for the app process. @ViewModelScoped: one per ViewModel.","terms":[{"term":"unscoped","def":"Hilt’s default: a new object for every class that asks for one."},{"term":"scope","def":"An annotation that makes Hilt build one object and share it for a certain lifetime."},{"term":"@Singleton","def":"One object for the whole app, for as long as the app’s process lives."},{"term":"@ViewModelScoped","def":"One object per ViewModel, shared by everything inside it, for as long as that ViewModel lives."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>GetQuickRecipesUseCase</code> has no scope. Two ViewModels ask for it. How many exist?","options":["2","1"],"a":0,"why":"Unscoped is the default, and it means a new object for every request."},{"q":"Ada turns the phone. What happens to the <code>@Singleton</code> database and the <code>@ViewModelScoped</code> <code>QuickLimit</code>?","options":["Both stay the same objects","Both are built again","Only the <code>QuickLimit</code> is built again"],"a":0,"why":"The app process keeps running, and the ViewModel survives the turn, so both lifetimes go on."},{"q":"When is the <code>@Singleton</code> database built again?","options":["When Android starts a new process for the app","On every turn of the phone","Never, even after the phone restarts"],"a":0,"why":"<code>@Singleton</code> lasts as long as the process. A new process builds everything again."}]},{"id":"modularization-arch","title":"Modules","tagline":"Split the app into feature and core modules with clear rules between them.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Split an app into an app module, feature modules and core modules","List modules in <code>settings.gradle.kts</code> and depend on one with <code>project(...)</code>","Keep features apart: they depend on core, never on each other"],"remember":"Features depend on core, never on each other; only :app sees every feature and links them.","terms":[{"term":"module","def":"A part of a Gradle project with its own folder and <code>build.gradle.kts</code>. It can only use modules it depends on."},{"term":"feature module","def":"A module for one part of the app the user sees, with its screens and ViewModels, like <code>:feature:tonight</code>."},{"term":"core module","def":"A module with code several features share, like <code>:core:data</code>. It never depends on a feature."},{"term":"library module","def":"A module made with <code>com.android.library</code>: its code goes into an app, but it is not an app itself."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Tonight needs to open the Recipes screen. What should it do?","options":["Take an <code>onOpenRecipes</code> lambda that <code>:app</code> passes in","Depend on <code>:feature:recipes</code>","Copy <code>RecipesScreen</code> into Tonight"],"a":0,"why":"<code>:app</code> sees every feature, so it links them. The features stay apart."},{"q":"Which file lists every module of the project?","options":["<code>settings.gradle.kts</code>, with <code>include</code>","<code>app/build.gradle.kts</code>","The manifest"],"a":0,"why":"Gradle reads <code>settings.gradle.kts</code> first to know which modules exist."},{"q":"Which module may depend on <code>:feature:recipes</code>?","options":["<code>:app</code>","<code>:core:data</code>","<code>:feature:tonight</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Core never depends on a feature, and features never depend on each other. Only <code>:app</code> does."}]},{"id":"clean","title":"Clean architecture","tagline":"Keep the heart of the app in plain Kotlin, with every arrow pointing in.","level":"intermediate","goals":["State the dependency rule: code points inward, toward the domain","Move the repository interface into the domain and implement it in data","Map a Room entity to a plain domain model"],"remember":"Code points inward: the domain is plain Kotlin and declares interfaces; data implements them and maps its own models.","terms":[{"term":"clean architecture","def":"A way to arrange an app in rings, with the domain in the middle and the UI, data and libraries around it."},{"term":"dependency rule","def":"Code may only depend on rings further in. The domain depends on nothing outside it."},{"term":"@Binds","def":"Marks an <code>abstract</code> function in a Hilt module that says which class to hand over for an interface, like <code>OfflineRecipeRepository</code> for <code>RecipeRepository</code>."},{"term":"mapper","def":"A small function that turns one layer’s model into another’s, like <code>RecipeEntity.toRecipe()</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In clean architecture, which ring knows nothing about the others?","options":["The domain, in the middle","The UI","The data layer"],"a":0,"why":"Every arrow points in toward the domain. It is plain Kotlin and depends on nothing around it."},{"q":"Where does <code>interface RecipeRepository</code> live?","options":["In the domain; data implements it","In data, next to Room","In the UI"],"a":0,"why":"The domain says what it needs. The data layer keeps that promise, so the arrow points in."},{"q":"A small app has one screen and one data source. Does it need these rings?","options":["Not yet: they cost classes and pay off as the app grows","Yes, always","Only if it uses Room"],"a":0,"why":"Clean architecture trades extra code for safety when things change. Add it when the app, the team or the data sources grow."}]}]},{"id":"data","title":"Data and networking","description":"Build a Recipes app that keeps its data: settings in DataStore, recipes in a Room database, documents in files, then recipes from a server with Retrofit, kept offline, loaded in pages and updated live.","requires":["android","compose","coroutines","flow"],"file":"tracks/data.js","lessons":[{"id":"choose","title":"Where to keep data","tagline":"Settings, lists, documents and shared data each have their own place.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why data in a variable is gone after the app closes","Name four places an app can keep data","Pick the right place for a setting, a long list, a document and shared data"],"remember":"A few settings: DataStore. Many records: a database. One document: a file. Shared with other phones: a server. Memory forgets.","terms":[{"term":"storage","def":"The phone’s long-term memory. What an app writes there is still there after the app closes."},{"term":"DataStore","def":"A Jetpack library that keeps a few small settings, as keys and values, in one file."},{"term":"database","def":"A program that keeps many records of the same shape, and finds, sorts and changes them one at a time."},{"term":"server","def":"A computer on the internet that many phones talk to, so they can share data."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Ada’s recipes were only in a <code>var</code>. She swipes the app away and opens it again. What does she see?","options":["An empty list","Her twelve recipes","The recipes from yesterday morning"],"a":0,"why":"A <code>var</code> lives in memory, and the memory is cleared when the app stops."},{"q":"Which place fits 300 recipes that the user sorts by cooking time?","options":["A database","DataStore","The app’s memory"],"a":0,"why":"Many records of the same shape, sorted and searched: that is what a database is for."},{"q":"Lin wants her friend Sam to see her recipes on his own phone. What does that need?","options":["A server","A file on Lin’s phone","DataStore on Lin’s phone"],"a":0,"why":"Everything on Lin’s phone stays on Lin’s phone. Both phones must talk to a server."}]},{"id":"datastore","title":"Small settings","tagline":"Remember the user’s choices after the app closes.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Keep an on/off setting in DataStore","Read it as a <code>Flow</code> and show it on screen","Save a change from a button with <code>rememberCoroutineScope</code>"],"remember":"preferencesDataStore once at the top level; read with data (a Flow), write with edit inside a coroutine.","terms":[{"term":"DataStore","def":"A Jetpack library that keeps small settings in a file. <code>data</code> reads them as a <code>Flow</code>; <code>edit</code> changes them."},{"term":"preferences key","def":"A typed name for one setting, like <code>booleanPreferencesKey(\"favorites_only\")</code>."},{"term":"edit","def":"A suspend function of DataStore: <code>store.edit { prefs -&gt; prefs[KEY] = value }</code> changes values and writes the file."},{"term":"rememberCoroutineScope","def":"Gives a composable its own scope. <code>scope.launch { }</code> starts a coroutine from a click; it is cancelled when the composable leaves the screen."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does <code>val Context.dataStore by preferencesDataStore(name = \"settings\")</code> go?","options":["Once, at the top level of a Kotlin file","Inside the composable that uses it","Inside every Activity"],"a":0,"why":"At the top level it is made once, so the app has exactly one store for the file."},{"q":"What does <code>store.data</code> give you?","options":["A <code>Flow</code> that emits the saved values now and after every change","The values once, then nothing","A list of every change ever made"],"a":0,"why":"That is why the screen updates by itself after <code>edit</code>."},{"q":"Why does the button use <code>scope.launch</code>?","options":["<code>edit</code> is a suspend function, and <code>onClick</code> is not a coroutine","It makes the button draw faster","Compose needs it to show <code>Text</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A suspend function runs only in a coroutine or another suspend function."}]},{"id":"room","title":"A local database","tagline":"Describe a table of recipes, and the questions you ask it.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what a table, a row, a primary key and a query are","Describe the recipes table with <code>@Entity</code>","Write its reads and writes as a DAO with <code>@Query</code> and <code>@Insert</code>"],"remember":"A table has rows (records) and columns (properties); @Entity describes the table, @Dao holds its queries.","terms":[{"term":"table","def":"Rows and columns in a database. Each row is one record (a recipe); each column is one property (its title)."},{"term":"primary key","def":"A column whose value no other row has, like the id. <code>@PrimaryKey</code> marks it."},{"term":"query","def":"A question to the database, written in SQL, like <code>\"SELECT * FROM recipes ORDER BY minutes\"</code>."},{"term":"DAO","def":"Data access object: an interface marked <code>@Dao</code> with the functions that read and write the tables."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In the recipes table, what is one row?","options":["One recipe","One property of every recipe, like all the titles","The whole database"],"a":0,"why":"Rows are records; columns are properties."},{"q":"What does <code>\"SELECT * FROM recipes ORDER BY minutes\"</code> give back?","options":["Every recipe, shortest cooking time first","Only the minutes column","The recipe that takes longest"],"a":0,"why":"<code>*</code> means every column; <code>ORDER BY minutes</code> sorts the rows, smallest first."},{"q":"Why are <code>getAll</code> and <code>insert</code> <code>suspend</code> functions?","options":["Reading and writing the file takes time, so Room does it on a background thread while the screen keeps working","Room needs them to be private","So they run only once"],"a":0,"why":"The caller waits in a coroutine; the screen does not freeze."}]},{"id":"room-setup","title":"Opening the database","tagline":"Make one database for the whole app, and put its recipes on screen.","level":"intermediate","goals":["List the tables of a database with <code>@Database</code>","Open it once for the whole app with <code>Room.databaseBuilder</code>","Add Room to the build, with a saved schema for every version"],"remember":"@Database lists the tables and the version; open it once for the whole app with Room.databaseBuilder; the Room plugin saves a schema per version.","terms":[{"term":"database class","def":"An <code>abstract</code> class marked <code>@Database</code> that extends <code>RoomDatabase</code>. It lists the tables and hands out the DAOs."},{"term":"version","def":"The number of the database’s shape in <code>@Database(..., version = 1)</code>. It goes up when a table changes."},{"term":"schema","def":"The description of a database: its tables, their columns and types. The Room plugin saves one file per version."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What do the square brackets in <code>entities = [Recipe::class]</code> hold?","options":["The list of tables in the database","The rows of the recipes table","The DAO functions"],"a":0,"why":"Each entity listed there becomes one table."},{"q":"Why does the app keep one database in <code>RecipesDb</code>?","options":["Opening a database is expensive, and the app needs only one","Room allows only one table per database","So the database is deleted when the app closes"],"a":0,"why":"One instance for the whole life of the app, as the Room guide says."},{"q":"What does the Room Gradle plugin save at every build?","options":["A schema file describing the tables of this version","A copy of every recipe","The SQL of every query you ran"],"a":0,"why":"You need the schema files of the old and new version when the database changes."}]},{"id":"room-flow","title":"Live queries","tagline":"Let the list update itself whenever the table changes.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Return a <code>Flow</code> from a DAO query","Show it on screen with <code>collectAsStateWithLifecycle</code>","Change a row with <code>@Update</code> and watch the list follow"],"remember":"A DAO query that returns Flow emits again after every change to its table; the screen only collects, the clicks only write.","terms":[{"term":"observable query","def":"A DAO function that returns a <code>Flow</code>. Room emits its result, and emits again after every change to the table."},{"term":"Update","def":"<code>@Update</code> marks a DAO function that replaces the row with the same primary key."}],"quiz":[{"q":"After <code>dao.insert(...)</code>, what must a screen that collects <code>getAll()</code> do?","options":["Nothing: Room emits the new list","Call <code>getAll()</code> again","Restart the app"],"a":0,"why":"The table changed, so Room runs the query again and emits."},{"q":"Why is <code>getAll()</code> no longer a <code>suspend</code> function?","options":["It returns a Flow at once; the query runs while the Flow is collected","Flow functions must run on the main thread","Room doesn’t allow suspend functions"],"a":0,"why":"Making the Flow is quick. The work happens during <code>collect</code>."},{"q":"How does <code>@Update</code> find the row to change?","options":["By its primary key, the id","By its title","By its place on the screen"],"a":0,"why":"That is why <code>copy</code> keeps the id and changes only <code>favorite</code>."}]},{"id":"relations","title":"Related tables","tagline":"Give every recipe its steps, and load both together.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Link two tables with a column that holds the other row’s id","Load a recipe with its steps using <code>@Embedded</code> and <code>@Relation</code>","Explain why the query needs <code>@Transaction</code>"],"remember":"The child table keeps the parent’s id; @Embedded plus @Relation builds the nested object; mark the query @Transaction.","terms":[{"term":"one-to-many relation","def":"One row in a table (a recipe) belongs with many rows in another (its steps), linked by a column like <code>recipeId</code>."},{"term":"Embedded","def":"<code>@Embedded</code> puts all the columns of an entity into a plain class, as one property."},{"term":"Relation","def":"<code>@Relation(parentColumns = [...], entityColumns = [...])</code> fills a list with the rows of another table whose column matches."},{"term":"Transaction","def":"<code>@Transaction</code> makes all the queries of one DAO function act as a single step on the database."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where is the link between a step and its recipe stored?","options":["In the steps table, in the column <code>recipeId</code>","In the recipes table, as a list of steps","In DataStore"],"a":0,"why":"The “many” side keeps the id of the “one” side."},{"q":"In <code>WHERE id = :id</code>, what is <code>:id</code>?","options":["The function’s parameter <code>id</code>, filled in by Room","A column named “:id”","A comment"],"a":0,"why":"A colon and a parameter name put that value into the query."},{"q":"Why does <code>getWithSteps</code> need <code>@Transaction</code>?","options":["Room runs two queries, and both must read the same moment of the database","Without it the steps come back in the wrong order","It makes the query faster"],"a":0,"why":"One query for the recipe, one for its steps: <code>@Transaction</code> keeps them together."}]},{"id":"migrations","title":"Changing the database","tagline":"Update the app without losing anyone’s recipes.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why a changed table needs a new version number","Let Room write a simple migration with <code>AutoMigration</code>","Write a migration yourself with <code>Migration</code>"],"remember":"Change a table: raise version, and give Room a migration, AutoMigration for simple changes or Migration with your own SQL.","terms":[{"term":"migration","def":"The steps that change a database file from one version to the next while keeping its rows."},{"term":"schema","def":"The description of a database: its tables, their columns and types. Room saves one file per version."},{"term":"AutoMigration","def":"<code>AutoMigration(from = 1, to = 2)</code> in <code>@Database</code>: Room compares the two schemas and writes the migration."},{"term":"Migration","def":"A class whose <code>migrate</code> function runs your own SQL to go from one version to another."}],"quiz":[{"q":"When must you raise <code>version</code>?","options":["Whenever a table or column changes","At every release of the app","Only when you add a whole new table"],"a":0,"why":"Any change to the shape of the database needs a new version and a migration."},{"q":"Why does the new column need <code>@ColumnInfo(defaultValue = \"0\")</code>, and not only <code>= 0</code> in Kotlin?","options":["The rows already in the file get their value from the database, and a Kotlin default lives only in your code","Kotlin defaults are not allowed in entities","It makes the column the primary key"],"a":0,"why":"The migration runs in the database; it never sees your Kotlin code."},{"q":"What does <code>AutoMigration</code> compare to write the migration?","options":["The saved schema files of the two versions","The recipes on the user’s phone","The old and the new app"],"a":0,"why":"That is why the schema files must exist for both versions."}]},{"id":"files","title":"Files","tagline":"Write a whole document into your app’s own folder, and read it back.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Find your app’s private folders with <code>filesDir</code> and <code>cacheDir</code>","Write and read a text file away from the main thread","Say what happens to those files on uninstall and when space runs low"],"remember":"filesDir keeps files until uninstall; cacheDir may be emptied by Android; read and write inside withContext(Dispatchers.IO).","terms":[{"term":"filesDir","def":"A property of every <code>Context</code>: the app’s private folder for files it keeps. Deleted on uninstall."},{"term":"cacheDir","def":"The app’s private folder for files it can make again. Android may delete them when space runs low."},{"term":"readText","def":"<code>file.readText()</code> reads the whole file as one <code>String</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which apps can read <code>File(context.filesDir, \"recipes.txt\")</code>?","options":["Only your app","Every app with a storage permission","Every app"],"a":0,"why":"<code>filesDir</code> is private to your app."},{"q":"What happens to files in <code>filesDir</code> when the user uninstalls the app?","options":["They are deleted","They stay for the next install","They move to Downloads"],"a":0,"why":"The whole private folder goes with the app."},{"q":"Why does <code>writeText</code> run inside <code>withContext(Dispatchers.IO)</code>?","options":["Writing to disk can be slow, and the main thread must keep drawing the screen","<code>writeText</code> works only there","It keeps the file private"],"a":0,"why":"On the main thread a slow write would freeze the screen."}]},{"id":"share-files","title":"Sharing files","tagline":"Hand one file to another app without opening your whole folder.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why another app can’t open a file in your <code>filesDir</code>","Set up <code>FileProvider</code> and get a <code>content://</code> address for one file","Share the file with <code>ACTION_SEND</code> and a temporary read permission"],"remember":"FileProvider turns one file in a listed folder into a content:// Uri; send it with ACTION_SEND and FLAG_GRANT_READ_URI_PERMISSION.","terms":[{"term":"FileProvider","def":"A ready-made provider from Jetpack’s core library that gives other apps a <code>content://</code> Uri for one of your files."},{"term":"authority","def":"The unique name of a provider, like <code>com.example.recipes.fileprovider</code>. It is the first part of its Uris."},{"term":"content Uri","def":"An address like <code>content://…/exports/recipes.txt</code> that names a file through a provider, without its real place."},{"term":"FLAG_GRANT_READ_URI_PERMISSION","def":"A flag on an Intent that lets the receiving app read the Uri it carries, until that app’s task is finished."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>file_paths.xml</code> decide?","options":["Which folders FileProvider may give Uris for","Where Room keeps the database","Which apps appear in the share sheet"],"a":0,"why":"Only files inside the listed folders can be shared."},{"q":"How long can the mail app read the file?","options":["Until its task is finished: the permission is temporary","Forever","Until your app closes"],"a":0,"why":"A permission from the Intent’s flag ends by itself."},{"q":"Why not send the file’s real path?","options":["Other apps can’t open your <code>filesDir</code>, so the path is no use to them","Paths are too long for an Intent","Intents can’t carry text"],"a":0,"why":"FileProvider opens the file for them, one Uri at a time."}]},{"id":"http-basics","title":"Talking to a server","tagline":"What a server, an HTTP request and JSON are, and how JSON becomes a Kotlin object.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what a server, an HTTP request and a response are","Read JSON: lists, objects and their keys","Turn JSON text into a Kotlin object with <code>@Serializable</code> and <code>Json</code>"],"remember":"A server answers HTTP requests with a status code and a JSON body; @Serializable and Json.decodeFromString turn that JSON into Kotlin objects.","terms":[{"term":"server","def":"A computer on the internet that keeps data and answers requests from apps."},{"term":"HTTP","def":"How apps and servers talk. A request has a method (<code>GET</code>, …) and a URL; a response has a status code (200 means OK) and a body."},{"term":"JSON","def":"A text format for data: <code>[ ]</code> holds a list, <code>{ }</code> holds an object made of <code>\"key\": value</code> pairs."},{"term":"DTO","def":"Data transfer object: a class with the shape the server sends, like <code>RecipeDto</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The server answers with status code 200. What does that mean?","options":["OK: the body has what you asked for","The server is busy","200 recipes are on the way"],"a":0,"why":"200 is the code for success. The data is in the body."},{"q":"In JSON, what do square brackets <code>[ ]</code> hold?","options":["A list","One object","A comment"],"a":0,"why":"Square brackets hold a list; curly braces hold one object."},{"q":"Reading <code>{\"id\":1,\"title\":\"Tomato soup\",\"minutes\":25}</code>, which property does the key <code>\"title\"</code> fill?","options":["<code>title</code>, the property with the same name","The first property, <code>id</code>","None: keys are only labels"],"a":0,"why":"Each key fills the property with the same name."}]},{"id":"retrofit","title":"Calling a server with Retrofit","tagline":"Describe the server’s requests as an interface, and let Retrofit send them.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Describe a server call as a Retrofit interface with <code>@GET</code>","Build one <code>RecipeApi</code> with a base URL and a JSON converter","Call it from <code>LaunchedEffect</code> and show the recipes"],"remember":"Retrofit turns an interface into HTTP requests: @GET names the path, baseUrl ends with /, and the converter reads the JSON.","terms":[{"term":"Retrofit","def":"A library that turns an interface with <code>@GET</code> and friends into HTTP requests."},{"term":"base URL","def":"The start of every address, like <code>https://recipes.example.com/</code>. Retrofit puts each path after it, so it must end with <code>/</code>."},{"term":"converter","def":"The part of Retrofit that turns a JSON body into Kotlin objects, here with <code>Json</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is <code>getRecipes()</code> a <code>suspend</code> function?","options":["So the coroutine can wait for the server without freezing the screen","So the request travels faster","Because it returns a list"],"a":0,"why":"A server answer can take seconds. A suspend function lets the coroutine wait while the screen keeps working."},{"q":"What does <code>create(RecipeApi::class.java)</code> do?","options":["Writes the code behind the interface and gives you an object to call","Sends the first request","Downloads the recipes"],"a":0,"why":"You only write the interface. Retrofit makes the object that sends the requests."},{"q":"Does the user see a dialog for the <code>INTERNET</code> permission?","options":["No: it is granted when the app is installed","Yes, the first time the app goes online","Yes, every time the app opens"],"a":0,"why":"It is a normal permission. One line in the manifest is enough."}]},{"id":"net","title":"Requests and errors","tagline":"No signal, a broken server, odd JSON: show a message, not a crash.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Name the three ways a server call fails","Catch them and show a message instead of crashing","Let the user try again"],"remember":"Catch IOException (no connection), HttpException (a failed status code) and SerializationException (JSON that doesn’t fit), and show each as a state.","terms":[{"term":"IOException","def":"Thrown when the request gets no answer: no connection, server not found, or no answer in time."},{"term":"HttpException","def":"Thrown by Retrofit when the server answers with a status code outside the 200s. <code>e.code()</code> gives the code."},{"term":"SerializationException","def":"Thrown when the JSON doesn’t fit the class, for example a missing or unknown key."},{"term":"status code","def":"The number at the start of a response: 200s success, 400s a wrong request (404 not found), 500s a server problem."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The server answers 404. What does the suspend Retrofit call do?","options":["Throws an <code>HttpException</code>","Throws an <code>IOException</code>","Returns an empty list"],"a":0,"why":"An answer arrived, but its status code isn’t in the 200s, so Retrofit throws an <code>HttpException</code>."},{"q":"Which status codes mean success?","options":["The 200s","The 400s","The 500s"],"a":0,"why":"200s are success, 400s a wrong request, 500s a server problem."},{"q":"Ada taps Try again, which runs <code>attempt++</code>. Why does the call run again?","options":["<code>attempt</code> is the key of <code>LaunchedEffect</code>, and a new key restarts its block","The button calls <code>getRecipes()</code> itself","Every recomposition restarts <code>LaunchedEffect</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A new key stops the old block and starts it again from the top."}]},{"id":"auth","title":"Signing in","tagline":"Sign in once, then let a short-lived token prove who you are.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what an access token and a refresh token are","Send the token with every request and refresh it when it runs out","Keep tokens where other apps can’t read them"],"remember":"Sign in once; send the access token in the Authorization header; on 401 refresh once and repeat; keep tokens in DataStore, never the password.","terms":[{"term":"token","def":"A long random-looking text the server hands out after sign-in. It proves who is asking, without the password."},{"term":"access token","def":"The short-lived token sent with every request, in the header <code>Authorization: Bearer …</code>."},{"term":"refresh token","def":"A longer-lived token that only gets a new access token when the old one has run out."},{"term":"header","def":"An extra named line in a request or response, like <code>Authorization</code>. <code>@Header</code> fills one from a parameter."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the app keep a token instead of the password?","options":["A token soon runs out and only works for this server; a password may open many accounts","Tokens are shorter to type","Servers can’t read passwords"],"a":0,"why":"A leaked token does little harm for a short time. A leaked password does much more."},{"q":"The refresh request gets a 401 too. What now?","options":["Show the sign-in screen","Refresh again and again","Keep using the old access token"],"a":0,"why":"Both tokens are no good any more, so Ada has to sign in again."},{"q":"Where is a good place to keep the tokens?","options":["DataStore, in the app’s own storage","In the code, as a <code>const val</code>","In a Logcat message"],"a":0,"why":"Android lets only your app read its own storage. Code and logs can be read by others."}]},{"id":"caching","title":"Caching","tagline":"Show the saved recipes at once, and let the network only refresh them.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Show saved recipes at once, even offline","Make the Room table the one place the screen reads from","Decide when the saved copy is too old"],"remember":"The screen reads only from Room; the network only refreshes Room, and only when the copy is too old or the user asks.","terms":[{"term":"cache","def":"A copy of data kept close by, here server recipes saved in Room, so they can be shown without asking again."},{"term":"offline first","def":"The screen reads only from the local database; the network only refreshes that database."},{"term":"@Upsert","def":"A Room DAO function that updates the row with the same primary key, or inserts the row if there is none."},{"term":"cache invalidation","def":"Deciding when the saved copy is too old and must be refreshed, for example after 15 minutes or when the user asks."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In an offline-first app, where does the recipe screen read its list from?","options":["The Room table","The server, and Room only when offline","Both, one after the other"],"a":0,"why":"One source for the screen: the table. The network only writes into it."},{"q":"A refresh brings a recipe whose id is already in the table. What does <code>@Upsert</code> do?","options":["Updates that row","Adds a second row with the same id","Throws an error"],"a":0,"why":"Same primary key: update. A new key: insert."},{"q":"The last refresh was 5 minutes ago and <code>MAX_AGE</code> is 15 minutes. The screen opens. What happens?","options":["No call: the saved recipes are shown","A full refresh","The table is emptied first"],"a":0,"why":"The copy is younger than <code>MAX_AGE</code>, so <code>refreshRecipes</code> returns early."}]},{"id":"paging","title":"Loading in pages","tagline":"Twenty thousand recipes, twenty at a time, as the user scrolls.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why a long list loads in pages","Write a <code>PagingSource</code> that loads one page from the server","Show the pages in a <code>LazyColumn</code> with <code>collectAsLazyPagingItems</code>"],"remember":"A PagingSource loads one page per key and returns nextKey (null at the end); Pager plus collectAsLazyPagingItems loads more as the user scrolls.","terms":[{"term":"page","def":"One slice of a long list that the server sends at a time, such as recipes 21 to 40."},{"term":"PagingSource","def":"Your class that loads one page for a key and says which keys come before and after it."},{"term":"LoadResult","def":"What <code>load</code> returns: <code>LoadResult.Page(data, prevKey, nextKey)</code> on success, <code>LoadResult.Error(e)</code> on failure."},{"term":"Pager","def":"Makes a Flow of pages from a <code>PagingConfig</code> and a PagingSource. <code>collectAsLazyPagingItems()</code> shows it in a <code>LazyColumn</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>nextKey = null</code> tell Paging?","options":["There are no more pages","Load page 1 again","This page failed"],"a":0,"why":"<code>null</code> means nothing comes after this page, so Paging stops loading."},{"q":"In <code>PagingSource&lt;Int, RecipeDto&gt;</code>, what is the <code>Int</code>?","options":["The key that names a page, here the page number","How many rows a page has","The recipe id"],"a":0,"why":"The first type is the key, the second the item."},{"q":"Page 4 fails because the signal is gone. What should <code>load</code> return?","options":["<code>LoadResult.Error(e)</code>","An empty <code>LoadResult.Page</code>","Nothing: let the exception crash the app"],"a":0,"why":"An error result keeps the loaded rows and lets <code>retry()</code> load that page again. An empty page would end the list."}]},{"id":"realtime","title":"Live updates","tagline":"Keep a connection open and let the server tell you when something changes.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why asking again and again is slow and wasteful","Turn WebSocket messages into a Flow with <code>callbackFlow</code>","Save each live change into Room so the screen updates by itself"],"remember":"A WebSocket stays open so the server can send changes at once; wrap it in callbackFlow, write each change into Room, and retry when it drops.","terms":[{"term":"polling","def":"Asking the server again and again, at fixed times, whether anything changed."},{"term":"WebSocket","def":"A connection that stays open, so the app and the server can each send messages at any time."},{"term":"server-sent events","def":"SSE: a response that never ends, in which the server writes one event after another. One way only, server to app."},{"term":"OkHttp","def":"The library Retrofit uses to send requests. It also opens WebSockets with <code>newWebSocket</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is polling every 10 seconds a poor fit for live changes?","options":["Most requests find nothing new, they cost battery, and changes still arrive up to 10 s late","Servers can’t answer that often","HTTP allows only one request a minute"],"a":0,"why":"Polling pays for every request but only learns about a change on the next one."},{"q":"An app only receives live scores and never sends anything. Which is the lighter choice?","options":["Server-sent events","A WebSocket","Polling every second"],"a":0,"why":"SSE is one way, server to app, over a normal HTTP response. A WebSocket is for both directions."},{"q":"A live change is upserted into Room. How does the recipe screen update?","options":["Room’s Flow sends the new list, and the screen redraws","The WebSocket code redraws the screen","Only after the app restarts"],"a":0,"why":"The screen reads Room. Any write to the table, from a refresh or a live message, reaches it through the Flow."}]}]},{"id":"views","title":"Views","description":"Read and change older screens built with Views: XML layouts, RecyclerView, Fragments, and mixing them with Compose.","requires":["android","compose"],"file":"tracks/views.js","lessons":[{"id":"xml","title":"XML layouts","tagline":"How older screens are written down in a file, then built as objects.","level":"beginner","goals":["Read a layout file from <code>res/layout</code>","Build its views with view binding and show them","Change a view that is already on screen"],"remember":"A layout file describes views; inflate builds them; setContentView(binding.root) shows them; you change a view instead of drawing it again.","terms":[{"term":"layout file","def":"An XML file in <code>res/layout</code> that describes the views of a screen as nested tags."},{"term":"inflate","def":"Read a layout file and build the view objects it describes."},{"term":"view binding","def":"A build feature that makes a class per layout, like <code>ActivityProfileBinding</code>, with one property for each view that has an id."},{"term":"findViewById","def":"The older way to get a view: look it up by id, like <code>findViewById(R.id.title)</code>. Mistakes only show up when the app runs."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which views in <code>activity_profile.xml</code> get a property in <code>ActivityProfileBinding</code>?","options":["Only views that have an <code>android:id</code>","Every view in the file","Only the outer view"],"a":0,"why":"The binding has one property per view with an id, plus <code>root</code> for the outer view."},{"q":"You rename <code>@+id/title</code> to <code>@+id/heading</code>. What happens to <code>binding.title.text = \"Hi\"</code>?","options":["It stops compiling: <code>Unresolved reference 'title'.</code>","The app crashes when it runs that line","It still works"],"a":0,"why":"The binding class is made again with a <code>heading</code> property, so <code>title</code> no longer exists."},{"q":"A <code>TextView</code> is on screen. How do you show new text in it?","options":["<code>binding.title.text = \"New\"</code>","Inflate the layout again","Call <code>Text(\"New\")</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A view is an object that stays on screen. You change its properties, and it updates itself."}]},{"id":"passes","title":"Measure, layout, draw","tagline":"Three passes turn a tree of views into pixels.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Name the three passes and what each one decides","Choose between <code>invalidate()</code> and <code>requestLayout()</code>","Read a small custom view"],"remember":"Measure (how big), layout (where), draw (paint). Look changed: invalidate(). Size changed: requestLayout().","terms":[{"term":"measure, layout, draw","def":"The three passes of a frame: work out every view’s size, then its position, then paint it."},{"term":"invalidate","def":"<code>invalidate()</code> asks for the view to be drawn again in the next frame, without measuring or placing it again."},{"term":"requestLayout","def":"<code>requestLayout()</code> asks for a new measure and layout pass in the next frame, because a size or position may change."},{"term":"custom view","def":"Your own class that extends <code>View</code> and paints itself in <code>onDraw</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In which order do the passes run?","options":["Measure, layout, draw","Draw, measure, layout","Layout, measure, draw"],"a":0,"why":"Sizes come first, positions use those sizes, and drawing uses the final size and position."},{"q":"A custom view changes only its color. Which call fits?","options":["<code>invalidate()</code>","<code>requestLayout()</code>","Neither: nothing is needed"],"a":0,"why":"The size and position stay the same, so a redraw is enough. Without any call, the old color stays on screen."},{"q":"You set <code>binding.title.text</code> on a built-in <code>TextView</code>. Do you call <code>requestLayout()</code> yourself?","options":["No: TextView asks for the passes it needs","Yes, every time","Only when the text gets longer"],"a":0,"why":"Built-in views ask for their own passes when their properties change. You only do it in your own custom views."}]},{"id":"recycler","title":"RecyclerView","tagline":"A long list built from a handful of reused rows.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why a list reuses its row views","Write an adapter with <code>onCreateViewHolder</code> and <code>onBindViewHolder</code>","Avoid rows that show the wrong data after scrolling"],"remember":"RecyclerView makes a screenful of rows and refills them. onBindViewHolder sets every part of the row, every time.","terms":[{"term":"RecyclerView","def":"A view that shows a long list by making only a screenful of rows and reusing them as you scroll."},{"term":"adapter","def":"The class that connects your data to RecyclerView: it makes rows (<code>onCreateViewHolder</code>), fills them (<code>onBindViewHolder</code>) and counts items (<code>getItemCount</code>)."},{"term":"ViewHolder","def":"An object that holds one row view so it can be filled again and again."},{"term":"layout manager","def":"The part that places the rows, like <code>LinearLayoutManager</code> for one row under another."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which part decides that rows go one under another?","options":["The layout manager","The adapter","The ViewHolder"],"a":0,"why":"<code>LinearLayoutManager</code> places the rows. The adapter only makes and fills them."},{"q":"When a reused row is shown for a new contact, which function runs for it?","options":["Only <code>onBindViewHolder</code>","<code>onCreateViewHolder</code>, then <code>onBindViewHolder</code>","Only <code>onCreateViewHolder</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The row already exists, so it only needs to be filled with the new contact."},{"q":"The bind code is <code>if (contact.starred) { holder.binding.star.isVisible = true }</code>. What goes wrong after scrolling?","options":["Stars show up on contacts who are not starred","Starred contacts lose their star","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"A reused row keeps the star from its last contact, because nothing ever hides it. Write <code>star.isVisible = contact.starred</code>."}]},{"id":"view-types","title":"Many item types","tagline":"Headers and rows in one list, each with its own layout.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Give each kind of row its own view type","Create and fill the right holder for each kind","Explain why holders of different kinds are never mixed"],"remember":"One kind of row, one view type. getItemViewType names it, onCreateViewHolder builds it, and holders are only reused within their kind.","terms":[{"term":"view type","def":"An <code>Int</code> that names one kind of row, like 0 for a header and 1 for a person. Each kind has its own layout and its own spare holders."},{"term":"getItemViewType","def":"The adapter function that returns the view type for a position. If you don’t override it, every position gets 0."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>getItemViewType</code> return if you don’t override it?","options":["0 for every position","The position","A different number for each layout"],"a":0,"why":"By default the adapter assumes one kind of row. Override it once rows look different."},{"q":"Only a header holder is free, and a person row scrolls in. What does RecyclerView do?","options":["Makes a new person holder","Fills the header holder as a person","Shows an empty row"],"a":0,"why":"Holders are reused only within their view type, so the bind code never gets a holder of the wrong kind."},{"q":"Inside <code>if (holder is HeaderHolder) { }</code>, why does <code>holder.binding.letter</code> work?","options":["After the <code>is</code> check, Kotlin knows <code>holder</code> is a HeaderHolder","Every holder has a <code>letter</code>","It doesn’t compile"],"a":0,"why":"It is a smart cast: inside that branch, Kotlin treats <code>holder</code> as a HeaderHolder."}]},{"id":"diff","title":"Efficient updates","tagline":"Hand over a new list, and only the rows that changed are redrawn.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Tell a <code>ListAdapter</code> how to compare items with <code>DiffUtil</code>","Update a list with <code>submitList</code>","Tell “the same item” from “the same contents”"],"remember":"Same id: same item. Same fields: same contents. Give submitList a new list every time.","terms":[{"term":"ListAdapter","def":"An adapter that keeps the list itself and, when you call <code>submitList</code>, updates only the rows that changed."},{"term":"DiffUtil","def":"The tool that compares an old and a new list and works out the inserts, removals, moves and changes. You answer its questions in a <code>DiffUtil.ItemCallback</code>."},{"term":"submitList","def":"<code>submitList(newList)</code> hands a ListAdapter its next list to compare and show."},{"term":"notifyDataSetChanged","def":"The old, blunt update: “everything may have changed”. Every visible row is filled again, with no animation."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A contact changes name but keeps its id. What does DiffUtil report?","options":["A change at that position","A removal and an insertion","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"Same id, so the same item. Different contents, so that one row is filled again."},{"q":"Why avoid <code>notifyDataSetChanged()</code> when only one contact changed?","options":["It fills every row again and plays no animation","It crashes on long lists","It deletes the list"],"a":0,"why":"RecyclerView can’t tell what changed, so it treats everything as changed."},{"q":"You add Dee to the same <code>mutableListOf</code> you submitted before, then call <code>submitList</code> with that same list. What happens?","options":["Nothing: it is the same list object","Dee’s row slides in","The whole list is filled again"],"a":0,"why":"ListAdapter sees the very same list it already has, so it has nothing to compare. Submit a new list instead."}]},{"id":"fragments","title":"Fragments","tagline":"A piece of a screen whose view can come and go while it stays.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Say what a Fragment is and where you meet one","Tell the Fragment’s lifecycle from its view’s lifecycle","Clear a binding in <code>onDestroyView</code>"],"remember":"A Fragment can outlive its view. Build the binding in onViewCreated and set it to null in onDestroyView.","terms":[{"term":"Fragment","def":"A piece of UI with its own layout and lifecycle, shown inside an Activity. Older apps often build each screen as one."},{"term":"view lifecycle","def":"The shorter life of a Fragment’s view, from <code>onCreateView</code> to <code>onDestroyView</code>. It can end and start again while the Fragment stays."},{"term":"onDestroyView","def":"The callback where a Fragment’s view is torn down, for example when it goes onto the back stack. Clear view references here."},{"term":"addToBackStack","def":"Records a Fragment change so that pressing Back undoes it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The inbox is replaced by a message, with <code>addToBackStack</code>. What happens to the inbox?","options":["Its view is destroyed; the Fragment stays","The Fragment is destroyed completely","Nothing: it stays resumed under the message"],"a":0,"why":"It gets <code>onDestroyView</code> but not <code>onDestroy</code>. Back gives it a new view."},{"q":"Which callback runs again on the same Fragment when you press Back to it?","options":["<code>onViewCreated</code>","<code>onCreate</code>","<code>onDestroy</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The Fragment object was kept, so only its view lifecycle starts again: <code>onCreateView</code>, then <code>onViewCreated</code>."},{"q":"Why set <code>_binding = null</code> in <code>onDestroyView</code>?","options":["So a waiting Fragment doesn’t keep its old view in memory","Because the app won’t compile otherwise","To make Back faster"],"a":0,"why":"The binding points at every view in the old layout. While the Fragment waits on the back stack, those views are useless."}]},{"id":"viewpager","title":"Paging screens","tagline":"Swipe between pages, and know which one is really on screen.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Show swipeable Fragment pages with <code>ViewPager2</code>","Write a <code>FragmentStateAdapter</code>","React when a page is the one on screen"],"remember":"ViewPager2 plus a FragmentStateAdapter (getItemCount, createFragment). Only the page on screen is resumed.","terms":[{"term":"ViewPager2","def":"A view that shows pages the user swipes between. It is built on RecyclerView."},{"term":"FragmentStateAdapter","def":"The adapter for Fragment pages: <code>getItemCount</code> says how many, <code>createFragment</code> makes the Fragment for a page."},{"term":"offscreenPageLimit","def":"How many pages the pager keeps built on each side of the current page."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Sports is on screen, with News and Weather built on each side. Which page is resumed?","options":["Only Sports","All three","None: pager pages stop at onStart"],"a":0,"why":"Neighbour pages stop at <code>onStart</code>. Only the current page gets <code>onResume</code>."},{"q":"Where should a page count “this page was viewed”?","options":["<code>onResume</code>","<code>onViewCreated</code>","<code>createFragment</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>onViewCreated</code> also runs for neighbour pages nobody has seen. <code>onResume</code> runs when the page becomes the current one."},{"q":"The pager opens on News with <code>offscreenPageLimit = 1</code>. Which callbacks has the Sports page had?","options":["<code>onViewCreated</code> and <code>onStart</code>, not <code>onResume</code>","All of them, including <code>onResume</code>","None yet"],"a":0,"why":"Sports is built ahead so a swipe shows it at once, but it is not on screen, so it is not resumed."}]},{"id":"compose-in-views","title":"Mixing Compose and Views","tagline":"Compose inside an old screen, and an old view inside Compose.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Show composables inside a View screen with <code>ComposeView</code>","Show a View inside a composable with <code>AndroidView</code>","Know what <code>factory</code> and <code>update</code> each do"],"remember":"ComposeView: Compose inside a View. AndroidView: a View inside Compose, where factory runs once and update runs whenever what it reads changes.","terms":[{"term":"ComposeView","def":"A View that shows composables. Fill it with <code>setContent { }</code>."},{"term":"AndroidView","def":"A composable that shows a View: <code>factory</code> creates it once, <code>update</code> copies state into it."},{"term":"ViewCompositionStrategy","def":"The rule for when a ComposeView ends its Compose content. In a Fragment, use <code>DisposeOnViewTreeLifecycleDestroyed</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is <code>ComposeView</code>?","options":["A View that shows composables","A composable that shows a View","A kind of Fragment"],"a":0,"why":"It is a View, so it fits anywhere a View fits: in XML, in code, or as a Fragment’s view."},{"q":"In an <code>AndroidView</code>, state that <code>update</code> reads changes. What runs again?","options":["Only <code>update</code>","<code>factory</code>, then <code>update</code>","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"The View already exists, so <code>factory</code> is not called again. <code>update</code> copies the new state into it."},{"q":"A ComposeView is a Fragment’s whole view. Which strategy fits?","options":["<code>DisposeOnViewTreeLifecycleDestroyed</code>","None: Compose content never ends","Call <code>setContent</code> again in <code>onDestroyView</code>"],"a":0,"why":"It ends the Compose content together with the Fragment’s view, in step with <code>onDestroyView</code>."}]}]},{"id":"compose2","title":"Compose in depth","description":"Take the water tracker further: stable types, derived state, the three phases, drawing, CompositionLocal, animation, shared elements, custom layouts, nested navigation and Navigation 3.","requires":["compose"],"file":"tracks/compose-advanced.js","lessons":[{"id":"stability","title":"Stability","tagline":"Promise Compose what your types do, and know why lambdas don’t stop a skip.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why passing a lambda doesn’t stop a composable from skipping","Choose between <code>@Immutable</code> and <code>@Stable</code> for a class Compose can’t judge","Spot a plain <code>var</code> that Compose never hears change"],"remember":"@Immutable: never changes. @Stable: changes only through Compose state. Lambdas are remembered by their captures. A plain var keeps both promises from working.","terms":[{"term":"@Immutable","def":"Your promise that no property of a class changes after it is made. Compose then treats the class as stable and compares it with <code>==</code>."},{"term":"@Stable","def":"Your promise that a class changes only through Compose state, like <code>var x by mutableStateOf(…)</code>, so Compose hears every change."},{"term":"capture","def":"A value a lambda uses from around it, like <code>day</code> in <code>{ onDelete(day.date) }</code>. With strong skipping, the lambda is remembered with its captures as keys."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Room sends every day again as new <code>DayTags</code> objects with equal values. <code>DayTags</code> holds a <code>List</code> and has no annotation. Does <code>TagsRow(tags)</code> skip?","options":["No: it is unstable, so it is compared with <code>===</code>, and these are new objects","Yes: the values are equal","Yes: a data class is always stable"],"a":0,"why":"The <code>List</code> makes <code>DayTags</code> unstable. Only the very same object would pass. With <code>@Immutable</code>, <code>==</code> decides and it skips."},{"q":"With strong skipping, what happens to <code>{ onDelete(day.date) }</code> inside a composable?","options":["The compiler remembers it, keyed by <code>onDelete</code> and <code>day</code>, so the same lambda comes back while they are unchanged","A new lambda is made on every run, so the child never skips","Compose ignores lambda parameters when it decides to skip"],"a":0,"why":"Every lambda in a composable is wrapped in <code>remember</code>, with its captures as keys."},{"q":"Which class fits <code>@Stable</code> rather than <code>@Immutable</code>?","options":["A class whose <code>var</code>s are all <code>by mutableStateOf(…)</code>","A data class of <code>val</code>s that never change","A class with a plain <code>var</code> that you change in place"],"a":0,"why":"<code>@Stable</code>: it may change, but Compose hears every change. A data class that never changes fits <code>@Immutable</code>, and a plain <code>var</code> breaks both promises."}]},{"id":"derived","title":"derivedStateOf","tagline":"Turn a value that changes all the time into one that changes only when it matters.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Spot a state that changes far more often than the screen needs","Wrap the check in <code>remember { derivedStateOf { } }</code>","Tell when <code>derivedStateOf</code> is not needed"],"remember":"Use remember { derivedStateOf { check } } when a state changes much more often than the check’s result.","terms":[{"term":"derivedStateOf","def":"Makes a state from a calculation on other states. It reports a change only when the result is different."},{"term":"animateScrollToItem","def":"On a list state: scrolls smoothly to the row with that index. A <code>suspend</code> function, so start it in a coroutine."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The top row index goes 0, 1, 2, 3, 4. How many times does <code>derivedStateOf { listState.firstVisibleItemIndex > 0 }</code> report a change?","options":["Once: <code>false</code> to <code>true</code>","4 times","5 times"],"a":0,"why":"The result is <code>true</code> from index 1 on. It only changes once."},{"q":"Which of these is worth a <code>derivedStateOf</code>?","options":["A <code>Boolean</code> that becomes <code>true</code> once the user scrolls past row 0","<code>\"$glasses of 8 glasses\"</code>","<code>glasses + 1</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The scroll index changes far more often than the <code>Boolean</code>. The other two change every time <code>glasses</code> does."},{"q":"Why is it wrapped in <code>remember { }</code>?","options":["So the derived state is made once and kept between runs","So it is saved when the phone rotates","So it runs on another thread"],"a":0,"why":"Without <code>remember</code>, every run would make a new derived state."}]},{"id":"phases","title":"Compose phases","tagline":"Each frame has up to three steps: what to show, where it goes, how it looks.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Name the three phases of a frame: composition, layout and drawing","Tell which phase reads a state, and what runs again when it changes","Keep a value that changes every frame out of composition"],"remember":"A state read in composition reruns all three phases; read in layout, only layout and drawing; read in drawing, only drawing.","terms":[{"term":"composition","def":"The first phase of a frame: Compose runs composables to decide what is on the screen."},{"term":"layout phase","def":"The second phase: each element is measured, then placed at its position."},{"term":"drawing phase","def":"The last phase: the elements paint their pixels."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In which phase does Compose measure elements and decide where they go?","options":["Layout","Composition","Drawing"],"a":0,"why":"Composition decides what is shown, layout where and how big, drawing how it looks."},{"q":"A state is read only inside <code>Modifier.offset { }</code>. It changes. What runs?","options":["Layout and drawing","All three phases","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"The read happened in layout, so Compose starts again from layout. Drawing comes after it."},{"q":"Which values gain the most from being read late?","options":["Values that change on every frame, like a drag or a scroll position","A title that never changes","A value read once when the screen opens"],"a":0,"why":"A value that changes 60 or 120 times a second would recompose that often if read in composition."}]},{"id":"drawing","title":"Drawing","tagline":"Paint your own shapes with Canvas when no ready-made composable fits.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Draw rectangles inside a <code>Canvas</code>","Place them with pixel coordinates from the top-left corner","Size a drawing from <code>size</code> and <code>toPx()</code> so it fits any phone"],"remember":"Canvas { } draws in pixels from the top-left, y down; size is the area, Offset a point, toPx() turns dp into pixels.","terms":[{"term":"Canvas","def":"A composable that gives you an area and a lambda to paint in, with functions like <code>drawRect</code>."},{"term":"drawRect","def":"Paints a rectangle. With only <code>color</code> it fills the whole area; <code>topLeft</code> and <code>size</code> choose a part."},{"term":"Offset","def":"A point, <code>Offset(x, y)</code>, in pixels from the top-left corner. y grows downwards."},{"term":"toPx","def":"<code>3.dp.toPx()</code> turns dp into this phone’s pixels, inside a Canvas."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In a Canvas, where is <code>Offset(0f, 0f)</code>?","options":["The top-left corner","The center","The bottom-left corner"],"a":0,"why":"x grows to the right and y grows down, both from the top-left corner."},{"q":"<code>size.height</code> is 480. A rectangle has <code>topLeft = Offset(0f, 400f)</code>. Where does it start?","options":["80 px above the bottom","400 px above the bottom","Above the Canvas"],"a":0,"why":"400 px down from the top leaves 480 − 400 = 80 px to the bottom."},{"q":"Why use <code>size</code> and <code>toPx()</code> instead of numbers like <code>360f</code>?","options":["Phones have different pixels per dp, so fixed pixels look bigger or smaller","Fixed numbers don’t compile in a Canvas","Canvas ignores fixed numbers"],"a":0,"why":"The same 120 dp can be 240 pixels on one phone and 360 on another."}]},{"id":"locals","title":"CompositionLocal","tagline":"Hand a value to a whole part of the screen without passing it through every composable.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Read a value with <code>.current</code>","Make your own with <code>compositionLocalOf</code> and give it with <code>CompositionLocalProvider</code>","Decide when a plain parameter is the better choice"],"remember":"compositionLocalOf { default } makes it, CompositionLocalProvider(Local provides value) { } gives it, Local.current reads it.","terms":[{"term":"CompositionLocal","def":"A value that a whole part of the tree can read without parameters, like the theme."},{"term":"compositionLocalOf","def":"Makes a new CompositionLocal. Its lambda gives the default value."},{"term":"CompositionLocalProvider","def":"Gives a CompositionLocal a value for everything inside its braces: <code>CompositionLocalProvider(LocalUnits provides Units.Oz) { }</code>."},{"term":"current","def":"<code>LocalUnits.current</code>: the value from the nearest provider above, or the default if there is none."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>LocalUnits.current</code> give back?","options":["The value from the nearest <code>CompositionLocalProvider</code> above","Always the default","The value of the newest provider anywhere in the app"],"a":0,"why":"It looks up the tree and takes the closest provided value."},{"q":"A Preview shows <code>Amount(250)</code> with no provider around it. What does it show?","options":["“250 ml”: the default from <code>compositionLocalOf</code>","It crashes","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"With no provider above, <code>.current</code> gives the default from the lambda."},{"q":"Which is the best fit for a CompositionLocal?","options":["The units that every amount on the screen shows","The drink one row should delete","A screen’s ViewModel"],"a":0,"why":"Units are used all over that part of the screen and have a good default. The others belong in parameters."}]},{"id":"animation","title":"Animation","tagline":"Let values glide to their new value, and let things fade in and out.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Animate a number with <code>animateFloatAsState</code>","Show and hide content smoothly with <code>AnimatedVisibility</code>","Read an animated value in the drawing phase"],"remember":"animateFloatAsState(target) glides to each new target; AnimatedVisibility(visible) fades content in and out.","terms":[{"term":"animateFloatAsState","def":"Gives a <code>Float</code> state that moves step by step, one frame at a time, to each new <code>targetValue</code>."},{"term":"AnimatedVisibility","def":"Shows its content when <code>visible</code> is <code>true</code>, and animates it in and out."},{"term":"fadeIn / fadeOut","def":"Enter and exit animations for <code>AnimatedVisibility</code>: the content fades in or out."},{"term":"toFloat","def":"Turns a number into a <code>Float</code>, like <code>toDouble()</code> does for <code>Double</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>animateFloatAsState</code> give you?","options":["A state that moves frame by frame from its old value to the target","The target, right away","A list of every value in between"],"a":0,"why":"You read it like any state. Its value changes a little on each frame."},{"q":"<code>level</code> is read only inside the Canvas lambda. While it animates, what runs on each frame?","options":["Only drawing","All three phases","Only composition"],"a":0,"why":"A read in the drawing phase reruns only drawing."},{"q":"<code>showTop</code> turns <code>false</code>. With <code>AnimatedVisibility(visible = showTop, exit = fadeOut())</code>, what happens to the button?","options":["It fades out, then leaves the composition","It disappears at once","It stays on screen, faded"],"a":0,"why":"The content stays while the exit animation runs, and is removed when it ends."}]},{"id":"shared-elements","title":"Shared elements","tagline":"Let a picture fly from one screen into the next, so the user sees where they went.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Wrap a <code>NavHost</code> in <code>SharedTransitionLayout</code>","Mark the same element on two screens with <code>sharedElement</code> and one key","Choose a key that is unique per item"],"remember":"SharedTransitionLayout around the NavHost; sharedElement with the same unique key on both screens.","terms":[{"term":"shared element transition","def":"An animation in which an element moves and changes size from its place on one screen to its place on the next."},{"term":"SharedTransitionLayout","def":"Goes around the screens that share elements. Inside it, <code>this</code> is a <code>SharedTransitionScope</code>."},{"term":"AnimatedVisibilityScope","def":"Tracks whether a screen is appearing or leaving. In a NavHost, it is <code>this@composable</code>."},{"term":"with","def":"<code>with(x) { }</code> runs the braces with <code>x</code> as <code>this</code>, like <code>x.run { }</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What links the small glass on History to the big one on the detail screen?","options":["The same key in <code>rememberSharedContentState</code>","The same size","The same composable"],"a":0,"why":"Compose matches shared elements by key, nothing else."},{"q":"Where does <code>SharedTransitionLayout</code> go?","options":["Around the NavHost, so both screens are inside it","Inside each screen","Around each glass"],"a":0,"why":"It has to contain both screens to move an element between them."},{"q":"Inside <code>composable&lt;History&gt; { }</code>, what is <code>this@composable</code>?","options":["The screen’s <code>AnimatedVisibilityScope</code>: whether it is appearing or leaving","The NavController","The <code>SharedTransitionScope</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The NavHost animates each screen in and out and hands you that scope as <code>this</code>."}]},{"id":"layout","title":"Custom layouts","tagline":"Measure the children yourself, then place each one where you want it.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Measure children inside <code>Layout</code> with <code>measure</code> and the constraints","Set the size and place children with <code>layout</code> and <code>placeRelative</code>","Build a row that wraps onto the next line"],"remember":"Layout { measurables, constraints -> }: measure each child once, then layout(width, height) { placeRelative(x, y) }.","terms":[{"term":"Layout","def":"The composable behind every layout. Its lambda gets the children (<code>measurables</code>) and the parent’s <code>constraints</code>."},{"term":"constraints","def":"The smallest and largest width and height a layout may use, from its parent, like <code>constraints.maxWidth</code>."},{"term":"measure","def":"<code>measurable.measure(constraints)</code> measures a child once and gives back a <code>Placeable</code> with a <code>width</code> and <code>height</code>."},{"term":"placeRelative","def":"Places a measured child at x and y pixels, mirrored for right-to-left languages. It goes inside <code>layout(width, height) { }</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>measure()</code> give back?","options":["A <code>Placeable</code> that knows its width and height","The child, already on the screen","The parent’s constraints"],"a":0,"why":"You measure first and get a <code>Placeable</code>. It appears on screen only when you place it."},{"q":"How many times may a layout measure one child?","options":["Once","As often as it likes","Twice: once small, once big"],"a":0,"why":"Compose allows one measurement per child. That keeps layout fast."},{"q":"<code>maxWidth</code> is 300. Chips 120 and 150 wide are on the line, so <code>x</code> is 270. A chip 40 wide comes next. Where does it go?","options":["At the start of a new line","On the same line, at x = 270","At x = 300"],"a":0,"why":"270 + 40 = 310, more than 300, so it doesn’t fit."}]},{"id":"navigation","title":"Navigation in depth","tagline":"Group screens into a nested graph, and clear the whole group from the back stack.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Group related screens into a nested graph with <code>navigation</code>","Remove screens from the back stack while navigating, with <code>popUpTo</code>","Keep route data small: an id, not a whole object"],"remember":"navigation<Group>(startDestination = First) { } groups screens; navigate(next) { popUpTo(route = Group) { inclusive = true } } clears them.","terms":[{"term":"nested graph","def":"A group of screens under one route, made with <code>navigation&lt;Route&gt;(startDestination = ...) { }</code> inside a NavHost."},{"term":"popUpTo","def":"An option of <code>navigate</code>: before adding the new screen, remove screens from the back stack down to the given route."},{"term":"inclusive","def":"In <code>popUpTo</code>: <code>true</code> removes the given route itself too, not only the screens above it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The back stack holds Welcome and PickGoal, inside Onboarding. You call <code>navigate(Today) { popUpTo(route = Onboarding) { inclusive = true } }</code>. What is on the stack now?","options":["Today","Welcome, Today","Welcome, PickGoal, Today"],"a":0,"why":"Everything down to and including Onboarding is removed, then Today is added."},{"q":"What does <code>navigate(Onboarding)</code> open?","options":["The group’s start screen, Welcome","Every screen of the group at once","The last screen of the group"],"a":0,"why":"A nested graph’s route opens its <code>startDestination</code>."},{"q":"Which route is the better choice?","options":["<code>DayDetail(val date: String)</code>","<code>DayDetail(val day: Day)</code>, with the whole day","Neither: routes can’t carry data"],"a":0,"why":"A small value is saved easily, and the screen loads fresh data with it."}]},{"id":"nav3","title":"Navigation 3","tagline":"The back stack is a list you own: add to go forward, remove to go back.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Hold the back stack yourself with <code>rememberNavBackStack</code>","Show it with <code>NavDisplay</code> and <code>entryProvider</code>","Navigate by adding to the list and removing from it"],"remember":"Navigation 3: the back stack is your list. add() goes forward, removeLastOrNull() goes back, NavDisplay shows the last key.","terms":[{"term":"NavKey","def":"An interface with nothing to implement that marks a Navigation 3 key. With <code>@Serializable</code>, the key can be saved."},{"term":"rememberNavBackStack","def":"Makes the back stack: a list of keys that is state and survives rotation and the app being stopped."},{"term":"NavDisplay","def":"Shows the screen for the last key in the back stack, and runs <code>onBack</code> on the Back gesture."},{"term":"entryProvider","def":"Turns each key into its screen. Inside it, <code>entry&lt;Key&gt; { key -> }</code> says what to show for that key type."}],"quiz":[{"q":"How do you open History in Navigation 3?","options":["<code>backStack.add(History(goal = 8))</code>","<code>navController.navigate(History(goal = 8))</code>","<code>NavDisplay(History(goal = 8))</code>"],"a":0,"why":"There is no controller. You change the list, and <code>NavDisplay</code> follows."},{"q":"Why must keys for <code>rememberNavBackStack</code> be <code>@Serializable</code> and implement <code>NavKey</code>?","options":["So the list can be saved and restored after rotation or the app being stopped","So they can be compared with <code>==</code>","So <code>NavDisplay</code> can draw them"],"a":0,"why":"Saving the back stack means storing every key in it."},{"q":"In <code>entry&lt;History&gt; { key -> }</code>, how do you read the goal?","options":["<code>key.goal</code>","<code>key.toRoute()</code>","<code>backStack.goal</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The entry hands you the key itself, with its data."}]}]},{"id":"a11y","title":"Accessibility and adaptive UI","description":"Make the water tracker work for everyone: with TalkBack, a keyboard, large text, small and big windows, foldables, and edge to edge.","requires":["compose"],"file":"tracks/a11y.js","lessons":[{"id":"why","title":"Why accessibility","tagline":"Some people hear your app, or use it with one switch or a keyboard. Make it work for them too.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name the ways people use Android without the usual touch and sight","Move through a screen the way TalkBack does","Give every button words a screen reader can say"],"remember":"TalkBack reads one element at a time. Everything that does something needs words; pure decoration gets null.","terms":[{"term":"accessibility","def":"Making an app work for everyone, including people who use a screen reader, a switch, a keyboard or large text. Often written a11y."},{"term":"TalkBack","def":"Android’s screen reader. Swipe right or left to move between elements; it reads each one aloud. Double-tap anywhere to press the one in focus."},{"term":"Switch Access","def":"A way to use Android with one or two switches (big buttons) instead of the touch screen. It steps through everything that can be pressed."},{"term":"Accessibility Scanner","def":"A free app from Google that checks the screen in front of you and suggests fixes for missing labels, small targets and low contrast."}],"quiz":[{"q":"TalkBack is on and focus is on the add button. How does Sam press it?","options":["Double-tap anywhere on the screen","Tap the button once","Swipe right"],"a":0,"why":"With TalkBack on, a touch only reads what is under the finger. A double-tap presses the element in focus."},{"q":"Who does accessibility work help?","options":["Only people who can’t see","People who use TalkBack, Switch Access, large fonts or a keyboard, and many others","Only people with old phones"],"a":1,"why":"People use Android in many ways. Clear labels, big targets and text that can grow help almost everyone."},{"q":"TalkBack reaches <code>Text(\"3 of 8 glasses\")</code>. What does it say?","options":["“3 of 8 glasses”","“Text”","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"A Text brings its own words, so it needs no description."}]},{"id":"semantics","title":"Semantics","tagline":"Tell TalkBack what each element is, what it does, and what belongs together.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain where TalkBack gets its words from","Mark a title as a heading and read a group as one stop","Say what a tap does with <code>onClickLabel</code> and <code>role</code>"],"remember":"Compose describes the screen in its semantics. heading() marks titles, mergeDescendants makes one stop, onClickLabel and role say what a tap does.","terms":[{"term":"semantics","def":"The description of the screen that Compose keeps next to what it draws: each element’s words, what kind of thing it is and what it does. TalkBack and UI tests read it."},{"term":"heading","def":"<code>Modifier.semantics { heading() }</code> marks a title, so TalkBack says “Heading” and users can jump between headings."},{"term":"mergeDescendants","def":"With <code>Modifier.semantics(mergeDescendants = true) {}</code>, an element and everything inside it become one TalkBack stop."},{"term":"Role","def":"What kind of control an element is, such as <code>Role.Button</code> or <code>Role.Checkbox</code>. TalkBack says it after the label."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does TalkBack get its words from?","options":["From the semantics Compose keeps for the screen","From the pixels on the screen","From your Kotlin source code"],"a":0,"why":"Compose builds a description of every element next to what it draws. TalkBack reads that description."},{"q":"What does <code>onClickLabel = \"add a glass\"</code> change?","options":["The hint: “Double tap to add a glass”","The text drawn on the screen","What happens on a tap"],"a":0,"why":"It only changes what TalkBack says. The tap still runs the lambda you gave <code>clickable</code>."},{"q":"What does <code>Modifier.semantics { heading() }</code> let TalkBack users do?","options":["Jump from heading to heading","Make the text bigger","Skip the element"],"a":0,"why":"Headings split a screen into sections, and TalkBack can move between them, the way you skim titles with your eyes."}]},{"id":"custom-actions","title":"Custom actions","tagline":"Offer every gesture as a named action that TalkBack can list.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why drags and swipes don’t reach your app with TalkBack on","Add a named action with <code>customActions</code>","Hide a duplicate button from TalkBack with <code>clearAndSetSemantics</code>"],"remember":"Every gesture needs a second way in: a CustomAccessibilityAction with a clear name, listed in TalkBack’s menu.","terms":[{"term":"customActions","def":"A semantics property with a list of extra actions an element offers. TalkBack shows them under Actions in its menu."},{"term":"CustomAccessibilityAction","def":"One extra action: <code>CustomAccessibilityAction(\"Delete\") { ... }</code>, a name and a lambda that returns <code>true</code> when it worked."},{"term":"clearAndSetSemantics","def":"A modifier that removes an element’s semantics and its children’s, keeping only what you set in its braces. With empty braces, TalkBack doesn’t see it at all."},{"term":"TalkBack menu","def":"A menu TalkBack opens with a three-finger tap, or a swipe down then right. Its Actions entry lists what the focused element can do."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does dragging a row do nothing with TalkBack on?","options":["TalkBack uses one-finger swipes to move its focus","TalkBack switches off all gestures in every app","The row is too small"],"a":0,"why":"TalkBack keeps those gestures for itself. A custom action gives Sam another way to the same result."},{"q":"Which name is best for a custom action?","options":["“Delete”","“Swipe left”","“Action 1”"],"a":0,"why":"The user picks a result from a list. Name the result."},{"q":"What does <code>Modifier.clearAndSetSemantics { }</code> do to a button?","options":["TalkBack no longer sees it; it still works for touch","It hides the button from the screen","It turns the button off"],"a":0,"why":"It only removes the description TalkBack reads. Drawing and taps are unchanged."}]},{"id":"targets","title":"Touch targets","tagline":"At least 48 by 48 dp for every finger, whatever size the icon is.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain the 48 dp rule for touch targets","Use <code>IconButton</code> for icons people tap","Give your own small clickable room with <code>minimumInteractiveComponentSize</code>"],"remember":"Every target at least 48 by 48 dp: IconButton for icons, minimumInteractiveComponentSize() for your own small clickables.","terms":[{"term":"touch target","def":"The area that reacts to a finger. It should be at least 48 by 48 dp, about 9 mm, even around a smaller icon."},{"term":"IconButton","def":"A button that shows one <code>Icon</code> and keeps 48 by 48 dp around it. TalkBack reads the icon’s <code>contentDescription</code> as its label."},{"term":"minimumInteractiveComponentSize","def":"A modifier that makes an element at least 48 dp wide and tall in the layout, without changing what it draws."}],"quiz":[{"q":"How big should a touch target be at least?","options":["48 by 48 dp","24 by 24 dp","12 by 12 dp"],"a":0,"why":"48 dp is about 9 mm, roughly a fingertip."},{"q":"An <code>IconButton</code> holds a 24 dp icon. How big is the icon drawn?","options":["24 dp, with 48 dp of room around it to touch","48 dp","12 dp"],"a":0,"why":"The icon keeps its size. The extra room is for the finger."},{"q":"Where does <code>minimumInteractiveComponentSize()</code> go in the chain?","options":["Before any <code>size(...)</code> on the element","At the very end","It doesn’t matter"],"a":0,"why":"Modifiers apply in order. A size that comes first would already limit the element, and the minimum couldn’t take effect."}]},{"id":"keyboard-nav","title":"Keyboard navigation","tagline":"Tab to move, Enter to press: make the screen work without touching it.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Move through a screen with Tab, the arrow keys and Enter","Tell which elements can take keyboard focus","Put focus where the user needs it with <code>FocusRequester</code>"],"remember":"Tab follows the screen order, arrows go to the nearest element, Enter presses. A FocusRequester puts focus where the user needs it.","terms":[{"term":"focus","def":"The one element that keys act on. Tab and the arrow keys move it; Enter presses the element that has it."},{"term":"D-pad","def":"Direction pad: the up, down, left and right buttons on a TV remote or game controller. It moves focus like the arrow keys."},{"term":"FocusRequester","def":"A handle attached to an element with <code>Modifier.focusRequester(...)</code>. Its <code>requestFocus()</code> moves the focus to that element."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Focus is on a button. Lin presses Enter. What happens?","options":["The button is pressed, as if tapped","Focus moves to the next element","Nothing: Enter only works in text fields"],"a":0,"why":"Enter presses the element in focus. Tab is the key that moves."},{"q":"Which of these can take keyboard focus?","options":["A <code>Button</code>","A plain <code>Text</code>","A decorative <code>Image</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Elements you can press or type into take focus. Text and pictures that do nothing are skipped."},{"q":"Focus is on the bottom button and nothing is below it. Lin presses the down arrow. What happens?","options":["Focus stays where it is","Focus jumps to the top","The app closes"],"a":0,"why":"The arrow keys look for the closest element in that direction. When there is none, focus doesn’t move."}]},{"id":"text-scale","title":"Large text","tagline":"Text in sp grows with the user’s font size. Leave it room to grow.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what the font size setting changes, up to 200%","Let rows grow with their text using <code>heightIn</code>","Check a screen at 200% with <code>@Preview(fontScale = 2f)</code>"],"remember":"Text in sp, everything else in dp. Give text minimum heights, never fixed ones, and check at 200%.","terms":[{"term":"font size setting","def":"The text size a user picks in Settings, up to 200% since Android 14. It changes every size given in <code>sp</code>."},{"term":"nonlinear font scaling","def":"Since Android 14, small text grows almost by the full setting, while large text grows less."},{"term":"heightIn","def":"<code>Modifier.heightIn(min = 56.dp)</code> sets a smallest height and lets the element grow taller when its content needs it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The user picks a larger font size. What grows?","options":["Sizes in sp: the text","Sizes in dp: spaces and pictures","Both"],"a":0,"why":"The setting is for text. Spaces, icons and pictures in dp stay the same."},{"q":"At 200%, which grows more compared to its normal size?","options":["14 sp body text","A 30 sp headline","Both double"],"a":0,"why":"Since Android 14 the growth is nonlinear: small text nearly doubles, big text grows less."},{"q":"A row has <code>heightIn(min = 56.dp)</code>. Its text needs 80 dp at 200%. How tall is the row?","options":["80 dp","56 dp","112 dp"],"a":0,"why":"The minimum is 56 dp. When the content needs more, the row grows to fit it."}]},{"id":"window-size","title":"Window sizes","tagline":"Pick the layout from the width of the app’s window, not from the kind of device.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Name the window size classes and their widths","Read the current size class with <code>currentWindowAdaptiveInfoV2</code>","Show one screen or two side by side, depending on the width"],"remember":"Compact under 600 dp, Medium to 839, Expanded from 840. Size classes describe the window, not the device.","terms":[{"term":"window size class","def":"A group of window widths (or heights) used to pick a layout: Compact, Medium, Expanded, and Large and Extra-large for very wide windows."},{"term":"breakpoint","def":"The width where one size class ends and the next begins, such as 600 dp or 840 dp."},{"term":"currentWindowAdaptiveInfoV2","def":"A Compose function from <code>material3-adaptive</code>. Its <code>windowSizeClass</code> describes the app’s window right now, and changes when the window does."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A window is 700 dp wide. Which width class is it?","options":["Medium","Compact","Expanded"],"a":0,"why":"Medium covers 600 to 839 dp."},{"q":"A tablet runs the app in split screen, 400 dp wide. Which layout fits?","options":["The Compact one, like on a phone","The tablet one, since it is a tablet"],"a":0,"why":"Size classes describe the window. 400 dp is Compact on any device."},{"q":"What happens to <code>windowSizeClass</code> when the user resizes the window?","options":["It changes, and the screen is drawn with the new layout","It keeps the value from when the app started","The app must be restarted"],"a":0,"why":"The value follows the window, so <code>if</code> picks the other layout on its own."}]},{"id":"foldables","title":"Foldables","tagline":"A screen that bends: read the posture and keep the fold out of the way.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Name the postures of a foldable: flat, tabletop and book","Split the screen at the fold with <code>windowPosture.isTabletop</code> and <code>weight</code>","Keep state when the phone folds and unfolds"],"remember":"Read the posture: in tabletop, content up, controls down. Every fold recreates the Activity, so keep state saveable.","terms":[{"term":"posture","def":"How a foldable is held: flat (fully open), tabletop (half open, fold from side to side) or book (half open, fold from top to bottom)."},{"term":"tabletop posture","def":"A foldable half open with the fold running side to side: the bottom half lies flat, the top half stands up."},{"term":"windowPosture","def":"Part of <code>currentWindowAdaptiveInfoV2()</code> that describes the fold. Its <code>isTabletop</code> is <code>true</code> in tabletop posture."},{"term":"weight","def":"In a Column (or Row), <code>Modifier.weight(1f)</code> gives a child a share of the free space. Two children with <code>1f</code> each get half."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A foldable is half open, with the fold running top to bottom like a book. Which posture is it?","options":["Book","Tabletop","Flat"],"a":0,"why":"Tabletop has the fold from side to side. Top to bottom is book posture."},{"q":"What happens to the Activity when the user unfolds the phone?","options":["It is recreated: a configuration change","Nothing","The app closes"],"a":0,"why":"The window’s size changes, and that recreates the Activity, as rotation does."},{"q":"A Column with <code>fillMaxSize()</code> has two children, each with <code>Modifier.weight(1f)</code>. How tall is each?","options":["Half the Column","As tall as its content","The whole Column"],"a":0,"why":"Equal weights share the space equally."}]},{"id":"resizability","title":"Resizable apps","tagline":"On big screens, Android ignores orientation locks. Build for any window.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what Android 16 does with orientation and resize locks on large screens","Remove locks from the manifest and keep state through resizing","Keep content readable on wide windows with <code>widthIn</code>"],"remember":"No orientation or resize locks: on large screens Android ignores them. Layouts that fit any window, state that survives resizing.","terms":[{"term":"smallest width","def":"The shorter side of the screen, in dp. Android counts 600 dp or more (written sw600dp) as a large screen: tablets and open foldables."},{"term":"split screen","def":"Two apps on screen at once. The user drags the divider between them, so your window can change size while the app runs."},{"term":"pillarboxing","def":"Showing a narrow app in the middle of a wide screen with empty bars on both sides. Android 16 stops doing it on large screens for apps that target API 36."},{"term":"widthIn","def":"<code>Modifier.widthIn(max = 480.dp)</code> lets an element be at most 480 dp wide, and narrower when there is less room."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The app targets API 36 and sets <code>screenOrientation=\"portrait\"</code>. What happens on an Android 16 tablet held sideways?","options":["The lock is ignored, and the app fills the screen","The app shows in an upright strip with bars","The app crashes"],"a":0,"why":"On screens with a smallest width of 600 dp or more, Android 16 ignores orientation locks for apps that target API 36."},{"q":"Which app still keeps its orientation lock on a large screen?","options":["A game","An app with <code>resizeableActivity=\"false\"</code>","Every app that targets API 37"],"a":0,"why":"Games are left alone. <code>resizeableActivity</code> is one of the settings that are ignored."},{"q":"The user drags the split screen divider. What happens to the Activity by default?","options":["It is recreated: a configuration change","Nothing at all","It moves to the background"],"a":0,"why":"The window changes size, which recreates the Activity. State in rememberSaveable or a ViewModel survives it."}]},{"id":"insets","title":"Edge to edge","tagline":"The bars and the keyboard cover parts of your window. Keep content out from under them.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Name the insets: status bar, navigation bar, cutout and keyboard","Keep a field above the keyboard with <code>imePadding</code>","Combine Scaffold’s <code>innerPadding</code> with the keyboard using <code>consumeWindowInsets</code>"],"remember":"safeDrawingPadding() covers bars and keyboard. In a Scaffold: padding(innerPadding), consumeWindowInsets(innerPadding), then imePadding().","terms":[{"term":"insets","def":"The parts of the window the system covers: status bar, navigation bar, camera cutout and, while open, the keyboard."},{"term":"IME","def":"Input method editor: Android’s word for a keyboard on the screen."},{"term":"imePadding","def":"<code>Modifier.imePadding()</code> adds space at the bottom as tall as the keyboard, and follows it as it opens and closes."},{"term":"consumeWindowInsets","def":"<code>Modifier.consumeWindowInsets(innerPadding)</code> tells the insets modifiers inside it which space is covered already, so they don’t add it twice."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these keeps content clear of the keyboard too?","options":["<code>safeDrawingPadding()</code>","Scaffold’s <code>innerPadding</code>","<code>padding(16.dp)</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The safe drawing area includes the bars, the cutout and the keyboard. innerPadding covers the bars only."},{"q":"The keyboard closes. What does <code>imePadding()</code> do?","options":["Its space goes away with the keyboard","It keeps the space, in case the keyboard returns","It crashes"],"a":0,"why":"It follows the keyboard: as tall as what the keyboard covers, which is nothing once it is closed."},{"q":"Your app targets Android 16 and runs on Android 16. Can it turn edge to edge off?","options":["No: from Android 16 the opt-out is gone","Yes, with a setting in the theme","Only on tablets"],"a":0,"why":"Android 15 made edge to edge the default; for apps that target Android 16, the way to opt out no longer works."}]}]},{"id":"testing","title":"Testing","description":"Check the Recipes app with code: unit tests with JUnit, fakes and MockK, coroutines and flows, Robolectric, Compose UI and screenshot tests, Hilt in tests, and measuring startup with baseline profiles.","requires":["arch"],"file":"tracks/testing.js","lessons":[{"id":"why-test","title":"Why we test","tagline":"Let code check the app after every change, so you can change it without fear.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain how a regression slips past checking by hand","Say what an automated test is and when it runs","Pick which rules of an app deserve a test first"],"remember":"A test is code that checks a rule with an input you chose. Run the tests after every change, and a regression shows up in seconds, not weeks.","terms":[{"term":"manual testing","def":"Checking an app by hand: opening it and looking at each screen. Slow, and easy to skip a step."},{"term":"automated test","def":"Code that runs your code with a chosen input and compares the answer with the expected value, the same way every time."},{"term":"refactor","def":"Changing how code is written without changing what it does, like moving the Quick rule into <code>quickRecipes</code>."},{"term":"edge case","def":"An input at the border of a rule, like a recipe of exactly 20 minutes. Bugs like to hide there."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is a regression?","options":["A bug in something that used to work","A test that fails","An app that got slower"],"a":0,"why":"Lemon pasta used to show on Tonight. After Sam’s change it didn’t: the app went back, “regressed”."},{"q":"Why do automated tests catch what checking by hand misses?","options":["They run every check, the same way, in seconds, after every change","They find bugs nobody wrote a check for","They make the app run faster"],"a":0,"why":"A test checks only what it was written to check, but it never gets tired or skips a step."},{"q":"Which rule of the Recipes app deserves a test first?","options":["The Quick filter: 20 minutes still counts as quick","The colour of the Refresh button","The order of the import lines"],"a":0,"why":"Start with the rules users depend on, the ones that would hurt most if they broke quietly."}]},{"id":"unit","title":"Unit tests","tagline":"Write real tests with JUnit, and let Android Studio or Gradle run them all.","level":"beginner","goals":["Write a test class with <code>@Test</code> functions in <code>src/test</code>","Check results with <code>assertEquals</code> and <code>assertTrue</code>","Run the tests and read a failure"],"remember":"Tests go in src/test; each @Test function is one test; assertEquals(expected, actual) fails when they differ; run them with ./gradlew test.","terms":[{"term":"unit test","def":"A test of one small piece of code on its own, like one function or one class."},{"term":"JUnit","def":"The test library Android projects use. It runs every function marked <code>@Test</code> and reports which ones failed."},{"term":"assertion","def":"A line that checks a result and fails the test when it is wrong, like <code>assertEquals(expected, actual)</code>."},{"term":"arrange, act, assert","def":"The three steps of a test: make the input, call the code, check the result."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does <code>QuickRecipesTest.kt</code> go?","options":["<code>app/src/test/java/com/example/recipes/</code>","<code>app/src/main/java/com/example/recipes/</code>","<code>app/src/main/res/</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The <code>test</code> folder holds tests that run on your computer. Nothing in it goes into the app."},{"q":"<code>assertEquals(listOf(soup, pasta), result)</code> fails with <code>expected:&lt;[soup, pasta]&gt; but was:&lt;[soup]&gt;</code> (shortened). What did <code>quickRecipes</code> return?","options":["Only the soup","Soup and pasta","An empty list"],"a":0,"why":"“but was” is the actual value, the one the code gave back."},{"q":"What does <code>testImplementation(\"junit:junit:4.13.2\")</code> do?","options":["Adds JUnit for the test code only; it is not in the app people install","Adds JUnit to the app","Runs the tests when the app starts"],"a":0,"why":"It works like <code>implementation</code>, but only for <code>src/test</code>."}]},{"id":"pyramid","title":"The testing pyramid","tagline":"Many small, fast tests at the base; a few big, slow ones on top.","level":"beginner","goals":["Tell unit, integration and UI tests apart by how much of the app they use","Say where each kind runs: on your computer or on a phone","Choose a sensible mix of tests for the Recipes app"],"remember":"Many fast unit tests in src/test, fewer integration tests, a few UI tests in src/androidTest for the paths that must never break.","terms":[{"term":"integration test","def":"A test of several real parts working together, like the DAO with a real Room database."},{"term":"instrumented test","def":"A test in <code>src/androidTest</code> that runs on a phone or emulator, next to the app, so it can use Android."},{"term":"flaky test","def":"A test that sometimes passes and sometimes fails with the same code, often because of timing."},{"term":"testing pyramid","def":"The mix to aim for: many small, fast tests at the base and a few big, slow ones on top."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which test is likely the fastest?","options":["<code>QuickRecipesTest</code>","<code>RecipeDaoTest</code>","<code>RecipesScreenTest</code>"],"a":0,"why":"It runs one function on your computer: no install, no database, no screen."},{"q":"A test fails, you run it again with no change, and it passes. What is it?","options":["A flaky test","A unit test","A passing test"],"a":0,"why":"Same code, different results: usually timing. Flaky tests teach people to ignore red, so fix them."},{"q":"Why keep most tests at the base of the pyramid?","options":["They are fast and point at the exact piece that broke","UI tests can’t find bugs","Gradle can only run unit tests"],"a":0,"why":"UI tests are worth having for the main paths, but they are slow and say less about where the problem is."}]},{"id":"fakes","title":"Test doubles","tagline":"Stand in for the database and the server, so a test is fast and gives the same answer every time.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why a test replaces Room and the server with a stand-in","Write a fake that implements <code>RecipeRepository</code> with a list in memory","Tell a fake, a stub and a mock apart"],"remember":"A fake is a light, working version of a dependency, kept in src/test. It works because the code under test asks for an interface.","terms":[{"term":"test double","def":"Anything that stands in for a real dependency during a test."},{"term":"fake","def":"A test double with a working, simpler implementation, like a repository that keeps its recipes in memory."},{"term":"stub","def":"A test double that only gives back fixed answers."},{"term":"mock","def":"A test double made by a library: you set its answers, and afterwards check which calls it received."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can <code>GetQuickRecipesUseCase</code> take a <code>FakeRecipeRepository</code>?","options":["It asks for the interface <code>RecipeRepository</code>, and the fake implements it","Hilt swaps the classes in tests","The fake extends <code>OfflineRecipeRepository</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Code that depends on an interface works with any class that implements it."},{"q":"Which of these is a fake?","options":["A class with a working, in-memory version of the repository","An object from a library whose calls you check afterwards","A value passed in but never used"],"a":0,"why":"A fake really works, just in a simpler way. The second one is a mock."},{"q":"Why does the test body run inside <code>runBlocking</code>?","options":["<code>first()</code> is a suspend function, and a test function is not","So the test runs on the main thread","It makes the test faster"],"a":0,"why":"A suspend function can only be called from a coroutine. <code>runBlocking</code> starts one and waits for it."}]},{"id":"mockk","title":"MockK","tagline":"Make a stand-in in one line, tell it what to answer, then check what it was asked.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Create a mock with <code>mockk</code> and give it answers with <code>every</code>","Check that a call happened with <code>coVerify</code>","Choose between a fake and a mock"],"remember":"mockk<T>() makes a mock; every / coEvery give it answers; verify / coVerify check the calls. Prefer a fake for your own small interfaces.","terms":[{"term":"MockK","def":"A Kotlin library that makes mocks: <code>mockk</code> creates one, <code>every</code> sets answers, <code>verify</code> checks calls."},{"term":"relaxed mock","def":"A mock made with <code>relaxed = true</code>: it answers every call with a simple default instead of throwing."},{"term":"verify","def":"Checking afterwards that a mock received a call, with <code>verify { }</code>, or <code>coVerify { }</code> for a suspend function."},{"term":"over-specified test","def":"A test that checks how the code works, call by call, instead of what it gives, so it breaks with every refactor."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A test uses <code>mockk&lt;RecipeDao&gt;()</code> with no <code>every</code>, and the code calls <code>dao.getAll()</code>. What happens?","options":["It throws a <code>MockKException</code>: no answer found","It returns an empty list","It returns <code>null</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Mocks are strict unless you make them relaxed. Every call needs an answer."},{"q":"Which line checks that the suspend function <code>dao.update</code> was called once?","options":["<code>coVerify(exactly = 1) { dao.update(row) }</code>","<code>verify { dao.update(row) }</code>","<code>coEvery { dao.update(row) }</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>co</code> is for suspend functions, <code>verify</code> checks, and <code>exactly</code> counts."},{"q":"<code>RecipeRepository</code> has three functions and is used by many tests. Which stand-in fits best?","options":["A fake, written once and reused","A new mock with <code>every</code> lines in each test","The real <code>OfflineRecipeRepository</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A small interface you own is quick to fake, and the fake reads well in every test."}]},{"id":"coroutines","title":"Testing coroutines","tagline":"Test a ViewModel with runTest, a test Main dispatcher, and a clock that skips delays.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Replace the Main dispatcher in tests with <code>Dispatchers.setMain</code>","Write a test with <code>runTest</code> and run queued coroutines with <code>advanceUntilIdle</code>","Explain why <code>delay</code> takes no time inside <code>runTest</code>"],"remember":"setMain(StandardTestDispatcher()) in @Before, resetMain() in @After, the body in runTest; advanceUntilIdle() runs what was queued, and delay costs no real time.","terms":[{"term":"runTest","def":"Runs a test’s body as a coroutine, with a test dispatcher and virtual time, so <code>delay</code> returns at once."},{"term":"test dispatcher","def":"A dispatcher for tests, like <code>StandardTestDispatcher</code>: it queues coroutines until the test runs them, for example with <code>advanceUntilIdle()</code>."},{"term":"virtual time","def":"The clock of <code>runTest</code>. <code>delay</code> moves it forward without waiting; <code>currentTime</code> reads it."},{"term":"@Before / @After","def":"Mark functions JUnit runs before and after every test of the class, for setting up and cleaning up."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does building <code>RecipesViewModel</code> in a plain local test crash?","options":["<code>viewModelScope</code> runs on <code>Dispatchers.Main</code>, and there is no Android main thread on your computer","The fake has no recipes","<code>@Test</code> functions can’t create classes"],"a":0,"why":"The message says it too: for tests, use <code>Dispatchers.setMain</code> from kotlinx-coroutines-test."},{"q":"With a <code>StandardTestDispatcher</code>, when does a coroutine started with <code>launch</code> run?","options":["When the test lets it, for example with <code>advanceUntilIdle()</code>","At once, before <code>launch</code> returns","Never, in a test"],"a":0,"why":"It waits in a queue. That gives the test control over the order things happen in."},{"q":"What happens to <code>delay(5000)</code> inside <code>runTest</code>?","options":["It returns at once, and the virtual clock moves 5 seconds","The test waits 5 real seconds","It throws an exception"],"a":0,"why":"<code>runTest</code> skips delays on its own clock, so tests of retries and timeouts stay fast."}]},{"id":"flows","title":"Testing flows","tagline":"Read every value a flow sends, one at a time, with Turbine.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Collect a flow in a test with Turbine’s <code>test { }</code>","Check each value in order with <code>awaitItem()</code>","Explain why a value left unread fails the test"],"remember":"flow.test { } puts every value in a queue; awaitItem() takes the next one; a value left unread fails the test.","terms":[{"term":"Turbine","def":"A small testing library that collects a Flow into a queue, so a test can check each value it sends, in order."},{"term":"test { }","def":"Turbine’s function on a Flow: <code>flow.test { }</code> collects the flow while the block runs, and fails if a value is left unread at the end."},{"term":"awaitItem()","def":"Takes the next value from Turbine’s queue, waiting for one if it is empty. Fails after 3 seconds with <code>No value produced in 3s</code>."},{"term":"queue","def":"A waiting line: values come out in the order they went in."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Inside <code>test { }</code>, you call <code>awaitItem()</code> and the flow sends nothing. What happens?","options":["It returns <code>null</code>","After 3 seconds the test fails with <code>No value produced in 3s</code>","It waits forever"],"a":1,"why":"<code>awaitItem()</code> waits, but not forever: after 3 seconds Turbine fails the test."},{"q":"The block ends while one value is still in the queue. What happens?","options":["The test passes","The test fails with <code>Unconsumed events found</code>","Turbine reads it for you"],"a":1,"why":"A value nobody read is a surprise. Read it, or end the block with <code>cancelAndIgnoreRemainingEvents()</code> if the rest doesn’t matter."},{"q":"The fake’s table is a <code>MutableStateFlow</code> holding Tomato soup. What is the first item in the queue after <code>test {</code> starts?","options":["The quick list for the table right now: <code>[Tomato soup]</code>","Nothing, until the table changes","An empty list"],"a":0,"why":"A <code>StateFlow</code> hands its current value to every new collector, so the first list arrives at once."}]},{"id":"robolectric","title":"Robolectric","tagline":"Run tests that need Android on your computer, with no phone.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why a local test can’t call Android code like <code>Uri.parse</code>","Run a test on a simulated Android with <code>@RunWith(RobolectricTestRunner::class)</code>","Read your app’s resources in a local test"],"remember":"Code that needs Android classes but no phone: @RunWith(RobolectricTestRunner::class) in src/test. Android starts once per run.","terms":[{"term":"Robolectric","def":"A library that runs a simulated Android inside local tests, so code that needs Android classes can be tested on the computer."},{"term":"@RunWith","def":"Names who runs the tests in a class. <code>@RunWith(RobolectricTestRunner::class)</code> starts the simulated Android first."},{"term":"RuntimeEnvironment.getApplication()","def":"Gives a Robolectric test the app’s <code>Context</code>, for resources and other Android calls."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a plain local test fail on <code>Uri.parse</code>?","options":["The copy of Android in local tests has every function, but with nothing inside","Uri only works with an internet connection","Kotlin can’t compile Android code on a computer"],"a":0,"why":"The code compiles against that copy, but calling a function throws <code>not mocked</code>. Robolectric fills the copy in."},{"q":"Why is the first Robolectric test in a run slower than the rest?","options":["It starts the simulated Android, once per run","It installs the app on a phone","It downloads Android"],"a":0,"why":"Starting Android takes a few seconds. The tests after it reuse it."},{"q":"Which of these still needs a test on a real phone?","options":["How fast the app really starts","Reading a string resource","Parsing a share link with <code>Uri</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Robolectric simulates Android’s classes, not the phone’s real hardware and speed."}]},{"id":"compose-ui","title":"Compose UI tests","tagline":"Find things on the screen by what they show, tap them, and check the result.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Show a composable in a test with <code>createComposeRule()</code> and <code>setContent</code>","Find a node with <code>onNodeWithText</code> and tap it with <code>performClick()</code>","Check the screen with <code>assertIsDisplayed()</code>"],"remember":"setContent shows it, onNodeWithText finds exactly one node, performClick taps it, assertIsDisplayed checks it.","terms":[{"term":"semantics tree","def":"Compose’s description of the screen: what each part shows and what can be tapped. Tests and screen readers read it."},{"term":"node","def":"One part in the semantics tree, like a Text or a Button. A finder such as <code>onNodeWithText</code> must find exactly one."},{"term":"debugImplementation","def":"Adds a library to the debug build only, inside <code>dependencies { }</code>. The release build, the one users get, leaves it out."},{"term":"rule","def":"A helper JUnit sets up before each test and cleans up after, marked <code>@get:Rule</code>. <code>createComposeRule()</code> gives a place to show composables."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does a Compose UI test read to find a node?","options":["The semantics tree: what each part shows and what can be tapped","The pixels on the screen","Your Kotlin source code"],"a":0,"why":"Compose keeps a description of the screen next to what it draws. Finders search that description."},{"q":"No node shows the text “Pancakes”. What does <code>onNodeWithText(\"Pancakes\").assertIsDisplayed()</code> do?","options":["Fails: the finder found no node","Passes, because nothing is wrong","Waits until Pancakes appears"],"a":0,"why":"A finder must find exactly one node. None fails the test."},{"q":"Why does <code>onNodeWithText(\"Try again\")</code> find something you can tap?","options":["The Button takes in the text inside it, so the node found is the button","Every Text can be tapped in tests","performClick taps whatever is nearest"],"a":0,"why":"In the semantics tree, the Button and its Text are one node, which can be tapped."}]},{"id":"screenshots","title":"Screenshot tests","tagline":"Save a picture of the screen, and fail when it changes.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what a reference image is and why it goes into Git","Draw and save a composable with Roborazzi’s <code>captureRoboImage()</code>","Record and verify with Gradle, and decide what a failure means"],"remember":"Record saves reference images to commit; verify fails when a picture changes. On purpose: record again. Not: fix the code.","terms":[{"term":"screenshot test","def":"A test that draws a screen into a picture and fails when it differs from a saved one."},{"term":"reference image","def":"The saved picture a screenshot test compares against, committed with the code."},{"term":"Roborazzi","def":"A library for screenshot tests on Robolectric: <code>captureRoboImage()</code>, plus the Gradle tasks <code>recordRoborazziDebug</code> and <code>verifyRoborazziDebug</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>./gradlew recordRoborazziDebug</code> do?","options":["Draws every screenshot test and saves the pictures as references","Compares the screen with the references","Takes a picture of your real phone"],"a":0,"why":"Record writes the references. Verify is the one that compares."},{"q":"Verify fails after a change you made on purpose. What next?","options":["Record again and commit the new pictures","Delete the test","Run verify until it passes"],"a":0,"why":"The new picture becomes the reference, and reviewers see the change in the pull request."},{"q":"Why does the class need <code>@GraphicsMode(GraphicsMode.Mode.NATIVE)</code>?","options":["So Robolectric really draws the pixels","So the test runs on a phone","So the images go into Git"],"a":0,"why":"Without it, Robolectric doesn’t draw for real, and there is no true picture to compare."}]},{"id":"hilt-testing","title":"Hilt in tests","tagline":"Open the whole app in a test, with a fake where the real data would be.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Swap a Hilt module for tests with <code>@TestInstallIn</code>","Open <code>MainActivity</code> in a test with <code>@HiltAndroidTest</code> and <code>HiltAndroidRule</code>","Order rules so Hilt is ready before the Activity starts"],"remember":"@TestInstallIn swaps a module in every test; @HiltAndroidTest with HiltAndroidRule at order = 0 opens the real app around the fake.","terms":[{"term":"@TestInstallIn","def":"Marks a test module that replaces an app module in every test of that folder: <code>replaces = [RepositoryModule::class]</code>."},{"term":"@HiltAndroidTest","def":"Marks a test class that uses Hilt, so Hilt builds its components for that test."},{"term":"HiltAndroidRule","def":"The rule that sets Hilt up for each test. It must run before any rule that opens an Activity."},{"term":"HiltTestApplication","def":"Hilt’s own Application for tests, used instead of the app’s <code>@HiltAndroidApp</code> class."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>@TestInstallIn(…, replaces = [RepositoryModule::class])</code> do?","options":["In tests, Hilt uses this module instead of RepositoryModule","It deletes RepositoryModule from the app","It adds a second repository next to the real one"],"a":0,"why":"The app keeps its module. Only the tests in that folder get the replacement."},{"q":"Why is <code>HiltAndroidRule</code> given <code>order = 0</code>?","options":["Hilt must be ready before the Compose rule opens the Activity","Rules with a lower number run faster","Hilt only allows one rule"],"a":0,"why":"The Activity asks Hilt for its ViewModel as it opens, so Hilt has to be set up first."},{"q":"In <code>MainActivityTest</code>, which part is not the app’s real code?","options":["The repository","The ViewModel","The screen"],"a":0,"why":"Only the module, and with it the repository, was swapped. Everything above it is real, which is what makes this test worth having."}]},{"id":"baseline","title":"Baseline Profiles","tagline":"Make the first start fast, and measure it.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why an app starts slower right after it is installed","Generate a Baseline Profile with <code>BaselineProfileRule</code>","Measure startup with Macrobenchmark, with and without the profile"],"remember":"A Baseline Profile lists the start code so Android translates it at install; check the gain with a Macrobenchmark on a real phone.","terms":[{"term":"machine code","def":"The instructions a phone’s processor runs. Android translates the app’s code into it, while running or ahead of time."},{"term":"Baseline Profile","def":"A list, shipped in the app, of the code its start and key screens use. Android translates that code at install."},{"term":"Macrobenchmark","def":"A test that runs your app many times on a phone and measures it, for example with <code>StartupTimingMetric</code>."},{"term":"cold start","def":"Opening an app that wasn’t running at all: <code>StartupMode.COLD</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is in a Baseline Profile?","options":["A list of the code the app’s start and key screens use","Pictures of the first screen","The user’s settings"],"a":0,"why":"It lists code, so Android knows what to translate into machine code at install."},{"q":"Why does an app start faster a few days after it was installed, even without a profile?","options":["Android translated the busy code ahead of time, while the phone was idle","The app downloaded an update","The phone got a faster processor"],"a":0,"why":"In the background Android translates the parts used most. A profile does that at install instead."},{"q":"Where should you run the startup benchmark?","options":["On a real phone","On an emulator","In Robolectric"],"a":0,"why":"Emulator numbers don’t match what users get, and Macrobenchmark refuses to measure one."}]}]},{"id":"perf","title":"Performance","description":"Find out why an app stutters, freezes, leaks memory or starts slowly, measure it with the right tool, and fix it. Then shrink the release build with R8, and keep the code it can’t see you use.","requires":["arch"],"file":"tracks/perf.js","lessons":[{"id":"frame","title":"Frames and jank","tagline":"The screen gets a new picture many times a second, and each one has a deadline.","level":"beginner","goals":["Work out how much time one frame gets at 60 and 120 Hz","Explain what jank is and why one slow job causes it","Find the Logcat line that says frames were skipped"],"remember":"60 Hz gives each frame 16.7 ms, 120 Hz only 8.3 ms. A frame that misses its deadline is jank.","terms":[{"term":"frame","def":"One picture on the screen. A smooth app gets a new one ready for every refresh."},{"term":"refresh rate","def":"How many frames the screen shows per second, in hertz (Hz): 60, 90 or 120 on most phones."},{"term":"frame budget","def":"The time the app has to get one frame ready: 1000 ms divided by the refresh rate, 16.7 ms at 60 Hz."},{"term":"jank","def":"A visible stutter: frames missed their deadline, so the screen showed the old picture again and then jumped."}],"quiz":[{"q":"At 60 Hz, how long does the app have to get one frame ready?","options":["60 ms","About 16.7 ms","One second"],"a":1,"why":"1000 ms ÷ 60 frames ≈ 16.7 ms per frame."},{"q":"A frame isn’t ready when its deadline comes. What does the screen show?","options":["A blank screen","An error dialog","The previous frame again"],"a":2,"why":"The screen keeps the old picture until a new frame is ready. The pause and the jump after it are jank."},{"q":"Scrolling stutters, but Logcat has no “Skipped … frames!” line. How can that be?","options":["Jank can’t happen when the line is missing","The line only appears after 30 or more missed frames in a row","Logcat only works at 60 Hz"],"a":1,"why":"Short stutters of a few frames don’t reach the limit of 30. They are still jank, and the profiler later in this track finds them."}]},{"id":"main-thread","title":"The main thread","tagline":"Let StrictMode point at the slow work hiding on the main thread.","level":"beginner","goals":["List the places where your code runs on the main thread","Turn on StrictMode in the debug build","Follow a StrictMode report to the line that blocks, and move that work off the main thread"],"remember":"Turn on StrictMode in the debug build. Every line it logs is main-thread work to move into withContext(Dispatchers.IO).","terms":[{"term":"StrictMode","def":"Android’s checker that watches the main thread for disk and network work and reports it. Set up with <code>StrictMode.setThreadPolicy(...)</code>."},{"term":"violation","def":"One time StrictMode saw what you asked it to watch for, such as a disk write on the main thread."},{"term":"penaltyLog","def":"Tells StrictMode to write each violation to Logcat, with a stack trace, under the tag <code>StrictMode</code>."},{"term":"BuildConfig.DEBUG","def":"<code>true</code> in the debug build, <code>false</code> in the release build. Needs <code>buildFeatures { buildConfig = true }</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these runs on the main thread?","options":["The code in a button’s <code>onClick</code>","The block inside <code>withContext(Dispatchers.IO)</code>","Both"],"a":0,"why":"Taps are handled on the main thread. <code>withContext(Dispatchers.IO)</code> runs its block on a background thread."},{"q":"What does <code>penaltyLog()</code> do?","options":["Crashes the app on the first violation","Moves the slow work to another thread","Writes each violation to Logcat with a stack trace"],"a":2,"why":"It only reports. Moving the work is your job, once you know where it is."},{"q":"Why turn on StrictMode only when <code>BuildConfig.DEBUG</code> is <code>true</code>?","options":["It is a tool for you while you build; users don’t need its checks or log lines","It doesn’t compile in the release build","It makes the debug build faster"],"a":0,"why":"StrictMode finds problems during development. The release build is the one you give to users."}]},{"id":"anr","title":"ANRs","tagline":"What makes Android say “isn’t responding”, and how to find the cause.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name the deadlines that trigger an ANR","Explain why waiting for other work on the main thread causes one","Find the ANR line in Logcat and see why Google Play counts them"],"remember":"An ANR means the main thread was stuck past a deadline: 5 s for a tap. Never make it wait, not even for another thread.","terms":[{"term":"Android vitals","def":"The part of the Google Play Console that counts ANRs, crashes and slow starts on your users’ phones."},{"term":"ANR rate","def":"The share of daily users who saw an ANR. At 0.47% or more, Google Play is likely to show the app to fewer people."},{"term":"Input dispatching timed out","def":"The <code>Reason</code> of an ANR where a tap or key press wasn’t handled within 5 seconds."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these can cause an ANR?","options":["A coroutine in <code>viewModelScope</code> that waits for the network","<code>runBlocking</code> around a network call in <code>onClick</code>","<code>delay(10000)</code> inside a <code>LaunchedEffect</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>runBlocking</code> blocks the main thread. The other two wait inside a coroutine, which leaves the thread free."},{"q":"Which Logcat line tells you an ANR happened?","options":["<code>Skipped 42 frames!</code>","<code>StrictMode policy violation</code>","<code>ANR in com.example.recipes (com.example.recipes/.MainActivity)</code>"],"a":2,"why":"Android writes <code>ANR in</code> and the app’s name, then a <code>Reason</code> line. The other two are warnings about slow work, not ANRs."},{"q":"Why does Google Play care about ANRs?","options":["It removes the app after the first one","At an ANR rate of 0.47% or more, it is likely to show the app to fewer people","It doesn’t: ANRs only happen in debug builds"],"a":1,"why":"Android vitals counts ANRs on real users’ phones, and a high rate hurts how often Play shows the app."}]},{"id":"leaks","title":"Memory leaks","tagline":"Let LeakCanary find the screens your app forgot to let go of.","level":"beginner","goals":["Add LeakCanary to the debug build only","Read a leak trace and find the reference that causes the leak","Fix a leak by keeping <code>applicationContext</code> instead of an Activity"],"remember":"Add LeakCanary with debugImplementation, read the trace top to bottom, and cut the reference underlined with ~~~.","terms":[{"term":"LeakCanary","def":"A library for the debug build that notices destroyed screens that stay in memory and shows why."},{"term":"retained","def":"Still in memory 5 seconds after it should be gone. LeakCanary counts retained objects."},{"term":"leak trace","def":"The chain of references that keeps a leaked object in memory. The suspect reference is underlined with <code>~~~</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is LeakCanary added with <code>debugImplementation</code>?","options":["So only the debug build has it, and users never get it","Because it only works in Kotlin","To make the release build smaller than the debug build"],"a":0,"why":"LeakCanary is a tool for you. Its checks and memory copies have no place on users’ phones."},{"q":"In a leak trace, what does a reference underlined with <code>~~~</code> mean?","options":["The object is fine to keep","The line has a typo","It is a suspect: one of these references causes the leak"],"a":2,"why":"LeakCanary rules out what it can and underlines what is left. Cutting the suspect reference fixes the leak."},{"q":"Which may keep an Activity without leaking it?","options":["A ViewModel","An <code>object</code>","Neither: both outlive the Activity"],"a":2,"why":"Both can live longer than the screen, so a kept Activity can’t be freed. They may keep <code>applicationContext</code>."}]},{"id":"profiler","title":"Profiling","tagline":"Measure where the time goes before you change anything.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Start a profiler task on your running app","Read a flame chart and find the function that takes the time","Measure a piece of code with <code>measureTimeMillis</code>"],"remember":"Measure before you fix: in a flame chart, the wide bar with nothing on top is the hotspot.","terms":[{"term":"profiler","def":"An Android Studio tool that records what your running app does: which functions take time, what is in memory."},{"term":"profiler task","def":"One kind of recording, such as <b>Find CPU Hotspots</b> or <b>Analyze Memory Usage (Heap Dump)</b>."},{"term":"flame chart","def":"A chart of bars, one per function, with the functions it called on top. Width is the share of the time, not a timeline."},{"term":"hotspot","def":"The code that takes most of the time: a wide bar with nothing wide on top of it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which profiler task shows which functions take the time?","options":["Analyze Memory Usage (Heap Dump)","Find CPU Hotspots","Layout Inspector"],"a":1,"why":"Find CPU Hotspots records the functions that run. A heap dump is a snapshot of memory, and Layout Inspector is not a profiler task."},{"q":"In a flame chart, what does a bar’s width show?","options":["When the function ran","How many lines the function has","The share of the recorded time spent in that function and what it called"],"a":2,"why":"All calls through the same chain are added up into one bar. Left to right is not time."},{"q":"Why measure before you change code?","options":["The profiler makes the app faster","The slow part is often not where you guess","Lint needs a recording first"],"a":1,"why":"Lin rewrote the DAO, but reading the recipes took only 3% of the time. One recording pointed at the real 90%."}]},{"id":"compose-perf","title":"Compose performance","tagline":"Help Compose skip the parts of the screen that didn’t change.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Tell a stable parameter from an unstable one","Explain why a new list on every run stops Compose from skipping","Check a fix with Layout Inspector’s recomposition counts"],"remember":"Stable values are compared with ==, unstable ones like List with ===. Don’t build a new list in a composable’s body.","terms":[{"term":"stable","def":"A type where Compose can tell whether the value changed, like <code>Int</code>, <code>String</code> or a data class of <code>val</code>s of stable types. Compared with <code>==</code>."},{"term":"unstable","def":"A type Compose can’t be sure about, like <code>List</code> or a class with a <code>var</code>. Compared with <code>===</code>: only the same object is skipped."},{"term":"strong skipping","def":"The rule, on since Kotlin 2.0.20, that lets Compose skip a composable with unstable parameters when it gets the very same objects."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which parameter type does Compose treat as stable?","options":["<code>String</code>","<code>List&lt;Recipe&gt;</code>","A class with a <code>var</code> property"],"a":0,"why":"<code>String</code> can’t change, so Compose can compare it with <code>==</code>. The other two are unstable."},{"q":"With strong skipping, how does Compose compare an unstable parameter like a <code>List</code>?","options":["Are the contents equal (<code>==</code>)?","It never compares and always runs","Is it the very same object (<code>===</code>)?"],"a":2,"why":"For unstable types only the same object counts as unchanged. A new list with equal contents still runs."},{"q":"How do you check that <code>RecipeList</code> is skipped now?","options":["Layout Inspector’s recomposition counts","Logcat with <code>tag:Choreographer</code>","LeakCanary"],"a":0,"why":"Layout Inspector counts, per composable, how often it ran and how often it was skipped."}]},{"id":"startup","title":"Startup time","tagline":"Show the first screen sooner by doing less before it.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Tell a cold, a warm and a hot start apart","Read the time to the first screen from Logcat","Move work the first screen doesn’t need out of startup with <code>by lazy</code>"],"remember":"Measure a cold start with the Displayed line, and move everything the first screen doesn’t need out of startup.","terms":[{"term":"cold start","def":"Starting the app with no process: Android starts one, runs <code>Application.onCreate</code>, then the Activity, then the first frame."},{"term":"warm start","def":"The process is still running, but the Activity has to be created again."},{"term":"hot start","def":"The Activity is still there and comes back to the front, as after pressing Home."},{"term":"Displayed","def":"The Logcat line from <code>ActivityTaskManager</code> with the time from the tap to the first frame, such as <code>+850ms</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Lin presses Home, then taps the Recipes icon again. Which kind of start is that?","options":["Cold","Warm","Hot"],"a":2,"why":"The process and the Activity are still there. The Activity only comes back to the front."},{"q":"In a cold start, what runs before the first frame?","options":["Only <code>MainActivity.onCreate</code>","Nothing: the screen comes first","<code>RecipesApp.onCreate</code>, then <code>MainActivity.onCreate</code>"],"a":2,"why":"Android makes the app’s <code>Application</code> object before any Activity, all on the main thread."},{"q":"Why doesn’t <code>package:mine</code> show the <code>Displayed</code> line?","options":["It only appears in release builds","Android writes it, not your app","It appears only after 5 seconds"],"a":1,"why":"<code>package:mine</code> keeps your app’s lines. The startup time is measured and written by the system."}]},{"id":"r8","title":"Shrinking with R8","tagline":"Turn on R8 for the release build, and try that build on your own phone.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Turn on R8 for the release build","Explain what R8 removes and renames","Build the release app, install it on your phone and keep its <code>mapping.txt</code>"],"remember":"optimization { enable = true } in release turns on R8. Try the signed release build on a phone, and keep its mapping.txt.","terms":[{"term":"R8","def":"The tool that shrinks, simplifies and renames the code of a release build when <code>optimization { enable = true }</code> is set."},{"term":"build type","def":"One way of building the app: <code>debug</code> for Run, <code>release</code> for users. Set in <code>buildTypes { }</code>."},{"term":"shrinking","def":"Removing the classes and functions that nothing in the app reaches."},{"term":"signing","def":"Adding a digital signature that shows who made the app. A phone installs only signed apps."},{"term":"mapping.txt","def":"The file each release build writes with every old name and its new short name, used to read crashes. Overwritten by every build."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does R8 remove?","options":["Classes and functions that nothing in the app reaches","Every comment and blank line","All library code"],"a":0,"why":"It follows every call from what Android can start. What it never reaches is removed."},{"q":"The release build has no signing setting. What happens when you try to install it on a phone?","options":["It installs like the debug build","The phone won’t install <code>app-release-unsigned.apk</code>","Gradle signs it with your own key"],"a":1,"why":"A phone installs only signed apps. For a test phone, sign the release build with the debug key."},{"q":"A crash from the release app shows names like <code>a.b</code>. What turns them back into real names?","options":["<code>mapping.txt</code> from that build","A lint baseline","LeakCanary"],"a":0,"why":"Every release build writes <code>mapping.txt</code>. Android Studio and the Play Console use it to show the original names. Keep a copy, because the next build overwrites it."}]},{"id":"keep-rules","title":"Keep rules","tagline":"Tell R8 to leave alone the code it can’t see you use.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why a class found by its name looks unused to R8","Recognise the crash this causes in the release app","Write a narrow keep rule in a <code>.keep</code> file"],"remember":"R8 removes what nothing names directly. Keep each class that is only found by its name, with a narrow rule in a .keep file.","terms":[{"term":"Class.forName","def":"Finds a class from its full name, given as text, while the app runs."},{"term":"ClassNotFoundException","def":"The crash when <code>Class.forName</code> gets a name of a class that isn’t in the app, for example because R8 removed it."},{"term":"keep rule","def":"A line that tells R8 to leave something alone, such as <code>-keep class com.example.Foo { &lt;init&gt;(); }</code>, in a <code>.keep</code> file."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does <code>WebImporter</code> need a keep rule?","options":["<code>Class.forName</code> is slow","Keep rules make the class faster","Its name is put together while the app runs, so R8 can’t see that it is used"],"a":2,"why":"R8 only follows code that names the class directly. Without a rule it removes the class."},{"q":"Without the keep rule, where does the crash show up?","options":["In the release app, when it reaches <code>Class.forName</code>","In the debug build too","During <code>./gradlew assembleRelease</code>"],"a":0,"why":"R8 runs only in the release build, and it removes the class without a warning. The build passes; the app crashes later."},{"q":"In <code>-keep class com.example.recipes.importers.WebImporter { &lt;init&gt;(); }</code>, what is <code>&lt;init&gt;()</code>?","options":["The class’s <code>init { }</code> block","The constructor","The first function in the class"],"a":1,"why":"Keep rules write the constructor as <code>&lt;init&gt;()</code>. The app needs it to make an importer from the class."}]}]},{"id":"media","title":"Media and sensors","description":"Play cooking videos with Media3, keep audio going with a media session, take photos and film clips with CameraX, and read sensors and location only while you need them.","requires":["android"],"file":"tracks/media.js","lessons":[{"id":"exoplayer","title":"Playing video","tagline":"Media3 ExoPlayer loads a video and plays it. You release it when the screen leaves.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Create an ExoPlayer and give it a video to play","Show the video with the Player composable and a play button","Release the player when the screen leaves"],"remember":"Build it, give it a MediaItem, prepare() it, show it with Player and PlayPauseButton, and release() it in onDispose.","terms":[{"term":"ExoPlayer","def":"The video and audio player of Jetpack Media3. Build one with <code>ExoPlayer.Builder(context).build()</code>."},{"term":"MediaItem","def":"A description of one thing to play, such as <code>MediaItem.fromUri(videoUrl)</code>."},{"term":"playback state","def":"Where the player is: <code>STATE_IDLE</code>, <code>STATE_BUFFERING</code>, <code>STATE_READY</code> or <code>STATE_ENDED</code>."},{"term":"Player","def":"The interface every Media3 player implements, ExoPlayer included. Also the composable <code>Player(player = ...)</code> that draws the picture."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which state means “waiting for more data”?","options":["<code>STATE_BUFFERING</code>","<code>STATE_IDLE</code>","<code>STATE_ENDED</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Buffering means the player can’t play from where it is yet, because more data must load."},{"q":"Why call <code>release()</code> when the screen leaves?","options":["To free the decoder, memory and connection for other apps","To save the playing position","To make the next video load faster"],"a":0,"why":"A player holds scarce parts of the phone. Release gives them back."},{"q":"You released a player. Can you call <code>play()</code> on it again?","options":["No: build a new player","Yes, it starts from the beginning","Yes, after <code>prepare()</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A released player must not be used again. Make a new one if you need to play more."}]},{"id":"session","title":"Media sessions","tagline":"Put the player in a MediaSessionService, and the lock screen, headphones and notification can control it.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Move the player into a MediaSessionService with a MediaSession","Declare the service for media playback in the manifest","Control it from a screen with a MediaController"],"remember":"The player and its MediaSession live in a MediaSessionService. Screens control it through a MediaController.","terms":[{"term":"MediaSession","def":"Connects a player to the notification, lock screen, headphones and other controllers, and passes their commands to it."},{"term":"MediaSessionService","def":"A foreground service from Media3 that holds the player and its session, so playback continues with no screen open."},{"term":"MediaController","def":"The screen’s side of the connection. It is a <code>Player</code>, and its commands go to the session in the service. It arrives in a <em>future</em>, a box whose value comes later."},{"term":"ComponentName","def":"Names one part of your app, such as a service class: <code>ComponentName(context, PlaybackService::class.java)</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Who posts the media notification with play and pause?","options":["Media3, from the player and session state","Your code, with NotificationCompat","The lock screen"],"a":0,"why":"A <code>MediaSessionService</code> creates and updates the media notification for you."},{"q":"The user said no to notifications (<code>POST_NOTIFICATIONS</code>). Does the media notification still work?","options":["Yes: media session notifications are exempt","No: nothing can be shown","Only on tablets"],"a":0,"why":"Notifications that belong to a media session don’t need that permission."},{"q":"Why does the service have <code>exported=\"true\"</code>?","options":["Controllers outside your app, like the system’s media controls, connect to it","So it runs faster","Without it, the player has no sound"],"a":0,"why":"An exported component can be reached from outside your app. The session must be reachable by the system and other devices."}]},{"id":"audiofocus","title":"Audio focus","tagline":"Only one app should be heard at a time: pause for a call, play quieter for a short sound.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what audio focus is and the ways it can be lost","Let ExoPlayer handle audio focus with audio attributes","Pause when headphones are unplugged"],"remember":"Describe the sound with AudioAttributes, pass true to setAudioAttributes, and let ExoPlayer handle audio focus.","terms":[{"term":"audio focus","def":"Android’s way of deciding which app is heard. One app holds it at a time and loses it when another asks."},{"term":"ducking","def":"Playing quieter for a moment while another app plays a short sound."},{"term":"AudioAttributes","def":"A description of your sound: what it is for (<code>setUsage</code>) and what kind it is (<code>setContentType</code>)."},{"term":"becoming noisy","def":"Sound moving from headphones to the phone’s speaker, for example when they are unplugged."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A movie plays and a short notification sound comes. With focus handling on, what does ExoPlayer do?","options":["Plays quieter until the sound is over","Stops for good","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"A short sound is a loss for a moment. A movie ducks."},{"q":"Another music app starts. What should your video do?","options":["Pause and stay paused","Duck and carry on","Play louder"],"a":0,"why":"That is a loss for good. The user chose the other app."},{"q":"Why use <code>AUDIO_CONTENT_TYPE_SPEECH</code> for a spoken guide?","options":["The player pauses instead of ducking, so no words are lost","Speech sounds louder","It turns on subtitles"],"a":0,"why":"Talking underneath another voice is hard to follow, so speech pauses."}]},{"id":"camerax","title":"Taking photos","tagline":"CameraX shows what the camera sees and saves a photo of your dish.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Bind a Preview and an ImageCapture to the lifecycle","Show the camera with CameraXViewfinder","Take a photo into a file"],"remember":"Bind Preview and ImageCapture to the lifecycle, draw the SurfaceRequest with CameraXViewfinder, and use the photo in onImageSaved.","terms":[{"term":"use case","def":"A job you ask CameraX to do, such as <code>Preview</code> or <code>ImageCapture</code>. You bind the ones you need."},{"term":"lifecycle owner","def":"Something with a lifecycle, like a screen in a NavHost or an Activity. In Compose: <code>LocalLifecycleOwner.current</code>. Bound use cases follow its start and stop."},{"term":"SurfaceRequest","def":"Preview’s request for a place to draw. <code>CameraXViewfinder</code> answers it and shows the picture."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which use case shows the picture on screen?","options":["<code>Preview</code>","<code>ImageCapture</code>","<code>CameraSelector</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>Preview</code> sends frames to the screen. <code>ImageCapture</code> takes photos, and the selector only picks the lens."},{"q":"When can you use the photo file?","options":["In <code>onImageSaved</code>","Right after <code>takePicture</code> returns","When the screen leaves"],"a":0,"why":"Saving happens later. The callback tells you it is done."},{"q":"To use the selfie camera instead, what do you pass to <code>bindToLifecycle</code>?","options":["<code>CameraSelector.DEFAULT_FRONT_CAMERA</code>","A second <code>Preview</code>","<code>LocalContext.current</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The selector picks the lens. To switch while the screen is open, <code>unbindAll()</code> and bind again with the other selector."}]},{"id":"video-capture","title":"Recording video","tagline":"VideoCapture and a Recorder film a clip. The file is ready when Finalize arrives.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Bind a VideoCapture with a Recorder","Start and stop a recording into a file","Wait for Finalize and check it for errors"],"remember":"prepareRecording, start, stop, and wait for Finalize. Check hasError() before you tell the user the video is saved.","terms":[{"term":"VideoCapture","def":"The CameraX use case for video. It sends camera frames to an output such as a <code>Recorder</code>."},{"term":"Recorder","def":"Encodes the frames and sound and writes the file. It runs one recording at a time."},{"term":"PendingRecording","def":"A recording with its output chosen. Nothing records until <code>start()</code>, which returns a <code>Recording</code>."},{"term":"VideoRecordEvent","def":"What your lambda gets during a recording: <code>Start</code>, <code>Status</code>, and finally <code>Finalize</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>prepareRecording()</code> give you?","options":["A <code>PendingRecording</code> that records nothing until <code>start()</code>","A <code>Recording</code> that is already running","The finished file"],"a":0,"why":"Preparing only picks the output and options. <code>start()</code> returns the active <code>Recording</code>."},{"q":"A recording is still running. Can the same Recorder start a second one?","options":["No: stop the first one, then start another","Yes, into a second file","Yes, the first one stops by itself"],"a":0,"why":"A Recorder runs one recording at a time."},{"q":"Which event tells you the video file is finished?","options":["<code>Finalize</code>","<code>Start</code>","<code>Status</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>Start</code> and <code>Status</code> come while recording. <code>Finalize</code> is last, with the result or an error."}]},{"id":"analysis","title":"Image analysis","tagline":"ImageAnalysis hands your code camera frames, the newest one each time.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Run your own check on camera frames with ImageAnalysis","Keep up with the camera by taking only the newest frame","Close every frame when you are done with it"],"remember":"ImageAnalysis gives your analyzer the newest frame. Work on a background executor and always close() the image.","terms":[{"term":"ImageAnalysis","def":"The CameraX use case that hands camera frames to your own code."},{"term":"ImageProxy","def":"One camera frame given to your analyzer. Call <code>close()</code> when you are done with it."},{"term":"array","def":"A fixed-size row of values, read with <code>[index]</code> like a list. <code>image.planes</code> and a <code>ByteArray</code> are arrays."},{"term":"backpressure strategy","def":"What happens when frames come faster than you can check them: keep only the newest, or wait in a queue."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You forget <code>image.close()</code>. What happens?","options":["The analyzer gets no more frames","Nothing changes","The photo is saved twice"],"a":0,"why":"CameraX waits for the frame to come back before it sends the next one."},{"q":"Why give <code>setAnalyzer</code> a single-thread executor instead of the main executor?","options":["So the checks don’t slow down the screen","Because the camera needs two threads","So the frames are brighter"],"a":0,"why":"The main thread draws the UI. Heavy work belongs on another thread."},{"q":"Which strategy is the default?","options":["<code>STRATEGY_KEEP_ONLY_LATEST</code>","<code>STRATEGY_BLOCK_PRODUCER</code>","There is no default"],"a":0,"why":"By default, frames you missed are dropped and you get the newest."}]},{"id":"sensors","title":"Sensors","tagline":"SensorManager reads the phone’s sensors. Listen only while the screen needs them.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Find a sensor with SensorManager, and handle a phone without one","Read its values with a SensorEventListener","Unregister when the screen leaves, to save battery"],"remember":"getDefaultSensor may be null. Register while the screen shows, unregister in onDispose, and pick the slowest delay that works.","terms":[{"term":"SensorManager","def":"Android’s service for sensors. You get it from the Context with <code>getSystemService</code>."},{"term":"SensorEventListener","def":"An interface with <code>onSensorChanged</code> (a new reading) and <code>onAccuracyChanged</code>."},{"term":"proximity sensor","def":"Measures how close something is to the top of the screen, in centimetres. Many know only near and far."}],"quiz":[{"q":"When does <code>getDefaultSensor(Sensor.TYPE_PROXIMITY)</code> return <code>null</code>?","options":["When the phone has no proximity sensor","When the screen is off","Never"],"a":0,"why":"Not every phone has every sensor, so check for <code>null</code> and keep the app working without it."},{"q":"A proximity sensor only ever reports 0.0 and 5.0, and its <code>maximumRange</code> is 5.0. What does 5.0 mean?","options":["Far","Near","An error"],"a":0,"why":"Near-and-far sensors report their maximum range for far and a smaller value for near."},{"q":"Which delay is about 200 ms between readings?","options":["<code>SENSOR_DELAY_NORMAL</code>","<code>SENSOR_DELAY_GAME</code>","<code>SENSOR_DELAY_FASTEST</code>"],"a":0,"why":"NORMAL is about 200 ms, UI 60 ms, GAME 20 ms, FASTEST as fast as the sensor allows."}]},{"id":"location","title":"Location","tagline":"The fused location provider finds where the phone is. Ask for approximate when that is enough.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Get the current location with the fused location provider","Choose between approximate and precise location permissions","Pick a priority that fits the feature and the battery"],"remember":"Ask for approximate unless you truly need precise, get the current location with getCurrentLocation, and handle null.","terms":[{"term":"fused location provider","def":"The location service in Google Play services. It mixes GPS, Wi-Fi and the mobile network for a good answer at low battery cost."},{"term":"approximate location","def":"A location within about 3 square kilometres, from <code>ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION</code>."},{"term":"precise location","def":"A location usually within about 50 metres, from <code>ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION</code>. The user can still choose approximate."},{"term":"Priority","def":"How hard to try: from <code>PRIORITY_HIGH_ACCURACY</code> (precise, more battery) to <code>PRIORITY_LOW_POWER</code> (city level)."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which permission gives an approximate location?","options":["<code>ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION</code>","<code>ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION</code>","<code>INTERNET</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Coarse is approximate, about 3 square kilometres. Fine is precise."},{"q":"Location is turned off in the phone’s settings. What can <code>getCurrentLocation</code> give you?","options":["<code>null</code>","The last place the phone was in","<code>0.0</code>, <code>0.0</code>"],"a":0,"why":"There may be no location to give, so the result can be <code>null</code>. Handle it."},{"q":"Which priority usually costs the most battery?","options":["<code>PRIORITY_HIGH_ACCURACY</code>","<code>PRIORITY_BALANCED_POWER_ACCURACY</code>","<code>PRIORITY_LOW_POWER</code>"],"a":0,"why":"High accuracy is likely to use GPS, which uses the most power."}]}]},{"id":"push","title":"Notifications and push","description":"Tell Recipes users about a new recipe: notifications and channels, rich styles and actions, Firebase Cloud Messaging, and links that open the right screen.","requires":["arch"],"file":"tracks/push.js","lessons":[{"id":"notifications","title":"Showing a notification","tagline":"A short message from your app that waits for the user outside it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Build a notification with a small icon, a title and a text","Ask for the notification permission when the user wants alerts","Post a notification with <code>notify</code>, and replace it by using the same id"],"remember":"A channel, a small icon, a title and a text. Ask for POST_NOTIFICATIONS when the user wants alerts; the same id replaces.","terms":[{"term":"notification","def":"A message from an app shown outside it: a small icon in the status bar and an entry in the notification shade."},{"term":"notification shade","def":"The list of notifications the user pulls down from the top of the screen."},{"term":"notification channel","def":"A kind of notification, made with <code>NotificationChannel</code>. Every notification belongs to one, and the user can turn each channel on or off in Settings."},{"term":"POST_NOTIFICATIONS","def":"The runtime permission an app needs from Android 13 (API 33, <code>TIRAMISU</code>) to show notifications."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Lin’s phone runs Android 14, and she tapped “Don’t allow”. The app posts a notification. What does she see?","options":["Nothing","The notification","The permission dialog again"],"a":0,"why":"Without <code>POST_NOTIFICATIONS</code>, the app can’t show notifications. Check first, and keep the app working without them."},{"q":"The app calls <code>notify(42, a)</code> and then <code>notify(42, b)</code>. What is in the shade?","options":["One notification: <code>b</code>","Two notifications","One notification: <code>a</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The same id replaces the notification that is there."},{"q":"Which of these must every notification have?","options":["A small icon, from <code>setSmallIcon</code>","A title","A sound"],"a":0,"why":"The small icon is the only part that is required. Title and text are what you will almost always add."}]},{"id":"channels","title":"Notification channels","tagline":"One channel for each kind of message, so the user can mute one and keep the other.","level":"beginner","goals":["Choose an importance for each kind of notification","Explain why the user, not your code, controls a channel once it exists","Check whether a channel is blocked and open its settings"],"remember":"One channel per kind, the quietest importance that fits. After the first create, the channel belongs to the user.","terms":[{"term":"importance","def":"How loud a channel’s notifications are, from <code>IMPORTANCE_HIGH</code> (sound and banner) to <code>IMPORTANCE_MIN</code> (only in the shade). <code>IMPORTANCE_NONE</code> means blocked."},{"term":"heads-up notification","def":"A banner that floats over the app on screen for a moment. Only channels with <code>IMPORTANCE_HIGH</code> show it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which importance makes a sound and shows a heads-up banner?","options":["<code>IMPORTANCE_HIGH</code>","<code>IMPORTANCE_DEFAULT</code>","<code>IMPORTANCE_LOW</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>DEFAULT</code> makes a sound without a banner. <code>LOW</code> is silent."},{"q":"Why give new recipes and reminders their own channels?","options":["So the user can turn off one and keep the other","Because a channel holds only one notification","So the app can change their sound later"],"a":0,"why":"Each channel has its own switch in Settings, and the user decides."},{"q":"A notification is posted to a channel with <code>IMPORTANCE_LOW</code>. What does the user notice?","options":["An icon in the status bar, no sound","A sound and a banner","Nothing at all"],"a":0,"why":"<code>LOW</code> is silent but shows an icon. <code>MIN</code> drops the icon too."}]},{"id":"styles","title":"Rich notifications","tagline":"Open the app on a tap, show more than one line, and add buttons.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Open the app when the user taps a notification, with a <code>PendingIntent</code>","Show long text or a list with <code>BigTextStyle</code> and <code>InboxStyle</code>","Add an action button that opens another part of the app"],"remember":"A PendingIntent for the tap, a style for more text, up to three actions, each PendingIntent with its own request code.","terms":[{"term":"PendingIntent","def":"An Intent wrapped so that Android can start it later for your app, for example when a notification is tapped."},{"term":"request code","def":"A number you give each <code>PendingIntent</code> so Android can tell apart ones whose Intents look the same."},{"term":"notification style","def":"A bigger view for a notification, set with <code>setStyle</code>: <code>BigTextStyle</code>, <code>InboxStyle</code> or <code>BigPictureStyle</code>."},{"term":"action button","def":"A button under a notification’s text, added with <code>addAction</code>. A notification can have up to three."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a notification need a <code>PendingIntent</code> to open your app?","options":["The notification lives in Android’s shade, so Android starts the Intent later for your app","A normal Intent is too slow","Only a PendingIntent can carry extras"],"a":0,"why":"Your code isn’t running when the user taps. Android starts the wrapped Intent for you."},{"q":"How many action buttons can a notification have?","options":["Up to three","One","As many as you like"],"a":0,"why":"Up to three, and none should repeat what the tap does."},{"q":"You add eight lines with <code>InboxStyle</code>. How many does Lin see?","options":["The first six","All eight","Only the last one"],"a":0,"why":"InboxStyle shows at most six lines."}]},{"id":"fcm","title":"Firebase Cloud Messaging","tagline":"Your server can’t reach a phone by itself. FCM can, even when the app isn’t running.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why push messages go through Firebase Cloud Messaging","Add Firebase to Recipes with the Firebase BoM","Receive a message in a <code>FirebaseMessagingService</code>"],"remember":"FCM delivers your server’s messages to the phone; your FirebaseMessagingService receives them in onMessageReceived.","terms":[{"term":"Firebase Cloud Messaging","def":"FCM: a Google service that delivers small messages from your server to your app, even when the app isn’t running."},{"term":"push message","def":"A message your server sends to the phone through FCM, instead of the app asking for it."},{"term":"FirebaseMessagingService","def":"The class you extend to hear from FCM. List it in the manifest as a <code>&lt;service&gt;</code> with the <code>MESSAGING_EVENT</code> action."},{"term":"google-services.json","def":"The file the Firebase console gives you for your app. It goes in the app folder, and the <code>google-services</code> plugin reads it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can’t Recipes keep its own connection to the server open, waiting for new recipes?","options":["It would drain the battery, and Android stops apps the user isn’t using","Servers can only send email","Android allows only one app on the internet at a time"],"a":0,"why":"FCM delivers the message for you, even when the app isn’t running."},{"q":"Why does the <code>firebase-messaging</code> line have no version?","options":["The Firebase BoM, inside <code>platform(...)</code>, picks it","Gradle always takes the newest one","<code>google-services.json</code> holds it"],"a":0,"why":"Like the Compose BOM, the Firebase BoM gives every Firebase library a matching version."},{"q":"Where does <code>google-services.json</code> go?","options":["In the app folder, next to the app’s <code>build.gradle.kts</code>","In <code>res/values</code>","On your server"],"a":0,"why":"The <code>google-services</code> plugin reads it from the app folder when the app is built."}]},{"id":"fcm-registration","title":"Registering with FCM","tagline":"FCM gives each install an ID. Your server needs it to reach that phone.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Receive this install’s ID in <code>onRegistered</code> and send it to your server","Upload it from an app-wide scope that keeps going after the service stops","Keep the server’s list right: register at every start, and tell the server on sign-out"],"remember":"FCM reaches the phone through its installation ID. Send every ID from onRegistered to your server, from an app-wide scope, and call register() at every start.","terms":[{"term":"installation ID","def":"The ID FCM gives one install of your app. Your server sends messages to it. It changes when the app is reinstalled, restored on a new phone, or its data is cleared."},{"term":"onRegistered","def":"The function in your <code>FirebaseMessagingService</code> that FCM calls with the installation ID: at the first registration, when the ID changes or is refreshed, and after each <code>register()</code>."},{"term":"app-wide scope","def":"One <code>CoroutineScope</code> for the whole app, made once (here by Hilt, with <code>@Singleton</code>). Nothing cancels it, so its coroutines outlive screens and services."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these gives Lin’s install a new installation ID?","options":["Clearing the app’s data","An update from Google Play","Turning the phone off and on"],"a":0,"why":"Clearing data, reinstalling and restoring on a new phone give a new ID. An update only refreshes the registration, with the same ID; <code>onRegistered</code> runs either way."},{"q":"Why does the upload run in the app-wide scope, not in a scope of the service’s own?","options":["Android stops the service as soon as <code>onRegistered</code> returns, and its own scope would be cancelled with it","A service can’t start coroutines","The app-wide scope makes the upload faster"],"a":0,"why":"The app-wide scope isn’t tied to the service, so the upload can finish after the service has stopped."},{"q":"Why call <code>register()</code> in <code>RecipesApp.onCreate</code>?","options":["Each call runs <code>onRegistered</code>, so every start sends the ID again and repairs a failed upload","Without it, notifications can’t be shown","It makes a new installation ID at every start"],"a":0,"why":"FCM keeps the ID it has. <code>register()</code> only makes <code>onRegistered</code> run again with it."}]},{"id":"messages","title":"Message types","tagline":"A notification message is shown for you. A data message is handed to your code.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Tell a notification message from a data message by its JSON","Predict who shows a push, Android or your code, with the app open or closed","Handle a data message in <code>onMessageReceived</code> and show your own notification"],"remember":"Notification message, app not on screen: Android shows it. Everything else goes to onMessageReceived.","terms":[{"term":"notification message","def":"An FCM message with a <code>notification</code> block. When the app isn’t on screen, Android shows it without running your code."},{"term":"data message","def":"An FCM message with only a <code>data</code> block of text keys and values. It always goes to <code>onMessageReceived</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Recipes isn’t on screen. A notification message arrives. What does your code do?","options":["Nothing: Android shows it, and your code doesn’t run","<code>onMessageReceived</code> shows it","It throws an exception"],"a":0,"why":"In that case, Android puts it in the shade by itself, in the manifest’s default channel."},{"q":"Recipes is on screen. A notification message arrives. What does Lin see?","options":["Nothing, unless <code>onMessageReceived</code> shows something","The notification, as when the app is closed","A heads-up banner"],"a":0,"why":"With the app on screen, every message goes to <code>onMessageReceived</code>, and your code decides."},{"q":"Why send <code>\"recipeId\": \"42\"</code> instead of the whole recipe?","options":["A message can carry at most 4 KB","FCM accepts only numbers","Data messages can’t have more than one key"],"a":0,"why":"Messages are small. Send the id, and let the app load the rest."}]},{"id":"deeplinks","title":"Deep links","tagline":"A link like recipes.example.com/recipe/42 opens recipe 42, not the start screen.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Name the parts of a link: scheme, host and path","Make a Navigation route answer to a link, with an intent filter in the manifest","Open one recipe from a notification, with Back leading to the list"],"remember":"A navDeepLink on the route, an intent filter in the manifest, and TaskStackBuilder so Back leads to the list.","terms":[{"term":"deep link","def":"A link that opens one place inside an app, like <code>https://recipes.example.com/recipe/42</code>."},{"term":"scheme, host, path","def":"The parts of a link: <code>https</code>, then <code>recipes.example.com</code>, then <code>/recipe/42</code>."},{"term":"BROWSABLE","def":"An intent filter category that lets links from web pages and messages reach your Activity."},{"term":"TaskStackBuilder","def":"Builds a PendingIntent that starts your app in a fresh task, so Back leads through your app instead of out of it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In <code>https://recipes.example.com/recipe/42</code>, which part is the host?","options":["<code>recipes.example.com</code>","<code>https</code>","<code>/recipe/42</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>https</code> is the scheme, and <code>/recipe/42</code> is the path."},{"q":"Recipes isn’t verified for its site. On Android 13, Lin taps <code>https://recipes.example.com/recipe/42</code> in a chat app. What opens?","options":["The browser","Recipes, on recipe 42","A list of apps to choose from"],"a":0,"why":"From Android 12, an https link from another app reaches your app only when your app is verified for that site."},{"q":"What does <code>BROWSABLE</code> in the intent filter allow?","options":["Links in web pages and messages can reach the Activity","The Activity can open a browser","Other apps can read your recipes"],"a":0,"why":"Without it, a link tapped in a browser or a message can’t lead to your Activity."}]},{"id":"app-links","title":"App Links","tagline":"Your website vouches for your app, so its links open the app instead of the browser.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why Android sends unverified web links to the browser","Link the site and the app with <code>autoVerify</code> and <code>assetlinks.json</code>","Check the result with adb"],"remember":"autoVerify in the manifest, assetlinks.json on every host with the right fingerprint, no redirects. Then links open the app.","terms":[{"term":"App Link","def":"A web link that Android has verified for your app, so it opens the app directly instead of the browser."},{"term":"autoVerify","def":"An intent filter attribute, <code>android:autoVerify=\"true\"</code>, that asks Android to verify the filter’s hosts when the app is installed."},{"term":"assetlinks.json","def":"The file at <code>/.well-known/assetlinks.json</code> on your site that names the apps allowed to open its links."},{"term":"certificate fingerprint","def":"A short code (SHA-256) made from the certificate that signs your app. Only an app signed with that key matches it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Recipes has <code>autoVerify=\"true\"</code>, but the site has no <code>assetlinks.json</code>. On Android 13, Lin taps a recipe link in a chat. What opens?","options":["The browser","Recipes","A list of apps to choose from"],"a":0,"why":"Without the file, the host can’t be verified, and since Android 12 unverified web links go to the browser."},{"q":"Where must the file be?","options":["At <code>/.well-known/assetlinks.json</code> on each host, over HTTPS, without redirects","In the app’s <code>res</code> folder","In <code>google-services.json</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Android downloads it from every host in your intent filters."},{"q":"Why does the file hold a certificate fingerprint, not only the package name?","options":["A package name is only a name another app could copy, but no other app can be signed with your key","Package names are too long","The browser needs it"],"a":0,"why":"The fingerprint ties the links to apps signed with your key, so a copy with the same package name can’t take them."}]}]},{"id":"background","title":"Background work","description":"Why Android pauses apps you aren’t looking at, and the right tool for each job Recipes does off screen: WorkManager for a nightly sync, a foreground service for a download, broadcast receivers for system events, and exact alarms for a cooking timer.","requires":["push","arch"],"file":"tracks/background.js","lessons":[{"id":"why-limits","title":"Why Android limits background work","tagline":"A phone must last the day, so Android decides when apps you aren’t looking at may run.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why Android pauses and ends apps that aren’t on screen","Describe what Doze switches off and when waiting work gets its turn","Put an emulator into Doze with adb to test your app"],"remember":"Apps you aren’t looking at can lose their process, and in Doze they lose the network. Hand work to Android instead of keeping loops alive.","terms":[{"term":"background work","def":"Work an app does while the user isn’t looking at it. Android decides when it may run."},{"term":"Doze","def":"A deep sleep for a phone that is unplugged, still and dark for a while: no network for apps, and scheduled work and alarms wait."},{"term":"maintenance window","def":"A short break in Doze where waiting work runs and apps get the network back. The windows get rarer the longer Doze lasts."},{"term":"App Standby Bucket","def":"Android’s ranking of an app by how recently and often it is used: active, working set, frequent, rare, restricted. Rarer buckets get fewer turns."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these ends Doze?","options":["A new recipe on the server","Lin picks the phone up or plugs it in","Recipes asks for the network again"],"a":1,"why":"Doze ends when the phone moves, the screen turns on or the charger is plugged in. Apps can’t end it themselves."},{"q":"During Doze, when does the work apps scheduled get to run?","options":["In short maintenance windows that come further apart over time","Every 5 minutes, as before","Never, until the phone is charged"],"a":0,"why":"Doze opens maintenance windows now and then. Waiting work runs in them, with the network back."},{"q":"Lin hasn’t opened Recipes for weeks. What happens to its background work?","options":["Nothing changes","Android deletes the app","It moves to a rarer standby bucket and gets fewer turns"],"a":2,"why":"Buckets follow how often the app is used. A rarely used app gets fewer turns; in the restricted bucket, about once a day."}]},{"id":"workmanager","title":"WorkManager","tagline":"Book a job with Android, say what it needs, and it runs when the moment is right, even after a restart.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Write a CoroutineWorker that reports success or asks to retry","Book a nightly sync that needs Wi-Fi and a charger","Book it only once, however often the app starts"],"remember":"A worker does the job, constraints say when it may run, and a unique name makes sure it is booked once.","terms":[{"term":"WorkManager","def":"A Jetpack library that saves a job and runs it when its conditions hold, even after the app closes or the phone restarts."},{"term":"worker","def":"The class that does one job. A <code>CoroutineWorker</code> overrides <code>suspend fun doWork(): Result</code>."},{"term":"constraint","def":"A condition a job waits for, such as a Wi-Fi network (<code>NetworkType.UNMETERED</code>) or a charger."},{"term":"unique work","def":"A request booked under a name. <code>KEEP</code> leaves an existing booking with that name alone."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>PeriodicWorkRequestBuilder&lt;SyncWorker&gt;(5, TimeUnit.MINUTES)</code>: how often can it run?","options":["Every 5 minutes","At most every 15 minutes","Only once"],"a":1,"why":"15 minutes is the shortest repeat. WorkManager raises the 5 and warns in Logcat: “Interval duration lesser than minimum allowed value; Changed to 900000” (milliseconds)."},{"q":"The phone restarts during the night. What happens to <code>\"nightly-sync\"</code>?","options":["It is still booked","It is gone until Recipes opens","It runs at once, without its constraints"],"a":0,"why":"WorkManager keeps requests in its own database and books them again after a restart."},{"q":"<code>RecipesApp.onCreate</code> calls <code>enqueueUniquePeriodicWork(\"nightly-sync\", KEEP, request)</code>, and Recipes starts 10 times. How many syncs are booked?","options":["Ten","None","One"],"a":2,"why":"With <code>KEEP</code>, a booking with the same name stays and the new request is ignored."}]},{"id":"workmanager-hilt","title":"WorkManager with Hilt","tagline":"WorkManager builds your workers. Hand it Hilt’s factory, and they get the repository too.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Mark a worker so Hilt can pass it the repository","Give WorkManager Hilt’s factory from RecipesApp","Switch off WorkManager’s default set-up in the manifest"],"remember":"WorkManager builds your workers: mark the worker @HiltWorker, hand WorkManager HiltWorkerFactory from RecipesApp, and switch off its default set-up in the manifest.","terms":[{"term":"worker factory","def":"What WorkManager asks to make a worker. The default one passes only a <code>Context</code> and <code>WorkerParameters</code>; <code>HiltWorkerFactory</code> also gets the rest from Hilt."},{"term":"assisted injection","def":"Hilt builds an object together with values someone else supplies. <code>@Assisted</code> marks those values; here they come from WorkManager."},{"term":"Configuration.Provider","def":"An interface from WorkManager. <code>RecipesApp</code> implements it to hand WorkManager its configuration, here with Hilt’s factory."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which values does WorkManager itself pass to <code>SyncWorker</code>’s constructor?","options":["The <code>Context</code> and <code>WorkerParameters</code>, marked <code>@Assisted</code>","The <code>RecipeRepository</code>","All three"],"a":0,"why":"WorkManager supplies the two <code>@Assisted</code> values. Everything else, here the repository, comes from Hilt."},{"q":"<code>RecipesApp</code> implements <code>Configuration.Provider</code>, but the manifest lines are missing. What happens?","options":["It works: WorkManager always asks <code>RecipesApp</code>","WorkManager sets itself up with its default factory, and <code>SyncWorker</code> can’t be made","The app won’t install"],"a":1,"why":"The default set-up runs as the app starts and wins. The manifest lines switch it off, so your configuration takes effect."},{"q":"Android Studio offers two classes named <code>Configuration</code> to import. Which one does <code>RecipesApp</code> need?","options":["<code>android.content.res.Configuration</code>","<code>androidx.work.Configuration</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>Provider</code> and <code>Builder</code> belong to WorkManager’s <code>androidx.work.Configuration</code>. The other one describes the screen and language, and has neither."}]},{"id":"fgs","title":"Foreground services","tagline":"Work the user started and is waiting for keeps going, with a notification that says so.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Tell when a foreground service fits better than WorkManager","Write a service that shows its notification and stops when it is done","Declare its type and permissions, and start it from a tap"],"remember":"Foreground service: started from the user’s tap, shows a notification at once, has a declared type, and stops itself when done.","terms":[{"term":"foreground service","def":"A service that shows a notification while it works, so it keeps running when the user leaves the app. For work the user is waiting for."},{"term":"foreground service type","def":"What the service does, such as <code>dataSync</code> or <code>mediaPlayback</code>. Declared with <code>android:foregroundServiceType</code>, with a permission of its own."},{"term":"Task Manager","def":"The list of running foreground services at the bottom of the notification shade, where the user can stop them."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Since Android 12, Recipes tries to start <code>OfflineService</code> while it is in the background, and no exception to the rule applies. What happens?","options":["It starts and shows its notification","<code>ForegroundServiceStartNotAllowedException</code>","It waits until Lin opens Recipes"],"a":1,"why":"An app in the background may not start a foreground service, apart from a few cases such as a high-priority FCM message or an exact alarm."},{"q":"Lin turned notifications off for Recipes. Can <code>OfflineService</code> still run?","options":["Yes; its notice shows only in the Task Manager","No, it needs the notification permission","Only on Wi-Fi"],"a":0,"why":"A foreground service doesn’t need the notification permission to start. Without it, its notice is hidden from the shade but still listed in the Task Manager."},{"q":"Which job fits a foreground service best?","options":["Refreshing recipes once a night","A download Lin just started and is waiting for","Noticing that the phone’s language changed"],"a":1,"why":"Foreground services are for work the user started and is watching. A nightly refresh can wait, so it belongs in WorkManager."}]},{"id":"receivers","title":"Broadcast receivers","tagline":"Android announces events like a new language or airplane mode. A receiver is how your app hears them.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Write a receiver in the manifest that wakes the app for a system event","Hand longer work from onReceive to WorkManager","Listen to an event only while a screen is open, and stop listening when it closes"],"remember":"Manifest receivers wake the app for a few system events; receivers registered in code listen while a screen is open. Either way, keep onReceive short.","terms":[{"term":"broadcast","def":"An intent Android or an app sends to every app that listens, such as <code>LOCALE_CHANGED</code> when the language changes."},{"term":"broadcast receiver","def":"A component that hears broadcasts: a class that extends <code>BroadcastReceiver</code>, listed in the manifest or registered in code."},{"term":"implicit broadcast","def":"A broadcast sent to every app rather than to one. Since Android 8, manifest receivers hear only a short list of them."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can’t a manifest receiver hear <code>ACTION_AIRPLANE_MODE_CHANGED</code>?","options":["Airplane mode switches all apps off","It needs the INTERNET permission","Since Android 8, manifest receivers hear only a short list of implicit broadcasts"],"a":2,"why":"Most broadcasts sent to every app reach only receivers registered in code, so one event can’t wake hundreds of apps."},{"q":"<code>onReceive</code> needs to download the recipes again. What should it do?","options":["Download them right there","Book a WorkManager request and return","Wait with a loop until the network is back"],"a":1,"why":"<code>onReceive</code> runs on the main thread and must return within seconds, and the process may end once it returns. WorkManager finishes the job."},{"q":"You call <code>ContextCompat.registerReceiver(context, receiver, filter, 0)</code>. What happens?","options":["It throws <code>IllegalArgumentException</code>: “One of either RECEIVER_EXPORTED or RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED is required”","It works and receives every broadcast","It registers but never receives anything"],"a":0,"why":"The flag is required. Pick <code>RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED</code> when only Android or your own app sends the broadcast."}]},{"id":"alarms","title":"Alarms","tagline":"When it has to happen at 45:00 sharp, even with the phone asleep, set an exact alarm.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Tell when an app needs an exact alarm and when an inexact one is enough","Set an exact alarm that fires a receiver, even in Doze","Check the exact alarm permission and send the user to turn it on"],"remember":"Exact alarms are for times the user set: check the permission, fall back to inexact, and set them again after a restart.","terms":[{"term":"exact alarm","def":"An alarm that fires at the time you set. <code>setExactAndAllowWhileIdle</code> fires even in Doze, and needs the exact alarm permission."},{"term":"inexact alarm","def":"An alarm that never fires early but may fire later, so Android can bundle it with other work. Needs no special permission."},{"term":"clock time","def":"The time of day in milliseconds, from <code>System.currentTimeMillis()</code>. <code>RTC_WAKEUP</code> alarms use it, so a saved time still means the same moment after a restart."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Recipes was installed today on an Android 14 phone. What does <code>canScheduleExactAlarms</code> return before the user does anything?","options":["<code>true</code>","<code>false</code>","It throws"],"a":1,"why":"Since Android 14, <code>SCHEDULE_EXACT_ALARM</code> starts off for newly installed apps. The user turns it on under Alarms &amp; reminders."},{"q":"Lin starts a 45-minute timer, and the phone restarts 20 minutes later. What happens to the alarm?","options":["It rings at the right time anyway","It rings as soon as the phone is back on","It is gone, unless a <code>BOOT_COMPLETED</code> receiver sets it again"],"a":2,"why":"Android clears alarms on restart. Save the end time as clock time, and set the alarm again from a <code>BOOT_COMPLETED</code> receiver."},{"q":"Lin starts a second timer, and <code>startTimer</code> builds the same <code>PendingIntent</code> again. What happens to the first alarm?","options":["The new alarm replaces it","Both ring","The second call is ignored"],"a":0,"why":"An alarm set with the same <code>PendingIntent</code> replaces the earlier one, and <code>FLAG_UPDATE_CURRENT</code> gives it the new title. Two timers at once need two different request codes, the <code>0</code> in <code>getBroadcast</code>."}]}]},{"id":"security","title":"Security and privacy","description":"Protect the Recipes app on phones you don’t control: encrypt the saved tokens with a Keystore key, keep traffic on HTTPS and secrets on the server, sign in with passkeys, lock with a fingerprint, and collect only what you need.","requires":["android"],"file":"tracks/security.js","lessons":[{"id":"threats","title":"Threats","tagline":"Before you protect anything, list what attackers want and what they can reach.","level":"intermediate","goals":["List what in the Recipes app is worth stealing","Name four kinds of attacker and what each can reach","Explain why decisions that matter belong on the server"],"remember":"The APK is public and the phone may not be on your side: list what each attacker can reach, and let the server make the decisions that matter.","terms":[{"term":"threat model","def":"A short list: what you protect, who wants it, what they can reach, and which defense answers each one."},{"term":"sandbox","def":"Android keeps each app apart: one app can’t open another app’s own storage."},{"term":"rooted phone","def":"A phone whose owner gave themselves full control of it. They can read every app’s files and change what apps do."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where should “may Ada delete this recipe?” be decided?","options":["On the server, for every request","In the app, by hiding the button","In DataStore"],"a":0,"why":"A changed copy of the app can skip any check in it. The server sees every request and can answer 403."},{"q":"What does the sandbox protect Recipes’ files from?","options":["Other apps on the same phone","Someone on the same Wi-Fi","The owner of a rooted phone"],"a":0,"why":"The sandbox keeps apps apart. Network traffic and a rooted phone need other defenses."},{"q":"What is a threat model?","options":["A short list of what you protect, who wants it and what they can reach","A tool that finds bugs","A kind of encryption"],"a":0,"why":"It is a plan, not a tool. It shows which defenses matter for your app."}]},{"id":"at-rest","title":"Data at rest","tagline":"Encrypt saved data, so a copied file is only noise.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what encryption, a key and an IV are","Encrypt and decrypt a text with AES-GCM","Explain why the key must not sit next to the data"],"remember":"Encrypt with AES-GCM and a fresh IV saved in front of the ciphertext; the data is only as safe as the place the key lives.","terms":[{"term":"data at rest","def":"Data saved on a disk, such as the DataStore file, as opposed to data travelling over the network."},{"term":"encryption","def":"Turning data into bytes that look like noise, with a key. Only the same key turns them back (decrypting)."},{"term":"key","def":"The secret random number that encrypts and decrypts. Whoever has it can read the data."},{"term":"IV","def":"A fresh random value for each encryption, 12 bytes for AES-GCM. Not secret: it is saved next to the ciphertext."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why save the IV next to the ciphertext?","options":["Decrypting needs it, and it isn’t secret","It is the key","Base64 needs it"],"a":0,"why":"The IV only makes each result different. Without the key it is useless to an attacker."},{"q":"Recipes encrypts the tokens and saves the key in the same DataStore file. Someone copies the file. What do they get?","options":["The tokens: the key is right there","Noise they can’t read","Only the IV"],"a":0,"why":"A key next to the data is a lock with the key taped to it. The key needs a home of its own."},{"q":"You decrypt with the wrong key. What happens with GCM?","options":["<code>doFinal</code> throws <code>AEADBadTagException</code>","You get random-looking text","You get the token anyway"],"a":0,"why":"GCM checks the result. A wrong key or a changed byte fails the check."}]},{"id":"keystore","title":"Keystore","tagline":"A key your app can use but never read, kept in the phone’s secure hardware.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Make an AES key inside the Android Keystore","Encrypt the session tokens before they go into DataStore","Explain what an attacker gets from a copied file"],"remember":"Make the key inside the Android Keystore and look it up by its alias; save only Base64 ciphertext in DataStore.","terms":[{"term":"Android Keystore","def":"Android’s safe for keys. It makes and keeps them, often in secure hardware, and does the encrypting itself; the app never sees the key bytes."},{"term":"alias","def":"The name a key is kept under in the Keystore, such as <code>\"session_key\"</code>."},{"term":"secure hardware","def":"A locked-off part of the phone’s chip, or a separate security chip (StrongBox), that the rest of Android can’t look into."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where are the bytes of a Keystore key?","options":["Inside the Keystore, often in secure hardware; never in your app","In the DataStore file","In the APK"],"a":0,"why":"Your app holds a handle. The data goes in to the Keystore and the result comes out."},{"q":"After an update, decrypting a saved token throws. What should <code>Session</code> do?","options":["Treat Ada as signed out, so she signs in again","Crash, so the bug gets noticed","Send the encrypted text as the token"],"a":0,"why":"A token that can’t be read is as good as none. One more sign-in fixes it."},{"q":"Why look the alias up before making a key?","options":["Making a key under the same alias replaces it, and older tokens can’t be decrypted","The Keystore allows only one lookup","Looking up is needed to encrypt faster"],"a":0,"why":"Make the key once, then reuse it. A new key can’t open what the old one encrypted."}]},{"id":"network","title":"Network security","tagline":"HTTPS keeps requests private and proves you reached the real server.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what HTTPS protects on a café Wi-Fi","Turn plain HTTP off with a network security config","Explain what certificate pinning is and why Android advises against it"],"remember":"HTTPS everywhere, written down in the network security config; debug-only rules go in debug-overrides, and avoid pinning unless you must.","terms":[{"term":"HTTPS","def":"HTTP sent through TLS: requests are encrypted on the way, and the server proves its name with a certificate."},{"term":"certificate","def":"A signed card a server shows, saying which name it belongs to. A certificate authority the phone trusts signs it."},{"term":"network security config","def":"An XML file, named in the manifest with <code>android:networkSecurityConfig</code>, with the app’s rules for connections."},{"term":"certificate pinning","def":"Accepting only certificate keys listed in the app in advance. Android’s guide advises against it, because a server change can lock old app versions out."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the server’s certificate prove?","options":["That the server really is recipes.example.com","That the app is signed","That the token is still fresh"],"a":0,"why":"A trusted authority signed it for that name. A fake server can’t get one."},{"q":"Why does Android’s guide advise against certificate pinning?","options":["When the server changes certificate authority, pinned app versions can’t connect until they update","It makes requests slower","It turns HTTPS off"],"a":0,"why":"The pins are inside every installed copy. Changing them needs an app update."},{"q":"A test server you run has a certificate the phone doesn’t trust. What is a safe fix?","options":["Trust your own test certificate in <code>debug-overrides</code>, or fix the server’s certificate","Trust every certificate","Switch to <code>http://</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>debug-overrides</code> only applies to the debug build. The other two turn protection off for everyone."}]},{"id":"secrets","title":"API keys and secrets","tagline":"Anything inside the APK is public, so real secrets stay on the server.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why a key in the APK can be read by anyone","Move a paid service’s key behind your own server","Keep a key out of Git with local.properties"],"remember":"Everything in the APK is public: real secrets stay on your server, and keys that must ship are limited to your app and kept out of Git.","terms":[{"term":"API key","def":"A password for apps that a service gives you. Requests that carry it are allowed, and often billed to you."},{"term":"secret","def":"Anything that must not be known outside your team, such as a paid service’s key. It can’t live in the APK."},{"term":"local.properties","def":"A file in the project’s top folder for settings of this computer only. A new project’s <code>.gitignore</code> already leaves it out of Git."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where can a paid service’s key live safely?","options":["On your server","In <code>BuildConfig</code>","In <code>local.properties</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Both other places end up in the APK or on a laptop. Only the server keeps it out of reach."},{"q":"What does <code>local.properties</code> protect a key from?","options":["Being pushed to Git","Being read from the APK","Being renamed by R8"],"a":0,"why":"It keeps the key out of the repository. Once it is in <code>BuildConfig</code>, it is inside the APK."},{"q":"A map key has to ship in the app. What helps most?","options":["Limit the key to your app’s id and signing key in the service’s console","Split it into two texts","Encode it with Base64"],"a":0,"why":"A copy of a limited key is useless in another app. Splitting and encoding only slow a reader down for a minute."}]},{"id":"credential-manager","title":"Signing in","tagline":"One system sheet for passwords and passkeys, with nothing to phish.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what a passkey is and why a fake site can’t use it","Ask Credential Manager for a saved password or passkey","Send the answer to your server and save the tokens"],"remember":"Ask Credential Manager with a password option and a passkey option, send what comes back to your server, and fall back to the form on GetCredentialException.","terms":[{"term":"Credential Manager","def":"The Jetpack API for signing in. It shows one system sheet with the user’s saved passwords and passkeys for your app."},{"term":"passkey","def":"A key pair that replaces a password. The private key stays in the user’s password manager, the server keeps the public key, and it only works for its own domain."},{"term":"challenge","def":"A new random text the server sends for each sign-in. The phone signs it, so an old answer can’t be used again."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the server keep for Ada’s passkey?","options":["Only the public key","The private key","Her password"],"a":0,"why":"The public key can only check signatures. A stolen copy of it signs nothing."},{"q":"Why is there a new challenge for every sign-in?","options":["So an old signed answer can’t be sent again","So the sheet looks different","Because passkeys run out"],"a":0,"why":"Each signature only fits its own challenge. A copied answer is useless the next time."},{"q":"Which context should <code>getCredential</code> get?","options":["The Activity, for example <code>LocalContext.current</code> in a composable","The application context","None"],"a":0,"why":"The sheet opens over a screen, so it needs the Activity the user is looking at."}]},{"id":"biometric","title":"Biometrics","tagline":"Ask for a fingerprint, a face or the screen lock with BiometricPrompt.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Lock the app behind Android’s fingerprint, face or screen lock prompt","Check that the phone can do it before you ask","Explain what your app learns from the prompt"],"remember":"Check canAuthenticate, then show BiometricPrompt with what counts; your app only learns that it succeeded.","terms":[{"term":"biometric","def":"Something about your body the phone can check, such as a fingerprint or a face."},{"term":"BiometricPrompt","def":"Android’s own prompt that asks for a fingerprint, a face or the screen lock, and tells your app whether it succeeded."},{"term":"device credential","def":"The phone’s screen lock: its PIN, pattern or password. <code>DEVICE_CREDENTIAL</code> accepts it."},{"term":"FragmentActivity","def":"A <code>ComponentActivity</code> with support for fragments, which BiometricPrompt uses inside."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does your app learn from Ada’s fingerprint?","options":["Only that it matched","An image of the fingerprint","Which finger she used"],"a":0,"why":"The fingerprint stays in secure hardware. The app gets <code>onAuthenticationSucceeded</code>, nothing more."},{"q":"Why does the app lock use <code>BIOMETRIC_WEAK or DEVICE_CREDENTIAL</code>?","options":["<code>BIOMETRIC_STRONG</code> with <code>DEVICE_CREDENTIAL</code> doesn’t work on Android 10 and lower","Weak sensors are faster","Strong sensors need the internet"],"a":0,"why":"Recipes runs from Android 8.0, so it uses the pair that works on every version it supports."},{"q":"Why does <code>MainActivity</code> extend <code>FragmentActivity</code> now?","options":["BiometricPrompt needs one","Hilt needs it","Compose needs it"],"a":0,"why":"BiometricPrompt from androidx.biometric is built on fragments. <code>setContent</code> keeps working, because a FragmentActivity is a ComponentActivity too."}]},{"id":"privacy","title":"Privacy","tagline":"Collect only what a feature needs, and ask for less.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Let the user pick one photo with no permission at all","Ask for approximate location when a city-sized area is enough","Explain what the Data safety form asks"],"remember":"Collect only what the feature needs: the photo picker instead of all photos, approximate instead of precise location, and an honest Data safety form.","terms":[{"term":"data minimization","def":"Collecting only the data a feature needs, and keeping it only as long as it needs."},{"term":"photo picker","def":"Android’s own screen for choosing photos. The app gets only the ones the user picks, with no permission."},{"term":"approximate location","def":"A location good to an area of about 3 square kilometers, from <code>ACCESS_COARSE_LOCATION</code>."},{"term":"Data safety form","def":"The form in Google Play where you declare which data your app collects and shares, and why. It is shown on the app’s store page."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the Data safety form ask about?","options":["Which data leaves the phone, why, and who it is shared with","How fast the app starts","Which Android versions it supports"],"a":0,"why":"It includes data sent by the libraries in your app, and every app on Google Play fills it in."},{"q":"An app asks for precise location on Android 12. What can Ada choose?","options":["Approximate instead, so the app must work with that","Only precise or nothing","Her street name"],"a":0,"why":"Since Android 12 the user picks how precise. Plan for approximate."},{"q":"The picker gave the app a <code>Uri</code>. How long can it read the photo?","options":["Until the phone restarts or the app stops, so copy it to keep it","Forever","For one second"],"a":0,"why":"The access is short on purpose. Copy the photo into <code>filesDir</code> if the recipe should keep it."}]}]},{"id":"build","title":"Build and release","description":"How Gradle builds your app, which versions and variants it makes, and how a signed App Bundle reaches users through Google Play, step by step.","requires":["android"],"file":"tracks/build.js","lessons":[{"id":"gradle","title":"Gradle","tagline":"A build is a chain of tasks, and Gradle skips every task whose work is already done.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Read the task lines that <code>./gradlew</code> prints","Explain when Gradle skips a task: <code>UP-TO-DATE</code> and <code>FROM-CACHE</code>","Turn on the build cache in <code>gradle.properties</code>"],"remember":"A build is a chain of tasks. Gradle skips a task whose inputs and outputs didn’t change (UP-TO-DATE) and copies outputs it already has in the build cache (FROM-CACHE).","terms":[{"term":"task","def":"One piece of build work with a name, such as <code>compileDebugKotlin</code>. A command like <code>assembleDebug</code> needs many tasks, which Gradle runs in order."},{"term":"inputs and outputs","def":"The files and settings a task reads, and the files it writes. Gradle compares them with the last build to decide whether the task must run."},{"term":"UP-TO-DATE","def":"Printed for a task Gradle skipped, because its inputs and outputs are the same as in the last build in this folder."},{"term":"build cache","def":"Task outputs Gradle keeps in your home folder, filed under their inputs. Turned on with <code>org.gradle.caching=true</code>. A task whose outputs are copied from it shows <code>FROM-CACHE</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You run <code>./gradlew assembleDebug</code> twice and change nothing in between. What does the second build do?","options":["Every task is <code>UP-TO-DATE</code>, so it takes a few seconds","It builds everything again, to be safe","It refuses: the APK already exists"],"a":0,"why":"Every task finds the same inputs and outputs as in the last build, so Gradle skips all of them."},{"q":"After <code>./gradlew clean</code>, many tasks show <code>FROM-CACHE</code>. Why not <code>UP-TO-DATE</code>?","options":["<code>clean</code> deleted the outputs, but the build cache still had outputs for the same inputs","<code>clean</code> also emptied the cache","The tasks ran again, faster"],"a":0,"why":"<code>UP-TO-DATE</code> needs the outputs on disk from the last build. <code>clean</code> removed them, so Gradle copied them from the cache in the home folder instead."},{"q":"You change one line in a Kotlin file of <code>:app</code>. Which tasks do work?","options":["The Kotlin compile task and the tasks that use its output","Every task of the build","Only <code>assembleDebug</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Only tasks whose inputs changed run. The resource tasks read other files, so they stay <code>UP-TO-DATE</code>."}]},{"id":"catalogs","title":"Version catalogs","tagline":"One list of versions for every module, and a way to see which version you really get.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Move a library from typed strings in several modules into the catalog","Explain why a misspelt <code>libs</code> name fails before anything is downloaded","Find the version Gradle really uses with <code>./gradlew :app:dependencies</code>"],"remember":"Every module asks for versions through the one catalog; ./gradlew :app:dependencies shows what Gradle really picked.","terms":[{"term":"dependency tree","def":"The list of every library a module uses, with the libraries they bring along, printed by <code>./gradlew :app:dependencies</code>."},{"term":"version conflict","def":"Two parts of the project asking for different versions of the same library. Gradle picks the highest and shows it with an arrow, as in <code>1.10.2 -&gt; 1.11.0</code>."},{"term":"releaseRuntimeClasspath","def":"The name Gradle gives the list of libraries inside the release app. Pass it to <code>dependencies</code> with <code>--configuration</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You type <code>libs.kotlinx.corutines.test</code> in a build file. When do you find out?","options":["At once: Gradle can’t compile the build file, and Android Studio underlines the name","Only after Gradle has asked every server for the library","When the tests run"],"a":0,"why":"The <code>libs</code> names are Kotlin code that Gradle makes from the catalog. A wrong name is an unresolved reference in the build file."},{"q":"The tree shows <code>kotlinx-coroutines-core:1.10.2 -&gt; 1.11.0</code>. What does it mean?","options":["Something asked for 1.10.2, and Gradle picked 1.11.0","The app has both versions","An update to 1.11.0 is available"],"a":0,"why":"The arrow marks a version that was changed: another part asked for a higher one, and the highest wins."},{"q":"Why does a catalog help most when a project has several modules?","options":["Every module reads the same versions from one file","Modules can’t have dependencies without it","It makes the app smaller"],"a":0,"why":"With typed strings each build file has its own numbers, which drift apart. The catalog is one list that every module uses."}]},{"id":"api-levels","title":"API levels","tagline":"compileSdk, minSdk and targetSdk each answer a different question.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Say what <code>compileSdk</code>, <code>minSdk</code> and <code>targetSdk</code> each change","Guard a call that is newer than <code>minSdk</code> after a <code>NewApi</code> lint error","Plan a <code>targetSdk</code> update the way Google Play requires it"],"remember":"compileSdk: what the code can use. minSdk: who can install, and every newer call needs a check. targetSdk: which behaviour changes apply, raised only after testing.","terms":[{"term":"compileSdk","def":"The Android version whose classes and functions the code is compiled against. It changes what you can call, not how the app runs on phones."},{"term":"behaviour change","def":"Something a new Android version does differently. Some apply to every app, others only to apps whose <code>targetSdk</code> is that version or higher."},{"term":"NewApi","def":"The lint error on a call that needs a newer API level than <code>minSdk</code>. Fix it with a check on <code>Build.VERSION.SDK_INT</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Lin raises <code>compileSdk</code> from 36 to 37 and changes nothing else. What changes for users?","options":["Nothing on their phones: the code can now call Android 17 functions","Android 17’s behaviour changes now apply","Android 16 phones can’t install the app any more"],"a":0,"why":"<code>compileSdk</code> only decides what the compiler knows. Who can install follows <code>minSdk</code>, and behaviour changes follow <code>targetSdk</code>."},{"q":"Recipes targets API 36 and runs on an Android 17 phone. Which Android 17 changes apply?","options":["Only the changes for all apps","All of them","None: the app runs as on Android 16 in every way"],"a":0,"why":"Changes meant only for apps that target 37 stay off until <code>targetSdk</code> is 37. Changes for all apps apply anyway."},{"q":"Since August 31, 2026, what must a new phone app or an app update target to be accepted by Google Play?","options":["API 36 or higher","API 34 or higher","The newest API level"],"a":0,"why":"New apps and updates must target Android 16, API 36. Watches, cars and TVs have lower numbers."}]},{"id":"variants","title":"Build variants","tagline":"Build types times flavors: several versions of the app from one project.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Add two product flavors, one for each server","Count the build variants and build one of them","Give one flavor its own files in a flavor folder"],"remember":"Flavors say what is built, build types how. Every pair is a variant with its own tasks, and the most specific source folder wins.","terms":[{"term":"product flavor","def":"One version of what the app is, such as <code>staging</code> or <code>prod</code>, made with <code>create(...)</code> inside <code>productFlavors { }</code>."},{"term":"flavor dimension","def":"A group of flavors, such as <code>server</code>. Each build variant picks one flavor from every dimension."},{"term":"build variant","def":"One flavor paired with one build type, such as <code>stagingDebug</code>. Each variant has its own tasks, like <code>assembleStagingDebug</code>."},{"term":"applicationIdSuffix","def":"Text added to the end of the application id for one flavor or build type, so that version installs as a separate app."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A server dimension with <code>staging</code> and <code>prod</code>, and the build types <code>debug</code> and <code>release</code>. How many build variants are there?","options":["4","2","6"],"a":0,"why":"Each flavor is paired with each build type: 2 × 2 = 4."},{"q":"Which task builds the debug version that talks to the test server?","options":["<code>assembleStagingDebug</code>","<code>assembleDebug</code> with a staging setting","<code>assembleStaging</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Each variant has its own tasks, named after the flavor and then the build type."},{"q":"Why can <code>stagingDebug</code> and <code>prodDebug</code> be installed on the same phone?","options":["They have different application ids","Debug apps never replace each other","They have different names on the home screen"],"a":0,"why":"<code>applicationIdSuffix</code> gives staging its own id, so Android treats it as another app."}]},{"id":"modules","title":"Gradle modules","tagline":"implementation or api: what other modules see, and what a change makes Gradle rebuild.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Choose between <code>implementation</code> and <code>api</code> for a dependency","Fix a “Cannot access class” error between modules","Predict which modules compile again after a change"],"remember":"implementation keeps a dependency private; api passes it on to every module above. Use api only for what your public functions show.","terms":[{"term":"api","def":"A way to depend on a module or library that passes it on: every module that depends on yours sees it too."},{"term":"public functions","def":"Functions and classes other modules can call. The classes they take or return must be visible to the callers."}],"quiz":[{"q":"When should <code>:core:data</code> use <code>api</code> for a dependency?","options":["When its public functions take or return that dependency’s classes","For every library, so nothing is missing","Never: only the app module may use it"],"a":0,"why":"Callers must see the classes they get back. Everything else stays private with <code>implementation</code>."},{"q":"Room is an <code>implementation</code> dependency of <code>:core:data</code>. Room is updated. Which modules compile again?","options":["Only <code>:core:data</code>","<code>:core:data</code> and every module above it","All modules"],"a":0,"why":"Nothing above <code>:core:data</code> sees Room, so nothing above it needs compiling again."},{"q":"<code>:core:model</code> is <code>api</code> in <code>:core:data</code>. Sam adds a field to <code>Recipe</code>. What compiles again?","options":["<code>:core:model</code>, <code>:core:data</code> and every module that depends on <code>:core:data</code>","Only <code>:core:model</code>","Only <code>:core:data</code>"],"a":0,"why":"With <code>api</code>, every module above <code>:core:data</code> sees <code>:core:model</code>, so a change there reaches all of them."}]},{"id":"signing","title":"Signing","tagline":"Your upload key, Google’s app signing key, and why a lost key isn’t the end.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Make an upload key with <code>keytool</code> and keep it out of Git","Sign the release build with it in <code>signingConfigs</code>","Explain what the upload key and the app signing key each do"],"remember":"You sign uploads with your upload key; Google signs what phones get with the app signing key. A lost upload key can be reset. Keep the key and its passwords out of Git.","terms":[{"term":"keystore","def":"A file, locked with a password, that holds one or more keys, each under a name: its alias. <code>keytool</code> makes one."},{"term":"upload key","def":"Your key, which signs every bundle you upload. Play Console checks it, and can reset it if it is lost or stolen."},{"term":"app signing key","def":"The key Google keeps and signs the APKs for phones with. Every update must be signed with it, for the whole life of the app."},{"term":"Play App Signing","def":"Google Play keeping your app signing key and signing what it sends to phones. Required for new apps."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which key do phones check when an update of Recipes installs?","options":["The app signing key","The upload key","The debug key"],"a":0,"why":"Play sends APKs signed with the app signing key. The upload key only proves to Play Console that an upload came from you."},{"q":"Sam builds a release with a new key he made himself and uploads it. What happens?","options":["Play Console refuses it: it isn’t the upload key Play knows","Play accepts it, because Play signs everything again anyway","It is accepted, but phones won’t install it"],"a":0,"why":"Play Console checks every upload against the registered upload key. A different key needs an upload key reset first."},{"q":"Where should the keystore file live?","options":["Outside the project folder, with a backup","In <code>app/</code>, next to the build file","In Git, so the whole team has it"],"a":0,"why":"Anything in the project folder can end up in Git. The keystore and its passwords stay on the computers that build releases, and a backup keeps you from losing it."}]},{"id":"bundles","title":"App bundles","tagline":"Upload one .aab, and each phone downloads only the parts it needs.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Build the App Bundle you upload with <code>./gradlew bundleProdRelease</code>","Explain how Play turns one bundle into split APKs for each phone","Try on your own phone what Play would send, with <code>bundletool</code>"],"remember":"Upload one .aab. Play cuts it into a base APK and configuration APKs, so each phone downloads only its own density, languages and processor parts.","terms":[{"term":"Android App Bundle","def":"The <code>.aab</code> file you upload to Google Play. It holds all the code and resources, and Play makes the APKs for each phone from it."},{"term":"split APKs","def":"Several APKs that install together as one app: a base APK plus configuration APKs for one density, the phone’s languages and its processor."},{"term":"density","def":"How sharp a screen is: how many pixels fit in an inch. Pictures come in a size for each density, like <code>xxhdpi</code>."},{"term":"bundletool","def":"Google’s command-line tool that makes, from a bundle, the APKs a phone would get from Play, and installs them."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What do you upload to Google Play for a new app?","options":["An Android App Bundle, <code>.aab</code>","One APK for every phone","The keystore and the code"],"a":0,"why":"Since August 2021, new apps are published with a bundle. Play makes the APKs from it."},{"q":"Why is the download from a bundle smaller than one APK with everything?","options":["Each phone gets only its own density, languages and processor parts","Play compresses the code harder","Pictures are left out"],"a":0,"why":"The base APK is the same for everyone. The configuration APKs carry only what that phone uses."},{"q":"How do you put on your phone exactly what Play would send it?","options":["<code>bundletool build-apks</code> with <code>--connected-device</code>, then <code>bundletool install-apks</code>","<code>adb install app-release.aab</code>","Run in Android Studio"],"a":0,"why":"A phone can’t install a bundle. bundletool cuts it for the connected phone and installs the parts together."}]},{"id":"tracks","title":"Release tracks","tagline":"Internal, closed, open, production: reach a few people first, then everyone, step by step.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Say who each of the four tracks reaches","Roll out to production in stages, and halt a bad release","Give every upload a higher <code>versionCode</code>"],"remember":"Release step by step: internal, closed, then production in stages. Halt a bad rollout and fix forward with a higher versionCode.","terms":[{"term":"track","def":"A release channel in Play Console: internal testing, closed testing, open testing or production. It decides who can get a release."},{"term":"staged rollout","def":"A production release that Play gives to a share of users you pick, which you raise step by step."},{"term":"halt","def":"Stopping a staged rollout with <b>Manage rollout › Halt rollout</b>: no more users get that version, and those who have it keep it."},{"term":"versionCode","def":"The number from the project files lesson that orders the versions of an app. On Play, every upload needs a new, higher one; the highest Play allows is 2100000000."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which track gets a new version to the team fastest?","options":["Internal testing","Open testing","Production"],"a":0,"why":"Internal testing is for up to 100 testers, and a new bundle reaches them within minutes."},{"q":"A staged rollout is at 20%, and you halt it. What happens?","options":["No more users get it; the 20% keep it","The 20% go back to the old version","The version goes to everyone at once"],"a":0,"why":"Halting stops the rollout. Play can’t take an update back from phones that have it."},{"q":"Production has versionCode 21. You publish versionCode 20 to closed testing. What do the closed testers get?","options":["21, from production: the highest of their tracks","20, because they are testers","Both, side by side"],"a":0,"why":"Everyone can get production, and users get the highest <code>versionCode</code> of all their tracks. A test version below production reaches nobody."}]}]},{"id":"kmp","title":"Kotlin Multiplatform","description":"Share the Recipes app’s Kotlin with an iPhone app: targets and source sets, expect and actual, shared networking with Ktor, a shared Room database, shared ViewModels, Compose Multiplatform and calling Kotlin from Swift.","requires":["kotlin","arch"],"file":"tracks/kmp.js","lessons":[{"id":"what-kmp","title":"What Kotlin Multiplatform is","tagline":"Write the app’s rules once in Kotlin and run them on Android and on the iPhone.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what Kotlin Multiplatform shares and what each platform keeps","Name the targets a shared module is built for","Read the layout of a KMP project: shared, androidApp and iosApp"],"remember":"KMP compiles one Kotlin module for several targets: Android gets its usual code, the iPhone gets machine code in a framework.","terms":[{"term":"Kotlin Multiplatform","def":"Compiling one Kotlin module for several platforms, its <em>targets</em>, like the Android target or <code>iosArm64</code> for real iPhones, so the apps share that code. KMP for short."},{"term":"JVM","def":"The Java Virtual Machine: a program that runs compiled Java and Kotlin (JVM bytecode) on computers. Android converts that bytecode for its own runtime, ART; iPhones run neither."},{"term":"Kotlin/Native","def":"The Kotlin compiler that turns Kotlin into machine code, for platforms with no JVM or ART, such as the iPhone."},{"term":"framework","def":"A bundle of compiled code an Xcode project can use. The shared module’s iPhone builds are packed into one, here <code>Shared</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does an iPhone run from the shared module?","options":["Kotlin code for a small JVM on the phone","Machine code in a framework, made by Kotlin/Native","The Kotlin source files, read as the app runs"],"a":1,"why":"iPhones have no JVM and no ART. Kotlin/Native compiles the shared code to machine code and packs it into a framework."},{"q":"Which target is the iPhone simulator on a Mac with an Apple chip?","options":["<code>iosArm64</code>","<code>iosSimulatorArm64</code>","The Android target"],"a":1,"why":"<code>iosArm64</code> is for real iPhones; the simulator needs its own build, <code>iosSimulatorArm64</code>."},{"q":"With AGP 9, where does <code>MainActivity</code> go?","options":["In <code>shared</code>","In its own <code>androidApp</code> module","In <code>iosApp</code>"],"a":1,"why":"The shared module is a library for every target. The Android app, with its activities, is a separate module that depends on it."}]},{"id":"shares","title":"commonMain and platform code","tagline":"Each file goes in the folder for the platforms it can run on.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Put code in commonMain, androidMain or iosMain","Tell which source sets a build compiles","Decide which parts of an app to share"],"remember":"commonMain goes into every target; androidMain only into Android; iosMain only into the iPhone builds.","terms":[{"term":"source set","def":"A folder of a KMP module whose code goes into some of the targets, like <code>commonMain</code> or <code>iosMain</code>."},{"term":"commonMain","def":"The source set compiled for every target. It may only use Kotlin and multiplatform libraries."},{"term":"platform code","def":"Code that uses one platform’s own classes, like <code>Build</code> on Android or <code>UIDevice</code> on iOS. It goes in <code>androidMain</code> or <code>iosMain</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which source set may use <code>Context</code>?","options":["<code>commonMain</code>","<code>androidMain</code>","<code>iosMain</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>Context</code> is an Android class, so only code that goes into the Android build may use it."},{"q":"What can a file in <code>iosMain</code> call?","options":["Code in <code>iosMain</code> and <code>commonMain</code>","Only code in <code>iosMain</code>","Code in every source set, <code>androidMain</code> too"],"a":0,"why":"A file sees its own source set and <code>commonMain</code>. <code>androidMain</code> isn’t in the iPhone build at all."},{"q":"Which of these belongs in <code>commonMain</code>?","options":["<code>MainActivity</code>","A WorkManager worker","<code>quickLabel(recipe: Recipe)</code>"],"a":2,"why":"<code>quickLabel</code> is plain Kotlin and works on every target. The other two are Android only."}]},{"id":"expect","title":"expect and actual","tagline":"Common code asks for something, and each platform supplies it.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Declare an expect function in commonMain and its actual in each platform","Explain what the compiler checks about the pairs","Choose between expect/actual and an interface"],"remember":"expect promises it in commonMain; each target keeps the promise with one actual, or that target doesn’t build.","terms":[{"term":"expect","def":"Marks a declaration in <code>commonMain</code> with no body: a promise that every target supplies one."},{"term":"actual","def":"Marks the platform’s version of an <code>expect</code> declaration, in the same package, with the same name, parameters and type."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>iosMain</code> has no actual for <code>platformName</code>. What happens?","options":["The iPhone build fails; the Android build still works","The iPhone app crashes when it calls it","The iPhone uses the Android actual"],"a":0,"why":"The compiler checks every target. The one without an actual doesn’t build."},{"q":"What does an <code>expect fun</code> contain?","options":["A default body the platforms may replace","Only its name, parameters and return type","A body for each platform"],"a":1,"why":"The <code>expect</code> has no body. The bodies are in the <code>actual</code> functions."},{"q":"Sharing needs an Android <code>Context</code>, and tests need a fake. What do the Kotlin docs recommend?","options":["An <code>expect class</code>","An interface in <code>commonMain</code> and one class per platform, passed in","Copying the code into each app"],"a":1,"why":"Each class takes what it needs in its constructor, and a test can pass a fake that implements the interface."}]},{"id":"data","title":"Shared networking with Ktor","tagline":"Ktor sends Recipes’ requests from commonMain; each platform supplies only the engine.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Call a server from commonMain with a Ktor HttpClient","Give each platform its own Ktor engine","Explain why Retrofit and other JVM-only libraries can’t go in commonMain"],"remember":"In commonMain, Ktor’s HttpClient sends the requests; only the engine is per platform: OkHttp on Android, Darwin on the iPhone.","terms":[{"term":"Ktor","def":"JetBrains’ multiplatform HTTP client. <code>HttpClient</code> sends requests; plugins like <code>ContentNegotiation</code> add JSON decoding."},{"term":"engine","def":"The part of Ktor that does the network work on one platform: OkHttp on Android, Darwin on the iPhone. Created with <code>OkHttp.create()</code> or <code>Darwin.create()</code>."},{"term":"kotlinx.io","def":"Kotlin’s multiplatform input/output library, which Ktor brings along. Its <code>IOException</code> is what shared code catches; on Android it is <code>java.io.IOException</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can’t the shared repository keep using Retrofit?","options":["Retrofit only runs on the JVM, and <code>commonMain</code> is also built for the iPhone","Retrofit can’t decode JSON","Room doesn’t allow it"],"a":0,"why":"Every library in <code>commonMain</code> must work on every target. Ktor does; Retrofit doesn’t."},{"q":"What does a Ktor engine do?","options":["Decodes JSON into data classes","The platform’s network work: OkHttp on Android, Darwin on the iPhone","Saves the answer in Room"],"a":1,"why":"The client and its plugins are shared. Only the engine that talks to the network is per platform."},{"q":"Shared code wants to handle “offline”. Which exception does it catch?","options":["<code>kotlinx.io.IOException</code>","<code>java.io.IOException</code>","None: Ktor returns an empty list"],"a":0,"why":"<code>java.io</code> is a JVM package, missing on the iPhone. Ktor throws <code>kotlinx.io.IOException</code>, which exists on every target."}]},{"id":"local-db","title":"Room in shared code","tagline":"Room 3 keeps the saved recipes in commonMain; each platform only says where the file goes.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Move Room’s entity, DAO and database to commonMain","Set up BundledSQLiteDriver and Room’s code generator for every target","Build the database and the repository on each platform"],"remember":"Room 3 runs in commonMain with BundledSQLiteDriver and KSP for every target. Only the database file location is per platform.","terms":[{"term":"BundledSQLiteDriver","def":"A Room driver that brings its own SQLite, so the database behaves the same on every platform. It works in <code>commonMain</code>."},{"term":"@ConstructedBy","def":"Names the <code>expect object</code> that makes the database. Room’s KSP code generator writes its <code>actual</code> for every target."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is per platform in the Room setup?","options":["The entity and the DAO","The SQL queries","Where the database file goes"],"a":2,"why":"Entity, DAO and database are in <code>commonMain</code>. Each platform only makes the builder with its own file location."},{"q":"Why does <code>room3-compiler</code> appear three times in <code>dependencies</code>?","options":["Room’s code generator runs once per target, to write each <code>actual</code>","Each copy adds more tables","Gradle needs three copies to be safe"],"a":0,"why":"KSP runs for <code>kspAndroid</code>, <code>kspIosArm64</code> and <code>kspIosSimulatorArm64</code>, and each run writes that target’s <code>actual</code> for the database object."},{"q":"Lin is offline and taps Refresh. What happens to the saved recipes?","options":["They are deleted","They stay: the request throws before anything is written","They are replaced by an empty list"],"a":1,"why":"<code>api.recipes()</code> throws first, so <code>upsertAll</code> never runs and Room keeps its rows."}]},{"id":"viewmodels","title":"Shared ViewModels","tagline":"One RecipesViewModel in commonMain, kept and cleared by each platform.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Move a ViewModel to commonMain with the multiplatform lifecycle library","Explain who keeps and clears a ViewModel on Android and on the iPhone","Keep a shared ViewModel free of Android and JVM classes"],"remember":"The ViewModel class is shared; its owner is not. Android’s Activity clears it for you; on the iPhone, clear its ViewModelStore when the screen closes.","terms":[{"term":"ViewModelStore","def":"Holds ViewModels for one screen. <code>clear()</code> clears every ViewModel in it, which runs <code>onCleared()</code> and cancels <code>viewModelScope</code>."},{"term":"ViewModelProvider","def":"Gets a ViewModel from a store, making it the first time. <code>ViewModelProvider.create(store, factory)[RecipesViewModel::class]</code>."},{"term":"viewModelFactory","def":"Describes how to make a ViewModel: <code>viewModelFactory { initializer { RecipesViewModel(repository) } }</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"On Android, who clears the shared <code>RecipesViewModel</code>?","options":["Swift code","The Activity’s ViewModelStore, when the Activity finishes","Nobody: it lives as long as the app"],"a":1,"why":"<code>viewModel { }</code> puts it in the Activity’s store. Android clears that store when the Activity finishes, not on rotation."},{"q":"On the iPhone, what must happen when the Recipes screen closes?","options":["Nothing: iOS clears ViewModels by itself","The screen calls <code>clear()</code> on the store that holds the ViewModel","The app restarts"],"a":1,"why":"iOS has no Activity to do it. Clearing the store runs <code>onCleared()</code> and cancels <code>viewModelScope</code>."},{"q":"Why does a shared ViewModel take its repository through a plain constructor?","options":["Hilt only runs on Android","Constructors are faster","ViewModels can’t have parameters"],"a":0,"why":"Shared code must compile for the iPhone too, where Hilt doesn’t exist. Each app passes the repository in its own way."}]},{"id":"compose-mp","title":"Compose Multiplatform","tagline":"The same composables draw the screen on Android and on the iPhone.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Move a Compose screen to commonMain with Compose Multiplatform","Show the shared screen from Android and from an iPhone app","Weigh a shared UI against a native one"],"remember":"With Compose Multiplatform, App() lives in commonMain; Android shows it with setContent, the iPhone with ComposeUIViewController.","terms":[{"term":"Compose Multiplatform","def":"JetBrains’ version of Compose that also runs on iOS, desktop and the web. Stable on iOS since 1.8.0."},{"term":"view controller","def":"iOS’s container for one screen, a <code>UIViewController</code>. It plays the role an Activity plays on Android."},{"term":"ComposeUIViewController","def":"Wraps composables in a <code>UIViewController</code>, so an iPhone app can show a Compose screen."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>ComposeUIViewController</code> do?","options":["Starts an Activity on the iPhone","Wraps composables in a view controller the iPhone app can show","Turns SwiftUI code into Compose"],"a":1,"why":"A view controller is iOS’s container for a screen. <code>ComposeUIViewController</code> puts a Compose screen in one."},{"q":"What is the status of Compose Multiplatform on iOS?","options":["Stable since 1.8.0","Alpha","It only runs on Android and desktop"],"a":0,"why":"iOS support became stable in Compose Multiplatform 1.8.0. The web is still Beta."},{"q":"In common code, why do you always write <code>viewModel { RecipesViewModel(repository) }</code>?","options":["Hilt needs it","On iOS the library can’t make a ViewModel from its class alone, so you say how","It makes the screen faster"],"a":1,"why":"The Kotlin docs: on iOS there is no way to make an object from its class alone, so common code passes the <code>{ }</code> that makes the ViewModel."}]},{"id":"swift","title":"Calling Kotlin from Swift","tagline":"How your shared Kotlin looks from the Swift side of the iPhone app.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Call shared classes and functions from Swift","Mark a function with @Throws so Swift can catch its errors","Tell Objective-C headers and Swift export apart"],"remember":"Swift sees Kotlin through an Objective-C header: Int is Int32, top-level functions sit on FileKt, and a suspend function needs @Throws for Swift to catch its errors.","terms":[{"term":"Swift","def":"Apple’s language for iPhone apps. <code>let</code> is like <code>val</code>, <code>func</code> like <code>fun</code>."},{"term":"header","def":"A file in the framework that describes the Kotlin classes and functions, in Objective-C, so Swift can call them."},{"term":"@Throws","def":"Lists the exceptions a function may throw, so Swift gets them as errors it can <code>catch</code>: <code>@Throws(Exception::class)</code>."},{"term":"Swift export","def":"A newer way, Alpha since Kotlin 2.4, to show Kotlin to Swift as Swift code directly, without the Objective-C header."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>fun formatPrice(cents: Int): String</code> is in <code>Prices.kt</code>. How does Swift call it, with the Objective-C header?","options":["<code>PricesKt.formatPrice(cents: 250)</code>","<code>formatPrice(cents: 250)</code>","<code>Shared.formatPrice(250)</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Each Kotlin file becomes a class named after it plus <code>Kt</code>, and its top-level functions sit on that class."},{"q":"What type does Kotlin’s <code>Int</code> have in Swift?","options":["<code>Int</code>","<code>Int32</code>","<code>Double</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Kotlin’s <code>Int</code> is 32 bits. Swift’s <code>Int</code> is bigger, so the value arrives as <code>Int32</code>."},{"q":"What is the status of Swift export in Kotlin 2.4?","options":["The default for every project","Alpha and opt-in; the Objective-C header is still the default","Removed"],"a":1,"why":"Swift export became Alpha in Kotlin 2.4. You turn it on yourself, and it is still changing."}]}]},{"id":"launch","title":"Launch and grow","description":"Keep Recipes healthy once it is on Google Play: crash reports, analytics events, feature flags, in-app updates and reviews, payments with Play Billing, and the Play policies every update must meet.","requires":["build"],"file":"tracks/launch.js","lessons":[{"id":"crashes","title":"Crash reporting","tagline":"Find out why the app crashed on a phone you will never hold.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Add Firebase Crashlytics to Recipes and read a crash report","Add log lines, keys and caught errors to the reports","Explain the crash rates Google Play watches in Android vitals"],"remember":"Crashlytics sends the stack trace at the next start, with real names thanks to the uploaded mapping.txt; Play judges you on user-perceived crashes and ANRs.","terms":[{"term":"Crashlytics","def":"Firebase’s crash reporter. It saves a report when the app crashes and sends it at the next start."},{"term":"non-fatal","def":"An exception your code caught and reported with <code>recordException(e)</code>. The app kept running."},{"term":"Android vitals","def":"Play Console’s numbers for crashes, ANRs and more, from your users’ phones. Play uses them to rate your app’s quality."},{"term":"user-perceived crash rate","def":"The share of daily users who had a crash while the app showed a screen or ran a foreground service."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Crashes from the release build would show names like <code>a.b.c</code>. What turns them back into your class names?","options":["The <code>mapping.txt</code> the Crashlytics plugin uploads with each release build","Installing a debug build on every phone","Nothing: renamed code can’t be read"],"a":0,"why":"<code>mapping.txt</code> lists every old and new name. Crashlytics uses the one uploaded for that build."},{"q":"A WorkManager worker crashes while no screen is open and no foreground service runs. Does that count in the user-perceived crash rate?","options":["No, but it counts in the plain crash rate","Yes, every crash counts there","Only if the user sees a dialog"],"a":0,"why":"User-perceived means the app showed a screen or ran a foreground service. Crashlytics still reports it, so fix it anyway."},{"q":"Overall, 0.6% of daily users see a crash, but one phone model is at 10%. Does Play care?","options":["Yes: one phone model at 8% or more is over a bad behaviour threshold too","No: 0.6% is under 1.09%","Only if that phone is new"],"a":0,"why":"Android vitals has an overall threshold and a per phone model threshold. Play can act on either."}]},{"id":"analytics","title":"Analytics","tagline":"Log what people do in the app, so a question can be answered with numbers.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Log an event with parameters using logEvent","Put changing details in parameters, not in event names","Keep personal data out, and wait for consent when needed"],"remember":"An event name says what the person did; the details that change go in parameters.","terms":[{"term":"event","def":"One thing a person did in the app, with a name and parameters, logged with <code>logEvent</code>."},{"term":"parameter","def":"A key and a value on an event, such as <code>source</code> = <code>step</code>. Once a parameter of your own is registered as a custom dimension, reports can split an event by it."},{"term":"Google Analytics for Firebase","def":"Firebase’s analytics: it collects the events from every phone and adds them up in the console."},{"term":"consent","def":"The user’s yes to collecting data. Where it is needed, collect nothing until you have it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You want to know where timer taps come from. Where does <code>\"step\"</code> go?","options":["In a parameter: <code>source</code> = <code>step</code>","In the name: <code>timer_start_step</code>","In Crashlytics"],"a":0,"why":"One event name with a parameter, registered as a custom dimension, can be split by it in reports."},{"q":"The recipe screen logs <code>timer_start</code> and the step screen logs <code>Timer_start</code>. What does the console show?","options":["Two different events","One event: names are lowercased","An error"],"a":0,"why":"Event names are case-sensitive. Logging every event from one class keeps the spelling the same."},{"q":"Users must agree before Recipes collects analytics. Where do you switch collection off until then?","options":["In AndroidManifest.xml, with the <code>firebase_analytics_collection_enabled</code> setting","In Play Console","Nowhere: Analytics asks the user itself"],"a":0,"why":"The manifest setting stops collection from the very first start. <code>setAnalyticsCollectionEnabled(true)</code> turns it on after the yes."}]},{"id":"flags","title":"Feature flags","tagline":"Ship new code switched off, then switch it on from a server: for some users, all, or none.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what a feature flag and a kill switch are","Read a flag from Firebase Remote Config, with a safe default","Say when a value from the server takes effect"],"remember":"Every flag has an in-app default; a change on the server reaches a phone at its next download and activate.","terms":[{"term":"feature flag","def":"A value read while the app runs that switches a piece of shipped code on or off."},{"term":"kill switch","def":"A flag you set to false on the server to turn off a broken feature without a new release."},{"term":"Remote Config","def":"Firebase’s service for values the app reads from the server, with defaults in the app."},{"term":"Task","def":"Google’s object for an answer that comes later. <code>addOnCompleteListener</code> runs your code when it is done."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Cook mode, behind a flag, crashes for many users. What is the fastest safe fix?","options":["Set <code>cook_mode</code> to false in the console","Publish a new release","Ask users to reinstall"],"a":0,"why":"The kill switch turns the code off on each phone at its next download. A release takes days to reach everyone."},{"q":"Without your own setting, how long does Remote Config wait between downloads?","options":["12 hours","1 minute","1 week"],"a":0,"why":"The default minimum fetch interval is 12 hours. Recipes sets one hour."},{"q":"Why does every parameter need a default in the app?","options":["Until the first download is activated, such as on a first start offline, there is nothing from the server","Remote Config won’t download without one","Defaults make downloads faster"],"a":0,"why":"Until a download is activated, <code>getBoolean</code> answers with the in-app default."}]},{"id":"updates","title":"In-app updates","tagline":"Ask Google Play to update the app, from inside the app.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Check from the app whether Google Play has a newer version","Run a flexible update and finish it with completeUpdate","Choose between a flexible and an immediate update"],"remember":"Flexible by default, immediate only when the old version can’t work; a flexible update installs when you call completeUpdate().","terms":[{"term":"flexible update","def":"An in-app update that downloads in the background while the app stays usable. <code>completeUpdate()</code> installs it."},{"term":"immediate update","def":"A full-screen in-app update: the app can’t be used until Play has installed it and restarted the app."},{"term":"AppUpdateManager","def":"The object from the Play In-App Updates library that checks for updates and starts them."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which kind of update lets Ada keep using the app while it downloads?","options":["Flexible","Immediate","Both"],"a":0,"why":"A flexible update downloads in the background. An immediate update covers the whole app."},{"q":"The listener reports <code>DOWNLOADED</code> for a flexible update. What must the app do?","options":["Show a Restart button that calls <code>completeUpdate()</code>","Nothing: Play restarts it","Start an immediate update"],"a":0,"why":"Play doesn’t restart the app for a flexible update. It waits for <code>completeUpdate()</code>."},{"q":"Ada declined the update. At the next start you want to offer the update again. What do you start the flow with?","options":["A new <code>AppUpdateInfo</code> from <code>appUpdateInfo</code>","The same <code>info</code> as last time","An immediate update"],"a":0,"why":"Each <code>AppUpdateInfo</code> can start one flow. Ask for a fresh one."}]},{"id":"review","title":"In-app review","tagline":"Ask for a rating at a good moment, and let Google Play decide whether to show it.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Show Google Play’s rating card inside the app","Pick a good moment to ask","Explain why the app never learns the result"],"remember":"Ask at a good moment with no question first, then carry on: the review API may show nothing and never reports what happened.","terms":[{"term":"in-app review","def":"Google Play’s rating card, shown on top of your app by the Play In-App Review API."},{"term":"ReviewInfo","def":"What <code>requestReviewFlow()</code> gives you to pass to <code>launchReviewFlow()</code>. It is valid only for a short time."},{"term":"review quota","def":"Play’s limit on how often one person can see the card. The number isn’t published and can change."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The listener after <code>launchReviewFlow</code> runs. What do you know?","options":["Only that the flow is over","That Ada rated the app","That the card was shown"],"a":0,"why":"The API never says whether the card showed or whether a rating was given. Carry on the same way."},{"q":"When is a good moment to call the review flow?","options":["Right after Ada finished cooking a recipe in the app","At the very first start","Right after an error message"],"a":0,"why":"Ask after she got something out of the app, so she has an opinion worth giving."},{"q":"Why not show the card from a “Rate us” button?","options":["Play may not show the card, and the button would seem broken","Buttons can’t call Play libraries","It costs money"],"a":0,"why":"Because of the quota, the flow may show nothing. A button should open the store page."}]},{"id":"billing","title":"Payments","tagline":"Sell Recipes Plus with Google Play Billing: connect, show the price, open Play’s purchase sheet.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Connect a BillingClient to Google Play","Load a product and show its price from Play","Open Play’s purchase sheet with launchBillingFlow"],"remember":"Connect first, load the product from Play and show its formattedPrice, then launchBillingFlow opens Play’s sheet.","terms":[{"term":"Google Play Billing","def":"Play’s way to sell digital things in an app: Play shows the purchase sheet and takes the payment."},{"term":"BillingClient","def":"The object that connects the app to Google Play to load products and start purchases."},{"term":"ProductDetails","def":"What Play returns for a product: its name, its <code>formattedPrice</code> and the offer token you buy it with."}],"quiz":[{"q":"When may the app call <code>queryProductDetailsAsync</code>?","options":["After <code>onBillingSetupFinished</code> reports <code>OK</code>","Before <code>startConnection</code>","Only after a purchase"],"a":0,"why":"The client must be connected to Play first. <code>onBillingSetupFinished</code> says when it is."},{"q":"What does <code>launchBillingFlow</code> do?","options":["Opens Google Play’s purchase sheet over the app","Takes the payment inside Recipes","Unlocks Plus"],"a":0,"why":"Play shows the sheet and takes the payment. The app hears the result in its listener."},{"q":"You send an update to Play in late November 2026. Which Play Billing Library may it use?","options":["Version 8 or newer, such as 9.1.0","Any version","Version 7 is enough"],"a":0,"why":"Since August 31, 2026, new apps and updates need version 8 or newer. The extension ran only to November 1, 2026."}]},{"id":"purchases","title":"Handling purchases","tagline":"Check each purchase on your server, acknowledge it, and catch the ones paid while the app was closed.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Grant only paid purchases that your server has verified","Acknowledge a purchase within three days","Find purchases paid while the app was closed with queryPurchasesAsync"],"remember":"Grant only PURCHASED, let the server verify the token, acknowledge within three days, and ask queryPurchasesAsync at every start.","terms":[{"term":"purchase token","def":"The string that identifies one purchase. Your server checks it with Google Play and saves it."},{"term":"acknowledge","def":"Telling Play that the purchase was granted. Without it, Play refunds the purchase three days after it was paid."},{"term":"pending purchase","def":"A purchase not paid yet, such as cash at a shop. Grant nothing until it becomes <code>PURCHASED</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What happens to a paid purchase that is never acknowledged?","options":["Play refunds it after three days and takes Plus away","Nothing","Play acknowledges it by itself"],"a":0,"why":"Acknowledging tells Play the purchase was granted. Without it, Play refunds after three days."},{"q":"The listener reports a <code>PURCHASED</code> purchase. Who decides to unlock Plus?","options":["Your server, after it checks the token with Google Play","The app, right away","Nobody: Play unlocks it by itself"],"a":0,"why":"A changed copy of the app can fake a purchase. The server checks the token before anything is unlocked."},{"q":"A <code>PENDING</code> purchase is paid while Recipes is closed. What picks it up?","options":["<code>queryPurchasesAsync</code>, after connecting and in <code>onResume</code>","The purchase listener, the next morning","A new <code>launchBillingFlow</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The listener only hears purchases while the app runs. <code>queryPurchasesAsync</code> returns what Ada owns, and <code>handle</code> treats it the same way."}]},{"id":"policy","title":"Play policies","tagline":"A few rules decide whether Google Play accepts your next update.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Check an update against Play’s target API rule for each kind of device","Keep the Data safety form in line with what the app and its libraries send","Spot permissions and foreground services that need a declaration in Play Console"],"remember":"Before each upload: targetSdk meets this year’s rule, the Data safety form matches every library, and sensitive permissions and foreground services are declared.","terms":[{"term":"Data safety form","def":"The form in Play Console that tells users which data the app collects and shares. It must match the app and all its libraries."},{"term":"collected","def":"In the Data safety form: data your app or its libraries send off the phone."},{"term":"shared","def":"In the Data safety form: data passed on to someone else, a third party."},{"term":"declaration","def":"A form in Play Console that explains why the app needs a sensitive permission or a foreground service type."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A Wear OS app sends an update to Play in late November 2026. Which <code>targetSdk</code> must it have at least?","options":["35","36","34"],"a":0,"why":"Phone apps need 36, Wear OS and Android Automotive OS apps 35, Android TV and Android XR apps 34."},{"q":"Recipes targets Android 16 and runs a <code>dataSync</code> foreground service. What does Play Console ask for?","options":["A declaration on the App content page, with a description and a video link","Nothing: the manifest is enough","A new upload key"],"a":0,"why":"Apps that target Android 14 or newer declare each foreground service type they use."},{"q":"You add a library that sends data off the phone. Who must make sure the Data safety form says so?","options":["You","The library’s makers","Google Play, automatically"],"a":0,"why":"What your app’s libraries send counts as your app’s data, and you are accountable for the form."}]}]},{"id":"cs","title":"Data structures and algorithms","description":"Big-O, the core data structures and the classic algorithm patterns, in Kotlin, to write faster code and solve problems with confidence.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/cs.js","lessons":[{"id":"big-o","title":"Big-O: how cost grows","tagline":"Count how the work grows with the input, not the milliseconds.","level":"beginner","remember":"Count the loops that depend on n. Nested loops multiply, loops in a row add, and halving gives log n.","terms":[{"term":"Big-O","def":"A description of how an algorithm’s work grows with input size, keeping only the fastest-growing term."},{"term":"Time complexity","def":"How the number of steps grows as the input grows, for example O(n) or O(n²)."},{"term":"Space complexity","def":"How much extra memory an algorithm needs as the input grows, including the call stack."},{"term":"O(log n)","def":"Growth where doubling the input adds only one more step, typical of halving the problem each time."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A function loops over a list, and inside that loop it loops over the same list again. What is its time complexity?","options":["O(n)","O(2n)","O(n²)"],"a":2,"why":"For each of the n items it does n more steps: n × n."},{"q":"Binary search halves the range on every step. About how many steps for 1,000,000 sorted items?","options":["About 20","About 1,000","About 500,000"],"a":0,"why":"2²⁰ is about 1,048,576, so 20 halvings get from a million down to one."},{"q":"<code>val copy = list.sorted()</code>. How much extra space does it use?","options":["O(1)","O(n), because it returns a new list"],"a":1,"why":"<code>sorted()</code> leaves the original alone and builds a new list of n items. <code>sort()</code> on a mutable list sorts in place."},{"q":"a and b are Lists with n and m items. What is the cost of <code>a.filter { it in b }</code>?","options":["O(n), one loop","O(n × m), because <code>in</code> on a List scans it","O(n + m)"],"a":1,"why":"The filter runs n times and each <code>in</code> walks b. Build <code>b.toHashSet()</code> once to make it O(n + m)."},{"q":"<code>fun sumTo(n: Int): Long = if (n == 0) 0 else n + sumTo(n - 1)</code>. How much extra memory does it use?","options":["O(1)","O(log n)","O(n), one stack frame per call that has not returned yet"],"a":2,"why":"Each call waits for the next one, so n frames sit on the call stack at once. The loop version uses O(1)."}]},{"id":"arrays-lists","title":"Arrays and lists","tagline":"Reading by index is instant. Inserting at the front moves everything.","level":"beginner","remember":"Index and append are cheap. Insert or remove at the front shifts everything: O(n).","terms":[{"term":"Amortized O(1)","def":"An operation that is sometimes slow but cheap on average over many calls, like ArrayList.add."},{"term":"Capacity","def":"How many slots a list’s backing array has, which can be more than the number of items in it."},{"term":"Boxing","def":"Wrapping a primitive like an Int in an object so it can live in a generic collection."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is <code>add()</code> at the end of an ArrayList called O(1) amortized?","options":["It never copies","It copies rarely, so the average cost per add stays constant","The JVM caches it"],"a":1,"why":"A full array is copied into a bigger one, but the growth is by a factor, so total copies stay proportional to the number of adds."},{"q":"Which call is O(n) on an ArrayList of n items?","options":["list[n / 2]","list.add(x)","list.add(0, x)"],"a":2,"why":"Inserting at index 0 shifts every item one slot right."},{"q":"You need cheap adds and removes at both ends. Which type?","options":["ArrayList","ArrayDeque","IntArray"],"a":1,"why":"<code>ArrayDeque</code> is a ring buffer: both ends are O(1) amortized."},{"q":"<pre>var i = 0\nwhile (i &lt; list.size) {\n  if (list[i] &lt; 0)\n    list.removeAt(i)\n  else i++\n}</pre>What is the worst-case cost, and what does it in one pass?","options":["O(n²); <code>list.removeAll { it &lt; 0 }</code>","O(n); it is already fine","O(n log n); sort the list first"],"a":0,"why":"Every removeAt shifts the rest of the list, so many removals cost O(n²). removeAll does the job in one pass."},{"q":"You read <code>linked[5_000]</code> on a java.util.LinkedList of 10,000 items. What does it cost?","options":["O(1), like an array","O(n), it walks node by node from one end","O(log n)"],"a":1,"why":"A linked list has no index math. It follows pointers from the nearer end, and the scattered nodes make the walk slow too."}]},{"id":"hashing","title":"Hash maps and sets","tagline":"Turn a key into a bucket number, and lookups stop depending on size.","level":"intermediate","remember":"equals and hashCode go together. Equal objects must have equal hash codes, or the map looks in the wrong bucket.","terms":[{"term":"Hash code","def":"An Int computed from an object, used by hash maps and sets to pick a bucket."},{"term":"Bucket","def":"One slot in a hash table’s array, holding every entry whose hash maps to that index."},{"term":"Collision","def":"Two different keys landing in the same bucket; equals() is used to tell them apart."},{"term":"Load factor","def":"How full a hash table may get before it grows; the JVM HashMap default is 0.75."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Two keys land in the same bucket. What does HashMap use to tell them apart?","options":["hashCode()","equals()","toString()"],"a":1,"why":"The hash code only picks the bucket. Inside it, keys are compared with <code>equals()</code>."},{"q":"Which of these keeps insertion order when you iterate?","options":["hashMapOf()","mutableMapOf()","HashSet()"],"a":1,"why":"<code>mutableMapOf()</code> returns a <code>LinkedHashMap</code>."},{"q":"Why is HashMap.get O(1) only on average?","options":["Because of garbage collection","If many keys collide in one bucket, the map has to search that bucket","Because Strings are immutable"],"a":1,"why":"With a good hash, buckets hold one or two keys. With a bad one, many keys pile into one bucket."},{"q":"<pre>data class Tag(var name: String)\nval tags = hashSetOf(Tag(\"a\"))\nval t = tags.first()\nt.name = \"b\"\nprintln(t in tags)</pre>What does it print?","options":["true","false","It throws ConcurrentModificationException"],"a":1,"why":"t is filed under the hash it had with name \"a\". The lookup uses the new hash and searches another bucket, so the entry is lost. Keys should be immutable."},{"q":"<pre>class P(val x: Int, val y: Int)\nval s = HashSet&lt;P&gt;()\ns.add(P(1, 2))\ns.add(P(1, 2))\nprintln(s.size)</pre>What does it print?","options":["2","1","It does not compile"],"a":0,"why":"A plain class inherits identity equals and hashCode, so the two points are different keys. A data class would print 1."}]},{"id":"stacks-queues","title":"Stacks and queues","tagline":"Last in, first out for undo and brackets. First in, first out for BFS and jobs.","level":"beginner","remember":"ArrayDeque for both: addLast + removeLast is a stack, addLast + removeFirst is a queue.","terms":[{"term":"Stack (LIFO)","def":"A collection where the last element added is the first one removed."},{"term":"Queue (FIFO)","def":"A collection where the first element added is the first one removed."},{"term":"Deque","def":"A double-ended queue that supports adding and removing at both ends."},{"term":"Ring buffer","def":"An array used in a circle: indexes wrap around from the end to the start, so nothing has to shift."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You push A, then B, then C onto a stack and pop once. What comes out?","options":["A","B","C"],"a":2,"why":"Last in, first out: C was pushed last."},{"q":"Which structure does BFS use to pick the next node?","options":["A stack","A queue","A hash map"],"a":1,"why":"A queue hands out nodes in the order they were found, which is the order of their distance."},{"q":"Why prefer ArrayDeque over LinkedList for a queue?","options":["It is thread-safe","Its elements sit in one array: less memory, faster walks, still O(1) at both ends","It keeps elements sorted"],"a":1,"why":"Neither is thread-safe. <code>ArrayDeque</code> avoids one node object per element."},{"q":"The bracket checker pushes openers and pops on closers. What does it return for <code>([)]</code>?","options":["true, every opener has a closer","false, at the end, because the stack is not empty","false, at <code>)</code>, because the top of the stack is <code>[</code>"],"a":2,"why":"On a closer the checker pops and compares. <code>)</code> pops <code>[</code>, which does not match, so it stops right there."},{"q":"With <code>compileSdk = 35</code> and <code>minSdk = 24</code>, you call <code>history.removeLast()</code> on a MutableList. What happens on an Android 14 phone?","options":["It works, Kotlin provides removeLast()","It returns null","NoSuchMethodError, because the call resolved to the Java method Android 15 added"],"a":2,"why":"With compileSdk 35, Kotlin picks the new Java List.removeLast(), which older devices lack. Use removeLastOrNull() or removeAt(lastIndex); lint’s NewApi flags it."}]},{"id":"two-pointers","title":"Two pointers and sliding window","tagline":"Move two indexes instead of trying every pair, and O(n²) becomes O(n).","level":"intermediate","remember":"Each pointer only moves in one direction, so the whole scan is O(n).","terms":[{"term":"Two pointers","def":"A technique that moves two indexes through an array by a rule, instead of checking every pair."},{"term":"Sliding window","def":"A range [left, right] that moves through an array, growing on the right and shrinking on the left."},{"term":"Complement","def":"The value still needed to reach a target, target − x, often looked up in a hash map."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Two pointers from both ends rely on what?","options":["The array is sorted","The array has no duplicates","The array size is even"],"a":0,"why":"Sorting is what tells you which pointer to move when the sum is too small or too big."},{"q":"Why is a sliding window O(n) even with a while loop inside the for loop?","options":["The while loop runs at most once","Each item enters the window once and leaves at most once","Kotlin optimizes the loop"],"a":1,"why":"<code>left</code> never moves back, so all the inner loop runs add up to at most n steps."},{"q":"Sorted array, pointers at both ends, the sum is too small. What moves?","options":["left moves right","right moves left","both move"],"a":0,"why":"Only a bigger left value can raise the sum."},{"q":"<code>maxSumOfK(intArrayOf(2, 1, 5, 1, 3, 2), k = 3)</code>. What does it return?","options":["8","9","11"],"a":1,"why":"The windows sum to 8, 7, 9 and 6. Each step adds the item entering and subtracts the one leaving, and the best is 9 (5 + 1 + 3)."},{"q":"An unsorted array, and you need the original indexes of two numbers that add up to a target. Which tool fits?","options":["Two pointers from both ends","Sort first, then two pointers","One pass with a hash map from each value you need to its index"],"a":2,"why":"The two-pointer rule needs sorted data, and sorting loses the original indexes. A map of what you have seen finds the pair in O(n)."}]},{"id":"binary-search","title":"Binary search","tagline":"Halve a sorted range on every check: a million items take about 20.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Pick what lo and hi mean, keep it true on every step, and make sure every branch shrinks the range.","terms":[{"term":"Binary search","def":"Finding a value in sorted data by checking the middle and dropping half of the range each time."},{"term":"Insertion point","def":"The index where a missing value would go to keep a list sorted."},{"term":"Lower bound","def":"The first index whose value is greater than or equal to the target."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>listOf(10, 20, 30).binarySearch(25)</code> returns?","options":["-1","-3","2"],"a":1,"why":"25 would go at index 2, so the result is −2 − 1 = −3."},{"q":"What does binary search need from the data?","options":["Sorted in the same order you search with","Stored in a HashMap","No duplicates"],"a":0,"why":"Duplicates are fine. Without sorting, dropping half the range is not safe."},{"q":"Inclusive bounds <code>lo..hi</code>, and <code>a[mid] &gt; target</code>. Which update is right?","options":["hi = mid","hi = mid - 1","lo = mid"],"a":1,"why":"<code>mid</code> is already ruled out and everything right of it is bigger still."},{"q":"<pre>var lo = 0\nvar hi = a.lastIndex\nwhile (lo &lt;= hi) {\n  val mid = (lo + hi) ushr 1\n  if (a[mid] == t) return mid\n  if (a[mid] &lt; t) lo = mid + 1\n  else hi = mid\n}</pre>Which line is the bug?","options":["<code>var hi = a.lastIndex</code>","<code>while (lo &lt;= hi)</code>","<code>else hi = mid</code>"],"a":2,"why":"With inclusive bounds, <code>hi = mid</code> can leave the range the same size when lo == hi, so the loop never ends. Use <code>hi = mid - 1</code>."},{"q":"<code>lowerBound(intArrayOf(10, 20, 20, 30), 20)</code>. What does it return?","options":["1","2","3"],"a":0,"why":"lowerBound returns the first index whose value is at least the target. The first 20 is at index 1."},{"q":"Why can binary search find the smallest truck capacity that ships every parcel in 5 days?","options":["The weights are sorted","The answer to \"can capacity c do it?\" is no up to some point and yes from there on","There are fewer than 1,000 parcels"],"a":1,"why":"Binary search works on any yes/no question that flips once. Each check is O(n), and the search takes O(log(range)) checks."}]},{"id":"sorting","title":"Sorting","tagline":"Use the stdlib sort, know that it is stable, and build comparators that read well.","level":"intermediate","remember":"sorted* is O(n log n) and stable for objects. Build comparators with compareBy and thenBy, not by hand.","terms":[{"term":"Stable sort","def":"A sort that keeps items with equal keys in their original relative order."},{"term":"Comparator","def":"An object that decides the order of two items, built in Kotlin with compareBy and thenBy."},{"term":"TimSort","def":"The merge-sort-based, stable sort the JVM uses for objects; it is fast on data that is already partly sorted."},{"term":"Partition","def":"The quick sort step that puts smaller items left of a pivot and bigger items right of it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does “stable” mean for <code>sortedBy</code>?","options":["It never throws","Items with equal keys keep their original order","It runs in O(n)"],"a":1,"why":"Stability is about ties: equal items do not swap places."},{"q":"Which sort is O(n log n) in the worst case and stable?","options":["Quick sort","Merge sort","Selection sort"],"a":1,"why":"Merge sort always splits in half and merges with ties going left. Quick sort can hit O(n²) and is not stable."},{"q":"You only need the biggest number in a list. Best call?","options":["sortedDescending().first()","maxOrNull()","sorted().last()"],"a":1,"why":"One pass, O(n), and no new list."},{"q":"<code>listOf(\"Ken\" to 2, \"Omar\" to 1, \"Lena\" to 2).sortedBy { it.second }</code>. What is the order of names?","options":["Omar, Ken, Lena","Omar, Lena, Ken","Ken, Lena, Omar"],"a":0,"why":"sortedBy is stable. Ken and Lena both have 2, so they keep their input order: Ken before Lena."},{"q":"The quick sort from the lesson always picks the last item as the pivot. You give it an array that is already sorted. What happens?","options":["It finishes in O(n), since nothing moves","It stays O(n log n)","It degrades to O(n²), because every partition splits off only one item"],"a":2,"why":"The pivot is the biggest item every time, so each partition leaves one side empty. That is n levels of O(n) work."}]},{"id":"trees","title":"Recursion and trees","tagline":"A tree is a node plus smaller trees, so recursive code follows its shape.","level":"intermediate","remember":"Base case first, then recurse on smaller pieces. DFS uses the call stack; BFS uses a queue.","terms":[{"term":"Recursion","def":"A function that solves a problem by calling itself on smaller versions of it."},{"term":"Base case","def":"The input a recursive function answers directly, without calling itself, so the recursion stops."},{"term":"DFS","def":"Depth-first search: follow one branch to the bottom before backing up, using recursion or a stack."},{"term":"Binary search tree","def":"A binary tree where every node’s left subtree holds smaller values and its right subtree bigger ones."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In-order traversal of a binary search tree gives?","options":["The values sorted ascending","The values level by level","The root last"],"a":0,"why":"Left subtree (smaller), then the node, then right subtree (bigger), at every level."},{"q":"What does every recursive function need?","options":["A loop","A base case that stops it","A global variable"],"a":1,"why":"Without one it never stops calling itself."},{"q":"Which order records the root first?","options":["Pre-order","In-order","Post-order"],"a":0,"why":"Pre-order records each node before its children, and the root is visited first."},{"q":"You build a binary search tree by inserting 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 in that order. What does <code>contains(5)</code> cost?","options":["O(n), because the tree has become a line","O(log n), because it is a binary search tree","O(1)"],"a":0,"why":"Search costs O(height). Sorted inserts make every node a right child, so the height is n."},{"q":"A tree can be 100,000 levels deep and each node has two children. Which fix does NOT avoid the StackOverflowError?","options":["Keep your own stack in an ArrayDeque","Mark the function <code>tailrec</code>","Wrap it in DeepRecursiveFunction"],"a":1,"why":"tailrec only works when the recursive call is the very last thing the function does. With two children, one call always has to come back first."}]},{"id":"graphs","title":"Graphs","tagline":"Nodes and edges. Store them as an adjacency list, then walk them with BFS or DFS.","level":"advanced","remember":"Graphs have cycles, so keep a visited set. BFS gives shortest paths when every edge costs the same.","terms":[{"term":"Adjacency list","def":"A graph stored as a map from each node to the list of its neighbours."},{"term":"Visited set","def":"The nodes a search has already seen, kept so cycles don’t make it loop forever."},{"term":"Unweighted shortest path","def":"The path with the fewest edges; BFS finds it because it explores in order of distance."},{"term":"Back edge","def":"In a DFS, an edge to a node that is still on the current path; in a directed graph it means a cycle."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does BFS find the shortest path in an unweighted graph?","options":["It visits nodes in order of their distance from the start","It tries every possible path","It uses recursion"],"a":0,"why":"The queue hands out all distance-1 nodes before any distance-2 node, so the first time it reaches a node is along a shortest path."},{"q":"How much memory does an adjacency list need for V nodes and E edges?","options":["O(V²)","O(V + E)","O(E²)"],"a":1,"why":"One entry per node plus one list item per edge (two for undirected edges)."},{"q":"In DFS cycle detection, you reach a node that is still gray. What does it mean?","options":["There is a cycle","It is a dead end","It is the shortest path"],"a":0,"why":"Gray nodes are on the current path, so you have walked back onto your own path."},{"q":"Each road has a driving time in minutes. You need the fastest route. Which algorithm fits?","options":["BFS, it finds shortest paths","Dijkstra with a priority queue","DFS with a visited set"],"a":1,"why":"BFS counts edges, but a route with more roads can still take fewer minutes. Dijkstra always expands the cheapest node found so far."},{"q":"<pre>val node = queue.removeFirst()\nif (!seen.add(node)) continue\nfor (n in g[node].orEmpty())\n  queue.addLast(n)</pre>The results are right, but it is slow on a dense graph. Why?","options":["It should use a stack","removeFirst() is O(n)","Nodes are marked when taken out, so the same node can sit in the queue many times"],"a":2,"why":"Mark a node the moment you add it to the queue. Then each node is queued once."},{"q":"A social graph has 1,000,000 users who follow about 200 accounts each. How do you store it?","options":["An adjacency list: O(V + E) memory","An adjacency matrix: O(1) edge checks are worth it","A list of all edges, scanned each time"],"a":0,"why":"A matrix would need 10¹² cells for a graph that has about 200 million edges. Sparse graphs fit an adjacency list."}]},{"id":"dp","title":"Dynamic programming","tagline":"Solve each small problem once, store the answer, and build the big one from it.","level":"advanced","remember":"Say what dp[i] means in one sentence, find how it builds from smaller i, set the base case, then fill in order.","terms":[{"term":"Dynamic programming","def":"Solving a problem by combining stored answers to smaller, overlapping subproblems."},{"term":"Memoization","def":"Top-down DP: recursion plus a cache, so each subproblem is computed once."},{"term":"Tabulation","def":"Bottom-up DP: filling a table from the base case upward in a loop."},{"term":"Overlapping subproblems","def":"The same smaller problem showing up many times inside a recursion, which is what makes DP pay off."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Memoization vs tabulation?","options":["Memoization is top-down recursion with a cache; tabulation fills a table bottom-up","They are the same thing","Tabulation always uses recursion"],"a":0,"why":"Both store subproblem answers. They differ in direction and in whether recursion drives the order."},{"q":"Why is the plain recursive <code>ways(n)</code> slow?","options":["Recursion is slow on Android","It solves the same subproblems many times","It uses Long"],"a":1,"why":"The number of calls grows exponentially because <code>ways(k)</code> is recomputed for every path that reaches it."},{"q":"Coins 1, 3 and 4. Fewest coins for 6?","options":["2","3","6"],"a":0,"why":"3 + 3. Greedy picks 4 + 1 + 1, which is 3 coins."},{"q":"<pre>fun ways(n: Int): Long {\n  val memo = HashMap&lt;Int,Long&gt;()\n  if (n &lt;= 1) return 1\n  return memo.getOrPut(n) {\n    ways(n - 1) + ways(n - 2)\n  }\n}</pre>What is wrong?","options":["It returns the wrong answer","Each call makes a new empty memo, so it is as slow as plain recursion","getOrPut cannot be called recursively"],"a":1,"why":"The answers are right, but nothing is ever found in a fresh map. Pass one shared memo through all calls."},{"q":"Which order does the lesson give for solving a DP problem?","options":["Write the code, then find the base case, then name the state","Fill the table, then define the transition, then the state","Define the state, then the transition, then the base case, then the fill order"],"a":2,"why":"Say what dp[i] means first. The transition and base case follow from it, and the fill order makes sure every value you read is already there."},{"q":"<code>gridPaths(3, 3)</code>: paths from the top-left to the bottom-right of a 3 × 3 grid, moving only right or down. What does it return?","options":["6","4","9"],"a":0,"why":"The first row and column are 1. Then dp[1][1] = 2, dp[1][2] = 3, dp[2][1] = 3, and dp[2][2] = 3 + 3 = 6."}]},{"id":"interview","title":"Solving a problem step by step","tagline":"Clarify, try examples, start simple, improve, then test out loud.","level":"beginner","remember":"Clarify, examples, brute force, optimize, test. Say each step out loud before you code it.","terms":[{"term":"Brute force","def":"The simplest correct solution, usually trying every option, used as a starting point."},{"term":"Edge case","def":"An input at the boundary, like empty, one item, or the maximum size, where bugs tend to hide."},{"term":"Trade-off","def":"Gaining one thing, like speed, by giving up another, like memory or simplicity."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What should you do first when you get a problem?","options":["Write the optimal code","Clarify the inputs, outputs and edge cases","Pick the fastest language"],"a":1,"why":"Questions about size, empty input and the no-answer case shape everything after."},{"q":"Why say the brute force solution out loud?","options":["It gives a correct baseline and a Big-O to improve on","Slow code is the goal","It is always the final answer"],"a":0,"why":"A working baseline shows you can solve it, and its cost points at the repeated work to remove."},{"q":"The input is sorted and you need a pair with a given sum. Which tool fits?","options":["Two pointers","Dynamic programming","A stack"],"a":0,"why":"Sorted input lets you move one pointer or the other based on the sum, in O(n)."},{"q":"Which order is the five-step routine?","options":["Brute force, clarify, optimize, examples, test","Clarify, examples, brute force, optimize, test","Examples, optimize, clarify, test, brute force"],"a":1,"why":"Clarifying first can change the problem, examples check your understanding, and the brute force gives a baseline to improve before you test."},{"q":"<code>fun maxGap(a: IntArray) = (1 until a.size).maxOf { a[it] - a[it - 1] }</code>. What does <code>maxGap(intArrayOf(5))</code> do?","options":["Returns 0","Returns 5","Throws NoSuchElementException"],"a":2,"why":"With one item the range 1 until 1 is empty, and maxOf throws on an empty range. One-item and empty inputs are the first tests to try."}]}]},{"id":"capkotlin","title":"Project: a budget tracker","description":"Build one small Kotlin program step by step: it reads expenses, adds them up, saves them and warns you about your budget.","requires":["kotlin","oop","solid"],"kind":"project","builds":"A budget tracker in plain Kotlin: it reads lines like \"12 food lunch\", skips bad ones, shows totals per category, saves to a text file and loads it back, warns when a category goes over its limit, and checks itself with small hand-made tests.","file":"tracks/cap-kotlin.js","lessons":[{"id":"plan","title":"What we’ll build","tagline":"Write down what the program must do, then cut the work into small steps.","level":"beginner","goals":["Turn an idea into a short list of checkable requirements","Cut a program into small steps that each end with code that runs","Decide what to leave out of the first version"],"remember":"Write down what the program must do as checkable sentences, then build it in small steps that each end with a program that runs.","terms":[{"term":"requirement","def":"A short sentence about what a program must do, written so that anyone can check it: “Show the total for each category.”"},{"term":"project scope","def":"What a project includes now, and what it leaves out on purpose (not the same as a variable’s scope)."},{"term":"build step","def":"A small piece of work that ends with a program that runs."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these is a good requirement?","options":["Show the total for each category","Make the code good","Use a lot of Kotlin"],"a":0,"why":"You can run the program and check whether it shows the totals. The others can’t be checked."},{"q":"Why should every step end with a program that runs?","options":["If something breaks, the mistake is in the code you just added","Kotlin can only run short programs","Then you never need to test"],"a":0,"why":"The last version worked, so a new problem comes from the new lines."},{"q":"The plan says amounts are whole numbers. Why leave cents out?","options":["To keep the first version small; cents can be a later step","Kotlin has no decimal numbers","Nobody ever needs cents"],"a":0,"why":"Project scope keeps a project finishable. A first version that works beats a big one that doesn’t."}],"refs":["kotlin.lists","kotlin.list-loop","git.commits"]},{"id":"model","title":"The data model","tagline":"Category, Expense and Tracker: the three types the rest is built on.","level":"beginner","goals":["Describe an expense with a data class and its category with an enum","Keep the list of expenses inside a class, behind a private property","Create, print and copy expenses"],"remember":"Category is an enum, Expense is a data class, and Tracker keeps its list private behind add and all().","terms":[{"term":"data model","def":"The types a program uses to describe its world. Here: <code>Category</code>, <code>Expense</code> and <code>Tracker</code>."},{"term":"List<Expense>","def":"The type of a list of expenses. Through this type you can read the list but not add to it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is the category an enum and not a String?","options":["Only the five categories can exist, so a typo won’t compile","An enum prints faster","A data class can’t hold a String"],"a":0,"why":"The enum is the full list of allowed values. Kotlin rejects anything else."},{"q":"<code>val dinner = lunch.copy(note = \"dinner\")</code>. What is <code>lunch.note</code> now?","options":["<code>\"lunch with Sam\"</code>, as before","<code>\"dinner\"</code>","An error: <code>note</code> is a <code>val</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>copy</code> makes a new object. The original keeps its values."},{"q":"Outside the <code>Tracker</code> class, which line won’t compile?","options":["<code>tracker.add(lunch)</code>","<code>tracker.all().size</code>","<code>tracker.expenses.add(lunch)</code>"],"a":2,"why":"<code>expenses</code> is private. Outside code goes through <code>add</code> and <code>all()</code>."}],"refs":["kotlin.data-classes","kotlin.enums","kotlin.encapsulation-basics","kotlin.mutable-lists","oop.domain-modeling"]},{"id":"input","title":"Reading input","tagline":"Turn a typed line like “12 food lunch” into an Expense, and skip lines that make no sense.","level":"beginner","goals":["Cut a line of text into parts with <code>split</code>","Turn each part into the right type, or <code>null</code> when it doesn’t fit","Skip bad lines without crashing"],"remember":"split(\" \", limit = 3) cuts the line; each part becomes a value or null; a null anywhere means the line is skipped.","terms":[{"term":"split","def":"Cuts text into a <code>List&lt;String&gt;</code> at a separator: <code>\"a b\".split(\" \")</code> is <code>[a, b]</code>."},{"term":"limit","def":"In <code>split</code>, the most parts to make. The last part keeps the rest of the text."},{"term":"lowercase()","def":"The same text in small letters: <code>\"Food\".lowercase()</code> is <code>\"food\"</code>."},{"term":"parse","def":"To read text and turn it into a value of some type, or say that it can’t be done."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is <code>\"9 travel bus to Oslo\".split(\" \", limit = 3)</code>?","options":["<code>[9, travel, bus to Oslo]</code>","<code>[9, travel, bus, to, Oslo]</code>","<code>[9 travel bus to Oslo]</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Three parts at most. The third keeps the rest of the line, spaces included."},{"q":"<code>parseExpense(\"abc food\")</code> gives <code>null</code>. Why doesn’t the program crash?","options":["<code>toIntOrNull()</code> gives <code>null</code> instead of throwing, and the line is skipped","Kotlin skips bad lines by itself","<code>split</code> removes the letters"],"a":0,"why":"<code>toInt()</code> would crash on <code>\"abc\"</code>. <code>toIntOrNull()</code> gives <code>null</code>, which the code checks."},{"q":"Why does <code>parseExpense</code> return <code>Expense?</code> and not <code>Expense</code>?","options":["Some lines make no sense, and <code>null</code> says “no expense here”","Every expense might be empty","Data classes must be nullable"],"a":0,"why":"The <code>?</code> makes the caller deal with a missing answer, which is how bad lines get skipped."}],"refs":["kotlin.null-safety","kotlin.when","kotlin.lists","kotlin.if-expression","kotlin.default-args"]},{"id":"reports","title":"Reports","tagline":"Add up what you spent, in total and per category.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Add up amounts with <code>filter</code>, <code>map</code> and <code>sum</code>","Write the totals as extension functions on <code>List&lt;Expense&gt;</code>","Go through every category with <code>Category.entries</code>"],"remember":"filter narrows, map picks the amount, sum adds; Category.entries lists every category for the report.","terms":[{"term":"report","def":"Numbers worked out from the data, like a total per category."},{"term":"entries","def":"A list of all the entries of an enum, in the order they are written: <code>Category.entries</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is <code>listOf(Expense(5, Category.FUN, \"\"), Expense(7, Category.FUN, \"\")).total()</code>?","options":["<code>12</code>","<code>2</code>","<code>[5, 7]</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>map</code> gives <code>[5, 7]</code>, and <code>sum()</code> adds them."},{"q":"What does <code>Category.entries</code> hold?","options":["All five categories, in the order they are written","Only the categories you have spent on","The number 5"],"a":0,"why":"It lists every entry of the enum, used or not. That is why <code>printReport</code> checks <code>spent &gt; 0</code>."},{"q":"Inside <code>fun List&lt;Expense&gt;.total()</code>, what is <code>this</code>?","options":["The list <code>total()</code> was called on","One <code>Expense</code>","The <code>Tracker</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The value before the dot is the receiver, and <code>this</code> is the receiver."}],"refs":["kotlin.collection-ops","kotlin.extensions","kotlin.lambdas","kotlin.enums"]},{"id":"storage","title":"Saving to a file","tagline":"Keep the expenses when the program stops, and load them next time.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Write lines to a text file and read them back with <code>File</code>","Hide where data is kept behind a <code>Storage</code> interface","Swap the file for a list in memory without changing the Tracker"],"remember":"One line per expense, written with writeText and read back with readLines; the Tracker only knows the Storage interface.","terms":[{"term":"import","def":"Makes a class from somewhere else usable in this file: <code>import java.io.File</code>."},{"term":"File","def":"A file on disk, found by its name or path: <code>File(\"expenses.txt\")</code>."},{"term":"writeText / readLines","def":"<code>writeText(text)</code> writes text into the file, replacing what was there. <code>readLines()</code> reads it back as a list with one item per line."},{"term":"\\n","def":"Inside quotes, a new line. <code>\"a\\nb\"</code> prints <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> on two lines."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>Expense(800, Category.RENT, \"\").toLine()</code> give?","options":["<code>\"800 RENT \"</code>, ending with a space","<code>\"800 RENT\"</code>","<code>\"Expense(amount=800, category=RENT, note=)\"</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The template puts a space before the note, and the note is empty. <code>split</code> later reads it back as an empty note."},{"q":"Why does <code>saveTo</code> take a <code>Storage</code> and not a <code>FileStorage</code>?","options":["So any storage works: a file, memory for tests, later a database","An interface runs faster","A class can’t be a parameter type"],"a":0,"why":"The tracker only needs the promise: save lines, load lines. Who keeps them is a detail it doesn’t depend on."},{"q":"On the first run, <code>expenses.txt</code> doesn’t exist. What does <code>FileStorage.load()</code> give?","options":["An empty list: <code>readLines()</code> throws, and <code>catch</code> returns <code>listOf()</code>","The program crashes","<code>null</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The <code>try</code>/<code>catch</code> turns the missing file into “nothing saved yet”."}],"refs":["kotlin.interfaces","kotlin.exceptions","kotlin.extensions","solid.dip"]},{"id":"rules","title":"Budget rules","tagline":"Set a limit per category, and get a clear answer: no limit, within it, or over.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Keep a limit per category in a <code>Map</code>","Answer with one of a closed set of results, using a sealed interface","Turn each result into text with a <code>when</code> that needs no <code>else</code>"],"remember":"Budget looks up the limit and answers NoLimit, Within(left) or Over(amount); describe handles all three with no else.","terms":[{"term":"Map<Category, Int>","def":"The type of a map whose keys are categories and whose values are <code>Int</code>s."},{"term":"validation","def":"Checking values against rules: is this line a real expense, is this spending within its limit."},{"term":"budget limit","def":"The most you plan to spend in one category."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The limits map has no RENT key. What is <code>limits[Category.RENT]</code>?","options":["<code>null</code>","<code>0</code>","The program crashes"],"a":0,"why":"A missing key gives <code>null</code>. <code>status</code> turns that into <code>NoLimit</code>."},{"q":"Why does the <code>when</code> in <code>describe</code> need no <code>else</code>?","options":["<code>BudgetStatus</code> is sealed, so Kotlin knows all three kinds are handled","<code>else</code> is not allowed with objects","Because it returns a String"],"a":0,"why":"All members of a sealed interface are known, so Kotlin can check that each has a branch."},{"q":"You add <code>class Near(val left: Int) : BudgetStatus</code>. What happens?","options":["<code>describe</code> stops compiling until it handles <code>Near</code>","Near statuses print nothing","Nothing changes"],"a":0,"why":"The <code>when</code> no longer covers every member, and Kotlin points you to it."}],"refs":["kotlin.maps","kotlin.sealed","kotlin.classes","oop.encapsulation"]},{"id":"tests","title":"Testing it","tagline":"Small checks that tell you, after every change, that the program still works.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Write a check as a name plus a true or false answer","Check parsing, totals, budget rules and saving","Use a failing check to find a bug fast"],"remember":"A test compares what the code gives with a value you wrote by hand; run them all after every change.","terms":[{"term":"test","def":"A small piece of code that checks one thing the program must do and reports PASS or FAIL."},{"term":"expected value","def":"What the result should be, written by hand in the test."},{"term":"regression","def":"A bug in something that used to work. Running the tests after each change catches it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a test write <code>parseExpense(\"800 rent\") == Expense(800, Category.RENT, \"\")</code>?","options":["Expense is a data class, so <code>==</code> compares the values","Kotlin compares any two objects by their values","Because the note is empty"],"a":0,"why":"A data class gets <code>equals</code>, so two expenses with the same values are equal."},{"q":"Why does the saving check use <code>MemoryStorage</code>?","options":["It keeps the <code>Storage</code> promise without a file, so the check runs anywhere, in Run it too","Files can’t be tested","It is faster to type"],"a":0,"why":"The tracker only needs a <code>Storage</code>. Any class that keeps the promise can stand in."},{"q":"When should you run the tests?","options":["After every change","Once, when the project is done","Only when something looks broken"],"a":0,"why":"Then a failing check points at the change you just made."}],"refs":["kotlin.functions","kotlin.data-classes","kotlin.lambdas","solid.dip"]},{"id":"recap","title":"Putting it together","tagline":"The whole program in one file, one run from start to finish, and what to build next.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Say what each part of the program does","Follow one run from typed lines to the saved file","Pick a next feature and plan it as one small step"],"remember":"Each part does one job: model, parse, report, store, rules, tests. runOnce wires them into one run.","terms":[{"term":"feature","def":"Something new the program can do, like dates or cents. Add each one as its own small step."},{"term":"end to end","def":"Following one run through every part, from the typed lines to the saved file."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which part decides that FUN is over its limit?","options":["<code>Budget</code>","<code>printReport</code>","<code>parseExpense</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>Budget.status</code> holds the rule. <code>printReport</code> only prints what it answers."},{"q":"Later you want to keep expenses in a database. What has to change?","options":["Add a new class that implements <code>Storage</code>; the tracker stays the same","Rewrite <code>Tracker</code>","Change <code>Expense</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The tracker depends only on the <code>Storage</code> promise, so a new kind of storage plugs in."},{"q":"You want to add dates. What is a good first move?","options":["Write it as a requirement and add a check for it to <code>runTests</code>","Rewrite the whole program at once","Delete the tests, since they will fail"],"a":0,"why":"A small, checkable step, the same way the whole project was built."}],"refs":["capkotlin.plan","solid.srp","oop.cohesion-coupling","git.commits"]}]},{"id":"capapp","title":"News reader","description":"Build one news reader app step by step, from an empty project to an app that keeps working offline.","requires":["compose","flow","arch"],"kind":"project","builds":"News: an Android app that lists headlines from a server, opens each article, refreshes with a pull, keeps working offline from a Room copy, shows clear loading, empty and error screens, and is wired together with Hilt.","file":"tracks/cap-app.js","lessons":[{"id":"setup","title":"Project setup","tagline":"Plan the app, make the project, and list its libraries in one catalog file.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Name what the News app must do and the eight steps that build it","Read a version catalog: versions, libraries and plugins","Add a library to the app through its libs. name"],"remember":"Every library and version lives once in libs.versions.toml; build files use them as libs.…, and the Compose BOM picks the Compose versions.","terms":[{"term":"version catalog","def":"The file <code>gradle/libs.versions.toml</code>: every library and plugin the project uses, with its version, under one name."},{"term":"libs","def":"How build files read the catalog: <code>libs.androidx.material3</code> is the catalog entry <code>androidx-material3</code>."},{"term":"alias","def":"<code>alias(libs.plugins.kotlin.compose)</code> adds a plugin from the catalog to a build file."},{"term":"Compose BOM","def":"A list of Compose versions that work together. Added with <code>platform(...)</code>, it gives every Compose library its version."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The catalog has the entry <code>androidx-navigation-compose</code>. How does <code>app/build.gradle.kts</code> name it?","options":["<code>libs.androidx.navigation.compose</code>","<code>libs.androidx-navigation-compose</code>","<code>\"androidx-navigation-compose\"</code>"],"a":0,"why":"In the <code>libs</code> name each dash becomes a dot."},{"q":"Why does <code>androidx-material3</code> have no version in the catalog?","options":["The Compose BOM supplies it","Gradle always picks the newest one","Material 3 comes with Android Studio"],"a":0,"why":"The BOM, added with <code>platform(...)</code>, gives every Compose library a version that works with the others."},{"q":"Why does every step of this project end with an app that runs?","options":["When something breaks, the cause is in the few lines just added","Gradle can only build small apps","So the version catalog stays short"],"a":0,"why":"The same idea as in the budget tracker project: small steps, each one checked."}],"refs":["android.project-files","android.first-app","arch.layers"]},{"id":"screens","title":"Screens and navigation","tagline":"A list of headlines and a detail screen, with made-up articles for now.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Describe an article with a data class and fill the list with sample data","Build the list and detail screens and move between them with routes","Find one item in a list with firstOrNull"],"remember":"Build the screens with sample data first; a route carries only the id, and the detail screen looks the article up.","terms":[{"term":"sample data","def":"Made-up values typed into the code, like <code>sampleArticles</code>, so screens can be built before the real data exists."},{"term":"firstOrNull","def":"<code>list.firstOrNull { it.id == \"a3\" }</code> gives the first item for which the lambda is <code>true</code>, or <code>null</code> if there is none."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does <code>ArticleDetail</code> carry only the id?","options":["A small route is cheap to save, and the screen looks up the current article","Routes can only hold one property","Strings are faster than data classes"],"a":0,"why":"Navigation keeps every route on the back stack. The id is all the detail screen needs to find the rest."},{"q":"<code>sampleArticles.firstOrNull { it.id == \"zz\" }</code>, and no article has that id. What do you get?","options":["<code>null</code>","The first article","A crash"],"a":0,"why":"The <code>OrNull</code> in the name says it: when nothing matches, the answer is <code>null</code>."},{"q":"Why does <code>ArticleRow</code> take <code>onClick</code> instead of the <code>navController</code>?","options":["The row only reports the tap; <code>NewsNavHost</code> decides what happens","Rows can’t use objects","Compose forbids navigation in rows"],"a":0,"why":"Events go up, as with hoisting. Then the row works anywhere, and only one place knows the routes."}],"refs":["compose.navigation","compose.lazy-keys","compose.scaffold","compose.hoisting"]},{"id":"viewmodel","title":"ViewModel and state","tagline":"A repository interface, a fake behind it, and ViewModels that hold each screen’s state.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Hide where articles come from behind a NewsRepository interface","Give the list a NewsViewModel with one NewsUiState, and the detail screen an ArticleViewModel","Show a spinner while the state is still loading"],"remember":"Screens read state from their ViewModel; the ViewModel reads from a NewsRepository interface, so the data source can change behind it.","terms":[{"term":"fake repository","def":"A class that implements the repository interface with ready-made data, like <code>FakeNewsRepository</code>. Useful before the real data exists, and later in tests."},{"term":"CircularProgressIndicator","def":"Material’s spinning circle. Without a value it spins until it leaves the screen."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In step 5 a repository that reads from Room replaces the network one. Which classes change?","options":["Only the new repository and the place that builds it","Both ViewModels and both screens","Every class in the app"],"a":0,"why":"The ViewModels ask for a <code>NewsRepository</code>, the interface. Any class behind it works."},{"q":"The user opens an article, then goes Back. What happens to its <code>ArticleViewModel</code>?","options":["It is cleared, and its <code>viewModelScope</code> is cancelled","It stays until the app closes","It moves to the list screen"],"a":0,"why":"A ViewModel lives as long as its screen’s place on the back stack. Back removes that place."},{"q":"Why does <code>NewsUiState</code> start with <code>isLoading = true</code>?","options":["Until the first list arrives, the screen should show a spinner, not an empty list","Kotlin requires a Boolean to start true","So the ViewModel loads twice"],"a":0,"why":"An empty list right at the start would look like “no news”. The spinner says “wait”."}],"refs":["arch.viewmodel","arch.ui-state","arch.udf","arch.repository","flow.lifecycle"]},{"id":"network","title":"Loading from the network","tagline":"Real headlines from news.example.com, through Retrofit and a new repository.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Describe the server’s JSON with @Serializable classes, using @SerialName for a different key","Call the API with Retrofit from OnlineNewsRepository","Start a refresh from the ViewModel and catch network errors"],"remember":"ArticleDto matches the server’s JSON (@SerialName for a different key); the repository calls the API, and the ViewModel catches what can fail.","terms":[{"term":"@SerialName","def":"<code>@SerialName(\"published_at\")</code> on a property says which JSON key fills it, when the key and the Kotlin name differ."},{"term":"DTO","def":"Data transfer object: a class shaped like the server’s data, like <code>ArticleDto</code>. The app turns it into its own <code>Article</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The server adds a new key, <code>\"image\"</code>, that <code>ArticleDto</code> doesn’t have. What happens with <code>Json { ignoreUnknownKeys = true }</code>?","options":["The key is skipped and everything else is read","A <code>SerializationException</code>","Retrofit sends the request again"],"a":0,"why":"That is what <code>ignoreUnknownKeys</code> is for: a newer server doesn’t break an older app."},{"q":"Which class had to change so that the ViewModel can ask for fresh articles?","options":["The <code>NewsRepository</code> interface got <code>refresh()</code>","The screen got a Retrofit object","<code>NewsUiState</code> got the API"],"a":0,"why":"The ViewModel talks only to the interface, so the new ability goes there. Each repository fills it in its own way."},{"q":"Why is the list empty when the app is opened offline the next day?","options":["The articles were only kept in memory, and the app was closed","Retrofit deletes old answers","The ViewModel clears the list at night"],"a":0,"why":"A <code>MutableStateFlow</code> lives in memory. When the app’s process ends, it is gone."}],"refs":["data.http-basics","data.retrofit","data.net","arch.repository"]},{"id":"cache","title":"Offline cache","tagline":"Keep the articles in Room, and let the network only refresh them.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Turn Article into a Room table with a DAO and a database","Write OfflineFirstNewsRepository: the screens read only Room, refresh() only writes to it","Explain why the app now works offline"],"remember":"Room is the single source of truth: the screens read only the database, refresh() only writes to it, and a failed refresh leaves the saved rows alone.","terms":[{"term":"single source of truth","def":"The one place the app reads a piece of data from. Here, the <code>articles</code> table: everything else only writes to it or shows it."},{"term":"offline first","def":"The screens show the local copy at once; the network only refreshes that copy."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The server sends an article whose id is already in the table, with a corrected title. What does <code>upsertAll</code> do?","options":["Updates that row with the new title","Adds a second row with the same id","Throws, because the id exists"],"a":0,"why":"Upsert means update or insert: a row with the same primary key is updated."},{"q":"Where does the list screen get its articles now?","options":["Only from Room, through the repository","From the server, then from Room if offline","From <code>sampleArticles</code>"],"a":0,"why":"One source of truth. The server’s answer reaches the screen only by going through the table."},{"q":"Which classes changed in this step, apart from <code>Article</code> and the new Room classes?","options":["Only the repository and the place that builds it","Both ViewModels","Every screen"],"a":0,"why":"The ViewModels and screens only know <code>NewsRepository</code>, so they keep working with the new one."}],"refs":["data.room","data.room-setup","data.caching","arch.ssot"]},{"id":"hilt","title":"Wiring with Hilt","tagline":"Let Hilt build the API, the database, the repository and the ViewModels.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Set Hilt up with an Application class and an entry point","Tell Hilt how to build NewsApi, NewsDatabase and ArticleDao in a module, and bind the repository","Read the route’s id in ArticleViewModel from SavedStateHandle"],"remember":"Hilt builds the app: @Provides for what you don’t own, @Binds for an interface, @Inject constructor for your classes, and hiltViewModel() on each screen.","terms":[{"term":"SavedStateHandle","def":"Given by Hilt to a <code>@HiltViewModel</code> that asks for it. It holds its screen’s route arguments: <code>savedStateHandle.toRoute&lt;ArticleDetail&gt;()</code>."},{"term":"wiring","def":"The code that builds each object and hands it the objects it needs. With Hilt, the modules and <code>@Inject</code> constructors."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does <code>NewsApi</code> come from a <code>@Provides</code> function instead of <code>@Inject constructor</code>?","options":["Retrofit makes the class behind the interface; you can’t annotate its constructor","Interfaces can’t be used with Hilt","<code>@Provides</code> is faster"],"a":0,"why":"You don’t own that class. A <code>@Provides</code> function tells Hilt how to get one: the Retrofit builder."},{"q":"Where does <code>ArticleViewModel</code> get the article’s id now?","options":["From <code>SavedStateHandle</code>, which holds the route’s arguments","From <code>MainActivity</code>","From the repository"],"a":0,"why":"<code>savedStateHandle.toRoute&lt;ArticleDetail&gt;().id</code> reads the id the list screen navigated with."},{"q":"What did this step change about what the app does?","options":["Nothing: only how its parts are built","It now works offline","It loads faster from the network"],"a":0,"why":"Wiring is about who builds what. The app behaves the same, but adding a screen is now one <code>hiltViewModel()</code> call."}],"refs":["arch.di","arch.hilt","arch.hilt-modules","arch.hilt-scopes","compose.navigation"]},{"id":"states","title":"Loading and errors","tagline":"A spinner, a message, a note over saved news, and pull to refresh.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Add isRefreshing and errorMessage to NewsUiState and set them in refresh()","Pick the right screen for every state with one when","Refresh with a pull, using PullToRefreshBox"],"remember":"One NewsUiState describes every situation; one when, checked top to bottom, picks the screen; PullToRefreshBox turns a pull into refresh().","terms":[{"term":"PullToRefreshBox","def":"A Material box around a scrolling list: pulling down calls <code>onRefresh</code>, and its spinner shows while <code>isRefreshing</code> is <code>true</code>."},{"term":"when without a value","def":"<code>when { a -> ...; b -> ... }</code>: each branch is a condition, checked from the top; the first true one runs."},{"term":"error state","def":"What the screen shows when loading failed: a message with Retry when there is nothing saved, or a note over the saved data."}],"quiz":[{"q":"At the very first launch, the list is empty and <code>isLoading</code> is <code>true</code>. Which branch runs?","options":["The spinner: <code>state.isLoading</code> comes first","“No news yet.”","The note over the list"],"a":0,"why":"A <code>when</code> without a value runs the first true branch, top to bottom."},{"q":"Why does <code>refresh()</code> return at once when <code>isRefreshing</code> is already true?","options":["So two pulls in a row don’t start two downloads","Because Room can only be written once","To hide the spinner"],"a":0,"why":"One refresh at a time. The second pull finds the first still running and does nothing."},{"q":"The server answers 500 and Room has saved articles. What does the user see?","options":["The saved list, with “Server problem (500). Showing saved news.”","The message screen with Retry","An empty list"],"a":0,"why":"An <code>HttpException</code> sets the message, but the list isn’t empty, so the last branch shows it with the note."}],"refs":["arch.ui-state-patterns","data.net","kotlin.when","compose.scaffold"]},{"id":"recap","title":"Putting it together","tagline":"The whole app, one tap at a time, and what to build next.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Follow one refresh through every layer of the app","Say what survives a rotation and what survives Android closing the app","Pick a next feature and add it the same way"],"remember":"Events go up to the ViewModel, the repository writes to Room, Room’s Flow brings the state back down; Hilt builds it all.","terms":[{"term":"end to end","def":"Through every part of the app, from the user’s tap to the server and back to the screen."},{"term":"process death","def":"Android closing an app in the background to free memory. ViewModels are gone; data in Room, and the saved back stack, come back."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The user turns the phone during a refresh. What happens to the refresh?","options":["It keeps running in <code>viewModelScope</code> and finishes","It is cancelled and starts again","It is cancelled and lost"],"a":0,"why":"The ViewModel survives rotation, and so do the coroutines in its scope."},{"q":"Which part is allowed to call <code>NewsApi</code>?","options":["Only <code>OfflineFirstNewsRepository</code>","Any screen that needs fresh data","Both ViewModels"],"a":0,"why":"Dependencies point down. The ViewModels know the repository interface; only the repository knows the API."},{"q":"You add a “saved for later” star to articles. Where do you start?","options":["The lowest layer: a new column in Room, then the repository, the ViewModel, the screen","The screen, with a <code>remember</code> for each star","A new Activity"],"a":0,"why":"Data first, then upward, each layer tested by running the app. A star kept only in <code>remember</code> would vanish on the next launch."}],"refs":["arch.layers","arch.ssot","arch.udf","arch.clean"]}]},{"id":"capquality","title":"Quality pass","description":"Take the finished News app and make it tested, smooth, leak-free, accessible, adaptive and quick to start, one checked step at a time.","requires":["capapp","testing","perf","a11y"],"kind":"project","builds":"The same News app, now with ViewModel and Flow tests, UI tests, rows that skip work they don’t need, no leaked Activity, a TalkBack-friendly Refresh button, a two-pane layout on wide screens, a Baseline Profile for a faster cold start, and a list of quality gates every change must pass.","file":"tracks/cap-quality.js","lessons":[{"id":"unit-tests","title":"Unit tests","tagline":"Pin down what NewsViewModel does today, before the pass changes anything.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Move FakeNewsRepository into src/test and give it switches for tests","Test NewsViewModel’s states with runTest and a test Main dispatcher","Catch a broken refresh() with a failing test"],"remember":"Hand NewsViewModel a fake through its interface, set a test Main dispatcher, run what it launched with advanceUntilIdle(), then check the state.","terms":[{"term":"quality pass","def":"Going over a working app to make it tested, fast and usable by everyone, one checked change at a time."},{"term":"switch (in a fake)","def":"A property a test sets to steer the fake, like <code>failRefresh = true</code> to make <code>refresh()</code> throw."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can a test hand <code>NewsViewModel</code> a <code>FakeNewsRepository</code>?","options":["The ViewModel asks for the <code>NewsRepository</code> interface, and the fake implements it","Hilt swaps it in during tests","Kotlin accepts any class as a parameter"],"a":0,"why":"Any class that implements the interface works. In the app Hilt binds <code>OfflineFirstNewsRepository</code>; in tests, the test passes the fake."},{"q":"In <code>starts with a spinner</code>, why is <code>isLoading</code> still <code>true</code>?","options":["The test dispatcher only queued what <code>init</code> launched; nothing has run it","The fake is slow","<code>runTest</code> waits a second first"],"a":0,"why":"With a <code>StandardTestDispatcher</code>, launched coroutines wait in a queue until the test runs them, for example with <code>advanceUntilIdle()</code>."},{"q":"Where do these tests run?","options":["On the computer, with <code>./gradlew test</code>: no phone needed","On an emulator, with <code>./gradlew connectedAndroidTest</code>","Only inside the release app"],"a":0,"why":"They live in <code>app/src/test</code>: local tests that run on the computer in seconds."}],"refs":["testing.unit","testing.fakes","testing.coroutines","capapp.states"]},{"id":"turbine","title":"Flow tests","tagline":"Check every state the screen goes through, in order, with Turbine.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Collect uiState with Turbine’s test { } and take each state with awaitItem()","Check the order of a first launch: spinner, list with the pull spinner, done","Catch a bug that a test of the end state misses"],"remember":"uiState.test { } queues every state; awaitItem() takes them in order; a state left over at the end fails the test.","terms":[{"term":"state sequence","def":"The states a screen goes through, in order, like spinner, list with the pull spinner, finished list. Turbine tests check it one <code>awaitItem()</code> at a time."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Inside <code>viewModel.uiState.test { }</code>, what does the first <code>awaitItem()</code> return?","options":["The state <code>uiState</code> holds right now","Nothing, until the state changes","The last state the flow will ever have"],"a":0,"why":"A <code>StateFlow</code> always gives a new collector its current value first."},{"q":"The block ends while one state is still in Turbine’s queue. What happens?","options":["The test fails","The state is ignored","Turbine waits for more states"],"a":0,"why":"Turbine checks at the end that every state was taken, so an unexpected state can’t slip by."},{"q":"Why did the missing spinner pass step 1’s tests?","options":["They read the state once, at the end, and the end was the same","They ran on the wrong dispatcher","The fake hid the bug"],"a":0,"why":"A test of the end state can’t see what happened on the way. That is what a state sequence test is for."}],"refs":["testing.flows","flow.hot","capapp.states"]},{"id":"ui-tests","title":"UI tests","tagline":"Hand ArticleListScreen a state, find nodes by their text, tap, check.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Show ArticleListScreen in a test with createComposeRule and a ready-made NewsUiState","Find nodes with onNodeWithText, tap with performClick, check with assertIsDisplayed","Check that taps send the right events up"],"remember":"Hand the stateless screen a state, find nodes by text, act with perform…, check with assert…, and let the lambdas record the events.","terms":[{"term":"merged node","def":"One semantics node that stands for a group, like a <code>clickable</code> row: its texts and its click action together. Finders find it by any of its texts."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can the test show <code>ArticleListScreen</code> without a ViewModel?","options":["It is stateless: it takes a <code>NewsUiState</code> and lambdas","Compose tests make ViewModels by themselves","<code>createComposeRule()</code> starts Hilt"],"a":0,"why":"State down, events up. The screen draws any state you hand it, which is exactly what a test needs."},{"q":"Why does <code>onNodeWithText(\"Researchers map a deep-sea reef\")</code> find something you can click?","options":["<code>clickable</code> merged the row’s texts and its click into one node","Text is always clickable","The finder clicks the nearest button"],"a":0,"why":"The merged node carries the headline, the source and the click action, so <code>performClick()</code> taps the row."},{"q":"Where do these tests live, and how do they run?","options":["<code>app/src/androidTest</code>, on an emulator, with <code>./gradlew connectedAndroidTest</code>","<code>app/src/test</code>, on the computer","Inside the release app"],"a":0,"why":"They run on an emulator or phone: instrumented tests, in <code>src/androidTest</code>."}],"refs":["testing.compose-ui","capapp.states","arch.udf"]},{"id":"recomposition","title":"Fixing recomposition","tagline":"A small feature made every row run again on every pull. Measure it, then give each row less.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Read recomposition counts in Layout Inspector","Explain why a row that takes the whole NewsUiState can never skip","Pass each row only what it shows, and measure again"],"remember":"Measure with Layout Inspector first; then give each composable only the values it shows, of stable types, so Compose can skip it.","terms":[{"term":"recomposition count","def":"In Layout Inspector: how many times a composable ran again, next to how many times Compose skipped it."},{"term":"stable type","def":"A type Compose can compare by content, like <code>String</code>, <code>Boolean</code> or a data class with only <code>val</code>s of stable types. Unstable types, like <code>List</code>, are compared by instance."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why did a row that takes <code>NewsUiState</code> recompose three times per pull?","options":["The pull made three new states, and a new state is never equal to the old one","Rows always recompose during a pull","Room deletes the rows first"],"a":0,"why":"<code>isRefreshing</code> on, the new list, <code>isRefreshing</code> off: three <code>copy()</code> calls, three new parameters for every row."},{"q":"Which tool shows how often each composable recomposed and skipped?","options":["Layout Inspector","Logcat","The version catalog"],"a":0,"why":"With recomposition counts on, Layout Inspector shows both numbers for each composable on the running app."},{"q":"After a refresh, Room returns new <code>Article</code> objects with the same values. Do the rows skip?","options":["Yes: <code>Article</code> is stable, so Compose compares it with <code>equals</code>","No: they are new objects","Only if the list is sorted"],"a":0,"why":"A data class with only <code>val String</code> properties is stable. Equal values mean equal parameters."}],"refs":["perf.compose-perf","perf.frame","compose.recomposition"]},{"id":"leak","title":"Fixing a leak","tagline":"LeakCanary finds an old MainActivity that never went away. Pair every add with a remove.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Add LeakCanary to debug builds and read its leak trace","Explain how a listener kept in an object holds on to a destroyed Activity","Add the listener in onStart, remove it in onStop, and check again"],"remember":"Anything that lives longer than an Activity must not keep it: pair every addListener with a removeListener, and let LeakCanary check.","terms":[{"term":"leak trace","def":"LeakCanary’s report: the chain of references from something that lives forever to the object that should have been freed, each marked <code>Leaking: NO</code>, <code>UNKNOWN</code> or <code>YES</code>."},{"term":"retained object","def":"An object LeakCanary expected to be freed, like an Activity after <code>onDestroy()</code>, that is still in memory."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the destroyed Activity stay in memory?","options":["<code>NewsEvents</code> lives as long as the app, and its list holds a lambda that holds the Activity","Android keeps old Activities for rotation","The Toast is still on screen"],"a":0,"why":"Memory is freed only when nothing that is still alive points at it. The leak trace shows the chain."},{"q":"Why is LeakCanary added with <code>debugImplementation</code>?","options":["It is a tool for developers; the release app that users get shouldn’t include it","It only compiles in debug","It makes the release build faster"],"a":0,"why":"LeakCanary pauses the app to save its memory to a file. That is fine while you test, not for users."},{"q":"Why is the lambda a property now?","options":["So the same lambda can be removed later","Properties are faster","Toasts need a property"],"a":0,"why":"<code>removeListener</code> needs the very lambda that was added. An inline lambda can’t be named again."}],"refs":["perf.leaks","android.lifecycle","compose.disposable-effect"]},{"id":"a11y-pass","title":"Accessibility pass","tagline":"Go through News with TalkBack and large text, and fix what gets in the way.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Walk through the app with TalkBack and the largest font size to find problems","Give pull to refresh a button, and give the rows a clear action label","Keep the fix with a UI test"],"remember":"Try the app with TalkBack and the largest font: every gesture needs a button, every action a name, and a test keeps the fix.","terms":[{"term":"actions","def":"The slot at the end of a <code>TopAppBar</code> for buttons: <code>actions = { TextButton(onClick = onRefresh) { Text(\"Refresh\") } }</code>."},{"term":"onClickLabel","def":"A parameter of <code>clickable</code> that names what a click does. TalkBack reads it as written after “Double tap to”, so write it in lowercase."},{"term":"accessibility pass","def":"Going through an app with TalkBack and large text, fixing what gets in the way, and adding a test so it stays fixed."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>onClickLabel = \"open article\"</code> change?","options":["TalkBack says “Double tap to open article” instead of “Double tap to activate”","The text on the row","The size of the row"],"a":0,"why":"It names the action for screen readers; nothing on screen changes."},{"q":"Why isn’t pull to refresh enough?","options":["With TalkBack, a one-finger drag explores the screen, and nothing announces the pull","Pulling is slower than a button","Pull to refresh doesn’t work offline"],"a":0,"why":"A gesture nobody can find doesn’t exist for that user. The button does the same thing and TalkBack can reach it."},{"q":"At the largest font size, why are the rows still fine?","options":["Text is in <code>sp</code> and the rows have no fixed height, so they grow","Android shrinks the text back","Rows ignore the font size"],"a":0,"why":"Sizes in <code>sp</code> follow the user’s font setting, and a row without a fixed height makes room for it."}],"refs":["a11y.semantics","a11y.targets","a11y.text-scale","capapp.states"]},{"id":"adaptive","title":"Adaptive layout","tagline":"On a wide window, show the list and the article side by side.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Read the window’s width class with currentWindowAdaptiveInfoV2()","Show list and article side by side from 840 dp up, and keep phones as they are","Keep the open article when the window rotates or changes size"],"remember":"Decide by the window’s width class, not the device: from 840 dp, list and article side by side; below, one pane and navigation.","terms":[{"term":"two-pane layout","def":"A list and the item it opened, side by side in one window, used when the window is wide enough."},{"term":"expanded width","def":"The window size class for windows at least 840 dp wide. In code, that 840 is <code>WIDTH_DP_EXPANDED_LOWER_BOUND</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which windows get two panes?","options":["Windows at least 840 dp wide","Every tablet","Every phone in landscape"],"a":0,"why":"The check is about the window, not the device. A tablet in split screen can be narrow; a foldable can be wide."},{"q":"Why not check “is this a tablet”?","options":["The window can change size: split screen, folding, resizing","Android can’t tell tablets apart","Tablets don’t run Compose"],"a":0,"why":"What matters is the space the app has right now, and that can change while it runs."},{"q":"Why does the article pane need no <code>ArticleViewModel</code>?","options":["<code>ArticleDetailScreen</code> takes an <code>Article?</code>, and the list’s state already holds every article","Panes can’t have ViewModels","Hilt doesn’t work in a <code>Row</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The screen is stateless, so any caller can hand it the article it should show."}],"refs":["a11y.window-size","a11y.resizability","a11y.foldables","compose.saveable"]},{"id":"startup","title":"Faster startup","tagline":"Measure the cold start, record a Baseline Profile, and measure again.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Measure News’s cold start with a Macrobenchmark before changing anything","Generate a Baseline Profile and ship it in the app","Report when the list is really drawn with ReportDrawnWhen"],"remember":"Measure the cold start, generate the Baseline Profile, measure again, and keep the benchmark to compare the next release.","terms":[{"term":"Baseline Profile","def":"A list of the classes and methods an app runs at startup, shipped in the app so Android translates them into machine code when it is installed."},{"term":"timeToFullDisplayMs","def":"How long a cold start takes until the app reports it is fully drawn, here with <code>ReportDrawnWhen</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does <code>StartupBenchmark</code> have a test with <code>CompilationMode.None()</code>?","options":["To have a number without the profile to compare with","Because the profile only works the second time","To make the build faster"],"a":0,"why":"Ten starts without the profile, ten with it: the difference between the medians is what the profile gives."},{"q":"What does <code>ReportDrawnWhen { !state.isLoading }</code> add to the measurement?","options":["A “fully drawn” time: when Room has answered and the list screen is drawn","A faster first frame","A crash report"],"a":0,"why":"Without it, only the first frame is measured, and that is the spinner."},{"q":"Why measure the release build, not the debug one?","options":["Users get the release build, and debug builds run differently and slower","Debug builds can’t start","The profile only exists in debug"],"a":0,"why":"Debug builds skip the work that makes release builds fast. Their numbers would mislead you."}],"refs":["testing.baseline","perf.startup","perf.profiler"]},{"id":"recap","title":"Review","tagline":"What the pass changed, and the quality gates that keep it that way.","level":"intermediate","goals":["List what each step added and the check that keeps it fixed","Tell the gates that run on every change from the ones a person runs","Plan the next quality step the same way: measure, change, check"],"remember":"Measure, change one thing, measure again, and leave a check behind; automatic gates run on every change, the rest before each release.","terms":[{"term":"quality gate","def":"A check every change must pass before it is merged, like <code>./gradlew test</code> on every pull request."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is a quality gate?","options":["A check every change must pass before it is merged","A screen that blocks users until the app is fast","A Gradle plugin that writes tests"],"a":0,"why":"Gates catch a broken fix while it is still a pull request."},{"q":"Which gates run automatically on every pull request?","options":["<code>./gradlew test</code> and <code>./gradlew connectedAndroidTest</code>","LeakCanary and Layout Inspector","<code>StartupBenchmark</code> only"],"a":0,"why":"Tests run without a person. LeakCanary and Layout Inspector need someone using the app."},{"q":"A teammate says the list feels slow since last week. What comes first?","options":["Measure: Layout Inspector’s counts, or the profiler","Rewrite the list","Turn off animations"],"a":0,"why":"Measure, then change one thing, then measure again. A guess may fix the wrong thing."}],"refs":["testing.pyramid","capapp.recap","perf.profiler"]}]},{"id":"capship","title":"Ship it","description":"Give the News app breaking-news alerts that open the right story, keep it in sync in the background, harden it, and release it on Google Play.","requires":["capquality","push","background","security","build","launch"],"kind":"project","builds":"The same News app, now with two notification channels, FCM registration, breaking news as data messages, an alert that opens its article with the list behind it, a WorkManager sync that has the story ready, HTTPS-only networking, a signed release bundle shrunk by R8, and a release on Google Play that goes out in stages.","file":"tracks/cap-ship.js","lessons":[{"id":"channels","title":"Notification channels","tagline":"Make room for alerts on the phone, and ask for them at the right moment.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Create a breaking and a local channel in NewsApp.onCreate","Ask for POST_NOTIFICATIONS from a card the reader taps","Pick each channel’s importance before the first release"],"remember":"One channel per kind of alert, importances chosen before release, and the permission asked from a card the reader taps.","terms":[{"term":"breaking news","def":"A big story that is happening right now, worth an alert at once, like a road closed by a storm."},{"term":"asking in context","def":"Asking for a permission at the moment the user wants the feature that needs it, like a tap on <b>Turn on</b> under “Get an alert when big news breaks?”."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does News have two channels instead of one?","options":["So a reader can keep breaking news and switch off local news, or the other way round","Android allows only one notification per channel","FCM needs a channel per message type"],"a":0,"why":"Settings lists each channel with its own switch. With one channel, the only choice is all or nothing."},{"q":"When does News ask for <code>POST_NOTIFICATIONS</code>?","options":["When the reader taps <b>Turn on</b> on the card","In <code>NewsApp.onCreate</code>","When the first push arrives"],"a":0,"why":"Asking in context: the card says what the alerts are for, and the tap starts the dialog."},{"q":"News 1.0 is out. Can 1.1 make the <code>local</code> channel louder with a new importance?","options":["No: the reader owns the channel now, and code can’t make it louder","Yes, the next start applies it","Yes, but only after a restart of the phone"],"a":0,"why":"That is why this step picks the importances before the first release. Changing one later needs a new channel with a new id."}],"refs":["push.notifications","push.channels","security.privacy","capapp.hilt"]},{"id":"tokens","title":"FCM registration","tagline":"Give your server every phone’s installation ID, and keep the list fresh.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Add Firebase Cloud Messaging to News with the BoM and google-services.json","Send the installation ID from onRegistered to your server, in the app’s scope","Explain why every start calls register() again"],"remember":"onRegistered sends the installation ID to your server in the app’s scope; register() on every start repairs a failed upload.","terms":[{"term":"device list","def":"The server’s table of installation IDs, each with the time it last heard from it. The server sends breaking news to every ID in it."},{"term":"stale ID","def":"An installation ID that no longer reaches a phone, for example after a reinstall. FCM says so when the server sends to it, and the server deletes the row."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does <code>onRegistered</code> launch the upload in <code>appScope</code>?","options":["Android stops the service when <code>onRegistered</code> returns; the app’s scope outlives it","Suspend calls are faster there","FCM requires <code>Dispatchers.IO</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A scope of the service would be cancelled with it. <code>AppModule</code> makes one scope for the whole app, and nothing cancels it."},{"q":"Is the installation ID a secret?","options":["No: it is an address for one install; only your Firebase project can send to it","Yes: anyone who has it can send alerts","Yes, so encrypt it in the Keystore"],"a":0,"why":"Sending needs your project’s server credentials, which never leave your server. Step 6 looks at those."}],"refs":["push.fcm","push.fcm-registration","arch.hilt-modules","data.net"]},{"id":"messages","title":"Handling messages","tagline":"Breaking news arrives as a data message, and News posts the alert itself.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Read a breaking or local data message in onMessageReceived","Post it on the right channel with one notification per story","Explain why News uses data messages, not notification messages"],"remember":"Breaking news is a data message: onMessageReceived picks the channel by type and posts one alert per story, tagged with its id.","terms":[{"term":"message type","def":"A key in the data, here <code>type</code>, that says what kind of message it is, so the app knows what to do: <code>\"breaking\"</code> or <code>\"local\"</code>."},{"term":"notification tag","def":"A text that, together with the number, names a posted notification. <code>notify(\"a7\", 0, notification)</code> again replaces only the alert tagged <code>\"a7\"</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"News is in the background. Why would a notification message be a problem?","options":["Android shows it itself and none of News’s code runs","It can’t carry a title","FCM drops it"],"a":0,"why":"Android draws it for you, so News can’t pick the channel by type, link to the article, or start a sync. A data message always reaches <code>onMessageReceived</code>."},{"q":"Why does the server send local news with normal priority?","options":["It can wait; high priority is for messages that show the user something now","Normal priority skips the channel","High priority is only for paid apps"],"a":0,"why":"High priority wakes the phone. FCM may lower the priority of apps whose high-priority messages show nothing, so keep it for news that can’t wait."}],"refs":["push.messages","push.styles","push.notifications"]},{"id":"deeplink","title":"Deep link to content","tagline":"A tap on the alert opens that article, with the list behind it for Back.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Give the ArticleDetail route a deep link with navDeepLink","Open it from the alert with TaskStackBuilder","Explain what the screen shows when the article isn’t in Room yet"],"remember":"The route answers to a link with navDeepLink; the alert opens it through TaskStackBuilder, so Back goes to the list.","terms":[{"term":"parent stack","def":"The screens put under the one a link opens, so Back works as if the user had come from the start. Here: <code>ArticleList</code> under <code>ArticleDetail(\"a7\")</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why can every alert use request code <code>0</code>?","options":["Each story’s Intent has its own link, and Android compares links; only extras don’t count","Request codes are ignored for activities","TaskStackBuilder picks a new code itself"],"a":0,"why":"Two PendingIntents are the same only if their Intents match apart from extras. Different links, different PendingIntents."},{"q":"What does the reader see after pressing Back on the article?","options":["The list of headlines","The home screen","The lock screen"],"a":0,"why":"The parent stack put <code>ArticleList</code>, the start destination, under the article."}],"refs":["push.deeplinks","push.styles","capapp.screens","capapp.hilt"]},{"id":"sync","title":"Background sync","tagline":"WorkManager fetches the story as the alert arrives, and the headlines every few hours.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Write SyncWorker as a @HiltWorker that calls refresh()","Book a one-time sync from onMessageReceived, waiting for a network","Keep the list fresh with a periodic sync"],"remember":"The push books a one-time SyncWorker that waits for a network; a periodic one keeps the list fresh; Room brings the result to the screens.","terms":[{"term":"push-triggered sync","def":"A sync that a push message books, so the data the message talks about is on the phone before the user taps."},{"term":"CONNECTED","def":"<code>NetworkType.CONNECTED</code>: a constraint that waits for any working network, Wi-Fi or mobile data. <code>UNMETERED</code> would wait for Wi-Fi."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why doesn’t the data message carry the whole article?","options":["A message holds at most 4 KB, and the app should fetch the rest itself","FCM can’t carry text","Room only accepts rows from Retrofit"],"a":0,"why":"Send the id and the title; <code>SyncWorker</code> fetches the full story into Room."},{"q":"How do the list and the article screen learn that <code>SyncWorker</code> wrote new rows?","options":["They observe Room through Flow, so they update by themselves","The worker calls the ViewModels","The user has to pull to refresh"],"a":0,"why":"Room is the single source of truth. The worker writes to it; every screen that observes it gets the new rows."}],"refs":["background.workmanager","background.workmanager-hilt","background.receivers","capapp.cache"]},{"id":"harden","title":"Hardening","tagline":"Before strangers install News, check everything it holds, sends and accepts.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Make News HTTPS-only with a network security config","Sort what may ship in the APK from what stays on the server","Check every way into the app: the service, links and messages"],"remember":"HTTPS only, the push key only on the server, and every way in checked: exported=\"false\", ids only as the query’s :id parameter.","terms":[{"term":"security pass","def":"Going over an app before release to check what it holds, what it sends and how others can reach it, and fixing one thing at a time."},{"term":"service account key","def":"The secret file your server uses to prove to Google that it may send messages for your Firebase project. It never goes into the app or into Git."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why doesn’t News use the Keystore yet?","options":["It stores nothing secret: headlines and an installation ID","The Keystore only works for Kotlin Multiplatform","R8 does the same"],"a":0,"why":"Encrypt what would hurt if copied, such as sign-in tokens. News has none; the day it gets accounts, the tokens go through a <code>TokenCipher</code>."},{"q":"Another app on the phone starts <code>MainActivity</code> with <code>https://news.example.com/article/zzz</code>, as the <code>adb shell am start</code> line in step 4 does. What happens?","options":["The article screen opens and stays empty: Room has no row zzz","News crashes","News runs the text as a database query"],"a":0,"why":"The id only goes into the <code>:id</code> parameter of the query. An unknown id finds nothing."}],"refs":["security.threats","security.network","security.secrets","security.keystore"]},{"id":"release-build","title":"Release build","tagline":"An upload key, R8 on, a signed bundle, and a test of what users will run.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Create an upload key and keep it and its password out of Git","Sign the release build with it and build app-release.aab","Test the release build on a phone before uploading"],"remember":"Sign the release with your own upload key, kept out of Git; build the bundle for Play and test the same release code on a phone first.","terms":[{"term":"release check","def":"Installing the release build on a phone and trying every path before uploading it, because R8 only changes the release build."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You lose <code>upload.jks</code>. What now?","options":["Ask for an upload key reset in Play Console; the app signing key phones check stays the same","Publish News as a new app","Nothing: Play doesn’t check the upload key"],"a":0,"why":"With Play App Signing, Google keeps the app signing key. The upload key only proves the upload is yours, so it can be replaced."},{"q":"Why test with <code>assembleRelease</code> and not with Run in Android Studio?","options":["Run installs the debug build, where R8 doesn’t run","Run can’t install on a phone","The debug build has no notifications"],"a":0,"why":"Only the release build is shrunk and renamed by R8, so only it shows what readers will get."},{"q":"What is <code>versionCode</code> for?","options":["Play compares it: each upload needs a higher number than the last","Readers see it in the store","It picks the signing key"],"a":0,"why":"Readers see <code>versionName</code>, like “1.0”. <code>versionCode</code> is the number that orders your uploads."}],"refs":["perf.r8","perf.keep-rules","build.signing","build.bundles","security.secrets"]},{"id":"ship","title":"Shipping","tagline":"Testing tracks first, then production, then updates that go out in stages.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Take News from internal testing to production","Roll out an update in stages and halt it when crashes rise","Keep older versions working with the server’s messages"],"remember":"Testing tracks first, a full first release, then every update in stages, halted when crashes rise and fixed with a higher versionCode.","terms":[{"term":"staged rollout","def":"Releasing an update to a share of users first, like 10%, and raising it while crash numbers stay calm. Only for updates, not the first release."},{"term":"halt (a rollout)","def":"Stopping a staged rollout, so no more users get that version. Users who already have it keep it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does News 1.0 go to internal testing before production?","options":["The team tries the version Play really delivers, with Play’s signature, before readers get it","Production refuses apps that skipped it","Internal testing makes the bundle smaller"],"a":0,"why":"Internal testing reaches up to 100 testers within minutes, through Play: the same path readers will use."},{"q":"Can News 1.0, the first release, use a staged rollout?","options":["No: staged rollouts are for updates; a first release goes out to everyone at once","Yes, from 1%","Only in closed testing"],"a":0,"why":"Play offers staged rollouts for updates. That is one more reason to test well on the testing tracks first."}],"refs":["build.tracks","launch.crashes","launch.policy"]}]},{"id":"cloud","title":"Cloud","description":"Where your app’s server runs: rented machines, Docker images, delivery pipelines, secrets, ready-made backends, monitoring and scaling.","requires":["tools","sysdesign"],"file":"tracks/cloud.js","lessons":[{"id":"what-cloud","title":"What the cloud is","tagline":"Computers you rent, in a place you pick, so your server keeps running when your laptop sleeps.","level":"beginner","goals":["Say what renting a server in the cloud means","Pick a region and a managed database for a small server","Check from the terminal that a server answers, with <code>curl</code>"],"remember":"The cloud is computers you rent: a VM in a region near your users for your server, a managed database for your data, paid for while they run.","terms":[{"term":"the cloud","def":"Computers you rent from a provider, in its data centers, instead of buying and running your own."},{"term":"virtual machine (VM)","def":"A computer made in software on one of the provider’s big machines. You choose its size and pay for every hour it is switched on."},{"term":"region","def":"The area where a provider’s data center is, such as Frankfurt. Choose one close to your users."},{"term":"managed service","def":"A part the provider runs for you, such as a database it installs, updates and backs up. You get an address and a password."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Mei’s VM is switched on all month, but Recipes is mostly used in the evenings. What does she pay for?","options":["Every hour it is switched on","Only the hours with requests","Nothing until it gets busy"],"a":0,"why":"A VM is billed while it is on, used or not. Switching it off, or choosing a smaller one, is what saves money."},{"q":"What does a managed database give Mei?","options":["The provider installs, updates and backs it up; she gets an address and a password","A database inside the app on each phone","A VM on which she installs the database herself"],"a":0,"why":"A managed service is a part the provider runs for you. The server connects to it with its address and password."},{"q":"<code>curl http://localhost:8080/health</code> prints <code>Failed to connect to localhost port 8080</code>. What does that mean?","options":["Nothing on this computer answers at port 8080: the server isn’t running there","The server answered with an error page","The internet connection is down"],"a":0,"why":"<code>localhost</code> is this computer. “Failed to connect” means no program answered there at all."}]},{"id":"docker","title":"Containers with Docker","tagline":"Pack the server with Java and everything else it needs, so it runs the same on every machine.","level":"beginner","goals":["Read a short Dockerfile line by line","Build an image and start a container from it","Connect a port with <code>-p</code> so you can reach the server"],"remember":"A Dockerfile describes an image: FROM, WORKDIR, COPY, CMD. docker build makes it, docker run starts a container, and -p connects a port.","terms":[{"term":"image","def":"A package with everything a program needs to run: basic system files, Java, your JAR and the start command. <code>docker build</code> makes one from a Dockerfile."},{"term":"container","def":"One running copy of an image, kept apart from the other programs on the machine. <code>docker run</code> starts one."},{"term":"Dockerfile","def":"A text file of instructions for building an image: <code>FROM</code>, <code>WORKDIR</code>, <code>COPY</code>, <code>EXPOSE</code>, <code>CMD</code>."},{"term":"port","def":"A numbered door on a computer where a server waits for requests, such as 8080. <code>-p 8080:8080</code> connects a port of your computer to one of the container."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the difference between an image and a container?","options":["An image is the package; a container is one running copy of it","They are two words for the same thing","A container is the package; an image is a running copy"],"a":0,"why":"One image can start many containers, the way one APK installs on many phones."},{"q":"Why does the server start on the VM now, although the VM has an old Java?","options":["Java 21 is inside the image, so the VM’s own Java doesn’t matter","Docker updates the VM’s Java","The fat JAR contains Java"],"a":0,"why":"<code>FROM eclipse-temurin:21-jre</code> puts Java 21 in the image. The VM only needs Docker."},{"q":"What does <code>EXPOSE 8080</code> do?","options":["It notes which port the server uses; it connects nothing","It connects port 8080 of the computer to the container","It starts the server on port 8080"],"a":0,"why":"Only <code>-p 8080:8080</code> on <code>docker run</code> connects a port of your computer to the container."}]},{"id":"pipelines","title":"Pipelines: CI/CD","tagline":"Every change that reaches main is tested, packed into an image and delivered, without anyone typing a step.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain what continuous delivery adds to CI","Read a workflow that tests the server, builds its image and uploads it to a registry","Go back to an earlier version by its image tag"],"remember":"CD: every push to main is tested, built into an image tagged with its commit hash and uploaded to a registry. Going back is running an earlier tag.","terms":[{"term":"continuous delivery, continuous deployment (CD)","def":"After CI’s checks pass on <code>main</code>, the workflow also builds what you ship and delivers it, every time, in the same order. In continuous deployment, it also starts the new version on the server automatically."},{"term":"pipeline","def":"The chain of steps from a push to a running server: test, build, upload, run. A failed step stops the steps after it."},{"term":"registry","def":"An online store for images, such as <code>ghcr.io</code>. Workflows upload (push) images to it; servers download them from it."},{"term":"GITHUB_TOKEN","def":"A password GitHub makes for each workflow run. <code>permissions:</code> says what it may do, such as <code>packages: write</code> to upload images."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The step <code>./gradlew test buildFatJar</code> fails on a push to <code>main</code>. What does the server run afterwards?","options":["The image it already ran: nothing new was built or uploaded","The new code, with the failing test","Nothing: the server stops"],"a":0,"why":"A failed step stops the steps after it. No image is built or uploaded, so the server keeps its current version."},{"q":"Why tag each image with <code>${{ github.sha }}</code>?","options":["You know which commit is running, and can start any earlier one again","It makes the image smaller","GitHub refuses images without it"],"a":0,"why":"The tag is the commit hash. Going back to an earlier version is running the image with that commit’s tag."},{"q":"Where does <code>${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}</code> come from?","options":["GitHub makes it for each run of the workflow","Mei types her own GitHub password into the file","Docker sends it to GitHub"],"a":0,"why":"GitHub creates <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code> automatically for every run. No password is written in the workflow."}]},{"id":"secrets","title":"Secrets","tagline":"Passwords and keys stay out of the code: the server asks for them by name, and a secret manager hands them over.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Read a secret from an environment variable in Kotlin","Keep a workflow’s secrets in GitHub’s repository secrets","Say what a secret manager does and what to do when a secret leaks"],"remember":"Code names a secret, the environment hands over its value: a secret manager in the cloud, repository secrets in GitHub. A leaked secret is rotated, not only deleted.","terms":[{"term":"environment variable","def":"A named value a program receives from whoever starts it. <code>System.getenv(\"NAME\")</code> reads it, or gives <code>null</code>."},{"term":"secret manager","def":"A cloud service that stores secrets encrypted, decides who may read each one, keeps every version and records each read and change."},{"term":"repository secret","def":"A secret stored in a GitHub repository’s settings and used in a workflow as <code>${{ secrets.NAME }}</code>. Its value is hidden in the logs."},{"term":"rotate","def":"Replace a secret with a new value and make the old one stop working. The answer to a leak."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The database password was in a commit that was pushed. What makes the leak harmless?","options":["Rotate it: a new password, and the old one stops working","Delete the line in a new commit","Make the repository private"],"a":0,"why":"Earlier commits and every clone still hold the old password. Only changing it makes that copy useless."},{"q":"What does <code>System.getenv(\"DATABASE_PASSWORD\")</code> give when the variable isn’t set?","options":["<code>null</code>","An empty text, <code>\"\"</code>","A crash"],"a":0,"why":"Its type is <code>String?</code>. That is why the code adds <code>?: throw Exception(...)</code>."},{"q":"A workflow uses <code>${{ secrets.CLOUD_TOKEN }}</code>, but nobody created that secret. What does the step get?","options":["An empty text","An error before the run starts","The value from Mei’s laptop"],"a":0,"why":"GitHub gives an empty text for a secret that isn’t set, so the step fails later, when it tries to use the key."}]},{"id":"baas","title":"Backend as a service","tagline":"Sign-in, a database and file storage that someone else runs, used straight from the app.","level":"beginner","goals":["Say what a backend as a service gives you and what it costs you","Read documents from Cloud Firestore in Kotlin","Protect the data with Security Rules"],"remember":"A BaaS runs sign-in, a database and storage for you. Security Rules are its checks, and keeping its calls in one repository keeps you free to leave.","terms":[{"term":"backend as a service (BaaS)","def":"A backend someone else runs (sign-in, a database, file storage), used from the app through the provider’s library. Firebase is one."},{"term":"Cloud Firestore","def":"Firebase’s database of documents. Each document is a set of named fields; documents are grouped in collections."},{"term":"Security Rules","def":"Rules you write in Firebase that decide who may read or write which data. Firebase checks them on every request from an app."},{"term":"vendor lock-in","def":"How hard and costly it is to move your app and its data to another provider."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Noor wants her own code to run on Firebase’s servers each time a list changes. What does she need?","options":["Cloud Functions, on the pay-as-you-go Blaze plan","Nothing extra: it works on the free plan","Her own VM"],"a":0,"why":"Cloud Functions runs your code on Firebase’s servers, and it needs the Blaze plan."},{"q":"Can an app whose data lives on its own server still use Firebase Cloud Messaging?","options":["Yes: any server can send messages through FCM","No: FCM needs Firestore","Only with Cloud Functions"],"a":0,"why":"FCM is a separate service. In the FCM lesson, your server sends the message; where your data lives doesn’t matter."},{"q":"What keeps vendor lock-in small?","options":["All Firestore calls in one repository class","Calling Firestore from every screen","Using the free plan"],"a":0,"why":"The rest of the app talks to your repository, so switching providers means rewriting one class."}]},{"id":"observability","title":"Logs, metrics and traces","tagline":"When users say “it’s slow”, the server’s own records tell you when, what and where.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Tell logs, metrics and traces apart, and what each one answers","Find the slow part of a request in a trace","Add the OpenTelemetry Java agent to the server’s image"],"remember":"Metrics show when, logs show what, traces show where. OpenTelemetry makes all three; on Java, its agent needs no code changes.","terms":[{"term":"log","def":"A record of one event, with its time, such as <code>GET /v1/recipes/42 200 2412ms</code>."},{"term":"metric","def":"A number measured over time, such as requests per minute or how long they take. Shown in charts and used for alarms."},{"term":"trace, span","def":"A trace follows one request through every part it touches. Each step in it is a span, with a start and an end."},{"term":"OpenTelemetry","def":"An open standard, with free tools, for making logs, metrics and traces that most monitoring services can read."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which signal tells you how many requests failed each minute last week?","options":["A metric","One trace","One log line"],"a":0,"why":"A metric is a number measured over time, made for questions like “how many, and when”."},{"q":"One request was slow. Which signal shows which part of it took the time?","options":["Its trace","The error-count metric","The crash report"],"a":0,"why":"A trace has a span for every step of that request, each with a start and an end."},{"q":"What does the OpenTelemetry Java agent need from you?","options":["Loading it with <code>-javaagent:</code> and a few environment variables","A call to it in every function","A new server written for it"],"a":0,"why":"The agent measures requests, database calls and calls to other services by itself. You start Java with it and set its environment variables."}]},{"id":"scaling","title":"Scaling","tagline":"More traffic: a bigger machine, or more copies of the server behind a load balancer.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Compare vertical and horizontal scaling","Work out how many copies of the server a peak needs","Explain why the copies must keep nothing in their own memory"],"remember":"Vertical: a bigger machine, simple but limited. Horizontal: more stateless copies behind a load balancer, added and removed as traffic changes.","terms":[{"term":"vertical scaling","def":"Giving the server a bigger machine: more processors and memory. No code changes, but there is a limit and it stays one machine."},{"term":"horizontal scaling","def":"Running more copies of the same server. With autoscaling, the cloud adds and removes copies as traffic changes."},{"term":"load balancer","def":"A server in front of the copies that hands each request to one of them, and skips copies that don’t answer <code>/health</code>."},{"term":"stateless","def":"A server that keeps nothing about users in its own memory between requests, so any copy can answer any request."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the catch with vertical scaling?","options":["The biggest machine has a limit, and it is still one machine that can fail","The code has to be rewritten","It only works with containers"],"a":0,"why":"A bigger machine needs no code change, but it can only grow so far, and when it fails, everything is down."},{"q":"Why must the copies be stateless?","options":["The next request may reach another copy, and removed copies lose their memory","Load balancers can’t send requests to servers with memory","Stateless servers are always faster"],"a":0,"why":"Anything kept in one copy’s memory is missing on the others, and gone when the autoscaler removes that copy."},{"q":"At the peak, 2,100 requests per second arrive, and one copy handles 500. How many copies does Recipes need? (Example numbers.)","options":["5","4","2,100"],"a":0,"why":"2,100 ÷ 500 = 4.2. Four copies handle only 2,000, so round up: 5. (In Kotlin, <code>2100 / 500</code> gives 4, because Int division rounds down.)"}]}]},{"id":"concurrency","title":"Concurrency fundamentals","description":"Make a program do two jobs at once with threads, see how shared values go wrong, and fix it with locks, @Volatile and safer tools.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/concurrency.js","lessons":[{"id":"concurrency-parallelism","title":"Concurrency and parallelism","tagline":"Several jobs in progress at once: taking turns, or truly side by side.","level":"beginner","goals":["Start a second thread with <code>thread { }</code> and wait for it with <code>join()</code>","Tell concurrency (taking turns) from parallelism (at the same instant)","Expect the order of two threads to change from run to run"],"remember":"thread { } starts a new thread and join() waits for it. Taking turns is concurrency; running at the same instant on several cores is parallelism. The order is never up to you.","terms":[{"term":"thread","def":"One worker inside a program that runs its own instructions in order. <code>thread { }</code> starts a new one."},{"term":"interleaving","def":"The way the steps of several threads get mixed together when they take turns."},{"term":"concurrency","def":"Several jobs in progress during the same time, even if only one runs at any instant."},{"term":"parallelism","def":"Several jobs running at the very same instant, on different cores."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A phone with one core runs two threads, and both make progress during the same second. What is this?","options":["Concurrency without parallelism","Parallelism","Neither"],"a":0,"why":"Both jobs are in progress at once (concurrency), but one core runs only one of them at any instant, so there is no parallelism."},{"q":"What does <code>worker.join()</code> do?","options":["The current thread waits until <code>worker</code> has finished","It starts <code>worker</code>","It joins two threads into one"],"a":0,"why":"<code>thread { }</code> already started it. <code>join()</code> only waits."},{"q":"You run a program with two threads twice and get a different order of lines each time. Is something broken?","options":["No, the order of threads can change on every run","Yes, Kotlin has a bug","Yes, the phone is too slow"],"a":0,"why":"The operating system decides who runs when, and that changes from run to run. Good concurrent code is correct in every order."}]},{"id":"race-conditions","title":"Race conditions","tagline":"When two threads change the same value, updates can get lost.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why <code>likes++</code> is really three steps","Spot shared state that two threads change","Recognize a lost update in a program’s output"],"remember":"likes++ is read, add 1, write. When two threads change the same variable, one thread’s update can be lost, and only on some runs.","terms":[{"term":"shared state","def":"A variable that more than one thread can read and change."},{"term":"race condition","def":"A bug where the result depends on the order in which threads happen to run."},{"term":"lost update","def":"Two threads read the same old value, and the second write wipes out the first."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>likes</code> is 5. Ada’s thread reads 5. Sam’s thread reads 5 and writes 6. Ada’s thread adds 1 and writes. What is <code>likes</code>?","options":["6","7","5"],"a":0,"why":"Ada’s thread worked from the old 5, so it writes 6 over Sam’s 6. One like is lost."},{"q":"A two-thread counter printed the right total five runs in a row. Is it free of races?","options":["Not necessarily: a bad order can be rare but still happen","Yes, five runs prove it","Yes, if the phone has several cores"],"a":0,"why":"The order changes from run to run. A lost update needs only one unlucky order, maybe on someone else’s phone."},{"q":"Which of these is shared state?","options":["A <code>var</code> that two threads both change","A <code>var</code> inside a function that only one thread runs","A number written in the code, like <code>100</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Shared state is something more than one thread can change. If only one thread touches it, it cannot race."}]},{"id":"locks","title":"Locks","tagline":"Let only one thread at a time into the code that changes shared state.","level":"beginner","goals":["Guard shared state with <code>synchronized(lock) { }</code>","Explain what a critical section is","Make sure every thread uses the same lock"],"remember":"Put every read and change of shared state inside synchronized(lock) { }, with one shared lock object. Only one thread at a time gets in.","terms":[{"term":"lock","def":"An object that only one thread can hold at a time. <code>synchronized(lock) { }</code> holds it for the block."},{"term":"critical section","def":"Code that reads and changes shared state, which only one thread at a time may run."},{"term":"mutual exclusion","def":"The rule that while one thread is in the critical section, every other thread is kept out."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is a critical section?","options":["Code that changes shared state and must run in one thread at a time","Code that runs first in <code>main</code>","Any code inside a lambda"],"a":0,"why":"It is the part where a second thread in the middle would break things, like the three steps of <code>likes++</code>."},{"q":"Ada’s thread uses <code>synchronized(lockA)</code> and Sam’s thread uses <code>synchronized(lockB)</code> around the same <code>likes++</code>. Is it safe?","options":["No, they never wait for each other","Yes, both used <code>synchronized</code>","Only if <code>likes</code> is a <code>val</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Different locks are different doors. Both threads walk right in, so updates can still be lost."},{"q":"Two threads each call <code>synchronized(lock) { likes++ }</code> 1000 times, starting from 0. What does <code>println(likes)</code> print after both are joined?","options":["2000","Something between 1000 and 2000","1000"],"a":0,"why":"With one shared lock, the three steps of each <code>++</code> can no longer be split, so all 2000 likes count."}]},{"id":"deadlock","title":"Deadlock","tagline":"Two threads each hold what the other needs, and both wait forever.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain how two locks can make threads wait in a circle","Prevent deadlock by always taking locks in the same order","Check whether a thread is still running with <code>isAlive</code>"],"remember":"A deadlock is threads waiting for each other’s locks in a circle. Take locks in the same order everywhere and the circle cannot form.","terms":[{"term":"deadlock","def":"Threads waiting for each other’s locks in a circle, so none of them can ever go on."},{"term":"lock ordering","def":"The rule that every thread takes several locks in the same fixed order, which prevents deadlock."}],"quiz":[{"q":"One thread runs <code>synchronized(a) { synchronized(b) { } }</code>, another runs <code>synchronized(b) { synchronized(a) { } }</code>. What can happen?","options":["Both wait forever","Kotlin refuses to compile it","The second thread always waits a little, then goes on"],"a":0,"why":"If each grabs its first lock before the other’s second, each holds what the other needs: a deadlock."},{"q":"Which change prevents that deadlock?","options":["Both threads take <code>a</code> first, then <code>b</code>","Add <code>Thread.sleep(100)</code> before the second lock","Use more threads"],"a":0,"why":"With one fixed order, the thread that waits holds nothing the other needs."},{"q":"What does a deadlocked app usually look like to the person using it?","options":["It freezes, with no error message","It shows a “deadlock” error and closes","It runs slowly but finishes"],"a":0,"why":"The threads are not broken, just waiting forever. Nothing crashes, so nothing reports a problem."}]},{"id":"memory-visibility","title":"Memory visibility","tagline":"A value one thread writes is not always seen by another, unless you ask for it.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why a thread may not see another thread’s write","Mark a shared flag with <code>@Volatile</code>","Know that <code>@Volatile</code> does not make <code>likes++</code> safe"],"remember":"A write on one thread is not sure to be seen by another. @Volatile makes a flag visible to all threads, but it does not make likes++ safe.","terms":[{"term":"cache","def":"A small, fast memory near each core that keeps copies of values, so it does not have to read main memory every time."},{"term":"visibility","def":"Whether a value one thread wrote can be seen by another thread."},{"term":"@Volatile","def":"An annotation that makes every read of a property get the latest value and every write visible to other threads."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which problem does <code>@Volatile</code> solve?","options":["A thread not seeing a value another thread wrote","Two threads losing updates in <code>likes++</code>","Two threads waiting for each other’s locks"],"a":0,"why":"It is about visibility. Lost updates need a lock, and deadlock needs a lock order."},{"q":"A class has the property <code>@Volatile var likes = 0</code>. Two threads each run <code>likes++</code> on it 100000 times. What does it print after both are joined?","options":["Often less than 200000","Always 200000","Always 100000"],"a":0,"why":"Each read and write is visible, but the three steps can still interleave, so updates are still lost."},{"q":"One thread sets the property <code>done</code> to <code>true</code> once; another checks <code>done</code> in a loop. Which property fits best?","options":["<code>@Volatile var done = false</code>","A plain <code>var done = false</code>","<code>val done = false</code>"],"a":0,"why":"One writer, other readers: a volatile flag is exactly this case. A plain <code>var</code> may never be seen, and a <code>val</code> cannot change."}]},{"id":"lock-free-tools","title":"Safer tools","tagline":"Atomic counters, data that never changes, and data only one thread touches.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Count safely from many threads with <code>AtomicInteger</code>","Share data safely by making it immutable","Avoid sharing by keeping a value inside one thread"],"remember":"First try not sharing (confinement) or not changing (immutable data). For a shared counter use AtomicInteger. Use a lock for the rest.","terms":[{"term":"atomic","def":"Done as one step that cannot be split, so no other thread sees it half done. <code>incrementAndGet()</code> is atomic."},{"term":"immutable","def":"Can never change after it is made, like a data class with only <code>val</code> properties."},{"term":"confinement","def":"Keeping a value inside one thread, so no other thread can touch it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which can many threads read at the same time with no lock?","options":["An object of <code>data class Song(val title: String)</code>","A list from <code>mutableListOf()</code> that threads add to","A <code>var count</code> that threads increase"],"a":0,"why":"With only <code>val</code> properties, the object can never change, so there is nothing to race."},{"q":"What does <code>likes.incrementAndGet()</code> do?","options":["Adds 1 in one step that cannot be split, and gives back the new value","Reads the value without changing it","Takes a lock and keeps it"],"a":0,"why":"It is the atomic version of <code>++</code>: read, add and write as one step."},{"q":"A <code>var total = 0</code> is created inside one thread’s lambda, and only that thread uses it. Does it need a lock?","options":["No, it is confined to one thread","Yes, every var needs a lock","Yes, unless it is <code>@Volatile</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Races need at least two threads touching the same value. A confined value has only one."}]}]},{"id":"sweng","title":"Software engineering practice","description":"How teams plan, build and ship software together: life cycle, agile, estimation, design docs, code review, technical debt and working in a team.","requires":["kotlin","git"],"file":"tracks/sweng.js","lessons":[{"id":"sdlc","title":"How software gets built","tagline":"Every change goes through the same five phases, from an idea to users’ phones.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name the five phases: requirements, design, build, test, release","Say what each phase hands to the next","Explain why a mistake found late costs more"],"remember":"Requirements, design, build, test, release: each phase hands something to the next, and a mistake costs less the earlier it is found.","terms":[{"term":"software development life cycle (SDLC)","def":"The phases every change goes through: requirements, design, build, test, release."},{"term":"requirement","def":"A short statement of what the software must do, written so anyone can check it."},{"term":"release","def":"Publishing a new version of the app so users can install it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which phase decides <em>what</em> a feature must do?","options":["Requirements","Design","Test"],"a":0,"why":"Requirements say what; design says how."},{"q":"A requirement was misunderstood. When is it cheapest to find out?","options":["While writing the requirements","During testing","After the release"],"a":0,"why":"Early, only a few sentences change. Later, the design, the code and the tests built on it change too."},{"q":"What does the build phase hand to testing?","options":["A list of what users asked for","Code that is written, reviewed and merged","A version in the app store"],"a":1,"why":"Each phase hands its result to the next. Build produces working code to test."}]},{"id":"agile","title":"Working in iterations","tagline":"Build a small piece, show it, learn, repeat.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why teams ship in short iterations","Use a backlog to choose what comes next","Give a short daily stand-up update"],"remember":"Work in short sprints: pull the top of the backlog, meet for 15 minutes a day, show the result, adjust.","terms":[{"term":"backlog","def":"The ordered list of tickets the team might work on, most important first. A <em>ticket</em> is a card for one piece of work."},{"term":"sprint","def":"One <em>iteration</em> (a short round of requirements, design, build, test and release on a small piece of work) with a fixed length, often two weeks. It starts with planning and ends by showing the finished work, then a retrospective."},{"term":"retrospective","def":"A short meeting at the end of a sprint about what to do differently next time."},{"term":"stand-up","def":"A daily meeting of about 15 minutes: yesterday, today, and anything that blocks you."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the backlog?","options":["The ordered list of work the team might do","The work finished last sprint","A list of bugs only"],"a":0,"why":"Ideas, requests and bugs all go in, most important first."},{"q":"Lin is stuck waiting for the rain data. When should she say so?","options":["At the next stand-up, or sooner","At the end of the sprint","Only if it lasts a week"],"a":0,"why":"A blocker said early can often be solved in minutes. Said late, it has cost days."},{"q":"Why do teams work in short iterations?","options":["Users see something early, and their feedback shapes the next piece","It skips the test phase","No planning is needed any more"],"a":0,"why":"Every phase still happens, on a small piece. The win is learning early."}]},{"id":"estimation","title":"Estimating work","tagline":"Break a job into small pieces, and answer with a range, not one number.","level":"beginner","goals":["Break a feature into small tasks you can picture","Give an estimate as a range","Spot the work that is easy to forget"],"remember":"Break the work into tasks of a day or less, count the work that isn’t code, and answer with a range you update as you learn.","terms":[{"term":"estimate","def":"A best guess of how long a piece of work will take, best given as a range."},{"term":"uncertainty","def":"What you don’t know yet about a piece of work. The more there is, the wider the range."},{"term":"breaking work down","def":"Splitting a feature into small tasks you can each picture from start to finish."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why break a feature into small tasks before estimating?","options":["Small tasks are easier to picture, and splitting turns up forgotten work","It makes the work itself go faster","So that each person gets exactly one task"],"a":0,"why":"You can guess a one-day task far better than a vague two-week one."},{"q":"Which answer is the most useful estimate?","options":["“3 to 6 days; I’ll know more once I see the data.”","“Exactly 4 days.”","“No idea.”"],"a":0,"why":"A range shows the uncertainty, and saying what would narrow it gives the team a plan."},{"q":"Which work is often left out of estimates?","options":["Tests, review comments and checks on different phones","Writing the main code","Choosing a ticket"],"a":0,"why":"It doesn’t feel like “the feature”, but it takes real days."}]},{"id":"design-doc","title":"Design documents","tagline":"Write the plan down, compare the options, and decide before you build.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name the parts of a short design doc","Compare options by their trade-offs","Explain why the doc comes before the code"],"remember":"Before big work, write one page: problem, goals, non-goals, options with their trade-offs, and a decision with its reason.","terms":[{"term":"design doc","def":"A short written proposal, made before the code: problem, goals, non-goals, options, decision."},{"term":"non-goal","def":"Something the team deliberately won’t do in this piece of work, written down so the work doesn’t keep growing."},{"term":"trade-off","def":"What an option gains, and what it pays for it, such as more features for more time."}],"quiz":[{"q":"When is a design doc most useful?","options":["Before the code, while changing the plan is cheap","After the release, to describe what was built","Only when something breaks"],"a":0,"why":"Its value is the cheap disagreement before the expensive work."},{"q":"What is a non-goal?","options":["Something the team deliberately won’t do in this work","A goal that failed","A bug nobody fixed"],"a":0,"why":"Writing it down settles “are we also doing this?” questions before they come up in review."},{"q":"What is a trade-off?","options":["What an option gains, and what it pays for it","Swapping two tickets in the backlog","Always choosing the cheapest option"],"a":0,"why":"Good designs compare trade-offs; the cheapest option is not always right."}]},{"id":"code-review","title":"Code review","tagline":"Comment on the code, not the person, and take comments as help.","level":"beginner","goals":["Write review comments that are kind, specific and say why","Mark how much a comment matters","Answer the comments on your own pull request"],"remember":"Review the code, not the person: say what, why and a fix, and label it Must, Suggestion or Nit. When you receive one, thank, fix and reply.","terms":[{"term":"review comment","def":"A note a reviewer leaves on a line of a pull request, ideally saying what, why and how to fix it."},{"term":"nit","def":"A tiny, optional point in a review, like a name or a space. Starting a comment with “Nit:” says it shouldn’t block the merge."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which comment follows the advice in this lesson?","options":["“Must: this reads one past the end of the list and crashes. Use <code>for (city in cities)</code>.”","“Wrong loop.”","“Why would you write it like this?”"],"a":0,"why":"It is about the code, says why, and suggests a fix."},{"q":"What does a comment that starts with “Nit:” mean?","options":["A tiny point that shouldn’t block the merge","A bug that must be fixed","The reviewer is not sure"],"a":0,"why":"Labels tell the author which comments are required and which are optional."},{"q":"A reviewer asks for a change you disagree with. What do you do?","options":["Reply with your reason and talk it through","Ignore it and merge","Close the pull request"],"a":0,"why":"The reviewer may know something you don’t, or you may know something they don’t. Talking finds out which."}]},{"id":"tech-debt","title":"Technical debt","tagline":"A shortcut saves time today and charges a little on every later change.","level":"beginner","goals":["Recognise a shortcut that becomes technical debt","Explain the “interest” it charges on later work","Pay it back in small steps, and write down what is left"],"remember":"Technical debt is a shortcut that makes later changes slower. Take it on purpose, write it down, and pay it back in small steps.","terms":[{"term":"technical debt","def":"A quicker, messier way of building something that saves time now and makes later changes slower."},{"term":"interest (technical debt)","def":"The extra time every later change costs because of the shortcut."},{"term":"refactoring","def":"Changing how code is built, without changing what it does, so it is easier to change next time."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is technical debt?","options":["A shortcut that saves time now and makes later changes slower","Money the team owes for its tools","A bug that users reported"],"a":0,"why":"The “loan” is the time saved; the cost comes later."},{"q":"What is the “interest” on technical debt?","options":["The extra time every later change in that code costs","A fee paid every month","The time it took to write the shortcut"],"a":0,"why":"You pay it each time you touch the messy code, not on a calendar."},{"q":"There is no time to clean up before tomorrow’s release. What is a good move?","options":["Take the shortcut, and add a ticket to the backlog to pay it back","Take the shortcut and forget about it","Delay the release for a big rewrite"],"a":0,"why":"Debt taken on purpose and written down is a fair deal. Forgotten debt keeps growing."}]},{"id":"teamwork","title":"Working in a team","tagline":"Give every job one owner, ask early, and say what you tried.","level":"beginner","goals":["Give every task one owner, and close the loop when it is done","Ask for help before you lose a day","Write a message someone can act on: what, what happened, what you tried"],"remember":"Every task has one owner. Ask early, in the open, saying what you tried. Close the loop when it is done.","terms":[{"term":"owner","def":"The one person responsible for seeing a task through, shown as its assignee."},{"term":"close the loop","def":"When a task is done, tell the person who asked for it, and close the ticket."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Three people read a bug report and each thinks someone else has it. What prevents this?","options":["One named owner, visible on the ticket","More people reading it","Waiting for the next sprint"],"a":0,"why":"When the ticket shows one name, nobody has to guess."},{"q":"You have been stuck for 30 minutes. What is a good next step?","options":["Ask the team, saying what you tried","Keep trying alone for two more days","Start another task without telling anyone"],"a":0,"why":"A teammate who knows the code may answer in minutes."},{"q":"The bug is fixed and released. What does “closing the loop” mean here?","options":["Telling the person who reported it, and closing the ticket","Deleting the branch","Starting the next sprint"],"a":0,"why":"The person who asked shouldn’t have to wonder whether anything happened."}]}]},{"id":"runtime","title":"How code runs","description":"What happens between your Kotlin and the phone’s chip: compilers, bytecode, DEX files, the Android Runtime, memory clean-up and app start.","requires":["android"],"file":"tracks/runtime.js","lessons":[{"id":"source-to-machine","title":"From source to machine code","tagline":"The phone’s chip doesn’t read Kotlin. Here is how your code gets translated.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what a compiler and an interpreter do","Say what bytecode is and why a VM runs it","Follow one line of Kotlin from your file to the chip"],"remember":"The CPU runs only machine code. A compiler translates before the program runs, an interpreter while it runs; bytecode is the in-between form a VM runs.","terms":[{"term":"compiler","def":"A program that translates a whole program into another form, such as bytecode or machine code, before it runs."},{"term":"interpreter","def":"A program that reads code while it runs and carries out each step directly."},{"term":"bytecode","def":"Simple steps written for a virtual machine instead of a real chip. Kotlin compiles to bytecode."},{"term":"virtual machine (VM)","def":"A program that acts like a simple computer and runs bytecode. On Android it is ART."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the only thing a CPU understands?","options":["Kotlin","Bytecode","Machine code"],"a":2,"why":"Everything else has to be translated into machine code before the chip can run it."},{"q":"You misspell <code>println</code>. Who stops you before the program runs?","options":["The compiler","The CPU","Nobody: it crashes later"],"a":0,"why":"The compiler checks the whole program before anything runs. That check is what a compile error is."},{"q":"Why compile to bytecode instead of straight to machine code?","options":["Bytecode is shorter to type","The same bytecode runs on any device that has the VM, whatever its chip","Bytecode never needs translating again"],"a":1,"why":"Phones use different chips. The VM on each phone turns the same bytecode into that phone’s machine code."}]},{"id":"kotlin-compile","title":"How Kotlin compiles","tagline":"Look inside the files kotlinc writes, and see what a property really is.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Compile a Kotlin file with <code>kotlinc</code> and find its <code>.class</code> file","Read what <code>javap</code> shows inside a class file","Explain what a <code>val</code>, a <code>var</code> and a custom getter compile to"],"remember":"kotlinc writes one .class file per class. A val is a private field plus a getter; a var adds a setter.","terms":[{"term":"kotlinc","def":"The Kotlin compiler as a terminal command. It writes <code>.class</code> files full of bytecode."},{"term":"javap","def":"A viewer that comes with Java. <code>javap -p</code> lists what is inside a <code>.class</code> file."},{"term":"field","def":"The private box inside an object that stores a property’s value."},{"term":"getter / setter","def":"The small functions a property compiles to: <code>getName()</code> reads it, <code>setAge()</code> changes it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>class Box(val size: Int)</code> compile to for <code>size</code>?","options":["A private field and <code>getSize()</code>","A getter and a setter","Nothing: properties exist only in Kotlin"],"a":0,"why":"A <code>val</code> is read-only: a private field plus a getter. A <code>var</code> would add <code>setSize</code>."},{"q":"Your file <code>Notes.kt</code> has a function outside any class. Which class file holds it?","options":["<code>Notes.class</code>","<code>NotesKt.class</code>","None: it stays in the .kt file"],"a":1,"why":"Bytecode keeps every function inside a class, so the compiler makes one named after the file plus <code>Kt</code>."},{"q":"<code>val isEmpty: Boolean get() = count == 0</code>. What appears in the class file for it?","options":["A field and a getter","Only the method <code>isEmpty()</code>","A setter <code>setEmpty()</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Its value is computed each time, so nothing is stored. A name starting with <code>is</code> keeps that name for its getter."}]},{"id":"dex","title":"DEX files","tagline":"Android runs its own kind of bytecode, packed into classes.dex.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why an Android build converts <code>.class</code> files to DEX","Say what D8 does when you build","Know what happens when an app outgrows one DEX file"],"remember":"D8 turns every .class file into DEX. One DEX file refers to at most 65,536 methods; past that, the build adds classes2.dex.","terms":[{"term":"DEX","def":"Dalvik Executable: the bytecode format Android runs, stored in <code>classes.dex</code> files inside the APK."},{"term":"D8","def":"The build tool that converts <code>.class</code> files into DEX. Android Studio runs it for you."},{"term":"method","def":"Bytecode’s name for a function, including the getters and setters properties compile to."},{"term":"multidex","def":"Splitting an app’s code over several DEX files: <code>classes.dex</code>, <code>classes2.dex</code> and so on."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which files does Android run?","options":["<code>.kt</code> files","<code>.class</code> files","DEX files"],"a":2,"why":"kotlinc writes <code>.class</code> files, and D8 converts them to DEX, the format Android runs."},{"q":"What does D8 do?","options":["Converts <code>.class</code> files into DEX","Checks your Kotlin for typos","Installs the app on the phone"],"a":0,"why":"Typos are the Kotlin compiler’s job. D8 comes after it and turns its output into DEX."},{"q":"Your app passes 65,536 method references and has <code>minSdk = 24</code>. What must you do?","options":["Remove libraries until it fits","Nothing: the build writes <code>classes2.dex</code> and ART loads it","Turn the app into two apps"],"a":1,"why":"With <code>minSdk</code> 21 or higher, multidex is on by default."}]},{"id":"art","title":"Android Runtime","tagline":"Interpreted, compiled while running, or compiled ahead: how ART runs your code.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Name the three ways ART can run a method","Explain why a fresh install starts slower","Say what a profile and a Baseline Profile do"],"remember":"Interpreter first, JIT for hot methods, AOT for what the profile lists. A Baseline Profile gives every user that list from launch 1.","terms":[{"term":"ART","def":"The Android Runtime: Android’s VM. It runs DEX code with an interpreter, a JIT compiler and AOT-compiled code."},{"term":"JIT compilation","def":"Compiling hot methods into machine code while the app runs. The code is kept in memory."},{"term":"AOT compilation","def":"Compiling methods into machine code before the app runs, done on the phone by <code>dex2oat</code>."},{"term":"profile","def":"A list of the methods that matter, which ART compiles ahead of time. A <em>Baseline Profile</em> is one shipped inside the app."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A fresh install has no profile and no Baseline Profile. How do its first launches run?","options":["Fully compiled ahead of time","In the interpreter, with hot methods JIT-compiled","Not at all until dex2oat finishes"],"a":1,"why":"Without a profile there is nothing to compile ahead of time, so ART starts with the interpreter and the JIT."},{"q":"Why doesn’t ART compile every method of every app ahead of time?","options":["It costs time and storage for code that rarely runs","AOT code is slower than the interpreter","Android doesn’t allow it"],"a":0,"why":"The profile points ART at the code that matters, so it compiles only that."},{"q":"What does shipping a Baseline Profile change?","options":["The app gets smaller","ART compiles the listed methods at or soon after installation, so the first launches are faster","The JIT is switched off"],"a":1,"why":"The profile is inside the app, so it is there from the first install instead of after the first idle-and-charging night."}]},{"id":"gc","title":"Garbage collection","tagline":"Objects stay in memory while something can reach them. The rest is cleaned up for you.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what makes an object reachable","Follow one mark-and-sweep collection step by step","Predict which objects a collection frees"],"remember":"Reachable from a GC root means kept. Everything else is freed, even objects that only point at each other.","terms":[{"term":"garbage collector (GC)","def":"The part of ART that finds objects the app can no longer reach and frees their memory."},{"term":"GC root","def":"Something surely in use, such as a local variable of a running function or a Kotlin <code>object</code>."},{"term":"reachable","def":"An object you can get to by following references from a GC root. Reachable objects are kept."},{"term":"mark and sweep","def":"Mark every object reachable from the roots, then free (sweep) all the others."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>A</code> and <code>B</code> point at each other, and nothing else points at either. What does the GC do?","options":["Keeps both, because each has a reference","Frees both, because neither is reachable from a root","Frees one and keeps the other"],"a":1,"why":"The only question is “reachable from a root?”. A pair cut off from the roots is garbage."},{"q":"A function ends. What happens to an object only its local variable pointed at?","options":["It is freed that same moment","It becomes unreachable, and a later collection frees it","It stays forever"],"a":1,"why":"The local variable is gone when the function ends. The object waits until the GC runs and finds it unreachable."},{"q":"You add an object to a list inside a Kotlin <code>object</code>. When can the GC free it?","options":["When the function that made it ends","Not while it is in that list","At the next collection"],"a":1,"why":"The <code>object</code> is a GC root, and the list inside it points at your object, so it stays reachable."}]},{"id":"references","title":"Strong and weak references","tagline":"A normal reference keeps an object alive. A WeakReference doesn’t.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain how a reference can keep a closed screen in memory","Hold an object with <code>WeakReference</code> and read it with <code>get()</code>","Decide when a weak reference helps and when to let go instead"],"remember":"A strong reference keeps an object alive; a WeakReference doesn’t, and its get() can return null. Never let something long-lived hold a screen strongly.","terms":[{"term":"strong reference","def":"A normal reference. While it exists, the object it points at can’t be freed."},{"term":"WeakReference","def":"A reference the GC ignores. <code>get()</code> returns the object, or <code>null</code> after it has been freed."},{"term":"memory leak","def":"An object the app no longer needs that is still reachable, so the GC can never free it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Only weak references point at an object. What can happen at the next GC?","options":["Nothing: weak references keep it too","It is freed, and <code>get()</code> then returns <code>null</code>","It turns into a strong reference"],"a":1,"why":"The GC ignores weak references, so the object is unreachable."},{"q":"Why is <code>ref.get()</code> read with <code>?.</code>?","options":["It returns <code>null</code> once the object has been freed","Kotlin requires <code>?.</code> after every function call","It makes the reference strong"],"a":0,"why":"The object may already be gone. <code>?.</code> skips the call instead of crashing."},{"q":"An Activity adds itself to a list in a Kotlin <code>object</code> in <code>onCreate</code>. What fixes the leak most directly?","options":["Removing it from the list in <code>onDestroy</code>","Rotating the phone less","Making the list a <code>val</code>"],"a":0,"why":"If the Activity leaves the list when it is destroyed, no root reaches it and the GC can free it."}]},{"id":"startup","title":"What happens at app start","tagline":"From tapping the icon to the first frame: each step, and where the time goes.","level":"intermediate","goals":["List the steps of a cold start in order","Explain why work in <code>Application.onCreate</code> delays every launch","Move heavy set-up out of the start with <code>by lazy</code>"],"remember":"Cold start: process, your code, Application.onCreate, the first Activity, the first frame, one after another on the main thread. Do less there, or do it later.","terms":[{"term":"cold start","def":"A start when the app isn’t running at all, so every step from creating the process to the first frame happens."},{"term":"process","def":"A running program with its own memory. Android creates one for your app."},{"term":"Zygote","def":"A process started when the phone boots, with Android’s code loaded. Every app process is a copy of it."},{"term":"main thread","def":"The one line of work that runs your start-up code and also draws the screen and answers taps."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which order is right for a cold start?","options":["Activity.onCreate → Application.onCreate → process","Process → Application.onCreate → Activity.onCreate → first frame","First frame → process → Application.onCreate"],"a":1,"why":"The process comes first, the <code>Application</code> before any Activity, and drawing last."},{"q":"Why does heavy work in <code>Application.onCreate</code> hurt so much?","options":["It runs on the main thread before the first screen, on every cold start","It runs only once, at install","It makes the APK bigger"],"a":0,"why":"Every cold start waits for it, and the person sees only a blank window meanwhile."},{"q":"A Baseline Profile speeds up which steps of a cold start?","options":["Creating the process from Zygote","The steps that run your app’s code","None of them"],"a":1,"why":"The profile changes how your DEX code runs. Creating the process is Android’s own work."}]}]},{"id":"db","title":"Databases","description":"Keep an app’s data in a database with SQLite: make tables, ask them questions in SQL, link them with keys, and keep the data fast, safe and up to date as the app grows.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/db.js","lessons":[{"id":"what-db","title":"What a database is","tagline":"A place where an app keeps its data in tidy tables.","level":"beginner","goals":["Say what a table, a row and a column are","Explain why apps keep data in a database instead of a text file","Make a table with <code>CREATE TABLE</code> in <code>sqlite3</code>"],"remember":"A table holds rows of one kind of thing; every column has a name and a type. CREATE TABLE makes one.","terms":[{"term":"database","def":"A program that keeps data safe and finds it fast. SQLite is one."},{"term":"table, row, column","def":"A table is a grid of data. Each row is one thing (one book); each column is one kind of value (the title)."},{"term":"SQL","def":"The language you use to talk to a database, with commands like <code>CREATE TABLE</code>."},{"term":"sqlite3","def":"A terminal program for SQLite: <code>sqlite3 library.db</code> opens that database file."}],"quiz":[{"q":"In Ada’s <code>books</code> table, what is one row?","options":["One book","Every title, top to bottom","The whole file"],"a":0,"why":"A row is one thing. Every title, top to bottom, is a column."},{"q":"Which column type fits a year like 2019?","options":["<code>TEXT</code>","<code>INTEGER</code>"],"a":1,"why":"A year is a whole number, so <code>INTEGER</code>."},{"q":"Why does a text file get hard to use as an app’s data grows?","options":["Text files cannot hold numbers","The program must read and cut apart every line itself to find anything","A computer can open only one text file"],"a":1,"why":"A database does the finding for you, and keeps the data safe while it saves."}]},{"id":"sql-select","title":"Reading data with SQL","tagline":"Ask a table a question and get back only the rows you need.","level":"beginner","goals":["Read columns from a table with <code>SELECT</code> and <code>FROM</code>","Keep only the rows that match with <code>WHERE</code>","Sort with <code>ORDER BY</code> and keep the first few with <code>LIMIT</code>"],"remember":"SELECT columns FROM table WHERE test ORDER BY column LIMIT n, in that order. Text goes in single quotes.","terms":[{"term":"query","def":"A question you ask the database, like <code>SELECT title FROM books;</code>. The answer is a small table."},{"term":"SELECT … FROM","def":"<code>SELECT</code> names the columns you want, <code>FROM</code> names the table. <code>*</code> means all columns."},{"term":"WHERE","def":"Keeps only the rows that pass a test, like <code>WHERE year &gt; 2018</code>."},{"term":"ORDER BY, DESC, LIMIT","def":"<code>ORDER BY</code> sorts the answer (<code>DESC</code> for biggest first); <code>LIMIT</code> keeps the first few rows."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>SELECT title FROM books WHERE author = 'mia hart';</code> print?","options":["Rain at Dawn and Night Train","Nothing: no row matches","An error"],"a":1,"why":"Text must match exactly, small and capital letters included."},{"q":"Which part keeps only some of the rows?","options":["<code>SELECT</code>","<code>WHERE</code>","<code>ORDER BY</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>SELECT</code> picks columns; <code>WHERE</code> picks rows."},{"q":"How do you get only the oldest book?","options":["<code>ORDER BY year LIMIT 1</code>","<code>ORDER BY year DESC LIMIT 1</code>","<code>LIMIT 1</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Without <code>DESC</code> the smallest year comes first. <code>LIMIT 1</code> alone gives no promised order."}]},{"id":"sql-write","title":"Changing data with SQL","tagline":"Add rows, change them and remove them.","level":"beginner","goals":["Add rows with <code>INSERT INTO</code> and <code>VALUES</code>","Change rows with <code>UPDATE</code> and <code>SET</code>","Remove rows with <code>DELETE FROM</code>, aimed by <code>WHERE</code>"],"remember":"INSERT adds rows, UPDATE changes them, DELETE removes them. UPDATE and DELETE hit every row unless WHERE aims them.","terms":[{"term":"INSERT INTO … VALUES","def":"Adds a row: <code>INSERT INTO books VALUES (6, 'Blue Hills', 'Ines Costa', 2023, 1);</code>"},{"term":"UPDATE … SET","def":"Changes columns in the rows that pass the <code>WHERE</code> test."},{"term":"DELETE FROM","def":"Removes the rows that pass the <code>WHERE</code> test. Without <code>WHERE</code>, all of them."},{"term":"NULL","def":"SQL’s “no value”, like <code>null</code> in Kotlin. A column you leave out of an <code>INSERT</code> holds <code>NULL</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>DELETE FROM books;</code> remove?","options":["Nothing: it needs a <code>WHERE</code>","Every row; the empty table stays","The whole table, columns and all"],"a":1,"why":"It removes all the rows. The table itself is still there, empty."},{"q":"After <code>INSERT INTO books (id, title) VALUES (9, 'Mist');</code>, what is in that row’s <code>year</code>?","options":["<code>0</code>","Nothing: it stays empty (<code>NULL</code>)","An error stops the INSERT"],"a":1,"why":"Columns you leave out hold <code>NULL</code>. <code>sqlite3</code> prints the row as <code>9|Mist|||</code>."},{"q":"Which command changes Night Train’s year to 2020?","options":["<code>UPDATE books SET year = 2020 WHERE id = 3;</code>","<code>INSERT INTO books VALUES (3, 2020);</code>","<code>UPDATE books WHERE id = 3 SET year = 2020;</code>"],"a":0,"why":"<code>SET</code> comes before <code>WHERE</code>. <code>INSERT</code> adds a new row instead of changing one."}]},{"id":"keys","title":"Keys and relations","tagline":"Give every row its own name, and let one table point at another.","level":"beginner","goals":["Make a column that names each row with <code>PRIMARY KEY</code>","Point from one table to another with <code>REFERENCES</code>","Turn on SQLite’s link checks with <code>PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON</code>"],"remember":"A primary key names each row; a foreign key points at another table’s primary key. In SQLite, run PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON first.","terms":[{"term":"primary key","def":"A column whose value is different in every row, so it names exactly one row: <code>id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY</code>."},{"term":"foreign key","def":"A column that holds another table’s primary key, like <code>book_id INTEGER REFERENCES books(id)</code>."},{"term":"relation","def":"The link between two tables made by a foreign key."},{"term":"PRAGMA","def":"Reads or changes a setting of SQLite, like <code>PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON;</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"<code>id</code> is the primary key of <code>books</code>, and a book with id 2 exists. What does another <code>INSERT</code> with id 2 do?","options":["Replaces the old row","Fails: <code>UNIQUE constraint failed: books.id</code>","Keeps both rows"],"a":1,"why":"A primary key value can belong to one row only."},{"q":"A loan has <code>book_id</code> 4. What is <code>book_id</code>?","options":["The loan’s primary key","A foreign key: it points at book 4 in <code>books</code>","A copy of the book’s title"],"a":1,"why":"It holds another table’s primary key, so it is a foreign key."},{"q":"You open <code>sqlite3</code> again tomorrow. Are the foreign key checks on?","options":["Yes, the file remembers","No: run <code>PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON;</code> again"],"a":1,"why":"The setting lasts only until the session ends."}]},{"id":"joins","title":"Joining tables","tagline":"Put rows from two tables side by side in one answer.","level":"beginner","goals":["Combine two tables in one query with <code>JOIN</code> and <code>ON</code>","Name a column by its table, like <code>books.title</code>","Explain why rows without a partner drop out"],"remember":"FROM a JOIN b ON a.foreign_key = b.id puts matching rows side by side. Rows without a partner drop out.","terms":[{"term":"join","def":"Combining rows of two tables into one answer: <code>FROM loans JOIN books ON ...</code>."},{"term":"ON","def":"In a join, the rule for which rows belong together, like <code>loans.book_id = books.id</code>."},{"term":"ambiguous column","def":"A column name that more than one table in the query has. Write <code>table.column</code> instead."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is Small Rooms missing from the joined loans?","options":["No loan points at it","Its id is the biggest","A join always skips the last row"],"a":0,"why":"A join keeps only rows that found a partner."},{"q":"What does <code>ON loans.book_id = books.id</code> say?","options":["Copy <code>book_id</code> into <code>books</code>","Which loan row goes with which book row","Sort the answer by id"],"a":1,"why":"<code>ON</code> is the rule for pairing rows."},{"q":"SQLite says <code>ambiguous column name: id</code>. What fixes it?","options":["Write <code>books.id</code> or <code>loans.id</code>","Add <code>DESC</code>","Remove the <code>ON</code> part"],"a":0,"why":"Naming the table makes clear which <code>id</code> you mean."}]},{"id":"normalization","title":"Designing tables","tagline":"Keep each fact in one place, so it can’t disagree with itself.","level":"beginner","goals":["Spot a fact that is copied into many rows","Split a wide table into tables that each describe one kind of thing","Use the normal forms as a plain-words checklist"],"remember":"Write each fact once: one table per kind of thing, linked by keys, and joins to put them back together.","terms":[{"term":"normalization","def":"Designing tables so each fact is stored once, with keys linking the tables."},{"term":"normal forms","def":"Checklist steps for normalization. In plain words: one value per cell, and every column describes what its table’s key names."},{"term":"anomaly","def":"A problem caused by copies: an update that misses a copy, a delete that erases a fact you still need, or a fact you can’t store yet."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A table copies each member’s city into every loan. What can go wrong?","options":["Changing the city in one row leaves other rows saying something else","The table runs out of columns","SQLite refuses text it has seen before"],"a":0,"why":"Every copy must change together, and it is easy to miss one."},{"q":"In Ada’s design, where does a member’s city belong?","options":["In <code>members</code>","In <code>loans</code>","In <code>books</code>"],"a":0,"why":"A city describes a member, so it sits next to the member’s key."},{"q":"What is first normal form, in plain words?","options":["One value per cell","Every table has one row","Use only one table"],"a":0,"why":"No lists like “Night Train, Paper Birds” packed into a single cell."}]},{"id":"indexes","title":"Indexes","tagline":"A sorted lookup list that lets the database jump straight to a row.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why a search in a big table is slow without an index","Make an index with <code>CREATE INDEX</code> and check it with <code>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN</code>","Name the cost: every change also updates the index"],"remember":"An index is a sorted list that turns a SCAN of every row into a quick SEARCH. It costs space and slows every write a little.","terms":[{"term":"full scan","def":"Reading every row to answer a query. <code>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN</code> shows it as <code>SCAN</code>."},{"term":"index","def":"A sorted list of one column’s values, each pointing at its row, made with <code>CREATE INDEX</code>."},{"term":"query plan","def":"How SQLite will run a query. <code>EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN</code> prints it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"The plan says <code>SCAN books</code>. What does SQLite do?","options":["Reads every row","Jumps to the row through an index","Sorts the table"],"a":0,"why":"A scan reads the whole table."},{"q":"What does an index cost?","options":["Nothing","Space, plus extra work on every <code>INSERT</code>, <code>UPDATE</code> and <code>DELETE</code>","It makes every <code>SELECT</code> slower"],"a":1,"why":"The sorted list has to be kept up to date on every write."},{"q":"There is an index on <code>title</code> only. Which search can use it?","options":["<code>WHERE title = 'Night Train'</code>","<code>WHERE author = 'Mia Hart'</code>","<code>WHERE year = 2021</code>"],"a":0,"why":"An index helps searches on the column it sorts."}]},{"id":"transactions","title":"Transactions","tagline":"Several changes that happen all together, or not at all.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Group changes with <code>BEGIN</code> and <code>COMMIT</code>","Throw away a half-done group with <code>ROLLBACK</code>","Explain ACID in plain words"],"remember":"BEGIN, the changes, then COMMIT to keep them all or ROLLBACK to drop them all.","terms":[{"term":"transaction","def":"A group of changes that count as one: all of them happen, or none do."},{"term":"COMMIT, ROLLBACK","def":"<code>COMMIT</code> makes every change since <code>BEGIN</code> permanent; <code>ROLLBACK</code> throws them all away."},{"term":"ACID","def":"Atomic (all or nothing), Consistent (rules still hold), Isolated (nobody sees it halfway), Durable (a commit survives a crash)."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>ROLLBACK;</code> undo?","options":["Only the last command","Every change since <code>BEGIN</code>","Every change ever made"],"a":1,"why":"It drops the whole open transaction."},{"q":"Which letter of ACID means “all of it or none of it”?","options":["A, atomic","D, durable","I, isolated"],"a":0,"why":"Atomic: the group can’t be split."},{"q":"Ada runs <code>COMMIT;</code> with no <code>BEGIN</code> before it. What does <code>sqlite3</code> say?","options":["Nothing","<code>Runtime error: cannot commit - no transaction is active</code>","It commits the last command again"],"a":1,"why":"Without <code>BEGIN</code>, each command was already final, so there is nothing to commit."}]},{"id":"nosql","title":"Other kinds of databases","tagline":"Not every database is made of tables.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Describe a key-value store and a document store","Say what each is good at, and when tables are better","Pick a fitting kind of store for a piece of app data"],"remember":"Tables for linked data that must agree, key-value for lookups by key, documents for data read as one piece.","terms":[{"term":"NoSQL","def":"A name for databases that don’t keep data in tables, such as key-value and document stores."},{"term":"key-value store","def":"Stores values under keys, like a Kotlin <code>Map</code> saved to disk. Finds things only by key."},{"term":"document store","def":"Stores each item as one nested document, so it can be read in one go."},{"term":"JSON","def":"A text format for data: names and values in curly braces, lists in square brackets."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which fits the app’s dark-mode setting best?","options":["A key-value store","Three tables and a join","One document per book"],"a":0,"why":"One value, found by one key."},{"q":"Documents copy a book’s title into each member who borrowed it. What is the risk?","options":["A correction must reach every copy, or they disagree","Documents can’t hold text","Each read needs a join"],"a":0,"why":"The same problem as copies in a wide table."},{"q":"What can a key-value store find quickly?","options":["The value for one key","Every key whose value is <code>dark</code>","Rows joined with another table"],"a":0,"why":"It is built to look things up by key, and only by key."}]},{"id":"schema-evolution","title":"Changing a schema","tagline":"Change the shape of a database that already holds people’s data.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Add a column to an existing table with <code>ALTER TABLE</code> … <code>ADD COLUMN</code>","Number schema versions and write one migration per step","Keep old code working by adding instead of renaming"],"remember":"A migration changes the schema one version at a time and keeps the rows. Add new things; remove old ones only when nothing uses them.","terms":[{"term":"schema","def":"The shape of a database: its tables and their columns."},{"term":"migration","def":"A script that moves the schema from one version to the next and keeps the data."},{"term":"user_version","def":"A number SQLite keeps in the file for you, used as the schema version: <code>PRAGMA user_version;</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What do old rows hold in a column added with <code>ADD COLUMN</code>?","options":["<code>0</code>","<code>NULL</code>: no value","A copy of the column before it"],"a":1,"why":"Nothing was ever stored there, so it is <code>NULL</code>."},{"q":"A phone is at <code>user_version</code> 1, and the new app expects 3. What should run?","options":["Only migration 2→3","Migration 1→2, then 2→3","Delete the database and start again"],"a":1,"why":"Each step builds on the one before, so they run in order."},{"q":"Why prefer adding a column over renaming one?","options":["Old code that uses the old name keeps working","SQL has no way to rename","Adding is faster to type"],"a":0,"why":"Renaming is possible, but it breaks every query that uses the old name."}]}]},{"id":"backend","title":"Backend basics","description":"Write the server your app talks to, in Kotlin: routes and handlers with Ktor, JSON and status codes, a database, safe password storage, sign-in with tokens, tests, deployment, and finally the Recipes app talking to your own server.","requires":["sysdesign","data"],"file":"tracks/backend.js","lessons":[{"id":"what","title":"What a backend does","tagline":"Behind every app that shares data: a server program, its API and a database.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name the three parts of a backend: server program, API and database","Decide which work belongs on the server and which on the phone","Read the Recipes API as a list of endpoints"],"remember":"A backend is a server program, its API (the endpoints it answers) and a database; it checks every request again, because the app can be changed.","terms":[{"term":"backend","def":"The server side of an app: a server program, the API it answers and the database behind it."},{"term":"API","def":"Application programming interface: the list of requests a server answers, with what each one takes and gives back."},{"term":"endpoint","def":"One request an API answers: a method plus a path, like <code>GET /v1/recipes</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which of these belongs on the server, not in the app?","options":["Checking that a token is real before sending Ada’s recipes","Drawing the list of recipes","Showing saved recipes while offline"],"a":0,"why":"The app can be changed by whoever holds the phone, so checks you must trust run on the server. Drawing and offline copies stay on the phone."},{"q":"What is an endpoint?","options":["A method plus a path, like <code>GET /v1/recipes</code>","The last line of a server program","A table in the database"],"a":0,"why":"An API is a list of endpoints. Each one is a method and a path."},{"q":"The app already refuses a recipe with an empty title. Must the server check it too?","options":["Yes: a changed app can send anything","No: the app has checked it already","Only on the first request"],"a":0,"why":"The phone is in the user’s hands. The server can trust only the checks it runs itself."}]},{"id":"http","title":"HTTP on the server","tagline":"A request comes in, a route matches it, a handler answers.","level":"beginner","goals":["Read an HTTP request and response as the text they really are","Explain what a route and a handler are","Write a tiny router in plain Kotlin"],"remember":"A route is a method plus a path; its handler reads the request and builds the response; no matching route means 404.","terms":[{"term":"route","def":"A rule on the server that sends one method and path, like <code>GET /v1/recipes</code>, to one handler."},{"term":"handler","def":"The code that runs for a route: it reads the request and builds the response."},{"term":"status line","def":"The first line of a response: the HTTP version, the status code and its name, like <code>HTTP/1.1 200 OK</code>."},{"term":"Content-Type","def":"A header that says what the body is, like <code>application/json</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is on the first line of a request?","options":["The method, the path and the HTTP version","The status code","The JSON body"],"a":0,"why":"For example <code>GET /v1/recipes HTTP/1.1</code>. Headers follow, then an empty line, then the body."},{"q":"A response has the header <code>Content-Type: application/json</code>. What does it tell the app?","options":["The body is JSON","The request was allowed","The server is written in Kotlin"],"a":0,"why":"<code>Content-Type</code> says what kind of text the body is."},{"q":"A request matches no route. What should the server answer?","options":["<code>404 Not Found</code>","<code>200 OK</code> with an empty body","Nothing: it ignores the request"],"a":0,"why":"With no route there is no handler to run. 404 tells the app the path doesn’t exist."}]},{"id":"ktor","title":"A server in Kotlin","tagline":"Ktor: a real server program in the language you already know.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Set up a Ktor server project with Gradle","Answer GET /v1/recipes with JSON from a route","Run the server and call it with curl"],"remember":"embeddedServer starts Ktor on a port; install adds plugins like ContentNegotiation; routing { route(\"/v1\") { get(path) { call.respond(...) } } } answers a route.","terms":[{"term":"Ktor","def":"A Kotlin library from JetBrains for writing servers (and HTTP clients). Handlers run in coroutines."},{"term":"engine","def":"The part of Ktor that talks to the network, such as Netty."},{"term":"port","def":"A number that tells apart the server programs on one computer. Here Ktor listens on 8080."},{"term":"plugin (Ktor)","def":"An add-on part of a Ktor server, added with <code>install</code>, like <code>ContentNegotiation</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>install(ContentNegotiation) { json() }</code> add?","options":["Turning objects into JSON, and JSON into objects","A database","Sign-in"],"a":0,"why":"It is the plugin that lets <code>call.respond</code> send objects as JSON, with kotlinx.serialization."},{"q":"What does <code>call.respond(recipes)</code> send, when nothing else is said?","options":["Status 200 and the list as JSON","Status 201 and nothing else","Only the status, no body"],"a":0,"why":"200 OK is the default status, and the plugin writes the list as JSON."},{"q":"In <code>embeddedServer(Netty, port = 8080)</code>, what is Netty?","options":["The engine that talks to the network","The database","The JSON library"],"a":0,"why":"Ktor can run on several engines. Netty accepts the connections and hands each request to your routes."}]},{"id":"rest","title":"REST APIs","tagline":"Paths name things, methods say what to do, status codes say how it went.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Design endpoints with nouns in the paths and methods as the actions","Read a path parameter and the JSON body of a request","Answer with 201, 400 and 404 where they fit"],"remember":"Paths name resources (/v1/recipes, /v1/recipes/3), methods are the verbs, and the status says how it went: 201 added, 400 wrong request, 404 no such thing.","terms":[{"term":"REST","def":"A common style for HTTP APIs: paths name resources, methods say what to do, status codes say how it went."},{"term":"resource","def":"A thing an API offers, with its own path: <code>/v1/recipes</code> (the list), <code>/v1/recipes/3</code> (one recipe)."},{"term":"path parameter","def":"A part of the path that changes, like <code>{id}</code> in <code>/v1/recipes/{id}</code>. <code>call.parameters[\"id\"]</code> reads it as text."},{"term":"201 Created","def":"The status for “added”. The body holds the new thing, with its new id."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which endpoint follows REST for “delete recipe 3”?","options":["<code>DELETE /v1/recipes/3</code>","<code>POST /v1/deleteRecipe</code>","<code>GET /v1/recipes/3/delete</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The path names the resource, recipe 3, and the method says what to do with it."},{"q":"The app sends a recipe with an empty title. Which status fits?","options":["400 Bad Request","404 Not Found","201 Created"],"a":0,"why":"The request itself is wrong. 404 would mean the thing it names doesn’t exist."},{"q":"In <code>get(\"/recipes/{id}\")</code>, what is <code>call.parameters[\"id\"]</code> for <code>/v1/recipes/7</code>?","options":["The text <code>\"7\"</code>","The number 7","The recipe with id 7"],"a":0,"why":"Path parameters arrive as text. <code>toIntOrNull()</code> turns it into a number, or into null when it isn’t one."}]},{"id":"sql","title":"Using a database","tagline":"Keep the recipes in tables, and query them from Kotlin with Exposed.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why a server keeps its data in a database, not in a list","Define a table and read and add rows with Exposed","Run each database call in a transaction, on Dispatchers.IO"],"remember":"A table is an object that extends Table; selectAll().where { } reads, insert { } adds; every call runs in transaction { }, inside withContext(Dispatchers.IO).","terms":[{"term":"Exposed","def":"JetBrains’ SQL library for Kotlin: tables are Kotlin objects, and queries are Kotlin calls."},{"term":"transaction","def":"A group of database steps that all happen, or none do. In Exposed every query runs inside <code>transaction { }</code>."},{"term":"JDBC URL","def":"The address of a database for a Java or Kotlin program, like <code>jdbc:h2:./recipes</code>: the kind of database and where it is."},{"term":"SQL injection","def":"An attack where text glued into SQL changes the SQL itself. Sending values apart from the SQL prevents it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You restart the server. Where are the recipes now?","options":["In the database file, <code>recipes.mv.db</code>, as before","Gone, as with the list","On Ada’s phone"],"a":0,"why":"The database keeps its rows on disk, so a restart loses nothing."},{"q":"Why run a transaction inside <code>withContext(Dispatchers.IO)</code>?","options":["It blocks its thread while it waits for the database","It makes the SQL shorter","Exposed only works on the main thread"],"a":0,"why":"Ktor runs handlers on a few shared threads. Dispatchers.IO has threads meant for waiting, so the others stay free for new requests."},{"q":"A query has a typo: <code>RecipesTabel</code>. When do you find out?","options":["When the code compiles","When a request runs that query","Never: the query returns no rows"],"a":0,"why":"In Exposed the table is a Kotlin object, so a wrong name is a compile error."}]},{"id":"passwords","title":"Storing passwords","tagline":"Sign-up and sign-in on the server: keep a hash, never the password.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why a server stores a password hash, never the password","Hash and check passwords with Argon2id","Answer sign-up with 201 or 409, and a wrong password with 401"],"remember":"Store an Argon2id hash with its own salt, never the password; Password.check compares at sign-in; a taken email gets 409, a wrong password 401.","terms":[{"term":"hash","def":"A scrambled form of a password that can’t be turned back. To check a password, <code>Password.check</code> scrambles it the same way and compares."},{"term":"salt","def":"Random bytes mixed into each hash, so the same password gives a different hash for every user."},{"term":"Argon2id","def":"The password hash OWASP recommends today: slow and memory-hungry on purpose, so guessing from a leak is expensive."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why store a hash and not the password?","options":["A leak then doesn’t reveal passwords that people reuse on other sites","A hash takes less space","The server can turn the hash back when Ada forgets her password"],"a":0,"why":"A hash can’t be turned back. The server only ever compares."},{"q":"Why is Argon2id slow and memory-hungry on purpose?","options":["Every guess against a leaked hash becomes expensive","So sign-in waits for the database","So the hash fits in a JWT"],"a":0,"why":"One sign-in takes a moment. Millions of guesses take far too long."},{"q":"Sam and Lin both choose the password <code>lemon-pasta-15</code>. Are their hashes the same?","options":["No: each hash gets its own random salt","Yes: the same password gives the same hash","Only if they sign up on the same day"],"a":0,"why":"<code>addRandomSalt(16)</code> mixes new random bytes into every hash, so a leak doesn’t show who shares a password."}]},{"id":"tokens","title":"Tokens","tagline":"Hand out tokens at sign-in, and check one on every request.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Make and check JWT access tokens with Ktor’s Authentication plugin","Answer refresh with a new pair, using each refresh token only once","Sign a user out by deleting their refresh tokens"],"remember":"Sign short-lived JWTs with a secret from the environment; put private routes inside authenticate { }; a refresh token works once, and logout deletes them all.","terms":[{"term":"JWT","def":"JSON Web Token: a small signed piece of JSON with claims, like the email and the expiry. Changing it breaks the signature."},{"term":"claim","def":"One fact inside a JWT, like <code>\"email\"</code>. <code>withClaim</code> adds it, <code>getClaim</code> reads it."},{"term":"environment variable","def":"A named value set outside a program and read with <code>System.getenv(\"JWT_SECRET\")</code>. It keeps secrets out of the code."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Anyone can read the JSON inside a JWT. Why can’t Sam change the email in his token to Ada’s?","options":["The signature would no longer match, and only the server has the secret","JWTs are encrypted","The app checks the email"],"a":0,"why":"The server signs the token with its secret. A changed token fails the check in <code>verifier</code>."},{"q":"A request to a route inside <code>authenticate(\"auth-jwt\")</code> has no token. What happens?","options":["Ktor answers 401, and the handler doesn’t run","The handler runs with no email","The server crashes"],"a":0,"why":"<code>authenticate</code> checks before the handler. No good token, no handler."},{"q":"Why delete a refresh token when it is used?","options":["So a stolen copy can’t be used again","To save space","Because the app forgets it anyway"],"a":0,"why":"Each refresh hands out a new one. An old one sent again is no longer in the table, so it gets 401."}]},{"id":"testing","title":"Testing a server","tagline":"Send requests to your server from a test, without a network.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Write a server test with testApplication and its client","Check status codes and JSON answers in a test","Give each test its own fresh database"],"remember":"testApplication runs the server inside the test; client sends requests; check response.status and the body; give each test its own in-memory database.","terms":[{"term":"testApplication","def":"Runs your Ktor server inside a test, with no network or port, and gives you a <code>client</code> to call it."},{"term":"test client","def":"The <code>client</code> inside <code>testApplication</code>: it sends requests straight to the test server."},{"term":"in-memory database","def":"A database kept only in memory, like <code>jdbc:h2:mem:test1</code>: fast, and gone when the tests end."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>testApplication</code> need to run your routes?","options":["Nothing more: it runs the server inside the test","A phone or an emulator","The server already running on port 8080"],"a":0,"why":"Requests go straight from the test client to the test server, with no network."},{"q":"Why <code>jdbc:h2:mem:</code> in tests?","options":["A fresh, fast database that is gone after the tests","It is the only database Ktor supports","It keeps test data for the next run"],"a":0,"why":"Each test starts from an empty database it fills itself, so tests don’t depend on each other."},{"q":"Which test would have caught Sam’s change, the route outside <code>authenticate</code>?","options":["GET /v1/recipes without a token expects 401","GET /v1/recipes with a token expects 200","POST /v1/login expects 200"],"a":0,"why":"With a token, both versions answer 200. Only the request without a token shows the difference."}]},{"id":"deploy","title":"Deploying","tagline":"Package the server as one file or a container image, and run it where every phone can reach it.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Read the port, the secret and the database address from environment variables","Build a fat JAR and a container image with the Ktor Gradle plugin","Explain what a hosting service does with your image"],"remember":"One build, settings from environment variables; buildFatJar makes one runnable JAR, buildImage a container image; on a hosting service, keep the data in the database it runs for you.","terms":[{"term":"fat JAR","def":"One JAR file with your code and all its libraries, run with <code>java -jar</code>."},{"term":"container image","def":"A package of a program with everything it needs, Java included, that runs the same on any computer with containers."},{"term":"Docker","def":"The common tool to load, run and share container images."},{"term":"hosting service","def":"A company that runs your server on its computers, with an address, HTTPS and restarts."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>./gradlew buildFatJar</code> make?","options":["One JAR with your code and every library","A container image","An APK"],"a":0,"why":"One file that <code>java -jar</code> runs. <code>buildImage</code> makes the container image."},{"q":"Why not keep the H2 file inside the container on a hosting service?","options":["A new version replaces the container, and the file is lost with it","H2 can’t run in a container","Hosting services don’t allow files"],"a":0,"why":"Containers are replaced on every deploy. Keep the data in a database that outlives them."},{"q":"In <code>docker run -p 8080:8080 …</code>, what does <code>-p</code> do?","options":["Connects the computer’s port 8080 to the container’s","Sets the password","Picks the image"],"a":0,"why":"Without it, the server listens inside the container, and nothing outside can reach it."}]},{"id":"client","title":"Connecting your app","tagline":"Point the Recipes app at your own server, and follow the whole trip.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Add a local flavor whose base URL is your laptop, as the emulator sees it","Allow plain HTTP for that one address, in that flavor only","Follow a sign-in and a refresh from the app to your server and back"],"remember":"From the emulator your laptop is 10.0.2.2; a local flavor sets that base URL, and its own network security config allows plain HTTP to that address only.","terms":[{"term":"10.0.2.2","def":"The Android emulator’s address for the computer it runs on: that computer’s <code>127.0.0.1</code>."},{"term":"domain-config","def":"A part of the network security config with rules for the domains listed inside it, like allowing plain HTTP to <code>10.0.2.2</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the network security config for <code>10.0.2.2</code> go in <code>src/local</code>, not <code>src/main</code>?","options":["So only local builds may use plain HTTP","Android reads only flavor folders","<code>src/main</code> can’t hold XML files"],"a":0,"why":"The most specific folder wins. Staging and prod keep the HTTPS-only file from <code>src/main</code>."},{"q":"After 15 minutes the server answers 401. Which part of the app handles it?","options":["<code>Session</code>: it refreshes and repeats the call once","Room","The network security config"],"a":0,"why":"That is the plan from the sign-in lesson. The server’s <code>post(\"/refresh\")</code> is its other half."},{"q":"The server renames the JSON key <code>accessToken</code> to <code>access</code>. What breaks?","options":["Reading every <code>Tokens</code> answer: sign-in and refresh","Nothing: Retrofit guesses the name","Only the recipe list"],"a":0,"why":"Each key fills the property with the same name, and <code>accessToken</code> is missing now. Both halves of an API must keep the same keys."}]}]},{"id":"net","title":"Computer networks","description":"What happens between your app and a server, step by step: packets, addresses, DNS, TCP and UDP, HTTP, HTTPS, HTTP versions, speed and finding what broke.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/net.js","lessons":[{"id":"what-network","title":"What a network is","tagline":"Computers passing small packets of data, one asking and one answering.","level":"beginner","goals":["Say what a network and a packet are","Tell the client from the server in a conversation","Ask a server for a page with <code>curl</code>"],"remember":"Data travels in small numbered packets. The client asks, the server answers.","terms":[{"term":"network","def":"Computers connected so they can send each other data. The internet is a network of networks."},{"term":"packet","def":"A small piece of data with a label saying where it goes and where it came from, usually with a number too."},{"term":"client","def":"The side that asks, such as an app or <code>curl</code>."},{"term":"server","def":"The side that waits for requests and answers them."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Ada’s app asks for new messages, and a computer in a data centre (a building full of servers) sends them. Which one is the server?","options":["Ada’s app","The computer in the data centre","Both are servers"],"a":1,"why":"The server is the side that waits and answers. The app that asks is the client."},{"q":"Why do most packets carry a number?","options":["So the receiver can put the pieces back in order","To make packets travel faster","To count how much the data costs"],"a":0,"why":"Packets can arrive in any order. The numbers let the receiver rebuild the original data."},{"q":"Can a laptop be a server?","options":["No, servers are special machines","Yes: any computer that waits for requests and answers them is a server"],"a":1,"why":"Client and server are roles in a conversation, not kinds of machines."}]},{"id":"ip","title":"Addresses and routing","tagline":"Every device has an address, and routers pass packets along hop by hop.","level":"beginner","goals":["Read an IP address","Explain what a router does with a packet","List the hops to a server with <code>traceroute</code>"],"remember":"Every device has an IP address. Routers pass each packet one hop closer to its destination.","terms":[{"term":"IP address","def":"The number that identifies a device on a network, like <code>192.168.1.23</code>."},{"term":"router","def":"A device that passes packets on toward their destination. Your Wi-Fi box is one."},{"term":"hop","def":"One step of a packet’s trip. <code>traceroute</code> prints one line per hop, naming the router (or server) reached."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does a router do with a packet?","options":["Reads the data inside and answers it","Looks at the destination address and passes it one hop closer","Stores it until the server asks for it"],"a":1,"why":"A router is a signpost: it only needs the destination address to choose the next hop."},{"q":"A traceroute ends with hop 12 being the server. How many routers did the packets pass before the server?","options":["11","12","13"],"a":0,"why":"Hops 1 to 11 are routers; hop 12 is the server itself."},{"q":"Which command lists the hops on Windows?","options":["<code>traceroute</code>","<code>tracert</code>","<code>curl</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Windows names it <code>tracert</code>. macOS and Linux use <code>traceroute</code>."}]},{"id":"dns","title":"Names to addresses","tagline":"DNS turns a name like example.com into an IP address, and remembers it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what a DNS lookup does","Look up a name with <code>nslookup</code>","Say why the second lookup is faster"],"remember":"DNS turns a name into an IP address. Answers are cached for TTL seconds, so repeats are fast.","terms":[{"term":"DNS, DNS lookup","def":"The Domain Name System. A lookup asks it for the IP address that belongs to a name."},{"term":"resolver","def":"The DNS server your device asks, usually run by your router or internet provider."},{"term":"cache, TTL","def":"A cache is a short-term memory of answers. The TTL says how many seconds an answer may stay in it."},{"term":"NXDOMAIN","def":"The DNS answer for a name that does not exist."}],"quiz":[{"q":"An answer comes with a TTL of 300. What does that mean?","options":["The server is 300 km away","The answer may be kept in a cache for 300 seconds","The lookup took 300 ms"],"a":1,"why":"TTL means time to live: how long a cache may keep the answer before asking again."},{"q":"<code>nslookup</code> prints <code>NXDOMAIN</code>. What happened?","options":["The name does not exist","The server is down","The internet connection is broken"],"a":0,"why":"DNS answered, and the answer was “no such name”. Check the spelling."},{"q":"Why does a DNS lookup happen before the app sends its request?","options":["Packets need an IP address, not a name","To check the password","To make the request smaller"],"a":0,"why":"Routers only understand IP addresses, so the name must be turned into one first."}]},{"id":"tcp-udp","title":"TCP and UDP","tagline":"Two ways to send packets: carefully and complete, or fast and fire-and-forget.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what TCP adds: a connection, order and resending","Say when UDP is the better choice","Read the port number in an address"],"remember":"TCP: a connection, every packet confirmed and in order. UDP: sent once, fast. A port picks the program.","terms":[{"term":"TCP","def":"A way of sending that opens a connection, confirms every packet and resends lost ones."},{"term":"UDP","def":"A way of sending without a connection: each packet is sent once, nothing is resent."},{"term":"connection","def":"An agreement between two sides to exchange data, opened by a handshake."},{"term":"port","def":"A number that picks a program on a computer, such as 443 for secure web pages."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A video call loses one packet. Which way of sending fits a call better?","options":["TCP: wait until the packet is sent again","UDP: skip it and keep the call going"],"a":1,"why":"For live audio and video, late data is useless. UDP keeps things moving."},{"q":"What does a port number choose?","options":["The computer","The program on that computer","The router"],"a":1,"why":"The IP address finds the computer; the port finds the program on it."},{"q":"Why does TCP cost a little time before the first data moves?","options":["The handshake must go there and back first","TCP packets are bigger","The server must restart"],"a":0,"why":"The connection opens with a handshake, one trip there and back, before any data is sent."}]},{"id":"http","title":"HTTP","tagline":"The request and the response: how apps and servers talk.","level":"beginner","goals":["Name the parts of an HTTP request and response","Read status codes like 200 and 404","Send a GET and a POST with <code>curl</code>"],"remember":"A request has a method, a path and headers. A response has a status code, headers and a body.","terms":[{"term":"HTTP, request, response","def":"HTTP is the language of the web. The client sends a request, the server sends back a response."},{"term":"method","def":"The first word of a request: <code>GET</code> to read, <code>POST</code> to send something new."},{"term":"status code","def":"The number at the start of a response: 2xx success, 4xx mistake in the request, 5xx problem on the server."},{"term":"header","def":"A line of extra information, <code>Name: value</code>, such as <code>Content-Type: application/json</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A response starts with <code>HTTP/1.1 500 Internal Server Error</code>. Whose problem is it?","options":["The request was wrong","Something broke on the server","The network is down"],"a":1,"why":"5xx codes mean the server failed. The network worked: an answer came back."},{"q":"Which method fits “show me today’s weather”?","options":["<code>GET</code>","<code>POST</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Reading something is a GET. POST sends something new."},{"q":"What is <code>Content-Type: application/json</code>?","options":["A header","A status code","The body"],"a":0,"why":"It has the form <code>Name: value</code>, so it is a header. It describes the body."}]},{"id":"tls","title":"HTTPS and TLS","tagline":"Locking the conversation so only the two ends can read it.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what encryption and a certificate do","Follow a TLS handshake in <code>curl -v</code>","Recognise certificate errors"],"remember":"HTTPS = HTTP inside TLS: encrypted, and the certificate proves who the server is.","terms":[{"term":"TLS, HTTPS","def":"TLS encrypts a connection and checks who the server is. HTTPS is HTTP sent inside TLS."},{"term":"encryption","def":"Scrambling data so only someone with the key can read it."},{"term":"certificate","def":"A server’s digital ID card: its name, an expiry date, and the signature of a trusted certificate authority."},{"term":"handshake","def":"The opening messages of a connection. In TLS they agree on keys and check the certificate."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Ada uses HTTPS on café Wi-Fi. What can the café’s router see?","options":["Everything, including her messages","Which server she talks to, but not what she sends or receives","Nothing at all"],"a":1,"why":"The router must see the server’s address to pass the packets on, but the contents are encrypted."},{"q":"What does a certificate prove?","options":["That the server is fast","That the server really is the name you asked for","That the data has no viruses"],"a":1,"why":"It is an ID card signed by someone your device trusts."},{"q":"When does the TLS handshake happen?","options":["After the TCP connection opens, before any HTTP","After the HTTP response","Before the DNS lookup"],"a":0,"why":"Order: DNS, TCP, TLS, then HTTP inside the encrypted connection."}]},{"id":"http-versions","title":"HTTP/1.1, 2 and 3","tagline":"Same requests, faster delivery: sharing one connection, then moving to QUIC.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why HTTP/1.1 makes requests wait in line","Describe multiplexing in HTTP/2","Say what HTTP/3 and QUIC change"],"remember":"HTTP/1.1: one request at a time per connection. HTTP/2: many at once on one TCP connection. HTTP/3: the same on QUIC.","terms":[{"term":"multiplexing","def":"Sending many requests and answers at the same time over one connection, each in its own stream."},{"term":"HTTP/2","def":"The HTTP version that multiplexes requests over a single TCP connection."},{"term":"HTTP/3, QUIC","def":"HTTP/3 runs on QUIC, a protocol built on UDP that resends lost packets per stream and sets up faster."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does multiplexing mean in HTTP/2?","options":["Many requests at once over one connection","Sending each request twice","Opening one connection per image"],"a":0,"why":"Each request gets its own stream, and all streams share the connection."},{"q":"HTTP/3 runs on which protocol?","options":["TCP","QUIC, built on UDP","DNS"],"a":1,"why":"QUIC brings its own resending per stream, on top of UDP."},{"q":"The server switches from HTTP/1.1 to HTTP/2. What changes in the requests you write?","options":["Every method gets a new name","Nothing: methods, headers and status codes stay the same","You must add a version header to each request"],"a":1,"why":"The version changes how requests travel, not what they mean."}]},{"id":"latency","title":"Latency and bandwidth","tagline":"Why a fast connection can still feel slow: round trips.","level":"beginner","goals":["Tell latency from bandwidth","Measure a round trip with <code>ping</code>","Count the round trips before a first response"],"remember":"Bandwidth is how much per second; latency is how long each trip takes. Small requests wait on round trips.","terms":[{"term":"bandwidth","def":"How much data a connection can carry per second."},{"term":"latency","def":"How long data takes to travel from one end to the other."},{"term":"round trip, RTT","def":"A trip there and back. The RTT is its time, measured by <code>ping</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"An app makes 30 tiny requests, one after another, on mobile data. What slows it down most?","options":["Bandwidth","Latency: 30 round trips","The size of the images"],"a":1,"why":"Tiny requests carry little data. The time goes into waiting for 30 trips there and back."},{"q":"What does <code>ping</code> measure?","options":["Bandwidth","Round-trip time","The number of hops"],"a":1,"why":"It times a tiny packet there and back."},{"q":"Why is a second request on an open connection faster than the first?","options":["It skips the DNS, TCP and TLS round trips","The server remembers the answer","It uses UDP"],"a":0,"why":"The connection is already set up, so only the request’s own round trip is left."}]},{"id":"debugging","title":"Seeing network traffic","tagline":"When a request fails, find out which step broke.","level":"beginner","goals":["Match a curl error to the step that failed","Tell a network failure from an HTTP error status","Explain what a debugging proxy shows"],"remember":"Find the failing step: DNS (6), TCP (7, 28), TLS (60). A status code means the server answered.","terms":[{"term":"timeout","def":"Giving up after waiting a set time for an answer that never came."},{"term":"proxy","def":"A program in the middle that requests pass through. A debugging proxy records them so you can read them."}],"quiz":[{"q":"curl prints <code>HTTP/1.1 500 Internal Server Error</code>. Did the network work?","options":["No, the connection failed","Yes: DNS, TCP and TLS worked, and the server answered with an error"],"a":1,"why":"Any status code means a response arrived. The problem is inside the server."},{"q":"What does a debugging proxy do?","options":["Makes requests faster","Sits in the middle and records each request and response","Fixes broken certificates"],"a":1,"why":"All traffic passes through it, so you can read every request and response."},{"q":"<code>curl http://localhost:8080</code> gives error 7. What is the likely cause?","options":["A typo in the name","Nothing is running on port 8080","An expired certificate"],"a":1,"why":"<code>localhost</code> always resolves, and plain <code>http://</code> has no certificate. The computer refused the connection: no program listens on that port."}]}]},{"id":"os","title":"Operating systems","description":"What happens inside your phone when apps run: how it shares out time, memory, files and battery between them, and why apps freeze or close. Tried with simple terminal commands, then linked to Android.","requires":["kotlin"],"file":"tracks/os.js","lessons":[{"id":"what-os","title":"What an operating system does","tagline":"The program that runs all the other programs.","level":"beginner","goals":["Say what an operating system does for the apps on your phone","Explain what the kernel is","Find out which kernel a computer runs with <code>uname</code>"],"remember":"Apps never touch the hardware. They ask the operating system, and its kernel does the work.","terms":[{"term":"operating system (OS)","def":"The program that runs all the other programs and shares the hardware between them, such as Android, Windows or Linux."},{"term":"kernel","def":"The heart of the OS: it controls the hardware and decides which program may do what. Android uses the Linux kernel."},{"term":"hardware","def":"The physical parts of a device: CPU, memory, storage, screen, camera."},{"term":"hardware abstraction, driver","def":"Apps make simple requests like “save this file”, and the OS turns them into work on the real parts. A driver is the code that knows one exact part."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A music app wants to play a sound. How does it reach the speaker?","options":["It asks the OS, which uses the speaker’s driver","It sends electricity to the speaker itself","It needs a different version for every phone model"],"a":0,"why":"Hardware abstraction: the app asks, and the OS and its driver handle the real part."},{"q":"Which part of the OS controls the hardware?","options":["The home screen","The kernel","The settings app"],"a":1,"why":"The home screen and settings are programs on top of the kernel."},{"q":"Android is built on which kernel?","options":["Windows","Linux","Darwin"],"a":1,"why":"Android uses the Linux kernel. Darwin is the kernel of macOS."}]},{"id":"processes","title":"Processes","tagline":"A running program, in its own walled-off space.","level":"beginner","goals":["Tell a program from a process","Explain why one app crashing doesn’t crash the others","List running processes with <code>ps</code>"],"remember":"A process is a running program with its own memory and PID. A crash ends only that process.","terms":[{"term":"process","def":"A program that is running, with its own memory. Opening an app starts one."},{"term":"PID (process ID)","def":"The number the OS gives each process, such as <code>4821</code>."},{"term":"memory isolation","def":"The wall the OS builds between processes: one can’t read or change another one’s memory."},{"term":"process lifecycle","def":"The life of a process: created, running, often waiting, and finally ended."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the difference between a program and a process?","options":["A process is a program that is running","They are two words for the same thing","A program is a process that has crashed"],"a":0,"why":"The program is the stored file of instructions. Running it starts a process."},{"q":"A game crashes while a map app is open. What happens to the map app?","options":["It crashes too","It keeps running: each process has its own memory","It loses everything in its memory"],"a":1,"why":"Memory isolation keeps a crash inside the process where it happened."},{"q":"You close Music and open it again. Does the new process have the same PID?","options":["Yes, always","No: the OS starts a new process, with a new PID"],"a":1,"why":"Every start is a new process, with a new number and empty memory."}]},{"id":"threads","title":"Threads","tagline":"Several jobs at once inside one process.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what a thread is","Say why an app freezes when its main thread is busy","List the threads of a process with <code>ps -T</code>"],"remember":"A thread is one line of work inside a process. Threads of a process share its memory; a busy main thread freezes the app.","terms":[{"term":"thread","def":"One line of work inside a process, running instructions one after another. A process can have many."},{"term":"main thread","def":"The thread an app starts with. It draws the screen and answers taps, so slow work belongs elsewhere."},{"term":"SPID","def":"The number of one thread, shown by <code>ps -T</code>. The PID says which process it belongs to."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What do threads of the same process share?","options":["Its memory","Nothing: they are walled off like processes","Only the screen"],"a":0,"why":"Memory isolation is between processes. Threads inside one process share its memory."},{"q":"An app freezes for a few seconds every time you tap Save. What is the likely cause?","options":["The main thread is doing slow work itself","The app has too many threads","The phone has no kernel"],"a":0,"why":"While the main thread works, it can’t draw or answer taps."},{"q":"In <code>ps -T</code> output, every row shows PID 5996. What does that tell you?","options":["They are threads of one process","They are separate processes","The command failed"],"a":0,"why":"The PID names the process. The SPID column tells the threads apart."}]},{"id":"scheduling","title":"Sharing the CPU","tagline":"Hundreds of threads, a few cores: the OS takes turns.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain how a few CPU cores run hundreds of threads","Describe a time slice and a context switch","Count cores with <code>nproc</code> and watch processes with <code>top</code>"],"remember":"A core runs one thread at a time. The scheduler gives out short time slices and switches so fast that everything seems to run at once.","terms":[{"term":"CPU core","def":"One part of the processor that runs one thread at a time. Phones have several."},{"term":"scheduler","def":"The part of the kernel that picks which thread runs next on each core."},{"term":"time slice","def":"The few milliseconds a thread may run before the scheduler gives another one a turn."},{"term":"context switch","def":"Pausing one thread and running another. The kernel saves where the first one was, so it can continue later."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does the scheduler decide?","options":["Which thread runs next on each core","Which files an app may open","How bright the screen is"],"a":0,"why":"It hands out time slices on the CPU cores."},{"q":"<code>top</code> says 311 of 312 processes are sleeping. What are they doing?","options":["Waiting for something, without using the CPU","They are broken and need restarting","Running in secret"],"a":0,"why":"A sleeping process waits for a tap, a timer or data. It needs no turns until then."},{"q":"What happens in a context switch?","options":["The kernel saves where one thread was and runs another","The app starts again from the beginning","The phone switches to another network"],"a":0,"why":"Saving the paused thread’s place lets it continue later as if nothing happened."}]},{"id":"memory","title":"Memory","tagline":"Where a running program keeps its values.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what RAM is and how it differs from storage","Tell the stack from the heap","Check how much RAM is free with <code>free -h</code>"],"remember":"RAM is fast working memory that is wiped when the power goes. Function values live on the stack, objects on the heap.","terms":[{"term":"RAM","def":"Fast working memory where running programs keep their values. It is wiped when the device switches off."},{"term":"stack","def":"Memory for the functions running right now: each call adds a frame with its local values, and returning removes it."},{"term":"heap","def":"Memory for objects such as lists, which can outlive the function that made them."},{"term":"virtual memory","def":"The private addresses each process sees. The kernel maps them to real places in RAM."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You switch the phone off and on again. What is gone?","options":["What was in RAM","Your photos in storage","The installed apps"],"a":0,"why":"RAM needs power to keep its contents. Storage keeps files without power."},{"q":"A function makes a local <code>val</code>, then returns. Where did the value live, and what happens to it?","options":["On the stack; it is removed when the function returns","On the heap; it stays forever","In storage; it is saved as a file"],"a":0,"why":"Local values live in the function’s stack frame, which goes away on return."},{"q":"Why can’t process A read process B’s memory?","options":["A’s virtual addresses don’t lead into B’s memory","B hides its memory with a password","RAM is split into two halves"],"a":0,"why":"The kernel maps each process’s addresses only to that process’s own places in RAM."}]},{"id":"low-memory","title":"When memory runs out","tagline":"Phones close apps you aren’t using to make room.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what happens when RAM is full","Predict which app a phone closes first","End a process yourself with <code>kill</code>"],"remember":"When RAM runs out, a phone kills the processes you are least likely to miss. A killed app starts over with empty memory.","terms":[{"term":"out of memory","def":"When programs need more RAM than is free."},{"term":"kill (a process)","def":"End a process on purpose. Phones do it to free RAM; in a terminal, <code>kill</code> and a PID does it."},{"term":"background","def":"An app is in the background when it is open but not on the screen, like music playing while you read."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You come back to an app and it opens on its first screen. What probably happened?","options":["The phone killed its process to free RAM","The app was deleted","The storage is full"],"a":0,"why":"A killed app starts again as a new process, with empty memory."},{"q":"Which app is the phone least likely to kill?","options":["A music app playing in the background","A chat app you left yesterday","A map you left an hour ago"],"a":0,"why":"You would notice the music stopping, so it is kept."},{"q":"What does <code>kill 6120</code> do?","options":["Ends the process with PID 6120","Deletes the file named 6120","Frees 6120 MB of RAM"],"a":0,"why":"<code>kill</code> takes a PID."}]},{"id":"ipc","title":"Processes talking","tagline":"Walled-off processes still pass messages.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why processes need the kernel to talk","Describe how a phone app asks the system to show a notification","Connect two commands with a pipe <code>|</code>"],"remember":"Processes talk by passing messages through the kernel. In the terminal, a pipe | passes one command’s output to the next.","terms":[{"term":"inter-process communication (IPC)","def":"Processes passing data to each other through the kernel, such as an app asking the system to show a notification."},{"term":"pipe","def":"The <code>|</code> between two commands: the kernel passes what the first prints into the second."},{"term":"wc","def":"Counts what it is given. <code>wc -l</code> counts lines."},{"term":"grep","def":"Keeps only the lines that contain a word, such as <code>grep sleep</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a phone app ask the system to show a notification, instead of drawing it itself?","options":["The top bar belongs to another process, the system","Apps can’t draw anything","Notifications are stored in a file"],"a":0,"why":"Memory isolation: only the system process may change what it owns, so the app sends it a request."},{"q":"In <code>ps -e | wc -l</code>, what does <code>|</code> do?","options":["Passes what <code>ps -e</code> prints into <code>wc</code>","Runs <code>ps</code>, then deletes its output","Means “or”, like <code>||</code> in Kotlin"],"a":0,"why":"A pipe connects two processes: the output of the first becomes the input of the second."},{"q":"Who carries a message from one process to another?","options":["The kernel","The screen","Nobody: processes read each other’s memory"],"a":0,"why":"Only the kernel can reach both sides of the wall."}]},{"id":"files","title":"Files and storage","tagline":"How the OS keeps files in folders, and who may open them.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what a file system does","Tell an absolute path from a relative one","Read permissions like <code>-rw-r--r--</code> in <code>ls -l</code>"],"remember":"The file system keeps files in folders. Every file has an owner and permissions (r, w, x), and the kernel checks them.","terms":[{"term":"file system","def":"The part of the OS that turns storage into named files inside folders."},{"term":"absolute path, relative path","def":"An absolute path starts at <code>/</code> and works anywhere. A relative path, like <code>notes.txt</code>, starts from the folder you are in."},{"term":"permissions","def":"Who may read (<code>r</code>), write (<code>w</code>) or run (<code>x</code>) a file. <code>ls -l</code> shows them."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which is an absolute path?","options":["<code>/home/ada/notes.txt</code>","<code>notes.txt</code>","<code>ada/notes.txt</code>"],"a":0,"why":"An absolute path starts at the top, <code>/</code>."},{"q":"A file shows <code>-rw-r--r--</code>. What may other users do with it?","options":["Only read it","Read and change it","Nothing at all"],"a":0,"why":"The last group, <code>r--</code>, is for everyone else: read, but no write."},{"q":"Why can’t one phone app open another app’s files?","options":["Each app has its own user and a private folder, and the kernel checks permissions","Files are hidden behind the screen","Apps can’t use storage"],"a":0,"why":"It is the same owner-and-permissions idea as on a shared computer."}]},{"id":"power","title":"Power and battery","tagline":"Why a phone sleeps, and why it limits apps that keep it awake.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain why waiting costs almost no battery","Describe what a wake lock does","Say why phones limit apps in the background"],"remember":"A waiting CPU sleeps and saves power. A wake lock keeps it awake, so Android limits apps in the background.","terms":[{"term":"sleep state","def":"A low-power mode the CPU enters when nothing needs to run. It wakes up when something happens."},{"term":"wake lock","def":"An app’s request to the OS: “don’t let the CPU sleep yet”. Forgotten ones drain the battery."},{"term":"Doze","def":"Android’s mode for a phone left still with its screen off: background work waits for short, shared windows."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does a sleeping CPU save battery?","options":["It uses very little power until something wakes it","It charges the battery","It closes all the apps"],"a":0,"why":"A sleep state is a low-power mode that ends when an event arrives."},{"q":"What does a wake lock ask the OS?","options":["Don’t let the CPU sleep yet","Lock the screen","Wake me up at 07:00"],"a":0,"why":"It keeps the CPU awake so the app can finish a job."},{"q":"<code>time sleep 2</code> shows <code>real 0m2.003s</code> and <code>user 0m0.002s</code>. What does that tell you?","options":["Waiting took real time but almost no CPU work","The computer is broken","<code>sleep</code> ran for only 2 milliseconds"],"a":0,"why":"A waiting process doesn’t use the CPU, which is why waiting is cheap."}]}]},{"id":"sysdesign","title":"Mobile system design","description":"Plan the server side of an app, step by step: requirements and estimates, APIs, pagination, caching, sync, real-time, push, media and reliability, then two full designs.","requires":["arch","data"],"file":"tracks/sysdesign.js","lessons":[{"id":"approach","title":"How to approach a system design","tagline":"Before any server code: what the system must do, how much work that is, and which parts do it.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Write down what a system must do before drawing anything","Estimate requests per second and storage from a few example numbers","Sketch the parts of a system and the job of each one"],"remember":"Requirements first, then rough example numbers, then the parts and their jobs. Then find the hard part.","terms":[{"term":"system design","def":"A plan for a whole system: which parts it has, what each one does, and how much work it must handle."},{"term":"requirements","def":"What the system must do (“see my recipes on every device”) and how well (“Explore opens in under a second”)."},{"term":"estimate","def":"A rough calculation from example numbers, such as requests per second or storage per year, to see the size of the job."},{"term":"API server","def":"The program on a server that answers the app’s requests, for Recipes at <code>recipes.example.com</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What comes first in a system design?","options":["Writing down the requirements","Choosing a database","Writing the server code"],"a":0,"why":"Every later choice should point back to something the system must do. Without requirements, each box is a guess."},{"q":"Example numbers: 50,000 daily users, 10 requests each. About how many requests per second is that on average?","options":["About 6","About 60","About 500"],"a":0,"why":"500,000 requests a day / 86,400 seconds is about 5.8, so about 6."},{"q":"Reads outnumber writes by about 100 to 1. Where should the design put most of its effort?","options":["Answering reads fast","Making saves faster","Neither: both happen equally often"],"a":0,"why":"Almost all the work is reads, so that is where speed matters most."}]},{"id":"api","title":"Designing an API","tagline":"Name requests after the things the app works with, and never break the apps already installed.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Name requests after resources, such as <code>GET v1/recipes/42</code>","Tell a safe API change from a breaking one","Put a version in the path, so old app versions keep working"],"remember":"Nouns in the path, the method says what to do. Add freely inside a version; anything that breaks old apps goes to the next version.","terms":[{"term":"API","def":"The requests a server answers and what each one sends back. The app and the server both depend on it."},{"term":"resource","def":"A thing the app works with, named as a noun in the path: <code>recipes</code>, <code>recipes/42</code>."},{"term":"breaking change","def":"A change that makes installed apps fail: renaming or removing a key, or changing its type. Adding a key is not one."},{"term":"API version","def":"A name in the path, like <code>v1/</code>, for one shape of the API. A breaking change goes to the next one, <code>v2/</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which change can stay in v1?","options":["Adding a new key, <code>\"servings\"</code>","Renaming <code>minutes</code> to <code>durationMinutes</code>","Changing <code>id</code> from a number to a text"],"a":0,"why":"Old apps skip a new key. A rename or a new type breaks every app that reads that key."},{"q":"Which request reads recipe 42 in the Recipes API?","options":["<code>GET v1/recipes/42</code>","<code>POST v1/getRecipe?id=42</code>","<code>DELETE v1/recipes/42</code>"],"a":0,"why":"The noun and the id are in the path, and <code>GET</code> says “read it”."},{"q":"v2 is out. Why does the server keep answering v1?","options":["Old app versions are still installed and call it","v2 needs v1 to work","The database only understands v1"],"a":0,"why":"People don’t update at once. v1 can go when its requests have nearly stopped."}]},{"id":"pagination","title":"Pagination on the server","tagline":"Page numbers repeat rows when new ones arrive. A cursor remembers where you stopped.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why page numbers repeat items when new rows arrive","Design a list endpoint that hands out a cursor","Switch a <code>PagingSource</code> from page numbers to cursors"],"remember":"Offsets count rows, so new rows shift every page. A cursor names the last row sent, so the next page starts right after it.","terms":[{"term":"offset pagination","def":"Pages made by skipping rows: page 2 is <code>LIMIT 20 OFFSET 20</code>. New rows at the top shift every page."},{"term":"cursor","def":"A text the server sends with a page, like <code>\"next\": \"19981\"</code>, that says where it stopped. The app sends it back unchanged for the next page."},{"term":"LIMIT, OFFSET","def":"SQL: <code>LIMIT 20</code> keeps at most 20 rows; <code>OFFSET 20</code> skips the first 20 before that."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Explore uses page numbers. Two recipes are added while Ada reads page 1. What happens on page 2?","options":["Its first two recipes repeat the end of page 1","Two recipes are skipped","Nothing changes"],"a":0,"why":"The new rows push every row down by two, and the offset still skips exactly 20."},{"q":"The answer says <code>\"next\": \"19981\"</code>. What does the app do with it?","options":["Sends it back unchanged as the cursor for the next page","Adds 20 to it","Shows it to the user"],"a":0,"why":"Only the server knows what is inside the cursor. The app hands it back as it is."},{"q":"What can cursors not do easily?","options":["Jump straight to page 50","Cope with new rows at the top","Tell the app that the list has ended"],"a":0,"why":"A cursor only knows where the last page stopped. For a list you scroll, that is all you need."}]},{"id":"caching","title":"Caching on the server","tagline":"Most requests ask for the same thing. Answer them from a copy before they reach the database.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Place caches in layers: the app, a CDN, the server cache, then the database","Tell shared caches what they may keep with <code>Cache-Control</code>","Throw away a cached copy when the data changes"],"remember":"Check the closest copy first: the app, the CDN, the server cache, the database. Public answers may be shared; private ones never.","terms":[{"term":"server cache","def":"Recent answers kept in memory next to the API server, so repeated requests don’t reach the database."},{"term":"hit, miss","def":"A hit is a request answered from the cache. A miss finds no fresh copy and has to ask further down."},{"term":"CDN","def":"Content delivery network: servers in many cities that keep copies of answers close to the users. Each of these servers is an <em>edge server</em>."},{"term":"Cache-Control","def":"A header on the answer that tells caches what they may keep: <code>public, max-age=60</code> (anyone, 60 seconds) or <code>private</code> (only the user’s own device)."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which answer may the CDN keep and share?","options":["The first page of Explore","Ada’s own recipes","The tokens from <code>login</code>"],"a":0,"why":"Explore is the same for everyone. The other two belong to one person."},{"q":"92 requests a second, and the CDN answers 80% of them. About how many reach the API server?","options":["About 18","About 74","All 92"],"a":0,"why":"20% of 92 is about 18. The other 74 are answered at the edge."},{"q":"Ada edits recipe 42. What should the server do with its cached copy of that recipe?","options":["Delete it right away","Keep it until its 60 seconds are up","Nothing: caches update themselves"],"a":0,"why":"Deleting it means the next request reads the new version from the database. Otherwise people see the old one for up to a minute."}]},{"id":"offline","title":"Sync and conflicts","tagline":"Two devices change the same recipe offline. The server notices, and nothing is lost.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain how two offline edits can conflict","Detect a conflict on the server with a version number","Merge after a <code>409 Conflict</code> instead of overwriting"],"remember":"Every save raises the version. An edit based on an old version gets 409 Conflict, never a silent overwrite.","terms":[{"term":"conflict","def":"Two changes to the same data, made without knowing about each other, for example on two devices while one was offline."},{"term":"last write wins","def":"A rule for conflicts: whatever arrives last replaces the rest. Simple, but the other change is lost."},{"term":"version number","def":"A number on each record that goes up by one at every save. An edit says which version it was based on."},{"term":"409 Conflict","def":"The status code for “your change is based on an old copy”. The answer carries the server’s current copy."}],"quiz":[{"q":"With last write wins, what is lost in Ada’s story?","options":["The tablet’s new title","Nothing","Both changes"],"a":0,"why":"The phone’s <code>PUT</code> arrives last and carries the whole recipe with the old title."},{"q":"The server’s recipe is at version 7. An edit based on version 7 arrives. What happens?","options":["It is saved as version 8","<code>409 Conflict</code>","It is saved, and stays version 7"],"a":0,"why":"Same version: nobody saved in between, so the edit is saved and the number goes up."},{"q":"After a 409, the two copies changed different fields. What should the app do?","options":["Keep both changes and send again with the new version","Send the same request again","Throw its change away"],"a":0,"why":"Sending the same request again gets another 409. Merging keeps both changes, and the new version number lets the save through."}]},{"id":"realtime","title":"Real-time at scale","tagline":"Thousands of open connections need their own servers, and a way to reach the right one.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why open WebSockets go to their own connection servers","Estimate how many connections the Recipes server holds at the dinner peak","Follow one live change through pub/sub to every phone of the right user"],"remember":"Open WebSockets live on connection servers; the API server publishes each change once, and pub/sub brings it to the servers holding that user’s phones.","terms":[{"term":"connection server","def":"A server that only holds open WebSockets and passes messages down them."},{"term":"load balancer","def":"A server in front of several others that hands each new request or connection to one of them, so none gets too many."},{"term":"pub/sub","def":"Publish and subscribe: one side publishes a message, and every subscriber gets a copy."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why do open WebSockets get their own connection servers?","options":["Each one stays open as long as the screen does, so these servers hold thousands of connections at once","WebSockets can’t carry JSON","The API server is only allowed one connection"],"a":0,"why":"A request is over in a moment. A WebSocket is held for minutes or hours, which is a different kind of work."},{"q":"Example numbers: 20,000 phones poll every 10 seconds. How many requests a second is that?","options":["2,000","200","20,000"],"a":0,"why":"20,000 / 10 = 2,000, and almost all of them find nothing new."},{"q":"Ada’s phone is on server B, her tablet on A. The API server saves her change. What happens next?","options":["It publishes one message; A and B both get it and pass it to Ada’s devices","It sends the change to B only, because it remembers where her phone is","Nothing, until the phone polls"],"a":0,"why":"With pub/sub the API server doesn’t need to know where anyone is connected."}]},{"id":"push-scale","title":"Push at scale","tagline":"One event, 150,000 phones: a queue, a worker, and batches of 500.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain fan-out: one event that becomes many deliveries","Split installation IDs into batches with <code>chunked</code> and send each with one FCM call","Clean up IDs that will never work again"],"remember":"Fan-out goes through a queue to a worker, which sends in batches of 500, deletes dead IDs and sets a time to live.","terms":[{"term":"fan-out","def":"One event that becomes many deliveries, one per device."},{"term":"queue","def":"A list of jobs kept on the server. Workers take them in turn, and a job stays until a worker marks it done."},{"term":"batch","def":"A group of items handled in one go, such as 500 installation IDs in one FCM call. <code>chunked(n)</code> makes them."},{"term":"time to live","def":"TTL: how long FCM keeps trying to deliver a message to a phone that is offline. Four weeks unless you set it shorter."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the API server put a job in a queue instead of sending the 150,000 messages itself?","options":["It can answer at once, and the job survives if the worker crashes","FCM only accepts calls from queues","Queues make messages smaller"],"a":0,"why":"The slow work happens in a worker. The job waits safely in the queue until it is done."},{"q":"FCM answers <code>UNREGISTERED</code> for one installation ID. What should the worker do?","options":["Delete that ID’s row","Try it again next Friday","Send it twice"],"a":0,"why":"That install is gone and the ID will never work again. Keeping it wastes a call on every fan-out."},{"q":"Lin’s phone is switched off from Friday to Monday. The weekly pick was sent with a time to live of two days. What happens?","options":["She doesn’t get it: it expired on Sunday","She gets it on Monday","FCM sends it to her tablet instead"],"a":0,"why":"FCM stops trying when the time to live runs out. Without one, she would get it, up to four weeks late."}]},{"id":"media","title":"Media at scale","tagline":"Photos go straight to photo storage, get copies in a few sizes, and reach phones through the CDN.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Upload a photo straight to photo storage with a signed upload URL","Pick the right size copy from the width the screen will draw","Explain why the CDN can keep photo copies for a year"],"remember":"Upload straight to photo storage with a signed URL, make a few sizes once, ask for the smallest that fits, and let the CDN keep them.","terms":[{"term":"photo storage","def":"A service that keeps files by name, cheap for huge amounts, and hands them out again."},{"term":"signed upload URL","def":"An address that lets the app send one file to storage for a short time. It carries a code only the server can make, which storage checks."},{"term":"image variant","def":"A copy of one photo at a set width, made once by the server, such as <code>p7-360.jpg</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does the app upload the photo with a signed upload URL instead of through the API server?","options":["The file goes straight to photo storage, so the API server isn’t busy for the whole upload","The API server can’t receive files","It makes the photo smaller"],"a":0,"why":"The API server only signs a short-lived address. Storage is built for big files."},{"q":"Example numbers: 20 thumbnails at 300 KB each, or at 30 KB each. How much data does the smaller copy save on one screen?","options":["5.4 MB: 6 MB down to 600 KB","270 KB","Nothing, the CDN shrinks them anyway"],"a":0,"why":"20 × 300 KB = 6 MB, 20 × 30 KB = 600 KB."},{"q":"Why can the CDN keep photo copies for a whole year?","options":["Copies are named after the photo id, so a name always shows the same picture","Photos are too big to download twice","The CDN ignores max-age"],"a":0,"why":"A replaced photo gets a new id, such as <code>p8</code>, so its copies get new URLs. An old name never goes out of date."}]},{"id":"reliability","title":"Reliability","tagline":"Retry safely with an idempotency key, and wait a little longer, a little randomly, each time.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Explain why a retried <code>POST</code> can save a recipe twice","Make a retry safe with an idempotency key the server remembers","Space retries out with exponential backoff and jitter"],"remember":"Send an idempotency key with every create, so a retry can’t save twice; retry with growing, random waits.","terms":[{"term":"idempotent","def":"Doing it twice has the same effect as doing it once. <code>GET</code>, <code>PUT</code> and <code>DELETE</code> are; a <code>POST</code> that creates is not."},{"term":"idempotency key","def":"A random id the app makes once per action and sends with every try, so the server can answer a retry without doing the work again."},{"term":"exponential backoff","def":"Waiting twice as long after each failed try (1 s, 2 s, 4 s…), up to a limit."},{"term":"jitter","def":"A random part added to each wait, so phones that failed together don’t retry together."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Which request is safe to retry even without an idempotency key?","options":["<code>GET v1/recipes/42</code>","<code>POST v1/recipes</code>","Any request, if you wait long enough"],"a":0,"why":"A GET only reads, so doing it twice changes nothing. A POST may create a second recipe."},{"q":"Why add jitter to backoff?","options":["So phones that failed at the same moment don’t all retry at the same moment","To make each retry faster","Because the server needs random numbers"],"a":0,"why":"Without it, 10,000 phones retry at 1 s, 2 s, 4 s together, in bursts that can keep the server down."},{"q":"The server answers 429 with <code>Retry-After: 120</code>. What should the app do?","options":["Wait at least 120 seconds before trying again","Try again after 1 second","Never try again"],"a":0,"why":"The server says how much it can take. Trying sooner only earns more 429s."}]},{"id":"news-feed","title":"Design: news feed","tagline":"The news reader’s server, end to end: requirements, numbers, and every piece of this track.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Walk through a design in order: requirements, estimate, pieces","Decide which answers the CDN can share and which must stay private","Choose push, not WebSockets, for breaking news, and say why"],"remember":"Requirements, then numbers, then pieces: a read-heavy feed lives on the CDN, personal data stays private, and breaking news goes by push.","terms":[{"term":"read-heavy","def":"A system that is read far more often than it is written, like a news feed. Its design is about cheap reads."},{"term":"hit ratio","def":"The share of requests a cache answers by itself. At 95%, only 5 of every 100 reach the server behind it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does breaking news go by push instead of a WebSocket to every reader?","options":["It happens only a few times a day; holding 1,000,000 open connections for that wastes servers and batteries","WebSockets can’t carry headlines","Push is always faster"],"a":0,"why":"Push needs no open connection of your own. A WebSocket fits when changes come often and the screen is open, as in chat."},{"q":"Example numbers: 368 requests a second and a 95% hit ratio. About how many reach the API server?","options":["About 18 a second","About 350 a second","None"],"a":0,"why":"5% of 368 is about 18. That is why the API server of a read-heavy app can stay small."},{"q":"Why does the breaking-news push get a time to live of one hour?","options":["A phone that comes back hours later shouldn’t get old news as breaking news","FCM refuses anything longer","It makes the push arrive faster"],"a":0,"why":"Without it, FCM keeps trying for up to four weeks. After an hour the story is in the normal headlines anyway."}]},{"id":"chat","title":"Design: chat app","tagline":"Chat in Recipes, end to end: never lost, never twice, in the same order on every phone.","level":"intermediate","goals":["Turn chat’s requirements into an estimate and a set of pieces","Order messages with a server sequence number, not phone clocks","Follow one message from pending to sent, delivered and read, also when the other phone is offline"],"remember":"Room first, an id from the app, a number from the server, receipts back, and push plus “everything after” for phones that were away.","terms":[{"term":"sequence number","def":"A counter the server gives each message of one conversation. Every phone sorts by it, and a missing number shows a gap."},{"term":"receipt","def":"A small message from the receiving phone back to the sender: “delivered” or “read”. It turns into the ticks."},{"term":"online list","def":"A list, shared by the connection servers, of who has an open WebSocket right now. Also called <em>presence</em>. Someone not on it gets a push instead."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What decides the order of messages on every phone?","options":["The server’s sequence number","Each phone’s clock","The order the push messages arrive"],"a":0,"why":"One counter on the server, one order for everyone. Clocks and networks can’t promise that."},{"q":"Sam’s phone has messages up to 41, then receives 43. What should it do?","options":["Ask the server for 42, then show them in order","Show 43 and forget 42","Renumber 43 as 42"],"a":0,"why":"Sequence numbers make a gap visible, so the phone can fetch what it missed."},{"q":"Ada sends a message while Sam’s phone is off. What does she see?","options":["One tick now, two when Sam’s phone stores it and sends a receipt","Two ticks at once","A clock until Sam reads it"],"a":0,"why":"One tick means the server has it. Two need Sam’s phone, which gets it after the push wakes it."}]}]},{"id":"start","title":"First steps","description":"Never programmed before? Start here: what a program is, your first lines of code, and what to do when it goes wrong.","requires":[],"file":"tracks/start.js","lessons":[{"id":"program","title":"What a program is","tagline":"A list of steps that a computer follows exactly.","level":"beginner","goals":["Explain what a program is","Break a job into small, exact steps","Put steps in the right order"],"remember":"A program is a list of exact instructions. The computer runs them in order and never guesses.","terms":[{"term":"program","def":"A list of instructions that a computer follows."},{"term":"instruction","def":"One step in a program, such as “move forward”."},{"term":"code","def":"The text of a program, written in a programming language."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A step in a program is unclear. What does the computer do?","options":["Guesses what you meant","Does exactly what the step says, or stops with an error","Skips it quietly and asks you later"],"a":1,"why":"Computers follow instructions literally. That is why programs must be exact."},{"q":"You swap the order of two steps. What happens?","options":["Nothing changes, the steps are the same","The program can now do something different","The computer puts them back in order"],"a":1,"why":"Order is part of the program. Steps run from top to bottom."}]},{"id":"first-program","title":"Your first program","tagline":"Write one line of Kotlin and see it print.","level":"beginner","goals":["Write and run a complete Kotlin program","Print a line of text with <code>println</code>","Know where a program starts: <code>fun main()</code>"],"remember":"A program starts at fun main(). println(\"...\") prints one line of text.","terms":[{"term":"Kotlin","def":"The programming language used in this course and in most Android apps."},{"term":"main","def":"Where a Kotlin program starts. Written <code>fun main() { }</code>."},{"term":"println","def":"Prints a line of text in the output."},{"term":"output","def":"Where a program shows its results, like the text a program prints."}],"quiz":[{"q":"Where does a Kotlin program start?","options":["At the first <code>println</code>","At <code>fun main()</code>","At the last line"],"a":1,"why":"<code>main</code> is the starting point. Its steps run top to bottom."},{"q":"What does this print?<pre class=\"code\"><code>println(\"Hi\")\nprintln(\"there\")</code></pre>","options":["<code>Hi there</code> on one line","<code>Hi</code>, then <code>there</code> on the next line","<code>there</code>, then <code>Hi</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Each <code>println</code> prints its own line, in order."}]},{"id":"comments","title":"Comments","tagline":"Notes in your code that the computer ignores.","level":"beginner","goals":["Write notes in code with <code>//</code> and <code>/* */</code>","Turn a line of code off by commenting it out"],"remember":"// and /* */ are notes for people. Kotlin ignores them, so they also turn code off.","terms":[{"term":"comment","def":"A note in code that the computer ignores. <code>// ...</code> or <code>/* ... */</code>."},{"term":"commenting out","def":"Turning a line of code off by making it a comment."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Kotlin do with a comment?","options":["Prints it","Ignores it","Runs it last"],"a":1,"why":"Comments are for people. The computer skips them."},{"q":"Which line is a comment?","options":["<code>println(\"// hi\")</code>","<code>// println(\"hi\")</code>","Both"],"a":1,"why":"In the first one, <code>//</code> is inside quotes, so it is just text to print."}]},{"id":"text","title":"Printing text","tagline":"Text goes in double quotes. Kotlin calls it a String.","level":"beginner","goals":["Write text in double quotes as a <code>String</code>","Print text exactly as written","Spot why text without quotes fails"],"remember":"Text goes in double quotes and prints exactly as written. That text is called a String.","terms":[{"term":"String","def":"A piece of text, written in double quotes: <code>\"like this\"</code>."},{"term":"empty string","def":"<code>\"\"</code>: text with nothing in it."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>println(\"10 - 3\")</code> print?","options":["<code>7</code>","<code>10 - 3</code>","<code>\"10 - 3\"</code> with the quotes"],"a":1,"why":"Quotes make it text. The quotes themselves are not printed."},{"q":"What is a String?","options":["A piece of text","A whole number","A line of output"],"a":0,"why":"A String is text, written in double quotes."}]},{"id":"numbers","title":"Numbers and arithmetic","tagline":"Without quotes, Kotlin does the maths.","level":"beginner","goals":["Add, subtract, multiply and divide with <code>Int</code> numbers","Know which sums Kotlin works out first","Use brackets to choose the order"],"remember":"Numbers without quotes are calculated. * and / go before + and -, brackets go first of all.","terms":[{"term":"Int","def":"A whole number, like <code>3</code>, <code>0</code> or <code>-12</code>."},{"term":"operator","def":"A symbol that does something to values, like <code>+</code> or <code>*</code>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>println(10 - 2 * 3)</code> print?","options":["<code>24</code>","<code>4</code>","<code>10 - 2 * 3</code>"],"a":1,"why":"<code>2 * 3</code> is 6 first, then 10 − 6 is 4."},{"q":"Which line prints <code>9</code>?","options":["<code>println(\"4 + 5\")</code>","<code>println(4 + 5)</code>","Both"],"a":1,"why":"Only the version without quotes does the maths."}]},{"id":"decimals","title":"Decimal numbers","tagline":"Numbers with a point, and the surprise in whole-number division.","level":"beginner","goals":["Calculate with decimal numbers (<code>Double</code>)","Explain why <code>7 / 2</code> gives 3","Find the remainder with <code>%</code>"],"remember":"Int / Int drops the decimals. Use a Double like 7.0 to keep them. % gives the remainder.","terms":[{"term":"Double","def":"A number with a decimal point, like <code>3.5</code>."},{"term":"remainder","def":"What is left over after dividing. Written <code>%</code>: <code>7 % 2</code> is 1."}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does <code>println(9 / 2)</code> print?","options":["<code>4.5</code>","<code>4</code>","<code>5</code>"],"a":1,"why":"Whole divided by whole drops the decimals. Nothing is rounded up."},{"q":"What does <code>println(9 % 2)</code> print?","options":["<code>1</code>","<code>4</code>","<code>4.5</code>"],"a":0,"why":"2 fits into 9 four times, with 1 left over."},{"q":"Which gives <code>4.5</code>?","options":["<code>9 / 2</code>","<code>9.0 / 2</code>","<code>9 % 2</code>"],"a":1,"why":"With a decimal number involved, the answer keeps its decimals."}]},{"id":"errors","title":"When code doesn’t work","tagline":"Errors are normal. Here is how to read them.","level":"beginner","goals":["Tell a compile error from a crash","Read an error message to find the problem","Fix the first error first"],"remember":"Errors are normal. Compile errors stop the program before it starts; crashes stop it partway. Read the message, fix the first one.","terms":[{"term":"error","def":"A message saying something in the program went wrong."},{"term":"compile error","def":"A problem Kotlin finds before running, such as a typo. Nothing runs."},{"term":"crash","def":"A problem while the program runs, such as dividing by zero. It stops at that step."},{"term":"bug","def":"A mistake in a program that makes it do the wrong thing."}],"quiz":[{"q":"You misspell <code>println</code> on the last line. What prints?","options":["Everything except the last line","Nothing: it is a compile error","Everything, with a warning"],"a":1,"why":"Compile errors are found before the program runs, so nothing runs."},{"q":"A program crashes on its third step. Did steps one and two run?","options":["Yes","No"],"a":0,"why":"A crash happens while running. The steps before it already happened."},{"q":"Your code shows three errors. Which do you fix first?","options":["The last one","The first one","The longest message"],"a":1,"why":"One mistake can cause the ones after it. Fixing the first often fixes the rest."}]}]}]);
